Book E - New Properties

Part 1 - Wakefulness

A person can be awake or asleep. A person is usually awake.

Part 2 - Hollowness

[Just the telescope, here. I considered making the definition 'A thing can be tubular', but that rather unfortunately invites the opposite term, 'grody'; so I decided otherwise. It's used for the 'looking through' action, defined above, and intended for keyholes, lenses, magic mirrors and other such apertures.]

A thing can be eyeable. A thing is usually not eyeable.

Part 3 - Clutter

A thing can be clutter. A thing is usually not clutter.

Part 4 - Known knowns, known unknowns, unknown unknowns, etc

[This is for subtleties involving names: an indistinct figure who we later find out is named the Duchess Du Mer, for example, begins life unknown and later becomes known. "Understand" lines and "printing the name of" rules can be written to take this into account.]

A thing can be known or unknown. A thing is usually known.

Book F - General changes to the world model

Last unlocking rule when the player has Horloge's keys:
	rule succeeds with result Horloge's keys;

Last unlocking rule when the player has my lucky clock key:
	rule succeeds with result my lucky clock key;

Part 1 - Unexamined / Examined

A thing can be examined or unexamined. 

Carry out examining something: 
	now the noun is examined.

Part 2 - Upstairs and downstairs

Understand "upstairs" as up.
Understand "downstairs" as down.

Part 3 - Cant Go message

[ Definition: a direction (called that way) is viable if the room that way from the location is a room and that way is not inside and that way is not outside. ]

After assembling available exits:
	remove {inside, outside} from the viable directions, if present;

The last instead of going nowhere:
	try listing exits instead;

[ The last instead of going nowhere when the number of viable directions is 1:
	say "The only way to go from here is [random viable direction].";

The last instead of going nowhere when the number of viable directions is at least 2:
	say "The only ways to go from here are [list of viable directions]."; ]

The replacement listing exits rule is listed instead of the Textfyre standard listing exits rule in the Report listing exits rules.

This is the replacement listing exits rule:
	if the viable directions is not empty:
		if the number of entries in the viable directions is 1:
			say "The only way to go from here is [viable directions].";
		otherwise:
			say "The only ways to go from here are [viable directions].";
	otherwise:
		say "There are no obvious exits from here.";

Book G - Conversation Tokens

Understand "calvin", "drake", "calvin and drake", "drake and calvin" as "[calvinanddrake]".
Understand "figure", "figure in grey/gray", "grey/gray figure" as "[figure]".

Understand "abbot", "abbott", "gubbler" as "[abbot]".

Understand "bishop", "archbishop", "arch-bishop", "steelspring", "steel-spring", "steel spring" as "[archbishop]".

Understand "cathedral", "cathedral of time", "church" as "[cathedral]".
Understand "abbey", "abbey of time" as "[abbey]".

Understand "reloh", "brother reloh" as "[reloh]".
Understand "sa'at", "brother sa'at", "saat", "brother saat" as "[sa'at]".
Understand "horloge", "brother horloge" as "[horloge]".

Understand "cook", "[calvinanddrake]", "[reloh]", "[horloge]" as "[abbeyfolk]"

Understand "clockwork", "clock-work", "klockwerk", "clock work", "clocks" as "[clockwork]".
Understand "saints" or "saint" or "ykea" or "maxwell" or "newton" or "babbage" or "breguet" or "st maxwell" or "st newton" or "st ykea" or "st babbage" or "st breguet" or "designers" or "inventors" or "euclid" as "[saints]".

Understand "me" or "myself" or "yourself" or "you" or "self" or "Wren" or "story" or "my story" as "[me]".
Understand "themselves", "themself" as "[himself]".
Understand "him" or "himself" as "[himself]".
Understand "her" or "herself" as "[himself]".
Understand "[himself]" as "[herself]". [in the interests of full equality] 

Understand "perpetuum" or "mobile" as "[perpetuum]".
Understand "stealing" or "theft" or "robbery" or "plan" or "plot" or "vault" or "vaults" or "crypt" as "[plot]".

Book 0 - Prologue

Part 1 - Introduction

When play begins:
	select the prologue channel;
	say "[my description][line break]";	
	say "'<Italic>HEY WREN![no line break]</Italic>'[paragraph break]Uh-oh.[paragraph break]'We've got a job for you!'[paragraph break]'Yeah, a promotion!'[paragraph break]This, coming from [as a noun]Drake and Calvin[end as], means something horrible is about to happen.[paragraph break]'You've got your [as a noun]rag[end as], don't you?'[paragraph break]'And your [as a noun]polish[end as]?'[paragraph break]'Yes,' I bleat, holding up the [as a noun]glass tumbler[end as] they've given me.[paragraph break]'Course you do. Well, we've got something for you to do.'[paragraph break]'Yeah,' chimes [as a noun]Calvin[end as].[paragraph break]One on each arm, they march me down the hall, towards the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]door[end as]. Am I in trouble? Going to be thrown out of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]?[paragraph break]'On three!' shouts [as a noun]Drake[end as].[paragraph break]'Tick!' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] counts. 'Tick! <Italic>Tock![no line break]</Italic>'[paragraph break]They shove me through the [as a noun]door[end as]. I fall into the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s personal chamber. I might not know much about the Abbey but I do know that I <Italic>really</Italic> shouldn't be in here.[paragraph break]";
	select the main channel;

Part 2 - Wren

Chapter 1 - Description of Wren

Rule for printing the description of the player:
	say "[my description]".

To say my description:
	say "When the monks took me, aged six months, into their care, they named me Wren. Maybe because I was small, insignificant, and happy to eat any crumbs they threw my way. But these days I'm Wren, 2nd Assistant Clock Polisher... and that's a role that's about as important in the workings of the Cathedral of Time as the large deaf man who re-stretches the worn-out springs.";

Chapter 2 - Wren's Possessions

A thing called my lucky clock key is privately-named, worn by the player. The description is "The one thing I've had all my life. It's been there, on a string around my neck, for as long as I can remember. I keep hoping that it's a last gift from my <Italic>true</Italic> family, and one day it'll open a treasure-chest or the door of one of the Great Houses. But then again, maybe it's just something I tried to eat as a baby. Either way, I've yet to find a lock it'll turn or a clock it'll wind." The printed name is "my lucky clock key". Understand "my", "lucky", "clock key", "lucky clock key", "key", "string" as the lucky clock key. 


Instead of dropping the lucky key:
	say "What? Lose my [as a noun]lucky clock key[end as]? And then what would I have left?";

Instead of putting the lucky key on something:
	try dropping the lucky key instead;

Instead of inserting the lucky key into a container:
	if the container is not held by the player:
		try dropping the lucky key instead;

Instead of throwing the lucky key at something:
	try dropping the lucky key instead;

Does the player mean doing something with the lucky key:
	it is unlikely;

A rag is carried by the player. The description is "The tool of the polisher's trade. And this old [as a noun]rag[end as] looks like it's been the tool of several polishers before me, too."

Instead of wearing the rag:
	say "Maybe I was small enough for that once, but now the [as a noun]rag[end as] would barely cover my face."

Understand "old", "dirty", "polishing" as the rag.

A tumbler is a container, open, carried by the player. The description is "A small [as a noun]glass[end as] with all that's left of my daily wood [as a noun]polish[end as] ration. How I'm supposed to do all the rest of my chores after this I don't know, but [as a noun]Drake and Calvin[end as] didn't seem too worried about that." 

Understand "glass tumbler", "goblet" as the tumbler. 
Understand "glass" as the tumbler when the number of visible windows is zero.

Rule for printing room description details of the tumbler:
	do nothing.

The printed name of the tumbler is "glass tumbler".

Understand "cup" as the tumbler when the player cannot see the teacup.

Rule for deciding whether all includes the small amount of polish: it does not.

Rule for emptying away the small amount of polish when in Inside Clock Case:
	remove the small amount of polish from play;
	say "Oh... well, then. I tip out the last of the [as a noun]polish[end as] onto the floor of the [as a noun]clock[end as]. At least that part will shine.";

Instead of emptying the empty tumbler:
	say "The tumbler's empty already."

Instead of emptying the tumbler when Heading for the Cathedral has happened:
	remove the small amount of polish from play;
	say "I tip out the [as a noun]polish[end as]. I'm done with clock-faces for the day, that's for certain."

Instead of emptying the tumbler when not in Inside Clock Case:
	say "I'll need to save every last drop. There's twenty more clocks to do today at least!";



Instead of attacking the tumbler:
	say "I need it. Can't be a clock polisher if you don't have anywhere to carry your clock [as a noun]polish[end as]!"

Instead of cleaning the tumbler when in the Kitchen for the first time:
	say "I'll never get a [as a noun]glass[end as] that's had clock [as a noun]polish[end as] in it clean enough to drink from, if that's what you're thinking..."

Instead of cleaning the tumbler:
	say "The tumbler's had [as a noun]polish[end as] in it. In a way, it's the cleanest thing I've got. In another way, it'll never really be clean ever again. 'Serving your function is the only functional way to serve,' as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] might say."

Inside the tumbler is a small amount of polish. Understand "wood", "ration", "daily ration" as the small amount of polish.

Before examining the small amount of polish: try examining the tumbler instead.

Before putting the small amount of polish on something:
	try polishing the second noun with the small amount of polish instead.

Before drinking or tasting or eating the small amount of polish:
	say "No way. (They say [as a noun]Brother Reloh[end as] did that as a boy and that's why he ended up like he did.)" instead.

Before smelling the small amount of polish:
	say "It smells of old wood and metal plates." instead.

Before doing something when the small amount of polish is physically involved:
	redirect the action from the small amount of polish to the tumbler, and try that instead;

Before polishing something with the tumbler when the player carries the rag:
	redirect the action from the tumbler to the rag, and try that instead;
	
Part 3 - First scene

Introduction is a scene. Introduction begins when play begins. Introduction ends when the player is taken to the attic.

When Introduction ends:
	move Abbot Gubbler to Abbot's Quarters;
	now the Grandfather Clock is scenery; [suppressing initial appearance] 
	now the Grandfather Clock is closed;

Definition: a room is in the Introduction if it is the Abbot's Quarters or it is Inside Clock Case.

Part 4 - Pendulums and Penduluums

EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is a trigger. EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM can be primed. EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM  is not primed.

To say penduluum:
	say "penduluum";
	if EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is unfired:
		if EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is not primed:
			now EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is primed;
		else:
			now EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is fired;

Every turn when EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is fired and EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is primed:
	now EXPLAIN_PENDULUUM is not primed;
	say "(I should probably explain: there are [as a noun]pendulum[end as]s and [as a noun]penduluum[end as]s. A weight on a string, that's a [as a noun]pendulum[end as]. Once it's put into a clock it is sanctified by the clockwork, it becomes a <Italic>[as a noun]penduluum[end as]</Italic>. Anyone can make or swing or polish a [as a noun]pendulum[end as]. Only the most holy can touch a <Italic>[as a noun]penduluum[end as]</Italic>.)";



Book 1 - The Abbot's Quarters

Part 1 - The Abbot's Room

Chapter 1 - Description

The Abbot's Quarters is a room. "The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s Quarters again. [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] is here, standing by his [as a noun]desk[end as] (no chair, remember?). He's looking right at me, waiting for me to explain why I'm interrupting him, and why I haven’t run away back west yet."

Rule for printing the description of the Abbot's Quarters during the Introduction:
	say "If I thought Abbots lived in luxury, then I was dead wrong. Even my attic's cozier than this. The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s got no furniture at all, except a [as a noun]desk[end as] and a [as a noun]cot[end as], and no decoration except for a [as a noun]bust of St. Newton[end as]. None of the axle-mounted, bevelled mobiles I was expecting. There's barely even any sunlight: one thin [as a noun]window[end as] to catch the sunrise, opposite the [as a noun]door[end as] to the hallway back west.";
	if the Clock Case has not been hidden in:
		select the tutorial channel;
		say "Some of the things you can do here include: 'EXAMINE COT', 'EXAMINE DESK', 'LOOK UNDER COT', and 'LOOK AT CLOCK'. You could also try to 'POLISH THE CLOCK' in case Drake or Calvin check on your work. But they won't, don't worry.";
		select the main channel.

Rule for printing the description of the Abbot's Quarters when Clock Case has been hidden in during the Introduction:
	say "I need to get out of this place as fast as I can and without attracting any more attention. The [as a noun]door[end as] to the hallway is back west and as for polishing the [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as] - well, forget it!";
	select the tutorial channel;
	say "Time to go 'WEST'.";
	select the main channel.

Check listening to the Abbot's Quarters:
	say "The clock-gears are chanting quietly to themselves." instead;

Instead of making to leave when the player is in the Abbot's Quarters:
	try going west instead.

Instead of facing outside when in the Abbot's Quarters:
	try facing west instead;

Chapter 2 - Grandfather Clock

Section 1 - The Clock Itself

Gubbler's Grandfather clock is a closed door, in the Abbot's Quarters. "[if in Abbot's Quarters][as a noun]St. Newton[end as] is staring at the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s unpolished [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as] with a severe frown.[otherwise]A little light shines in through the [as a noun]keyhole[end as], which is almost covered by the Abbot's spare [as a noun]robe[end as] hanging inside the case.[end if]".

The description is "No wonder [as a noun]Calvin and Drake[end as] didn't want to do this. [as a noun]Abbot Gubbler[end as]'s [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as] is enormous: the [as a noun]face[end as] is the size of a dinnerplate and the [as a noun]cabinet[end as] below is big enough to be a wardrobe. Even just polishing the [as a noun]doors[end as] will take half an hour. The whole thing might take all day." 

Understand "Abbot Gubbler's", "Abbot's" as the Grandfather Clock.
Understand "face", "cabinet", "doors", "case" as the Grandfather Clock.

The Grandfather Clock is inside from Abbot's Quarters, outside from Inside Clock Case.

Instead of inserting something into the Grandfather Clock:
	say "I'm not here to litter his [as a noun]clock[end as] up with things.";
	
Instead of unlocking the Grandfather Clock with something:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as]'s not locked."

Instead of locking the Grandfather Clock with something:
	say "I don't have the key for it (and of course I don't, it's the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]clock[end as] and I'm a Second Assistant Polisher. I shouldn't even be <Italic>near</Italic> his [as a noun]clock[end as], let alone fiddling with it.)";

Instead of pushing or pulling or turning the Grandfather Clock:
	say "It's massive - much bigger than me!"

Instead of attacking the Grandfather Clock:
	say "Heresy. I'd be struck down by a counterweight where I stand!"

Instead of touching the Grandfather Clock:
	say "The wood is smooth and venerable and old. A bit like the [as a noun]Abbot[end as], only he's not so much venerable as veiny."

Section 2 - Actions - Polishing

Instead of polishing the Grandfather Clock with the rag:
	say "[one of]I whisper the correct mantra against the evils of Friction and Dust, and start polishing. This'll take forever![or]I polish a little more. I'm not getting anywhere![stopping]";

Instead of polishing the Grandfather Clock with the rag when Voices have been Heard during the Introduction:
	say "If you think the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] will come in here, and say, 'Oh, good, young Wren's polishing my [as a noun]clock[end as] all the time that <Italic>I</Italic> thought I was on my own in here,' then you must have come unsprung. If he finds me here now he'll have me declared unmechanistical and maybe even throw me out of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]!";

Before cleaning the Grandfather Clock:
	if the rag is carried:
		say "(with [the rag])[command clarification break]";
		try polishing the noun with the rag instead;
	otherwise:
		say "I can't polish anything without a [as a noun]cloth[end as]." instead.


Section 3 - Actions - opening

[This was dreadfully buggy, and the cause was implicit opening... well, just poor door handling here. ]

[Rule for implicitly opening the Grandfather Clock when in Abbot's Quarters and Clock Case has not been hidden in and Voices have not been Heard:
	do nothing;]

After opening the Grandfather Clock when the implicitly opening activity is not going on:
	say "I open the [as a noun]clock[end as] door and peek inside. [run paragraph on]";
	try searching the Grandfather Clock instead;

Instead of opening the Grandfather Clock when in the Abbot's Quarters and AB_QUART4 is fired and the implicitly opening activity is not going on and Inside Clock Case is not visited: [only auto-enter when the player hasn't auto-entered before ]
	try entering the Grandfather Clock instead.

Instead of searching the closed Grandfather Clock when in Abbot's Quarters:
	try opening the Grandfather Clock instead;

Before entering the closed Grandfather Clock when in Inside Clock Case during Hiding In The Clock:
	try opening the Grandfather Clock instead; [fails]

Before entering the closed Grandfather Clock when in Inside Clock Case:
	now the Grandfather Clock is open;
[	try silently opening the Grandfather Clock; [succeeds]]

Instead of searching the Grandfather Clock:
	say "For a big [as a noun]clock[end as] it's got a pretty small <Italic>[penduluum]</Italic>, about the size of [as a noun]Calvin[end as]'s fist and not as big as [as a noun]Drake[end as]'s. There's even enough space in front of it for the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] to hang up one of his spare [as a noun]robe[end as]s."

Instead of opening the Grandfather Clock when Hiding in the Clock has ended and the implicitly opening activity is not going on and the player is in Inside Clock Case:
	now the Grandfather Clock is open;
	try exiting instead.

Instead of opening the Grandfather Clock during Hiding in the Clock:
	say "You've got a spring missing if you think I can slip out, unnoticed, or come up with a decent excuse as to why I've been hiding in the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s grandfather. No, I'm staying put and hoping nothing happens to make the old man need to change his clothes.";

Instead of going nowhere when the location is Inside Clock case during Hiding in the clock:
	try opening the Grandfather Clock.

After going through the Grandfather Clock from Inside Clock Case to Abbot's Quarters during Introduction:
	now the player is casually overhearing;
	say "[one of]I spill gratefully out of the [as a noun]clock[end as], gasping for breath as though I've been underwater. I've just been holding it in, of course. As for what I've heard – well, I probably shouldn't have, and the quicker I can get out of here, the better.[once only]";
	continue the action;

Instead of making to leave when in Inside Clock Case:
	try entering the grandfather clock;

Section 4 - Actions - entering

Instead of going through the Grandfather Clock from Abbot's Quarters when Voices have not been heard:
	say "I should polish the outside of the [as a noun]clock[end as] first, and that'll probably take more than long enough."

Instead of going through the Grandfather Clock when in Abbot's Quarters and Voices have been heard and Hiding in the Clock has not happened:
	repeat with x running through the current script begin;
		now x is fired;
	end repeat;
	change the current script to { CCASE1 };

Instead of hiding inside the Grandfather Clock when in Abbot's Quarters and Voices have been heard and Hiding in the Clock has not happened:
	try entering the noun instead;

Instead of hiding behind the Grandfather Clock when in Abbot's Quarters and Voices have been heard and Hiding in the Clock has not happened:
	say "No space behind - but plenty inside. [run paragraph on]";
	try entering the noun instead;

Instead of hiding from view when Voices have been heard and the Grandfather Clock is not visited and the Introduction is happening:
	say "Where? There's the [as a noun]cot[end as], the [as a noun]table[end as], but none of those seem too good..."

Section 5 - Components - Clock Door and Keyhole

The clock-door is privately-named, part of the Grandfather Clock. The description is "The [as a noun]clock door[end as] is smooth lacquered wood, finest quality: no knotholes or splinters here! It's covered with the usual [as a noun]springs[end as] and [as a noun]levers[end as], and a nice [as a noun]gear-train motif[end as] around the [as a noun]keyhole[end as]."

Understand "usual", "springs", "levers", "smooth", "lacquered", "wood", "wooden" as the clock-door.

The printed name is "door of the clock".
Understand "[something related by reversed incorporation] door" as the clock-door.
Understand "door of [something related by reversed incorporation]" as the clock-door.
Understand "door" as the clock-door when the location is Inside Clock Case.

Instead of opening the clock-door: try opening the Grandfather Clock;
Instead of closing the clock-door: try closing the Grandfather Clock;
Instead of entering the clock-door: try entering the Grandfather Clock;

Instead of unlocking the clock-door with something:
	try unlocking the Grandfather Clock with the second noun instead.

Instead of locking the clock-door with something:
	try locking the Grandfather Clock with the second noun instead.

Instead of polishing the clock-door with something:
	try polishing the Grandfather Clock with the second noun;

Instead of cleaning the clock-door:
	if the player is carrying the rag:
		try polishing the Grandfather Clock with the rag;
	else:
		say "I can't polish anything without my cloth."

Instead of pushing the clock-door in Inside Clock Case:
	try opening the clock-door instead;

Instead of pulling the clock-door in Abbot's Quarters:
	try opening the clock-door instead;

A keyhole is part of the clock-door. The description of the keyhole is "[if the location is the Abbot's Quarters or the Grandfather Clock is open]It's just a [as a noun]keyhole[end as]. It might be only ornamental.[otherwise if Hiding in the Clock is happening]I can make out the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s hands, which are shaking badly. But I can see nothing of the [as a noun]Figure[end as].[otherwise]Through the keyhole I can see the corner of the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]desk[end as], but the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] and the [as a noun]Figure[end as] have gone."

Understand "conversation" as the keyhole when Hiding in the Clock is happening.
Understand "gear-train", "gear", "train", "motif" as the keyhole.

Instead of inserting something into the keyhole:
	try unlocking the clock-door with the noun instead.

Instead of unlocking the keyhole with something:
	try unlocking the Grandfather Clock with the second noun instead.

Instead of locking the keyhole with something:
	try locking the Grandfather Clock with the second noun instead.

Instead of polishing the keyhole with something:
	try polishing the Grandfather Clock with the second noun;

Instead of cleaning the keyhole:
	if the player is carrying the rag:
		try polishing the Grandfather Clock with the rag;
	else:
		say "I can't polish anything without my [as a noun]cloth[end as]."

Instead of approaching or entering the keyhole:
	say "My name's Wren, not Worm!"

Instead of searching the keyhole:
	try examining the keyhole instead;

Section 6 - Scoping and Reaching into the Clock

After deciding the scope of the player when the location is Abbot's Quarters and the Grandfather Clock is open:
	place the contents of Inside Clock Case in scope;

Rule for reaching inside Inside Clock Case when the Grandfather Clock is open:
	allow access;

Chapter 3 - Exits

Instead of going through the Abbot's Door when AB_QUART1 is unfired:
	say "I'm not staying here. I walk straight out again... but [as a noun]Drake[end as]'s waiting to push me back.[paragraph break]'Polish the [as a noun]clock[end as], loose-screw!' he clangs. 'And don't come out till it's shining!'";
	remove AB_QUART1 from the current script, if present;
	add AB_QUART1A at entry 1 in the current script;

Instead of going through the Abbot's Door when Voices have not been heard:
	say "Curses to counterweights! It's not on my rota, this [as a noun]clock[end as], so why should I finish off my [as a noun]polish[end as] on it? I head for the doorway – then freeze. Voices, coming down the hall. It's the [as a noun]Abbot Gubbler[end as] himself, coming this way!";
	remove AB_QUART2 from the current script, if present;
	remove AB_QUART_PAUSE from the current script, if present;
	remove AB_QUART3 from the current script, if present;
	add AB_QUART3A at entry 1 in the current script; [dummy event for good timing]

Instead of going through the Abbot's Door when Clock Case has not been hidden in:
	say "The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s voice is getting nearer... If he sees I've been in here, he'll have me turning the paddles in the laundry room for a month! There's nothing for it, I'll <Italic>have</Italic> to escape, and some other way than the door!";

After going west through the Abbot's Door when Hiding in the Clock has ended during Introduction:
	say "I'm going to have to tell someone what I just heard. A theft, from the Vaults! But if the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s involved – who else might be? After all, the [as a noun]figure[end as] he was talking to could have been <Italic>anyone</Italic> in the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]!";
	continue the action;

Rule for implicitly opening the Abbot's Door:
	[no command clarification message]
	try silently opening the Abbot's Door; 

After going through the Abbot's Door from the Corridor of Contemplation when Introduction has ended:
	say "I knock before entering the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s study.";
	continue the action;

Rule for printing the description of the Abbot's Door:
	if Hiding in the Clock has ended and Introduction is happening:
		say "That's the way I need to go to get out of here.";
	otherwise if Hiding in the Clock is happening:
		say "I can't see the door from here. I wish I could do <Italic>more</Italic> than see it.";
	otherwise if Voices have been heard:
		say "People are coming this way!";
	otherwise:
		say "It's a heavy oak [as a noun]door[end as].";

[Rule for implicitly opening the closed Abbot's Door when Hiding in the Clock has ended:
	try opening the Abbot's Door;
	consider the scene changing rules;
	rule fails;]

[ The point of the following rule is to force a one move gap between exiting the clock and exiting the Abbot's Quarters, to spread out the reaction messages. Yes, this means that going out will just open the door, but I think that's okay. ] 

 Before going through the closed Abbot's Door when Hiding in the Clock has ended:
	try opening the Abbot's Door instead; 

Report opening the Abbot's Door when Hiding in the Clock has ended:
	say "I haul open the [as a noun]door[end as]." instead.

Chapter 4 - Scenery Items
	
Section 1 - Cot

The Abbot's cot is scenery, in the Abbot's Quarters. "The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]cot[end as] is a hard wooden pallet, with no pillow and one small [as a noun]blanket[end as]. 'Mustn't sleep,' I can hear him saying. 'Time doesn't sleep. Sleep is our great weakness.'" Understand "bed", "pallet", "blanket" as the cot.

Instead of taking the cot:
	say "Why would I want to go wandering off with the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s bed-things?"

Instead of entering the cot:
	say "No thanks. It looks horribly uncomfortable. And coming from me, considering where <Italic>I</Italic> sleep, that's really saying something."

Instead of hiding under the cot when Voices have been heard during Introduction:
	if Inside Clock Case is visited:
		say "I don't want to hide, I want to get out of here!" instead;
	say "The space under the [as a noun]cot[end as] is too small for me! Somewhere else, and quick!" instead;

Instead of looking under the cot when Voices have been heard during Introduction:
	try hiding under the cot;

Instead of looking under the cot:
	say "There's nothing of interest under the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]cot[end as]. No spare [as a noun]polish[end as], for example." instead;

Instead of cleaning the cot:
	say "I'm having nothing to do with it. The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] probably sheds his dead skins there every night."

Section 2 - Desk

The Abbot's desk is scenery, in the Abbot's Quarters. "[as a noun]Gubbler[end as]'s [as a noun]desk[end as] is bare and old. The surface is scratched a little with [as a noun]geometric designs[end as]." Understand "table" as the desk.

Instead of pushing or taking or pulling or turning the desk:
	say "I can't shift it (and I'd be in trouble if I did)."

Instead of polishing the desk with the rag:
	say "But I'm going to need all the [as a noun]polish[end as] I've got for the [as a noun]clock[end as]!";

Instead of cleaning the Abbot's desk:
	say "But I'm going to need all the [as a noun]polish[end as] I've got for the [as a noun]clock[end as]!";

Instead of cleaning the Abbot's desk when Voices have been Heard during introduction:
	say "No way. I need to get out of here, and fast.";

Instead of polishing the Abbot's desk with the rag when Voices have been Heard during introduction:
	say "No way. I need to get out of here, and fast.";

Instead of entering the Abbot's desk:
	say "I'm not trying to get myself thrown out of here, you know!"

Instead of hiding under the desk when Voices have been heard during Introduction:
	say "The space under the [as a noun]desk[end as] is too small for me! Somewhere else, and quick!" instead;

Instead of looking under the desk when Voices have been heard during Introduction:
	try hiding under the desk instead;

Instead of looking under the desk:
	say "There's nothing of interest under the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]desk[end as]. No spare [as a noun]polish[end as], for example." instead;

Instead of opening the desk:
	say "I'm not here to go rummaging around in the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s papers. They'd have me thrown into prison for spying!";

Instead of searching the desk:
	try examining the geometric designs.

Some geometric designs are part of the Abbot's desk. Understand "scratch", "surface", "table's/desk's/table/desk surface/top", "scratches", "design" as the geometric designs.

Rule for printing the description of the geometric designs: say "Years of designers have imprinted the footprints of their thoughts into the surface of the wood itself. In another hundred years, the desk itself will be a Relic in the Cathedral Reliquary." 

Instead of polishing the geometric designs with the rag:
	say "They might be important: I shouldn't just fill them over."

Instead of cleaning the geometric designs:
	say "But they might be important!"

Section 3 - Bust of St Newton

The Bust of St Isaac Newton is a man, scenery, in the Abbot's Quarters. "I can feel the well-fitted eyes of [as a noun]St. Newton[end as] gazing right down into my workings. Quickly, I make the sign of <Italic>[penduluum]</Italic> and look away." The printed name is "bust of St. Newton". Understand "icon", "head", ["face", [removed - conflict with the clock face] ]"eyes", "frown", "severe", "of" as the Bust.
Understand "saint", "st", "isaac", "newton", "the", "thinker" as the Bust.

Before doing something when the bust of St Newton is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "It's clear from his expression that [as a noun]St. Newton[end as] disapproves of that idea." instead;

Rule for speaking with the bust of St Isaac Newton:
	say "You're losing your marbles, Wren, if you think you can talk to <Italic>that</Italic>."

Section 4 - Window

A window called the barred window is scenery, in the Abbot's Quarters. "The narrow-barred [as a noun]window[end as] catches the morning sunshine. The same sunshine that [as a noun]Drake and Calvin[end as] are out enjoying while I'm stuck in here." Understand "bar", "bars", "sun", "sunshine", "sun-rise", "sunrise", "sun rise", "sun shine", "narrow-barred", "morning" as the window.

Instead of entering the barred window:
	try opening the barred window;

Instead of opening the barred window:
	say "The bars are too narrow to slip through, and too firm to break.";

Section 5 - Door

An open door called the Abbot's Door is scenery. The Abbot's Door is west of the Abbot's Quarters. The Abbot's Door is east of the Corridor of Contemplation.

Rule for printing the description of the Abbot's Door when in the Corridor of Contemplation during Introduction:
	say "There's no-one in the Abbot's Quarters now.";

Rule for printing the description of the Abbot's Door when in the Corridor of Contemplation:
	say "It sounds like there's someone inside.";

Chapter 5 - Scripted Events

Section 1 - Intro

AB_QUART1 is a scripted event. The display text is "'And don't come out till that [as a noun]clock[end as]'s shining!' shouts [as a noun]Drake[end as] from the hall.[paragraph break]'Yeah,' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] says. 'It's got to look as good as if we did it!'[paragraph break]'Idiot,' [as a noun]Drake[end as] mutters. 'Come on.'[paragraph break]Their voices disappear down the hall."

AB_QUART1A is a scripted event. The display text is "'Yeah,' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] says. 'It's got to look as good as if we did it!'[paragraph break]'Idiot,' [as a noun]Drake[end as] mutters. 'Come on.'[paragraph break]Their voices disappear down the hall."

AB_QUART_PAUSE is a scripted event. The display text is "This isn't fair. I'm just a Second Assistant Clock Polisher. I should be watching someone else polishing a [as a noun]clock[end as]. And I've only got one [as a noun]glass tumbler[end as] half full of [as a noun]polish[end as]: it'll take a miracle to do the whole thing."

After firing AB_QUART1A:
	now AB_QUART1 is fired;

AB_QUART2 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]grandfather clock[end as] ticks off a minute with a stately <Italic>thunk</Italic>. Quickly, I make the sign of the winding gear."

Section 2 - Voices heard

AB_QUART3 is a scripted event. The display text is "From the hallway outside comes the echo of voices. Too shaky to be [as a noun]Calvin or Drake[end as]. More like – oh, no. It's the [as a noun]Abbot[end as], coming back to his room. And if he finds me here, then my springs won't be worth the spindles they're sprung to..."

AB_QUART3A is a scripted event. [A dummy 'Voices heard', for cases where the player directly triggers the event.] 

After firing AB_QUART3:
	move the glimpsed-Abbot backdrop to all rooms that are the Abbot's Quarters;
	select the tutorial channel;
	say "Try to 'HIDE IN THE CLOCK'.";
	select the main channel.

After firing AB_QUART3A:
	now AB_QUART3 is fired;

To decide if Voices have been Heard:
	if AB_QUART3 is fired, yes;
	no;

To decide if Voices have not been Heard:
	if Voices have been heard, no;
	yes;

AB_QUART4 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s voice is getting closer. The old man may move slower than a short hand, but he's definitely coming this way."

AB_QUART4B is a scripted event. The display text is "Oh, no! I shouldn't <Italic>be</Italic> here!"

[Added for tutorial. --JM]
[After firing AB_QUART4B: jump to tutorial step 7.]

AB_QUART5 is a scripted event. The display text is "Oh, widdershins! [as a noun]Abbot Gubbler[end as] is right outside the door! I'd better hide <Italic>somewhere</Italic>!"

Chapter 6 - Glimpses of people

Section 1 - Glimpsed-Abbot

Glimpsed-Abbot is an animate glimpse backdrop. Understand "[abbot]" as the glimpsed-Abbot. The printed name is "Abbot Gubbler". "[if Inside Clock Case is unvisited]He's coming this way![else]He's gone - for now, at least![end if]"

Instead of approaching the glimpsed-Abbot when in the Abbot's Quarters:
	try going west instead;

Every turn when in the Corridor of Contemplation:
	remove the glimpsed-Abbot from play;

Section 2 - Glimpsed-Figure

Intro-glimpsed-Figure is an animate glimpse backdrop. Understand "[figure]" as the Intro-glimpsed-Figure. "I didn't see the [as a noun]Figure[end as] clearly." The printed name is "figure in grey".

Instead of approaching the Intro-glimpsed-Figure:
	say "No thanks. I hope I never run into that Figure again.";

Every turn when in the Corridor of Contemplation:
	remove the Intro-glimpsed-Figure from play;

Section 3 - Glimpsed-Calvin and Drake

Intro-glimpsed-CandD is an animate glimpse backdrop in the Abbot's Quarters, in Inside Clock Case, in Corridor of Contemplation. Understand "[calvinanddrake]" as the Intro-glimpsed-CandD. The printed name is "Calvin and Drake". "[as a noun]Calvin and Drake[end as] are bigger than me, meaner, and I could do a lot better without them."

Instead of approaching Intro-glimpsed-CandD:
	try going west instead;

Every turn when in the Attic Room:
	remove the Intro-glimpsed-CandD from play;


Part 2 - Inside the Clock

Chapter 1 - Description

There is an unceilinged, aerial room called Inside Clock Case.

The description of Inside Clock Case is "I'm wedged inside the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]Grandfather Clock[end as], [penduluum] at my back. Time to go, I think!"

Rule for printing the description of Inside Clock Case when Hiding in the Clock has not ended and the player is casually overhearing:
	say "As the phrase goes, I'm stuck between a rack and a gear-trace; except here I'm in the narrow gap between the [as a noun]clock[end as] case [as a noun]door[end as] and the heavy swinging <Italic>[penduluum]</Italic> behind. And if I get in the way of that, and disrupt the [as a noun]clock[end as]'s holy timings... well, there's no way they wouldn't notice when the clock-hands stopped moving, let's just say.";
	select the tutorial channel;
	say "Try to 'LISTEN WITH TUMBLER' and then 'LISTEN'. You can always repeat a command with the shortcut 'G' or simply 'WAIT' with the shortcut 'Z'.";
	select the main channel.

Rule for printing the description of Inside Clock Case when Hiding in the Clock has not ended:
	say "I'm leaning up against the [as a noun]door[end as] of the [as a noun]clock[end as], with my ear pressed against the [as a noun]glass tumbler[end as]. The [penduluum] behind is like someone breathing in my ear. If I'm caught in here, I'm dead.";
	select the tutorial channel;
	say "Just 'LISTEN' and when they leave, you can go 'OUT'.";
	select the main channel.

Index map with Inside Clock Case mapped east of Abbot's Quarters.

Index map with Abbot's Quarters mapped east of Corridor of Contemplation.

Instead of shouting at someone when in Inside Clock Case:
	say "[one of]What? Pretend to be a ghost and hope to scare them away?[or]No chance. That would be madness.[stopping]";

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Robe

A spare robe is scenery, in Inside Clock Case. "It's a thick black woollen habit, with a long deep hood and sleeves big enough to fit a five-and-a-quarter transmission with room to spare. Being the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]robe[end as] means it has a [as a noun]softer lining[end as], and a [as a noun]little sewn-in pocket[end as] for his tissues, spectacles, and that sort of thing." The printed name is "robe".
Understand "Abbot's", "robes", "hood", "sleeve", "sleeves", "soft/softer", "lining" as the spare robe.

Before wearing the spare robe:
	say "One day, maybe, once I've worked my way up to the status of clock-watcher and can start learning the Twelve Devotions. But not yet." instead;

Before searching the spare robe when in Inside Clock Case:
	say "I peek into the pockets looking for an ear-trumpet to use on the door... but don't find one there." instead.

Before searching the spare robe:
	say "I can't go peeking into the Abbot's things!" instead.

Before doing something when the spare robe is physically involved:
	say "Better not. My fingers are all greasy with [as a noun]polish[end as]." instead;

The little sewn-in pocket is a container, open, not openable, part of the spare robe. Understand "pockets", "pockets/pocket of [something related by reversed incorporation]" as the sewn-in pocket.

Instead of doing something when the little sewn-in pocket is physically involved:
	say "Better not. My fingers are all greasy with polish.";

Instead of examining or searching the little sewn-in pocket:
	try searching the spare robe instead;

Section 2 - Penduluum

The penduluum is scenery in Inside Clock Case. "The <Italic>[penduluum]</Italic> is a large amount of Holy Brass, properly blessed and bevelled. It swings back and forth, raising the [as a noun]weight of Precision[end as] against the [as a noun]counterweight of Slapdashery[end as]." The printed name is "[penduluum]".

Understand "mechanism", "workings", "brass", "holy", "blessed", "bevelled", "beveled", "weight", "counterweight", "precision", "slapdashery", "pendulum" as the penduluum.

Instead of doing something when the penduluum is physically involved and the current action is physical:
	say "To stop a [as a noun]clock[end as] is murder. To shift the balance... that's something worse still.";
	
Instead of listening to the penduluum:
	say "A steady ticking: the heartbeat of the World."

Instead of smelling the penduluum:
	say "A smell of flax oil and brass."

Section 2b - Weights

A clock-weight is a kind of thing.

The weight of Precision is scenery, clock-weight in Inside Clock Case. "The [as a noun]weight of Precision[end as] falls in delicate steps, moving - the Abbot says - like a dancer across a ballroom floor. Not something I've seen myself."

The counterweight of Slapdashery is scenery, clock-weight in Inside Clock Case. "The [as a noun]counterweight of Slapdashery[end as] climbs its chain in fits and jerks."

Understand "weights" as the plural of clock-weight.

Instead of pushing or pulling or taking a clock-weight:
	say "Interfering with the internal mechanisms of a [as a noun]clock[end as] is unthinkable!"

Section 3 - Abbot Gubbler (through the keyhole)

The keyhole-abbot is a man, privately-named, proper-named, scenery. [initially out of play] "I can't see much through the [as a noun]keyhole[end as], except the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s stout waist and shaking hands. Not much good for winding anything now, but if I said as much to anyone then I'd be in real trouble." The printed name is "Abbot Gubbler".

Understand "man", "men", "old", "person", "people", "abbot", "gubbler", "hands" as the keyhole-abbot.

Before listening to the keyhole-Abbot:
	try listening to the clock-door instead.

Instead of doing something when the keyhole-abbot is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "Draw attention to myself? I may be honest, but I'm not stupid. If I get kicked out of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] I'll be nothing but a street urchin for the rest of a very short life."

Section 4 - Figure in Grey (through the keyhole)

The keyhole-figure is a man, privately-named, scenery. [initially out of play] "The glimpse I caught earlier was a tall – man? – wearing a suit of grey leather and a long flowing cape. Now I can't see him at all – it's almost as though he knows I'm hiding, and he's avoiding the keyhole's line of sight." The printed name is "figure in grey".

Understand "figure in", "man in", "grey", "gray", "man", "men", "person", "people", "figure", "cape", "leather", "suit" as the keyhole-figure.

Before listening to the keyhole-figure:
	try listening to the clock-door instead.

Instead of doing something when the keyhole-figure is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "Draw attention to myself? I may be honest, but I'm not stupid. If I get kicked out of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] I'll be nothing but a street urchin for the rest of a very short life."

Section 5 - Monk (through the keyhole)

The keyhole-monk is a man, privately-named, scenery. "I can't see him." Understand "monk", "third voice" as the keyhole-monk. The printed name is "monk".

Instead of doing something when the keyhole-monk is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "The [as a noun]monk[end as] is outside, and wouldn't help me anyway.";

Before listening to the keyhole-monk:
	try listening to the clock-door instead.


Chapter 4 - Listening puzzle

Yourself can be casually overhearing or eavesdropping. Yourself is casually overhearing.

To eavesdrop-in-clock:
	if the tumbler contains something:
		try emptying the tumbler;
	now the player is eavesdropping;
	say "I put the [as a noun]glass tumbler[end as] carefully against the [as a noun]door[end as], then lean down to press my ear against it. Fantastic! I can hear much more clearly now!";

Instead of listening with the tumbler to the clock-door when the player is casually overhearing during Hiding in the Clock:
	eavesdrop-in-clock.

Instead of listening with the tumbler to the clock-door when the player is eavesdropping during Hiding in the Clock:
	say "The tumbler is already fast against the door.";

Instead of listening [without the tumbler] to the keyhole during Hiding in the Clock:
	say "I put one ear to the [as a noun]keyhole[end as] but the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s voice is still too faint. I try cupping one hand around my ear, to the [as a noun]keyhole[end as]. Still no good[one of]. If I had something I could use to listen better then maybe I'd at least have some warning before they open the clock and skin me alive[once only].";

Before putting the tumbler on the clock-door:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler on the Gubbler's Grandfather clock when the location is Inside Clock Case:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler on the keyhole:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler on Gubbler's Grandfather clock when the location is Inside Clock Case:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler against Gubbler's Grandfather clock when the location is Inside Clock Case:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler against the clock-door:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Before putting the tumbler against the keyhole:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Instead of listening with the tumbler to the keyhole:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Instead of using the tumbler with the keyhole:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;

Instead of using the tumbler with the clock-door:
	eavesdrop-in-clock;
	
Instead of listening to Inside Clock Case:
	[eg > LISTEN with no objects]
	if the player is casually overhearing:
		eavesdrop-in-clock;
	otherwise if the current script is not empty:
		stop the action; [allow the scripted message to appear directly]
	continue the action;

Instead of listening with the tumbler to Inside Clock Case:
	[eg > LISTEN WITH TUMBLER with no second noun]
	eavesdrop-in-clock.

Instead of listening to clock-door:
	eavesdrop-in-clock.

Before listening to Gubbler's Grandfather clock when in Inside Clock Case:
	eavesdrop-in-clock.

Chapter 5 - Scripted Events

Hiding in the clock is a scene. Hiding in the clock begins when the player is in Inside Clock Case. Hiding in the clock ends when the clock event is finished.

When Hiding in the Clock begins:
	carry out the firing activity with CCASE2;
	change the current script to { CCASE3, CCASE4, CCASE5, CCASE5B, CCASE6, CCASE6B };
	move the keyhole-figure to Inside Clock Case;
	move the keyhole-abbot to Inside Clock Case;
	now the Grandfather Clock is closed;

When Hiding in the Clock ends:
	remove the keyhole-abbot from play;
	remove the keyhole-figure from play;

Section 1 - Clock Case Event

CCASE1 is a scripted event. The display text is "'We'll talk in here,' I hear the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] saying, from right outside the door. 'More private. More quiet. Much better.' The old man steps inside the room, followed by a tall [as a noun]figure in grey[end as]. In the last tick before his old eyes can see me, I dive inside the gigantic [as a noun]clock[end as] case and pull closed the [as a noun]door[end as].";
 
After firing CCASE1:
	now the Grandfather Clock is closed;
	move the Intro-glimpsed-Figure backdrop to all rooms that are the Abbot's Quarters;
	move the player to Inside Clock Case;
	[added this to keep the tutorial in sync. --JM]
[	jump to tutorial step 8.]

To decide if Clock Case has been hidden in:
	if CCASE1 is fired, yes;
	no;

To decide if Clock Case has not been hidden in:
	if Clock Case has been hidden in, no;
	yes;

Section 2 - Scripted dialogue

CCASE2 is a scripted event. The display text is "Oh, no... That click outside was the sound of the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] closing the door. Looks like he – and the [as a noun]grey figure[end as] I glimpsed with him – are going to be here for a while... And if they find me here, I'll be unwound!"

After firing CCASE2:
	now the Abbot's Door is closed;

CCASE3 is a scripted event. The display text is "'I told you now didn't I tell you?' old [as a noun]Abbot Gubbler[end as] is saying, plaintively. 'Here? Why here? You shouldn't have come here.'[paragraph break][if the player is eavesdropping]The [as a noun]Figure[end as] says something in reply. With the [as a noun]glass[end as], I can hear quite clearly. That was a good idea. 'We have been disappointed with your failure. The cause and effect principle, [as a noun]Abbot[end as]. Such failure must bring consequences.'[otherwise]The [as a noun]Figure[end as] says something in reply. I can only hear two words, but they're enough to stop my heart. 'Failure,' and 'Consequences.' I'm straining to hear more. Something awful is going on here, but what?[end if]"

After firing CCASE3:
	if the player is eavesdropping:
		select the tutorial channel;
		say "Just 'LISTEN' and when they leave, you can go 'OUT'.";
		select the main channel.

CCASE4 is a scripted event. The display text is "'But these things, these things are not always tick-tock, they never are,' the old man replies. His voice is quivering, quite different from the steady drone he has when intoning in Chapel. 'The – item you asked about – it's in the Vaults, you see? The Vaults. Even if I could get in, I ...'[paragraph break][if the player is eavesdropping]'Your excuses we find disappointing. Very disappointing. More of this and, [as a noun]Abbot[end as], we may need to unslip your gears completely.'[otherwise]I still can't hear anything properly! The [as a noun]Figure[end as] is too far from the keyhole and speaks too quietly. 'Disappointing,' he says; something else, then 'unslip your gears.'[end if]"

After firing CCASE4:
	if the player is eavesdropping:
		select the tutorial channel;
		say "Just 'LISTEN' and when they leave, you can go 'OUT'.";
		select the main channel.

CCASE5 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] is spluttering and choking. I've seen him like this only once, when as a young foundling I dropped and smashed an altimeter right before the Archbishop was due to visit.[paragraph break][if the player is eavesdropping]The [as a noun]Figure[end as] continues. 'Clearly you're too old to steal the item for us. We have a better mechanism for obtaining it. But you must provide us with the opportunity. Is that understood?'[otherwise]The [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s quiet voice continues. I can make out; 'too old to steal', 'a better mechanism', 'the opportunity.'[end if][paragraph break]The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] nods violently, shaking out agreement the way I might beat dirt from a duster. 'My money?' he asks."

After firing CCASE5:
	select the tutorial channel;
	say "Just 'LISTEN' and when they leave, you can go 'OUT'.";
	select the main channel.

CCASE5B is a scripted event. The display text is "For a moment, there's nothing more. Then the [as a noun]Figure[end as] says, all too clearly: 'You understand what you're doing? The Church will never recover from this.'[paragraph break]Maybe the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] replies: just a badly-oiled squeak."

CCASE6 is a scripted event. The display text is "There's the clink, of a pouch of golden minutes. A heavy pouch. The [as a noun]Figure[end as], paying the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]...? Then suddenly, there's a third voice. 'Sorry, Father, to interrupt, but you said...'[paragraph break]It's a [as a noun]monk[end as] of the Abbey. 'Get out!' the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] screeches. His bearing's lost its oil, it seems. 'Get out, get out!'";

CCASE6B is a scripted event. The display text is "'But Father,' the [as a noun]monk[end as] continues, 'it's <Italic>time</Italic>, you know. You told me to tell you when it was <Italic>time</Italic>.'[paragraph break]'Oh! Of course of course,' he mumbles. 'I'll... We'll continue this discussion later, then,' he says, to the [as a noun]Figure[end as].[paragraph break][if the player is eavesdropping]'We certainly will,' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] replies.[otherwise]The Figure intones something in reply.[end if][paragraph break]The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] leaves, and that leaves me alone, inside this [as a noun]clock[end as], with the stranger outside. He's coming nearer... I can hear his fingers on the [as a noun]door[end as] of the case! I must have nudged the <Italic>[penduluum]</Italic>, he must have seen the clock-hands quiver... but then, there's the swish of his cloak as he moves away, and the click as the door closes. Phew."

[Added to keep the tutorial in sync. --JM]
[After firing CCASE6B:
	jump to tutorial step 10.]

To decide if the clock event is finished:
	if CCASE6B is fired, yes;
	no;

To decide if the clock event is not finished:
	if the clock event is finished, no;
	yes;

After firing CCASE6:
	move keyhole-monk to the location;

After firing CCASE6B:
	remove keyhole-monk from play;
	move the Intro-glimpsed-Figure backdrop to all rooms. [ dangerous, but we'll remove it from play again in a moment. ]

Section 3 - After the clock scene

Heading for the Cathedral is a scene. Heading for the Cathedral begins when the location is the Abbot's Quarters and the Abbot's Door is open and the Inside Clock Case has been visited. Heading for the Cathedral ends when the location is the Cathedral Yard.

When Heading for the Cathedral begins:
	say "I can't stop thinking about that [as a noun]Figure in Grey[end as]. Stealing from the Vaults... I always knew [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] was a rat, even if he <Italic>is</Italic> the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]! But whatever they're up to, I'm probably the only one who knows about it - which means I'm the only one who can stop it. Cog's Teeth! I might get made a novice for it! Even better, a 1st Assistant Clock Polisher - then I'd outrank [as a noun]Calvin and Drake[end as]!"

Book 2 - The Abbey Of Time

A thing can be Drake-hideable. A thing is seldom Drake-hideable.

Part 1 - Corridor of Contemplation

Before making to leave in the Corridor of Contemplation: try going west instead.

Chapter 1 - Description

The Corridor of Contemplation is a room. "The ancient stones of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] rise to about shoulder-height before arching over, forcing me to bow my head in supplication before the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s [as a noun]door[end as], back east. To the west, I can hear the gentle echo of ticks and tocks coming from the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]'s main hall.[paragraph break]Carved into the walls are a series of pious [as a noun]engravings[end as], depicting the long path from [as a noun]initiate[end as] to [as a noun]Abbot[end as]."

Check listening to the Corridor of Contemplation: 
	say "Ticks and tocks to the west that all say one thing. Hurry up!" instead.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Some echoestickstocks are privately-named, scenery, in the Corridor of Contemplation. "The ticks and tocks are everywhere in the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]."  Understand "echo", "echoes", "tick", "ticks", "tock", "tocks", "echoes of", "echo of", "gentle" as the echoestickstocks. The printed name is "gentle echoes of ticks and tocks". 

Instead of doing something when the echoestickstocks are physically involved:
	say "They're just sounds."

Instead of listening to the echoestickstocks:
	try examining the noun instead.

Section 1 - Engravings

Some engravings are scenery, in the corridor of contemplation. "The carvings start with a young child, polishing and smoothing brass under the eye of some old monk. I don't remember them ever smiling at me that nicely. Then a bit along and further up than I am there are older children at the benches, placing cogs onto spindles. Then there's a long period of study, learning the Twelve Devotions of the Greater Rotation; then the monk's habit, the clock-face hair-cut and hours spent doing incision with the stylus... And beyond that takes me closer to the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s door than I'd like. I don't want him to think I'm eavesdropping, do I?"

Understand "pious", "path", "initiate", "Abbot" as the engravings.

Rule for printing the description of the engravings during Introduction:
	say "If someone comes, that's what I'll do, to explain why I'm here. But no-one's coming, and that means I'd better get away, fast.";

Instead of doing something when the engravings are physically involved:
	say "The engravings are old and half worn away from people trying things just like that."

Chapter 2 - Exits

Instead of going west from the Corridor of Contemplation during Introduction:
	carry out the firing activity with ATTIC1;

ATTIC1 is a scripted event. 

Rule for firing ATTIC1 when Attic Room is unvisited:
	say "I dart out of the tunnel only to be stop-clocked by a depressing sight. It's [as a noun]Calvin and Drake[end as], hurrying in through the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]'s Great Entry. Their mouths are white with sugar.[paragraph break]'Hey, you!' [as a noun]Drake[end as] shouts. In a moment he's got me by the ear. 'What were you doing in the Abbot's room? That's not on your rota!'[paragraph break]'Yeah!' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] adds. 'And why are you slipping away when we told you to stay there?'[paragraph break]'Idiot,' [as a noun]Drake[end as] hisses. Louder – for the benefit of any passing monks – he says, 'That's it, Wren. You're consigned to your room.' For good measure, he punches me on the arm, then the two of them drag me away, heels over flagstones, right to the bottom of the [as a noun]ladder[end as].[paragraph break]'Up you go,' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] says. 'And you're not to come down till dark!'[paragraph break]'What about dinner?' I ask.[paragraph break]'Should have thought of that, shouldn't you?' [as a noun]Drake[end as] says.[paragraph break]'More for us,' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] adds. 'Only two hours to go. I'm starved.'[paragraph break]With that, they punch me again until I scurry out of reach up the [as a noun]ladder[end as].";
	end the chapter;

Rule for firing ATTIC1 when the caretaker's ladder is carried by the player:
	say "[one of]'Wren!' [as a noun]Drake[end as] exclaims. 'Where are you going with that? Think I can't send you back to your room without it, do you?' [or]'Wren!' [as a noun]Drake[end as]'s face is boiling. 'I can't believe you've tried to steal that again? Turn it into firewood, would you?' He spells out the last few words with punches. [or]'I should take that [as a noun]ladder[end as] away while you're up there!' [as a noun]Drake[end as] exclaims, in irritation. [stopping]Then he [if the location is not the Rickety Stair][one of]marches[or]hauls[at random] me back to the corner of the hall, [end if][one of]props up[or]puts down[at random] the [as a noun]ladder[end as], and [one of]punches[or]whallops[or]whackes[at random] [one of]me[or]my arm[at random] [one of]till I climb it[or]until I climb[at random].";
	move the caretaker's ladder to the Rickety Stair;

Rule for firing ATTIC1 when the caretaker's ladder is carried by Drake:
	say "'There you are!' [as a noun]Drake[end as] exclaims. He's carrying my [as a noun]ladder[end as]. 'I don't know how you got down without this, but <Italic>both</Italic> of you are going back!' With that, he [if the location is not the Rickety Stair] marches me to the corner of the hall,[end if] props up the [as a noun]ladder[end as], and punches me till I climb back up.";
	move the caretaker's ladder to the Rickety Stair;



Rule for firing ATTIC1:
	say "[one of]'Wren!' [as a noun]Drake[end as] exclaims. He wallops me on the arm. 'I thought I told you to stay in your room?' He doesn't wait for an excuse before I'm dragged off to the [as a noun]ladder[end as]. Then he waits – and wallops me – until I give in and climb all the way back up.[or][as a noun]Drake[end as] looks like he's swallowed a bee. Not only have I disobeyed him, but I've done it <Italic>twice</Italic>. 'I'm going to have your skin,' he hisses into in my ear. 'I'm going to use it as a cloak come winter. Understand?' With that he drags me back off to my attic. This time, I can barely climb the stairs.[or]Oh, no, not again... Drake storms in and grabs me by the nose. I'm pulled off to the [as a noun]ladder[end as] base. 'Please,' I beg. 'I'm too tired to climb it again.' He looks at me as if I'm stupid. 'Then don't climb back down this time!' he says.[or][final attic trip][stopping]";


To say final attic trip:
	say "[as a noun]Drake[end as] [one of]wallops[or]punches[or]hits[or]kicks[at random] me, [one of]boiling[or]shaking[or]simmering[at random] with [one of]rage[or]anger[or]fury[or]irritation[at random]. Back to the [as a noun]ladder[end as], and up I go...";

After firing ATTIC1:
	move the player to Attic Room;
	say "[one of]Through my [as a noun]window[end as] I can see the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] across the yard outside. If I was a bird, I could escape easily, and tell someone what I've overheard. Someone at the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] would listen: I could tell the Archbishop himself, maybe. He might remember me from the time he visited, when he helped me repair an altimeter I'd broken. If only I could fly.[paragraph break]But perhaps I can escape on foot. If I'm clever. <Italic>Clockwise</Italic>.[or]That was careless, getting caught. But I can still make it to the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]. I'll just have to be more cunning this time. Remember, Wren, it's easy: the whole world runs on clockwork. Just a matter of staying ahead of the biting teeth.[or]A quick pause for breath and... right then. I'm ready. I'm going to get out of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] somehow, and warn the Archbishop. And I won't give up till I've got there![stopping]"

To decide if the player is taken to the attic:
	if ATTIC1 is fired, yes;
	no;

To decide if the player is not taken to the attic:
	if the player is taken to the attic, no;
	yes;

Part 2 - Attic Bedroom

Chapter 1 - Description  

The Attic Room is a room. "[if the player is in the Attic Room for more than the first time]Back in the [as a noun]attic[end as]. The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] is still just as far away, visible through the [as a noun]hole[end as] in the ceiling above my cot. But the [as a noun]ladder[end as]'s the only way out of here.[otherwise]This isn't really an [as a noun]attic[end as]. It isn't really a room either. It's a couple of [as a noun]floorboards[end as] laid across some roof [as a noun]rafters[end as] right in the ceiling of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]. There's enough floor-space for a [as a noun]cot[end as] and a [as a noun]laundry crate[end as], but I have to be careful not to roll out of [as a noun]bed[end as], because if I do, the thick [as a noun]cobwebs[end as] all around aren't going to stop me from falling...[paragraph break]A little [as a noun]hole[end as] in the ceiling provides some sunlight: and when it rains, it means I can wash my hair, too. It's right above the rickety [as a noun]ladder[end as] down to the ground (and that means the [as a noun]ladder[end as] is starting to rot and bend)."

Understand "cobwebs", "thick cobwebs", "cobweb", "floorboards/boards/board" and "beam/beams/rafters/rafter" as the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room.

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	say "[one of]There isn't a floor here, just a few [as a noun]rafters[end as] and a long drop on either side, with [as a noun]cobwebs[end as] to covers the gaps![or]Really. I don't have a floor, just some [as a noun]beams[end as] and a [as a noun]bed[end as] and a [as a noun]cobweb[end as] pretending to be a carpet.[stopping]"

Instead of searching the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	say "There's nothing there except a long way down."

Instead of cleaning the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	say "There's nothing I can do about the [as a noun]floor[end as]. The dirt is all that's holding it together."
	
Instead of touching or smelling or tasting or eating the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	say "I get enough dirt on me normally without going looking for more."

Instead of jumping when in the Attic Room:
	say "Doesn't sound like a good idea to me!"
	
Before swinging the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	try jumping instead.

Instead of sleeping when in the Attic Room:
	say "No time for that now!"

Instead of approaching or entering the backdrop-floor when in the Attic Room:
	say "I'm certainly not going to step off them!"

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Cot

A supporter called my cot is enterable, scenery, in the Attic Room. "My [as a noun]cot[end as] is made of pinewood – flimsy stuff that you'd never make a clock from. At least the bedframe has edges, so I can find it in the dark. I'd love to have a more solid [as a noun]bed[end as] – a horsehair mattress and everything – but it'd be so heavy it'd go straight through my [as a noun]floorboards[end as]. 'You'd better not grow,' [as a noun]Drake[end as]'s told me before. 'Or you'll go straight through yourself.'[paragraph break]Beside the cot is a [as a noun]pencil sketch[end as] of my family." Understand "pine", "pinewood", "bedframe", "frame", "wood", "wooden", "bed" as my cot.

Instead of entering my cot:
	say "[one of]Stretching out on the [as a noun]cot[end as] gives me a good view through my [as a noun]window[end as]. I can see the spires of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], tipping with [as a noun]Time's Arrows[end as] and flocked with [as a noun]birds[end as]. The [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] would listen, I'm thinking. If only I could reach him.[paragraph break]I'll just have to try. I get back to my feet.[or]There's no time for that, Wren![stopping]";

Instead of searching my cot:
	say "There's nothing on my [as a noun]bed[end as] (and not much <Italic>to</Italic> it, either).";

Instead of looking under my cot:
	say "There's a long, long way down there."
	
Instead of pushing or pulling or taking my cot:
	say "Where would it go? Down?"
	
Instead of attacking my cot:
	say "Why? It's my only real friend round here."

Instead of cleaning my cot:
	say "It'll do. I don't mind."

[This method of understanding is horribly inefficient. Corrected. --JM]

[Understand "make [something]" as cleaning when the player's command matches the text "bed", case insensitively.
Understand "make [something]" as cleaning when the player's command matches the text "cot", case insensitively.]

Understand "make [my cot]" as cleaning.

Section 2 - Crate

A crate is a container, scenery, in the Attic Room. "A [as a noun]laundry crate[end as] containing my spare [as a noun]clothes[end as]. Or my spare [as a noun]clothe[end as], anyway."
Understand "laundry" as the crate.

Instead of opening or searching or taking the crate:
	say "Laundry day's not for at least three weeks.";

Instead of taking or pushing or pulling the crate:
	try taking my cot instead.

Instead of looking under the crate:
	try looking under my cot instead.
	
Instead of attacking the crate:
	say "It'll fall and break itself one of these days; I don't need to do anything."


A spare clothe is in the crate.  Understand "clothes", "clothing" as the spare clothe. The description is "That's my other top."

Instead of smelling the spare clothe:
	say "Clean."

Instead of taking the spare clothe:
	say "Leave it. I'll need it soon enough, when they take this top away forcibly on wash day."


Section 3 - Picture

A pencil sketch is scenery, in the Attic Room. The description is "It's a [as a noun]pencil sketch[end as] of my [as a noun]family[end as], or at least, that's what the monks say it is. Really, the mother and father don't look a thing like me, and the swarm of children around them don't either. But still, they'd explain why I was donated to the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]. Too many mouths to feed already." The printed name is "picture".

Understand "picture", "family", "children", "mother", "father", "drawing" as the pencil sketch.

Instead of taking the pencil sketch:
	say "I wouldn't want to lose it, or damage it. It's really the only thing I've got, apart from the [as a noun]clock key[end as] round my neck."

Section 4 - Window

A window called my window is scenery, in the Attic Room. "On tiptoes, I can see right across the city of [as a noun]St. Philip[end as]. It's a maze of [as a noun]rooftops[end as] like a tangle of thorn bushes. If people could only see this view, then they might not be so strict about Precision and Good Order... Or maybe they would, because looming over all the houses are the great spires of the [as a noun]Cathedral of Time[end as], its great clock keeping time for the whole [as a noun]city[end as].[paragraph break]And the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] is where I would find the Archbishop. I've got to tell him what I heard!". Understand "hole", "little hole", "tile", "roof tile", "missing roof tile", "missing tile" as my window.

Instead of entering or getting off my window:
	say "The [as a noun]window[end as] is too small for me to climb through, and anyway, despite my name, I'm no bird. If I'm going to get to the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], I'm going to have to go through the [as a noun]Abbey[end as].";

Instead of going up when in the Attic Room: try entering my window instead.

Instead of opening my window:
	say "I call it a [as a noun]window[end as]. It's really just a missing [as a noun]roof tile[end as].";

Section 5 - Distant city

The distant city of St Philip is scenery, in the Attic Room. 
Understand "city", "rooftops", "view", "houses", "St Philip", "city of St Philip" as the distant city of St Philip.

Instead of examining the distant city:
	try examining my window;

Instead of doing something when the distant city is physically involved:
	say "It's outside the [as a noun]window[end as].";

Section 6 - Cathedral Spires

The cathedral spires are scenery, in the Attic Room. "[as a noun]Birds[end as] flit between the twelve [as a noun]spires[end as] of the [as a noun]Great Dome[end as] of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]. The [as a noun]spires[end as] mirror the Twelve Devotions of the Greater Rotation[one of]. Rumour among initiates has it that the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] was once a sundial that marked the time by casting shadows on numerals scattered about the city, but then St Newton abolished that, tore up the numerals and hid them away.[paragraph break]They say there are twelve different numbers that used to be the first twelve but now no-one is allowed to use them. I don't believe a word of it, of course[once only]." The printed name is "Cathedral"

Understand "twelve spires", "dome", "great dome", "birds", "church", "time's arrows", "arrows", "time's arrow", "times arrow", "times arrows" as the cathedral spires.

Before doing something when the cathedral spires are physically involved:
	say "It's right across the [as a noun]city[end as] from me!" instead;

Understand "Cathedral of Time", "clock", "great clock" as the Cathedral.

Section 7 - Ladder (StaircaseTop / StaircaseBottom)

The old construction ladder is an open door, scenery, down from the Attic Room. Through it is [, up from] the Rickety Stair. "One of the old construction [as a noun]ladder[end as]s left over from the building of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]. It's a real antique. Some rungs are missing and the middle bends inwards. I can still climb it in my sleep, of course. [if in Attic]The [as a noun]ladder[end as] leads down all the way to ground level.[otherwise]The [as a noun]ladder[end as] leads up to my room.[end if]". The printed name is "ladder".

Understand "rungs", "rung" as the old construction ladder.

Understand "top", "top of" as the old construction ladder when the location is the Attic Room.
[Understand "bottom", "bottom of" as the old construction ladder when the location is the Attic Room.]

Before climbing up the old construction ladder in Attic Room:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] only goes down from here." instead;

Before climbing down the old construction ladder in Rickety Stair:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] only goes up from here." instead;

Instead of climbing the old construction ladder:
	try entering the old construction ladder;

Instead of taking or pulling the old construction ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as]'s pretty long. I couldn't pull it up here.";



[After going up through the old construction ladder:
	say "I scramble up the [as a noun]ladder[end as]. It's a long way, and pretty exhausting.";
	continue the action;]

After going down through the old construction ladder:
	say "I scramble quickly down the [as a noun]ladder[end as].";
	continue the action;

Chapter 3 - Exits

Instead of going east through the Abbot's Door during Introduction:
	say "No fear. I'm not going back in there.";

Instead of opening the Abbot's door when in the Corridor of Contemplation during Introduction:
	say "No fear. I'm not going back in there.";

Part 3 - Rickety Stair

Chapter 1 - Description                                                                                           

The Rickety Stair is a room. The description is "I'm in the western corner of the Great Hall[if caretaker's ladder is in location], just by the foot of the [as a noun]ladder[end as] up to my room (the rest of the hall is east). There's a [as a noun]note[end as] attached to the third rung asking Amble the caretaker not to put the [as a noun]ladder[end as] back in the garden just yet.[else]. My bedroom is somewhere overhead.[end if]"

After assembling available exits of the Rickety Stair:
	if the caretaker's ladder is in the Rickety Stair:
		add up to the viable directions, if absent;

The printed name of Rickety Stair is "Western Corner of the Great Hall".

Chapter 2 - Ladder 

The caretaker's ladder is a thing in the Rickety Stair. The description is "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] is about twelve feet long[if the caretaker's ladder is in Rickety Stair], leading up to the attic room[else if the caretaker's ladder is in Herb Garden], and clears the wall with ease[end if]."

Understand "caretakers", "amble's", "ambles", "rung", "rungs", "bottom of", "wooden" as the caretaker's ladder.

Before climbing down the caretaker's ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] only goes up from where I'm standing." instead;

Instead of pushing or pulling or turning the caretaker's ladder when the caretaker's ladder is in the Rickety Stair:
	say "It wobbles, a little wave up towards the ceiling."
	
Instead of looking under the caretaker's ladder:
	if the player is carrying the caretaker's ladder:
		say "I'm there." instead;
	say "Under the [as a noun]ladder[end as] is Bad Luck, whether the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] agrees with such beliefs or not."

Before hiding behind the caretaker's ladder:
	say "Standing under a [as a noun]ladder[end as] is like asking for the sky to fall. Bad luck." instead.

Instead of attacking the caretaker's ladder:
	say "'To break something useful is to suck the stars from the sky,' so the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says."

Instead of touching the caretaker's ladder:
	say "The wood is old, dry, frail - all the things you wouldn't want [as a noun]ladder[end as] wood to be."

Section - In the Rickety Stair

