Understand "brew a brew" as a mistake ("That's exactly what the tea maker's for.") when the location is the Kitchen.

Chapter 1 - Description

The Kitchen is east of the Upper Hall. "[if Gong Sounding is not happening]If there was ever a proof of the perfection of Clockwork over Nature, it's that the dirtiest and most hectic room in the whole Abbey is its Kitchen. Shelves, walls and floor are covered by [as a noun]machines[end as] belching, slicing, steaming, chopping, stirring, boiling, broiling, frying, pureeing ... the list goes on. The noise, smell and heat are almost overpowering, and the [as a noun]Cook[end as], who controls this madhouse, can only stand it because he's stone deaf. [otherwise]The [as a noun]Cook[end as] is working like a double-escapement all of a sudden, probably trying to get dinner ready two hours too soon! [end if][if Quest for Tea is happening][paragraph break][tea machine details][end if][paragraph break]The cool of the Hall is west. South, through a broad arch, is the Refectory[if Gong Sounding is happening], where the [as a noun]Refectory Clock[end as] is throwing up a wall of sound[end if]. North is the dark recess of the Pantry."

To say tea machine details:
	say "In one corner is the only machine I'm allowed to use: the [as a noun]Tea Maker[end as]. [if the bracket contains the empty teacup]In the [as a noun]bracket[end as] of the machine is a [as a noun]teacup[end as]. [otherwise if the bracket contains the full teacup]In the [as a noun]bracket[end as] of the machine is a steaming [as a noun]cup of tea[end as]. [end if]";

Instead of inserting the teacup into the tea-machine:
	try inserting the teacup into the bracket;

Instead of putting the new gear on the tea-machine:
	try inserting the new gear into the gear train;

Instead of inserting the new gear into the tea-machine:
	try inserting the new gear into the gear train;

Instead of putting the small gear on the tea-machine:
	try inserting the small gear into the gear train;

Instead of inserting the small gear into the tea-machine:
	try inserting the small gear into the gear train;

Instead of inserting the handful of tea leaves into the tea-machine:
	try inserting the handful of tea leaves into the basket;

Chapter 2 - The Cook

Section 1 - Description

The Cook is a man, scenery, in the Kitchen. "[if Gong Sounding is not happening]The story goes that the [as a noun]Cook[end as] lost his hearing while setting rat-traps inside the great [as a noun]Refectory Clock[end as]. He'd been late about his chores and so instead of being there at 4.30 like his rota told him to, he was there at 5.00. When the gongs rang for dinner, he was still inside the case. If it's true, then never mind his ears, it's a wonder he can still <Italic>see</Italic> or <Italic>think</Italic>. That [as a noun]clock[end as] is <Italic>seriously</Italic> loud.[otherwise]The [as a noun]Cook[end as] has gone into double-time.[end if]"

Rule for speaking with the cook:
	say "The [as a noun]Cook[end as] doesn't waste time trying to lip-read. He picks me up, puts me to one side, and gets back to [one of]walloping the toaster[or]scooping soot from the grill[or]injecting steam through a waxed cloth pipe into a pot[or]cooking[or]plugging holes in a hot-[as a noun]water[end as] pipe with bread dough[or]setting dials on the furnaces[or]stirring a pan using a three-foot spoon[at random]." instead.

Instead of knocking on the cook:
	say "He's too busy buried in his cooking equipment to notice me."

Section 2 - Idle actions

Every turn when the player can see the Cook and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds:
	if the slice of lemon was off-stage and the slice of lemon is in the teacup:
		do nothing;
	otherwise:
		say "The [as a noun]cook[end as] [one of]pushes[or]bustles[or]scurries[or]knocks[at random] past me, to [one of]unclip[or]wind[or]respring[or]tighten[or]thump[at random] the [as a noun][one of]whisker[or]grater[or]steamer[or]masher[or]broiler[or]oven[or]crumb-sucker[at random][end as].";

Chapter 3 - Scenery

Section 1 - Machines

Some machines are scenery, in the Kitchen. The description is "[as a noun]Pipes[end as] run like cobwebs between a hundred fantastic [as a noun]devices[end as]. One entire corner is given over to automatic egg-boiling: 'A Holy Problem of Timing,' [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] once said. Other [as a noun]wires[end as], [as a noun]pulleys[end as], [as a noun]ducts[end as] and [as a noun]cog-teeth[end as] cross the floor and ceiling, and the [as a noun]Cook[end as] is constantly sweeping muck and crumbs out from the mechanisms in a desperate attempt to keep them in good order.[paragraph break]The one machine I'm allowed to use is the [as a noun]Tea Maker[end as], which is a whole world of [as a noun]gears[end as] and [as a noun]basket[end as]s and [as a noun]spigot[end as]s in itself."

Understand "pipes", "devices", "wires", "pulleys", "ducts", "cog-teeth", "whisker", "grater", "cooker", "boiler", "crumb sucker", "steamer", "masher", "broiler", "oven", "crumb-sucker", "shelf", "shelves", "sink" as the machines.

Instead of doing something when the machines are physically involved:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]Cook[end as] hurtles over and beats me away with a towel.[or]The [as a noun]Cook[end as] scowls and slaps me away.[or]I might lose a finger if I tried that![at random]";

Section 2 - a pantry to hide in

A pantry-ghost is scenery, privately-named, in the Kitchen. The description is "The [as a noun]pantry[end as] is a dark recess to the north[one of]. Strong smells wander out like labourers from a beer break[once only]."

Understand "pantry" as the pantry-ghost. The printed name of the pantry-ghost is "pantry".

Instead of smelling the pantry-ghost:
	say "I pop my head inside...";
	try going north;
	say "...and take a good sniff. Mmm...";
	try smelling instead.

Before hiding inside the pantry-ghost:
	if the location is not Drake's future destination and the location is not Drake's next destination:
		say "I can't hear [as a noun]Drake[end as] coming so I'm clock-on for now." instead;
	otherwise:
		say "I duck into the [as a noun]pantry[end as].";
		try going north instead;

Instead of searching or entering the pantry-ghost:
	try going north instead;

Instead of hiding from view when the location is the Kitchen and Drake's Patrol is happening:
        try hiding inside the pantry-ghost;

Part T - The Tea Machine

An internal gubbin is a kind of thing.

Instead of doing something when an internal gubbin (called the bit) is physically involved:
	say "[The bit] is central to the machine, literally. Well out of reach." instead;

The tiny sail is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine.
The thread is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine.
The gauze is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine.
The thin metal strut is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine. Understand "struts", "frame", "framework", "arms", "arm-brace", "arm brace" as the thin metal strut.
The pin is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine.
The belly spring is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine. The printed name of the belly spring is "spring".
The flint-iron is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine. Understand "flint", "iron", "striker", "match" as the flint-iron.
The hot-plate is an internal gubbin, part of the tea-machine. Understand "hot plate", "plate" as the hot-plate.

Rule for printing the description of the tiny sail:
	say "The [as a noun]sail[end as] is used to lift the [as a noun]flint-iron[end as] and strike the light."

Rule for printing the description of the thread:
	say "The [as a noun]thread[end as] pulls the [as a noun]flint-iron[end as] up."

Rule for printing the description of the gauze:
	say "The [as a noun]gauze[end as] collects steam from the [as a noun]kettle[end as]. When there's enough steam, the [as a noun]gauze[end as] tilts and the [as a noun]kettle[end as] is taken off the heat."

Rule for printing the description of the thin metal strut:
	say "The [as a noun]thin metal strut[end as] is how the [as a noun]gauze[end as] is attached to the [as a noun]kettle[end as]."

Rule for printing the description of the pin:
	say "The [as a noun]pin[end as] is a small switcher for closing off the [as a noun]gas pipe[end as]."

Rule for printing the description of the belly spring:
	say "The [as a noun]spring[end as] is the heart of the machine. Every machine must have one - even you!"

Rule for printing the description of a flint-iron:
	say "The [as a noun]flint-iron[end as] is the traditional clockwork part for making flame."

Rule for printing the description of an internal gubbin:
	say "It's part of the tea machine's Holy Workings."

Definition: a thing is tea-critical:
	if it is the tea-machine lever, no; [can be pulled even when the machine is on]
	if it is part of the Tea-Machine, yes;

Instead of doing something to a tea-critical thing when the Tea-Machine is switched on:
	say "Don't interfere. The machine is making its right and proper supplications.";

Instead of opening the Tea-Machine:
	say "I'm a Second Assistant Clock Polisher. I'm not allowed to go poking around inside [as a noun]Tea Machine[end as]s, their insides are far too Holy."

Chapter 1 - Machine 'Chassis'

The Tea-Machine is a privately-named device, scenery, in the Kitchen. 

Rule for printing the description of the Tea-Machine when the Tea-Machine is switched off:
	say "Very complex, but robustly built and able to perform its functions a few thousand times without needing oil. ";
	if the new gear is part of the gear train:
		say "There's a shiny [as a noun]new gear[end as] in place in the [as a noun]gear train[end as]";
	else:
		say "It consists of a framework of [as a noun]arms[end as] and [as a noun]strut[end as]s, most of which move, though there's a plate at the front to hold the primary [as a noun]gear-train[end as] that's between the [as a noun]lever[end as] and the [as a noun]winding key[end as]";	
	say ".[paragraph break]Inside the machine I can make out a [if the kettle is full]filled [end if][as a noun]kettle[end as], a [as a noun]spigot[end as], and a [as a noun]basket[end as] [if the basket is full]filled with [as a noun]tea-leaves[end as] [end if]suspended over a semi-circular [as a noun]bracket[end as]";
	if the teacup is in the bracket:
		say ", containing a [as a noun]tea cup[end as]";
	say ". There's a [as a noun]burner[end as] somewhere, too";
	if the tea-machine key is wound:
		say ", and the [as a noun]spring[end as] is fully wound";
	say "."


Rule for printing the description of the Tea-Machine:
	say "The machine is clanking, spinning, whirring and generally doing its thing.";


Instead of taking the Tea-Machine:
	say "The machine is plumbed, weighted, counter-balanced, sprung and assembled with all the Holy Engineering that the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] can spare for its kitchens. I can't just walk off with it even if I <Italic>could</Italic> pick it up."

The printed name of the tea-machine is "Tea Maker".
Understand "machine", "maker", "tea machine", "tea maker" as the tea-machine.

Before switching on the switched off tea-machine:
	say "It does no good to talk to a machine so casually, as Gubbler would say. Each sacred part must be treated as an individual operating within a larger purpose. [as a noun]Key[end as]s must be wound, [as a noun]lever[end as]s pulled, [as a noun]spigot[end as]s opened... that sort of thing." instead;

Instead of switching on the switched on tea-machine:
	try pulling the tea-machine lever instead;
	[ resulting in 'The machine is working already.' ]

Instead of turning the tea-machine [ winding ]:
	try turning the tea-machine key instead.

Chapter 2 - Spigot

The spigot is a device, part of the tea-machine. The description is "[one of]The [as a noun]spigot[end as] is a short tube [if the tea-machine is switched off]above the [as a noun]kettle[end as] [end if]with a hook-shaped [as a noun]valve[end as] halfway up that can be opened. Somewhere in the other corner of the Kitchen it's plumbed into the [as a noun]water[end as] tank, which in turn is fed by ducts from the ceiling, into which [as a noun]water[end as] is loaded by thirsty initiates – and so on it goes; a Great Rotation.[or]Basically, it's a tap over the [as a noun]kettle[end as].[stopping]".

Understand "valve", "tube", "tap", "faucet" as the spigot.

Instead of turning or opening the spigot:
	try switching on the spigot;

Before switching on the spigot when the kettle is full:
	say "There's plenty of [as a noun]water[end as] in the [as a noun]kettle[end as], thanks." instead;

Instead of switching on the spigot:
	now the kettle is full;
	say "The [as a noun]spigot[end as] releases a steady dribble of [as a noun]water[end as] into the [as a noun]kettle[end as], filling it nicely. Across the room, the clouds coming from the steamer suddenly thin out.[paragraph break]The [as a noun]Cook[end as] stops and gives me the most evil of evil stares. I turn the spigot closed again." instead;

