		say "I don't have another empty [as a noun]tea cup[end as]." instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the kettle is empty,
		say "The [as a noun]Cook[end as] gawps, then troops over and belts me around the head. He points firmly at the [as a noun]kettle[end as] in the middle of the machine – the <Italic>empty</Italic> [as a noun]kettle[end as] in the middle of the machine – then stalks back over to his chopping machine." instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the basket is not full,
		say "Don't you think the tea would be rather bland if I don't put some <Italic>tea</Italic> in? The machine is clockwork but it's not <Italic>magic</Italic>, you know!" instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the teacup is not in the bracket,
		say "Everything's set, certainly, except there's nowhere for the tea to go – except out of the [as a noun]basket[end as], through the semi-circular [as a noun]bracket[end as] where the [as a noun]tea-cup[end as]'s supposed to go, and then all over my feet." instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the tea-machine key is unwound,
		say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] moves without resistance. It's been long enough since breakfast, clearly, that the [as a noun]spring[end as] has wound down." instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the small gear is part of the gear train:
		say "[one of]The [as a noun]lever[end as] locks down and sets the first few [as a noun]gears[end as] in motion. But it doesn't seem to be carrying over correctly[or]The [as a noun]lever[end as] doesn't do anything[stopping]. [run paragraph on]";
		try examining the gear train;
		say "I release the [as a noun]lever[end as] and it springs back up." instead;

A tea-making rule:
	if the new gear is not part of the gear train,
		say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] locks down but there's a [as a noun]gear[end as] missing on the front of the machine, so the [as a noun]train[end as] of cogs can't connect. I release the [as a noun]lever[end as] again and it springs back up." instead;

Section 4 - Tea making script

TEA1 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]gear train[end as] whirrs into life, swinging the [as a noun]kettle[end as] across inside the machine. Its base knocks open the [as a noun]gas-pipe[end as] tap. A jet of gas hisses out, blowing up a tiny [as a noun]sail[end as] attached by [as a noun]thread[end as] to the [as a noun]flint-iron[end as]. The [as a noun]burner[end as] is quickly alight."

After firing TEA1:
	now the tea-machine is switched on;
	now the burner is lit;

TEA2 is a scripted event. The display text is "After a minute or so, steam begins to rise from the [as a noun]kettle[end as]. This steam collects on a fine [as a noun]gauze[end as] over the [as a noun]kettle[end as], which slowly weighs down on a thin metal [as a noun]strut[end as]. Eventually, the pressure is enough to flip a counterweight, and the [as a noun]gauze[end as] tilts, pushes the [as a noun]kettle[end as] onto its side. Boiling water pours from the [as a noun]spout[end as] and into the [as a noun]basket[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaves[end as]. As the [as a noun]kettle[end as] empties, its weight shifts and it swings back. A [as a noun]pin[end as] on one side knocks closed the [as a noun]gas pipe[end as]."

After firing TEA2:
	now the kettle is empty;
	now the burner is unlit;

TEA3 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]basket[end as] on the [as a noun]tea-maker[end as] slowly filters [as a noun]tea[end as] into the waiting [as a noun]cup[end as]. Meanwhile, the slowing [as a noun]gears[end as] move the [as a noun]kettle[end as] back to its original position below the [as a noun]spigot[end as], and the [as a noun]water[end as] on the [as a noun]gauze[end as] sheet evaporates away. All is restored: 'Every cog comes full circle,' as they say."

After firing TEA3:
	do nothing;

TEA4 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]lever[end as] springs back. The [as a noun]tea[end as] is finished. In a final convulsion, the machine spits the used [as a noun]tea leaves[end as] out the back and into a composter duct, ready to be fed to the cows that provided the gas... It's certainly neat."

After firing TEA4:
	now the basket is empty;
	now the teacup is full;
	now the tea-machine is switched off;

TEA1 is clustered with TEA2, TEA3 and TEA4.

Rule for firing a scripted event (called the happening) that is clustered with TEA1:
	if the player can see the tea-machine,
		say "[display text of the happening][paragraph break]";
	otherwise do nothing;

Instead of taking the teacup when the tea-machine is switched on:
	say "The [as a noun]cup[end as]'s locked into place inside the machine."

Section 5 - - - And a slice of lemon

The slice of lemon is a thing. The description is "A slice of lemon."

Instead of doing something when the slice of lemon is physically involved:
	say "Don't. That would spoil the tea.";

Before eating or tasting the slice of lemon:
	say "I'm not <Italic>that</Italic> hungry!" instead;

After taking the full teacup when the teacup was in the bracket:
	move the slice of lemon to the teacup;
	say "I lift the [as a noun]cup[end as] carefully from the machine. The [as a noun]Cook[end as] turns and for the first time seems to notice me. He looks furious. 'And where you thin['] you go wi['] tha[']?' he demands. 'Wait. Wa['].'[paragraph break]After a bit of tidying, he finds a button to press. A machine coughs and wheezes, and eventually a small half-[as a noun]slice of lemon[end as] squeezes from a slot. The [as a noun]Cook[end as] snatches it and drops it into my [as a noun]tea cup[end as]. 'Ri['] you are,' he says, and then waves me away.";

Instead of drinking the full teacup:
	say "After all that? No way. I prefer milk anyway.";

Instead of emptying the full teacup:
	say "After all that? No way.";

Instead of inserting something into the full teacup:
	say "Don't. That would spoil the tea."

Part P - Pantry

Instead of making to leave when the player is in the Pantry:
	try going south instead.

Instead of jumping when in the Pantry:
	say "None of the shelves are that high.";

Chapter 1 - Description

The Abbey Pantry is a room. It is north of the Kitchen. "The Pantry is as tiny as a clock-case and filled with shelves that smell of herb and oil - but it's dark enough to hide someone like me..."

Section 1 - Using your nose

SMELLPANTRY is a trigger.

Instead of smelling in the Abbey Pantry:
	say "The strongest smell comes from the [as a noun]jar[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaf[end as] on the [as a noun]shelves[end as].";
	fire SMELLPANTRY;

Section 2 - Giving it away for players who don't notice

Instead of going south from the Pantry when SMELLPANTRY is unfired:
	say "I'm distracted for a moment by a strong smell from one of the [as a noun]jar[end as]s. It's only [as a noun]tea[end as] but it makes my stomach turn circles. <Italic>How</Italic> long till five o'clock and dinner time?";
	fire SMELLPANTRY;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Some pantry-shelves are a supporter, in the Abbey Pantry. The printed name is "pantry shelves".

Rule for writing a paragraph about the pantry-shelves when SMELLPANTRY is fired:
	say "The strongest smell in the room comes from a [as a noun]jar[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaf[end as] on the [as a noun]shelves[end as]." instead;

Rule for writing a paragraph about the pantry-shelves:
	now the pantry-shelves are mentioned instead;

Understand "pantry", "shelf/shelves", "jars", "canisters", "containers", "herb", "herbs", "spice", "spices", "oil", "oils" as the pantry-shelves.

Instead of putting something on the pantry-shelves:
	say "I'm an Assistant Clock-polisher, not a [as a noun]shelf[end as]-stacker!";

Instead of examining the pantry-shelves:
	try searching the pantry-shelves.

Instead of searching the pantry-shelves:
	fire SMELLPANTRY;
	say "The [as a noun]shelves[end as] are cluttered with all sorts of things, including a [as a noun]jar[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaf[end as].";

Section 1 - Jar of tea leaf

[No need to make the jar of tea leaves privately named. --JM]

[A jar of tea leaves is a container, privately-named, portable, on the pantry-shelves. The description is "A glass apothecary [as a noun]jar[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaves[end as], that come shipped in by Zepplin from the Asynchronous Continent." The printed name is "tea leaves".]

A jar of tea leaves is a container, portable, on the pantry-shelves. The description is "A glass apothecary [as a noun]jar[end as] of [as a noun]tea leaves[end as], that come shipped in by Zepplin from the Asynchronous Continent." The printed name is "tea leaves".

Understand "tea", "leaf", "leaves" as the jar of tea leaves when the location does not enclose the handful of tea leaves.

[Understand performance problems. --JM]

[Understand "glass", "apothecary", "jar", "jar of", "tea", "leaves", "leaf" as the jar of tea leaves when the player's command matches the text "jar", case insensitively.]

Understand "glass jar", "apothecary jar", "tea jar", "leaves jar", "leaf jar" as the jar of tea leaves.

Instead of taking the jar of tea leaves:
	fire SMELLPANTRY;
	if the player carries the handful of tea leaves:
		say "I take a couple more [as a noun]leaves[end as].";
	otherwise:
		move the handful of tea leaves to the player;
		set pronouns from the handful of tea leaves;
		say "I scoop up a handful of [as a noun]tea leaves[end as].";

[Before inserting the jar of tea leaves into something when the player does not carry the handful of tea leaves:
	say "(first taking some [as a noun]tea[end as] from the [as a noun]jar[end as])[command clarification break]";
	now the player carries the handful of tea leaves;
	try inserting the handful of tea leaves into the second noun instead; ]

Before printing the name of the jar of tea leaves when asking which do you mean:
	say "[as a noun]jar[end as] of ";

Instead of inserting something into the jar of tea leaves:
	say "I'd be in big trouble if I messed up the [as a noun]tea[end as] in this place. It's the sacred drink of the workshop men, and it's pretty expensive too.";

Instead of opening or closing the jar of tea leaves:
	say "The [as a noun]jar[end as] doesn't have a stopper.";

[ Some dummy tea leaves are privately-named, inside the jar of tea leaves. The printed name is "tea leaves". Understand "loose" as the dummy tea leaves.

Instead of taking the dummy tea leaves: try taking the jar of tea leaves;
Instead of examining the dummy tea leaves: try examining the jar of tea leaves;

Before printing the name of the dummy tea leaves when asking which do you mean:
	say "loose ";

After printing the name of the dummy tea leaves when asking which do you mean:
	say " (inside the [as a noun]jar[end as])"

Does the player mean doing something with the dummy tea leaves:
	it is very unlikely; ]

Section 3 - Handful of tea leaves

A handful of tea leaves is a thing. The description is "A handful of tea leaves." Understand "leaf" as the handful of tea leaves.

Instead of inserting the handful of tea leaves into the jar of tea leaves:
	remove the handful of tea leaves from play;
	say "I put the [as a noun]tea leaf[end as] back into the [as a noun]jar[end as].";

Before inserting the handful of tea leaves into the tumbler:
	say "[one of]I can't make [as a noun]tea[end as] in a tumbler[or]That's not the right place for them to go[stopping]." instead;

Before inserting the handful of tea leaves into the teacup:
	say "[one of]I can't just skip the whole machine part[or]That's not the right place for them to go[stopping]." instead;

Before smelling the handful of tea leaves:
	say "The [as a noun]tea[end as] smells sharp, like ink or copper." instead.

Before tasting or eating the handful of tea leaves:
	say "I touch them with my tongue. Bitter, with a taste like metal." instead.

Instead of dropping the handful of tea leaves:
	remove the handful of tea leaves from play;
	say "I scatter the [as a noun]tea leaves[end as]. They quickly disintegrate and are gone.";

Instead of putting the handful of tea leaves on something:
	try dropping the handful of tea leaves instead;

Instead of inserting the handful of tea leaves into something:
	try dropping the handful of tea leaves instead;

Instead of inserting the handful of tea leaves into the kettle:
	say "Come off it, Wren! There's this whole mechanism built with Holy Precision just to stop bits of [as a noun]tea-leaf[end as] ending up in the tea, and you want to just dunk the whole lot in?"

Instead of putting the handful of tea leaves on the kettle:
	try inserting the handful of tea leaves into the kettle.

Instead of inserting the handful of tea leaves into the teacup:
	if the teacup is in the bracket:
		redirect the action from the teacup to the kettle, and try that instead;
	otherwise:
		placeholder "What, and have [as a noun]tea leaves[end as] all swimming about in the [as a noun]tea[end as]?";

Instead of putting the handful of tea leaves on the teacup:
	try inserting the handful of tea leaves into the teacup.

Part 6 - Library

Chapter 1 - Description

The Library is a room. "The Abbey's library is small compared to the one in the Cathedral. There's nothing on the shelves but the key texts: the [as a noun]Principia[end as], the [as a noun]Mechanistica[end as] and the [as a noun]Determininium[end as]. Only [as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as], the Abbey's Primary Reader ever seems to use them – he's here at the moment, studying at his desk by the poor light coming through the [as a noun]stained glass windows[end as]. [if Horloge's keys are on the wheeled table]By his elbow is a fat bunch of [as a noun]keys[end as]. [otherwise if Horloge's keys are in the location]On the floor by his feet is a fat bunch of [as a noun]keys[end as]. [end if]On his shoulder is his [as a noun]mechanical owl[end as], wings folded.[paragraph break]An archway leads east back into the hall. From the southwest comes the clatter of Writing hammers and the sharp smell of burnt ink.[if Gong Sounding is happening][paragraph break][as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as] doesn't seem to notice the ringing gongs. He's too engrossed in the smells from his [as a noun]teacup[end as].[end if]"

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Shelves

Some shelves are scenery, in the Library. "The [as a noun]shelves[end as] have a lean to them because the <Italic>[as a noun]Determininium[end as]</Italic> by St Babbage is such a weightier tome than the <Italic>[as a noun]Principia[end as]</Italic> of St Newton. 'Truly blessed are the smaller and most intricate,' as they say."

Understand "shelf", "shelving" as the shelves.

Instead of putting something on the shelves:
	say "The [as a noun]shelves[end as] are only for the holiest of texts.";

Instead of climbing the shelves:
	say "They'd collapse in a moment.";

Section 2 - Books

Some books are scenery, in the Library. "Old leather bound tomes."

Understand "leather", "bound", "tomes", "holy", "texts", "determinium", "principia", "mechanistica", "leather-bound" as the books.

Instead of taking the books:
	say "[one of]A 2nd Assistant Clock Polisher isn't even allowed to dust the Holy Texts. Besides, I only read Latin and these [as a noun]books[end as] are all in Mathematik.[or]Really, these [as a noun]books[end as] are in Mathematik. I barely speak my own language.[or]I can't read these [as a noun]books[end as]! They're all Holy Gibberish![stopping]";

Instead of reading the books:
	try taking the books;

Instead of researching when in the Library:
	try taking the books.

Instead of consulting the books about when in the Library:
	try taking the books.

Instead of smelling the books:
	say "They smell of dust and damp and animal skin."

Section 3 - Stained Glass Window

The library astrolabe window is a stained glass window, scenery, in the Library. "The glass [as a noun]panels[end as] are stained to depict a [as a noun]watch escapement[end as], an [as a noun]astrolabe[end as] and below them both, the wide-spread arms of a beatific [as a noun]orrery[end as]. Not much light gets through them at all."

Understand "windows", "panels", "panel", "watch", "escapement", "astrolabe", "orrery" as the library astrolabe window.

Section 4 - Table

A wheeled table is fixed in place, Drake-hideable, in the Library. The description is "The table is set on little brass [as a noun]castors[end as] to allow Brother Horloge to follow the feeble patch of sunlight around the room."

Understand "castor", "caster", "castors", "casters", "wheel", "wheels" as the wheeled table.

Rule for writing a paragraph about the wheeled table:
	[we suppress any "on the wheeled table is..." style messages:]
	now the wheeled table is mentioned;

Section 5 - Manuscript

A manuscript is scenery, in the Library. "[as a noun]Horloge[end as] is poring over a [as a noun]complex diagram[end as], depicting the inner works of a <Italic>[as a noun]fusee[end as]</Italic>."
Understand "fusee", "diagram", "complex diagram" as the manuscript.

Instead of taking the manuscript:
	say "[as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as] is busy with it.";

Chapter 3 - Horloge

Section 1 - Description

Horloge is a monk, scenery, in the Library. "[as a noun]Horloge[end as]'s eyes are screwed up behind the most complicated pair of [as a noun]spectacles[end as] I've ever encountered. They make it hard to even see his eyes – or rather what's left of them. He's read in the darkness here in the Library for so long he's basically blind. On his shoulder sits his pet [as a noun]owl[end as]. Same as [as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as] it looks more fearsome than it actually is.[if Horloge's keys are on the wheeled table][paragraph break]By his elbow is a fat bunch of [as a noun]keys[end as].[otherwise if Horloge's keys are in the location][paragraph break]On the floor by his feet is a fat bunch of [as a noun]keys[end as].[end if][paragraph break][if Horloge is sipping his tea]He's sipping his [as a noun]tea[end as], quite content, eyes half-closed.[otherwise]He's poring over a page of [as a noun]manuscript[end as], muttering quietly to himself.[end if]"

Horloge's spectacles are carried by Horloge. The description is "[as a noun]Horloge[end as] was probably born wearing [as a noun]spectacles[end as]. They're made of bottle-glass, ground to the right thickness.  '[as a noun]Spectacles[end as] are either expensive,' as he's told me, 'or simply not very good.'"

Before doing something when Horloge's spectacles are physically involved:
	say "I'd better leave them be[one of]. He's blind without them[once only]."

Understand "glasses", "bottle glass", "bottle-glass", "lenses" as Horloge's spectacles.

Section 2 - Idle Actions

The HorlogeCounter is a counter. The top end is 4. The internal value is 2. [We have the 'quest for tea' event on the first turn we enter.]

Every turn when the player can see Horloge:
	increment the HorlogeCounter;

Rule for firing the HorlogeCounter:
	say "[if Horloge is not sipping his tea][one of][as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as] mumbles as he traces over the sheet of [as a noun]manuscript[end as].[or]The sun shifts a little, and the [as a noun]Brother[end as] nudges the table to follow the pool of light.[or][as a noun]Horloge[end as]'s finger shakes terribly as he traces the Holy Lines across the sheet.[or]Horloge reaches up to pet his [as a noun]owl[end as]. The bird is motionless.[or][as a noun]Horloge[end as] chucks his pet [as a noun]owl[end as] under its beak. There is no reaction.[at random][otherwise][one of][as a noun]Brother Horloge[end as] leans back in his chair and sips his [as a noun]tea[end as] with eyes closed.[or][as a noun]Horloge[end as] is clearly enjoying his [as a noun]cup of tea[end as].[or][as a noun]Horloge[end as] reaches up to stroke his owl. The bird is motionless.[at random][end if]";

Section 2b - Asking for

[ In future though, we might consider removing the "asking it for" action altogether, and have instead:
	[ Understand "ask [someone] for [text]" as asking it about. ]

...And dealing with whatever (hopefully lesser) problems that brings. ]

Instead of asking Horloge for Horloge's keys:
	if the player has Horloge's keys:
		say "I don't need to!";
	else:
		try taking Horloge's keys.

Instead of asking Horloge for the mechanical owl:
	say "He'd never give me his [as a noun]owl[end as]. It'd be like giving me his lungs, or one of his arms."

Instead of asking Horloge to try turning or switching on the mechanical owl:
	say "I think the [as a noun]Brother[end as] knows how to wind a clockwork. He doesn't need <Italic>me</Italic> to tell him."

Section 3 - Conversation

TRIG_WHOTSIT is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_WHOTSIT:
	say "I open my mouth to speak.[paragraph break]'Look here, Whotsit,' he interrupts. 'I've been at these books all day. Grappling with the Mysteries of the Whotsit. A cup of whotsit, that's all I want.'[paragraph break]";

Rule for speaking with Horloge when TRIG_WHOTSIT is unfired during the Quest For Tea:
	fire TRIG_WHOTSIT;

After speaking with Horloge when Horloge's keys are on the wheeled table:
	move Horloge's keys to the location;
	say "[one of]He's so enthusiastic as he talks that he knocks his [as a noun]keys[end as] onto the floor, though he doesn't seem to notice.[or]He knocks his [as a noun]keys[end as] back onto the floor.[or]He knocks his [as a noun]keys[end as] onto the floor again.[stopping]";

The conversation table of Horloge is the Table of Horloge's Conversation.

Table of Horloge's Conversation
topic					conversation
"tea"					CT_HOR1_TEA
"drink" or "a drink" or "cup" or "a cup"		CT_HOR1_DRINK
"whotsit/wotsit/whatsit/watsit"		CT_HOR1_WHOTSIT

CT_HOR1_DRINK is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'A drink?' I ask." The response text is "'A good cup of that whotsit, yes. Now run.' He waves a finger vaguely. 'Run along.'".

CT_HOR1_TEA is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'A cup of [as a noun]tea[end as]?' I ask.". The response text is "'Of course a cup of whotsit!' [as a noun]Horloge[end as] replies hotly. 'Do I have to say everything twice? Do I have to...' He pauses, appearing to have forgotten what he was saying. 'Say everything twice?' he finishes, uncertainly. 'Good.'" 		

CT_HOR1_WHOTSIT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'A whotsit?' I ask.". The response text is "'Yes, quite right,' he replies, shaking a finger. 'Nice and hot.'"

The default topic of Horloge is CT_HOR1_DEFAULT.

CT_HOR1_DEFAULT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "I open my mouth to speak." The response text is "[one of]'Don't stand there babbling,' [as a noun]Horloge[end as] interrupts. 'I mean, really, very simple. Just a cup of... a cup of... you know. Yes.'[or]'What kind of scullery maid are you?' he interrupts. Blinder than I thought, clearly. Any monk should know a 2nd Assistant Polisher when they see one.[or]'A cup of whotsit, whotsit,' he insists. 'And don't spare the horses.'[stopping]"

Section 4 - Owl

Horloge's mechanical owl is carried by Horloge. The description is "[as a noun]Horloge[end as]'s [as a noun]mechanical owl[end as] sits on his shoulder. It seems to be asleep, its spring unwound." The printed name is "Horloge's mechanical owl". Understand "rachael", "spring" as the mechanical owl.

Instead of turning Horloge's mechanical owl:
	say "The [as a noun]owl[end as] seems to have lost its key somewhere. It's probably on [as a noun]Horloge[end as]'s [as a noun]keyring[end as].";

Instead of unscrewing Horloge's mechanical owl with the lucky clock key:
	say "My [as a noun]clock key[end as] would never fit [as a noun]Horloge[end as]'s bird: it's far too delicate a mechanism.";

Instead of turning Horloge's mechanical owl when Horloge's keys are carried:
	say "I don't want [as a noun]Horloge[end as] to see I've got his keys.";

Instead of taking or touching or pushing or attacking Horloge's mechanical owl:
	say "Most of the monks have an animal of some kind - it's the height of rudeness to play with them, however. I don't touch."

