The LHALL_glimpse is a locative glimpse backdrop in the Scriptorium, localising the Lower Hall.

The KITCHEN_glimpse is a locative glimpse backdrop in the Library, in the Scriptorium, in the Central Hall, in the Lower Hall, in the Upper Hall, in the Rickety Stair, in the Attic Room, in the East Refectory, in the West Refectory, in the Abbey Herb Garden, in the Entry, in the Corridor of Contemplation, in the Abbot's Quarters, localising the Kitchen.

The SCRIPT_glimpse is a locative glimpse backdrop in the Library, in the Kitchen, in the Central Hall, in the Lower Hall, in the Upper Hall, in the Rickety Stair, in the Attic Room, in the East Refectory, in the West Refectory, in the Abbey Herb Garden, in the Entry, in the Corridor of Contemplation, in the Abbot's Quarters, localising the Scriptorium.

The ABBEYLIBRARY_glimpse is a locative glimpse backdrop in the Kitchen, in the Scriptorium, in the Central Hall, in the Lower Hall, in the Upper Hall, in the Rickety Stair, in the Attic Room, in the East Refectory, in the West Refectory, in the Abbey Herb Garden, in the Entry, in the Corridor of Contemplation, in the Abbot's Quarters, localising the Library.

The ENTRY_glimpse is a locative glimpse backdrop  in the Library, in the Scriptorium, in the Central Hall, in the Lower Hall, in the Upper Hall, in the Rickety Stair, in the Attic Room, in the East Refectory, in the West Refectory, in the Abbey Herb Garden, in the Kitchen, in the Corridor of Contemplation, in the Abbot's Quarters, localising the Entry.

Chapter 2 - The Machinery

The revolving metal plate is scenery in the Herb Garden. "The entire [as a noun]garden[end as] floor is one revolving [as a noun]metal plate[end as], set on [as a noun]castors[end as], that ticks slowly round to follow the motion of the sun around the [as a noun]garden[end as]. There's even a low [as a noun]cover[end as] at one end, to shield the [as a noun]plants[end as] from interested [as a noun]birds[end as] at four in the morning.[paragraph break]In the centre is the large [as a noun]lens[end as] that drives the mechanism." 

Understand "disc", "floor", "garden" as the revolving metal plate.

Instead of taking or pushing or attacking the metal plate: say "It's enormous: would take four men just to roll it into place. The garden [as a noun]walls[end as] were probably built around it."

Instead of looking under the metal plate: say "There are [as a noun]castors[end as] there - I can't see them, but I'm a believer in clockwork - I don't have to see it to know it's there."

Instead of setting or turning the metal plate: say "It doesn't have any levers or pulleys. The whole [as a noun]plate[end as] is powered by the rays of the sun, focused through the [as a noun]lens[end as] at its centre."

Understand "castors/castor" as the revolving metal plate.

Instead of entering the metal plate:
	say "Where do you think I'm standing now?"

Section 2 - giant lens

The large lens is an eyeable supporter, part of the revolving metal plate. The description of the large lens is "The [as a noun]lens[end as] drives the mechanism, by collecting sunlight and using it to heat a [as a noun]spring[end as] of copper underneath the [as a noun]plate[end as]. The [as a noun]spring[end as] then winds and unwinds as it extends and shrinks, and the [as a noun]plate[end as] turns around - keeping the [as a noun]day-plants[end as], [as a noun]herbs[end as] and [as a noun]vegetables[end as], in the best sunshine possible, and at night, letting the [as a noun]mushrooms[end as] come out to collect the dew[if there is something on the large lens].[paragraph break]On the [as a noun]lens[end as] is [the list of things on large lens][end if].";

Instead of looking through the large lens:
	say "Peering through the [as a noun]lens[end as] I can just see the outline of the gigantic copper [as a noun]spring[end as] that drives the [as a noun]garden[end as] round and around."

Instead of looking under the large lens:
	say "Looking under I can't do. Through might be more possible."

Instead of entering the large lens:
	say "I can't pass through glass. Only springs of light can go through glass!"

Instead of standing on the large lens:
	say "I might break the glass. (Or get it really muddy.)";

To revolve into daytime:
	now the plants are day-plants;
	remove the short stake from play;
	say 	"[one of]The [as a noun]garden[end as] revolves back as light hits the [as a noun]lens[end as] once more. The [as a noun]herbs[end as] and [as a noun]vegetables[end as] come out of hiding into the sunshine! [or]The [as a noun]lens[end as] lights up and the [as a noun]day-beds[end as] revolve back out from the under the [as a noun]cover[end as][stopping][one of]. The movement almost knocks me over[or]. I have to balance to stay upright as the [as a noun]plate[end as] turns[or][cycling].";

To revolve into nighttime:
	now the plants are night-plants;
	if the knife is not handled, move the short stake to the Abbey Herb Garden; 
	say "[one of]There's a pause, and then the whole [as a noun]garden[end as] begins to revolve! The [as a noun]day-plants[end as] move under the shade of the metal [as a noun]hood[end as], and the [as a noun]night-plants[end as] - [as a noun]mushrooms[end as] and [as a noun]fungi[end as] - emerge in their place[or]The [as a noun]garden[end as] revolves once more, bringing the [as a noun]night-plants[end as] back into view[stopping][if knife is not handled]. Stuck in the beds amongst them is a short [as a noun]stake[end as][end if].";

Instead of closing or touching the large lens when the plants are day-plants:
	say "I put both hands over the [as a noun]lens[end as]. ";
	revolve into nighttime;

Definition: something is away from the lens if it is not the large lens and it is not enclosed by the player.

Before doing something in the Abbey Herb Garden when something away from the lens is physically involved or going west: 
	if there is no thing on the large lens and the plants are night-plants:
		say "I lift my hand away from the [as a noun]lens[end as]. ";
		revolve into daytime;
		if the short stake is physically involved or the plants are physically involved, stop the action;
	continue the action;

Before putting the tumbler on the large lens:
	say "The [as a noun]glass[end as] wouldn't block the [as a noun]light[end as]. Glass doesn't block light-springs. The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says so." instead.

Before putting something on the large lens:
	if the noun is not the rag:
		say "[The noun] [is-are] tiny compared to the size of the [as a noun]lens[end as]." instead;

After putting something on the large lens:
	say "I cover the [as a noun]lens[end as] with [the noun]. ";
	if the plants are day-plants:
		revolve into nighttime instead;

Instead of taking something when the noun is on the large lens:
	say "I remove [the noun] from the [as a noun]lens[end as]. ";
	now the noun is carried by the player;
	revolve into daytime instead;

Section 3-  the Hood

The metal hood is scenery, in the Herb Garden. Understand "cover", "low cover" as the hood. "The metal [as a noun]hood[end as] comes up to my knees, and is snugly built for the [as a noun]plants[end as] to slide in underneath without being hurt. It keeps the [as a noun]mushrooms[end as] shady (and warm) during the day, and the [as a noun]vegetables[end as] protected from snails at night."

Instead of hiding under or entering or hiding behind the metal hood: 
	[ a bit rubbish attempt to distinguish "on" and "in" ]
	if the player is clambering or the player's command includes "on", try climbing the metal hood instead;
	say "The [as a noun]hood[end as] is filled with [as a noun]flowerbeds[end as]. There's no room for me in there!"

Instead of opening the metal hood:
	say "It's fixed and solid, not something I could move."

Instead of looking under the metal hood:
	say "Under the [as a noun]hood[end as] is the other half of the revolving [as a noun]garden[end as]."

Instead of standing on or climbing the metal hood:
	say "[one of]I clamber onto the top of the [as a noun]hood[end as] - slippery, and rounded, and I soon slide off again. If I'm looking for a way over the [as a noun]walls[end as], I'll have to try something else.[or]I can't climb onto the [as a noun]hood[end as] - and even if I could, the [as a noun]walls[end as] are twelve feet tall and the [as a noun]hood[end as]'s only up to my knees![or]The [as a noun]hood[end as]'s not going to do me any good.[stopping]"

The copper spring is scenery, part of the revolving plate. "There's a [as a noun]spring[end as] somewhere underneath, that expands and contracts in the heat from the [as a noun]lens[end as], and drives the whole [as a noun]garden[end as] around."

Instead of doing something when the copper spring is physically involved:
	say "I can't see the [as a noun]spring[end as], it's underneath the [as a noun]garden[end as] floor.";

Chapter 3 - the Plants

Section 1 - Soil Beds

Some soil beds are scenery in the Herb Garden. Understand "flowerbed/bed", "flower bed/beds", "tub", "tubs", "ceramic", "earth" as the soil beds. "The [as a noun]soil beds[end as] are [as a noun]ceramic tubs[end as], deep enough for growing tubers and wide enough to hold a good range of [as a noun]plants[end as]. The metal [as a noun]plate[end as] they're set on revolves them to follow the sun around the [as a noun]garden[end as], so each plant gets exactly what it needs.[paragraph break]In the visible [as a noun]tubs[end as] are [if plants are night-plants][as a noun]mushrooms[end as] and [as a noun]nightcaps[end as][else][as a noun]herbs[end as] and [as a noun]vegetables[end as][end if]." 

Understand "day-beds", "day bed/beds", "day-bed" as the soil beds when the plants are day-plants.
Understand "night-beds", "night bed/beds", "night-bed", "mushroom bed/beds" as the soil beds when the plants are night-plants.

Instead of putting something on the soil beds: 
	try taking the plants.

Instead of inserting something into the soil beds:
	try taking the plants.

Instead of searching the soil beds:
	say "I'm not a dog!"

Instead of cleaning the soil beds:
	say "[as a noun]Soil[end as] is pretty clean already, or so the gardener always says."

Section 2 - plants

Some plants are scenery in the Herb Garden. The plants can be day-plants or night-plants. The plants are day-plants.

Rule for printing the description of the day-plants plants:
	say "The [as a noun]day-plants[end as] are lush and verdant, thanks to the metal [as a noun]plate[end as] that keeps them out of the shadows of the walls."

Rule for printing the description of the night-plants plants:
	say "The [as a noun]night-plants[end as] look dry and withered, like old men, or the oldest of the old monks, the ones who need straws to eat."

Understand "herb/herbs/plant/flower/flowers/basil/potatoes/vegetables" or "day-plants" or "day plants" as the plants when the plants are day-plants. 
Understand "mushroom/mushrooms/fungi/fungus/nightcap/thyme" or "night-plants" or "night plants" as the plants when the plants are night-plants. 

Instead of taking or pulling or attacking the day-plants plants:
	say "I don't think [as a noun]Brother Saffron[end as] would appreciate me messing with his [as a noun]plants[end as]."

Instead of eating or tasting the day-plants plants:
	say "Some need cooking before they're edible: some the leaves are tough as leather and only what's below the surface is any good. And I'm a 2nd Assistant Clock Polisher, so I certainly don't know tick from tock."

Instead of eating or tasting or touching or taking or pulling or attacking the night-plants plants:
	say "Plenty of these [as a noun]mushrooms[end as] are grown for the [as a noun]Apothecary[end as]. Some might drive you mad, or blind, or worse. I'm not touching any." 

Instead of smelling in the Herb Garden:
	say "There's a whole range of smells, from sweet to spicy."

Instead of searching the night-plants plants when the short stake is in the location:
	say "In amongst the plants is this short wooden [as a noun]stake[end as]."

Instead of searching or looking under the plants:
	say "There's nothing but [as a noun]soil[end as] under the [if plants are day-plants][as a noun]leaves[end as][else][as a noun]caps[end as][end if]."

Section 3 - Hidden plants

[EU: No need for hidden plants to be privately-named.]
Some hidden plants are [privately-named,] scenery in the Herb Garden.  The printed name is "hidden plants".

Understand "herb/herbs/plant/flower/flowers/basil/potatoes/vegetables" or "day-plants" or "day plants" as the hidden plants when the plants are night-plants. 
Understand "mushroom/mushrooms/fungi/fungus/nightcap/thyme" or "night-plants" or "night plants" as the hidden plants when the plants are day-plants. 

Before doing something when the hidden plants are involved:
	say "Those [as a noun]plants[end as] are out of the daylight and out of reach, underneath the metal [as a noun]hood[end as]." instead.

Section 5 - the stake in the ground

The short stake is a thing. "Sticking from one of the [as a noun]beds[end as] newly emerged from the cover is a short [as a noun]stake[end as]."

Instead of taking or pulling or looking under or pushing the short stake:
	[ we reward the player with a knife, and remove the Tome in the library where the player would otherwise pick this up in Chapter 3. ]
	remove the gigantic Tome from play;
	remove the short stake from play;
	move the knife to the player;
	now the knife is handled;
	now the knife is a garden-knife;
	say "I pull the [as a noun]stake[end as] free of the [as a noun]soil[end as], only to find it's the handle of a [as a noun]gardener's knife[end as]! Someone must have left it here by accident. Still, could be useful if I meet any [as a noun]Grey Figure[end as]s..."

Understand "short", "stake" as the knife when the knife is a garden-knife.
Understand "handle" as the knife when the player cannot see the dowel handle and the player cannot see the makeshift torch.

Book 3 - The Cathedral Of Time

The Cathedral Space is a region.

Part 1 - Cathedral Yard

Chapter 1 - Description

The Cathedral Yard is a room, exterior, in the Cathedral Space. "The Yard is where we go on Saturdays for exercise, cleaning up the muck left behind by the market (worse once a month when the horse-traders from the south come up and leave their presents for the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] gardens). Today it's quiet, at least, with the space between the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] to the east and the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] to the west empty but for the usual collection of [as a noun]beggars[end as], [as a noun]unfortunates[end as], [as a noun]wretches[end as] and the [as a noun]disadvantaged poor[end as].[one of][paragraph break]The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] really is enormous – but it doesn't have many rooms, so finding the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] shouldn't take too long. Which is lucky, because I don't know how long I've got before [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] – and the mysterious [as a noun]Figure[end as] – put their plan into action. If I warn the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] too late it'll be no good. He might just blame me for whatever it is they're planning to steal![once only]"

Instead of going east in the Cathedral Yard:
	say "No, I need to find the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as], not get rounded up by [as a noun]Calvin[end as] and [as a noun]Drake[end as] and set to work polishing again[if Gong Sounding is happening]. Besides, by the sound of the clock, it's 5pm in there, which would be far too late![else].[end if]"

Instead of entering the Abbey Doors in the Cathedral Yard:
	try going east.

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-sky when in the Cathedral Yard:
	say "Clouds are turning around, but only slowly."

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-floor when in the Cathedral Yard:
	say "The yard is dusty underfoot."

Understand "dust" as the backdrop-floor when in the Cathedral Yard.


Instead of going inside in the Cathedral Yard: try going west.
Instead of making to leave in the Cathedral Yard: try going west.

Chapter 1b - Wretches

Section - Description


Some poor wretches are men in the Cathedral Yard. Understand "beggar/beggars", "unfortunates", "disadvantaged", "leper/lepers" as the poor wretches.  The description of the poor wretches is "In a civilised city like [as a noun]St Philip[end as] there's no reason for anyone to beg. That's what the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says. There's always a spring that needs winding somewhere, so no-one need be short of a coin unless they're too Vice-ridden to work... But then, I did just see the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] taking money for no work at all..."

Rule for writing a paragraph about the poor wretches:
	if BEGGAR_YARD1 is unfired:
		now the poor wretches are mentioned;
	else:
		say "Two [as a noun]beggars[end as] are either side of me, close enough to grasp at my arm!";

Instead of giving something to the poor wretches:
	try dropping the noun;

Instead of touching or pushing or attacking the poor wretches:
	say "I don't want to go near them. Who knows what I'll catch.";

After dropping something in the Cathedral Yard when BEGGAR_YARD2 is unfired:
	remove the noun from play;
	now the poor wretches are scenery;
	say "I drop [the noun] and the [as a noun]beggars[end as] fall on it like hungry wolves, scrabbling and fighting. One gets hold and soon both are gone in a cloud of dust.";
	now BEGGAR_YARD2 is fired;

Report dropping something in the Cathedral Yard:
	say "I drop [the noun] but the [as a noun]beggars[end as] don't seem interested." instead;

Section 2 - Blocking your way

Instead of going west when BEGGAR_YARD1 is fired and BEGGAR_YARD2 is unfired and BEGGAR_WONT_MOVE is unfired:
	fire BEGGAR_WONT_MOVE;

Rule for firing unfired BEGGAR_WONT_MOVE:
	say "The [as a noun]beggars[end as] are in my way! I'm staying clear.";

Instead of going west when BEGGAR_YARD1 is fired and BEGGAR_YARD2 is unfired:
	now BEGGAR_YARD2 is fired;
	say "<Italic>Time to wind your courage, Wren</Italic>. I put my elbows out and push my way between the grasping hands.";
	continue the action;

Instead of touching or pushing or attacking the poor wretches when BEGGAR_YARD1 is fired and BEGGAR_YARD2 is unfired:
	now BEGGAR_WONT_MOVE is fired;
	try going west instead;

Rule for speaking with the poor wretches:
	say "I don't want to hear anything from them!" instead.

Section 2 - Beggars get in the way

BEGGAR_YARD1 is a trigger. BEGGAR_YARD2 is a trigger. BEGGAR_WONT_MOVE is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired BEGGAR_YARD1:
	say "A couple of [as a noun]beggars[end as] shuffle over to me, their hands held out pitifully. They're diseased, like rats, and covered in sores. I hang back, pretty disgusted."

Every turn when BEGGAR_YARD1 is fired and BEGGAR_YARD2 is unfired and a random chance of 1 in 2 succeeds:
	if going west or entering the Cathedral of Time or going inside:
		do nothing;
	else if BEGGAR_YARD1 has been fired for at least 6 turns:
		fire BEGGAR_YARD2;
	else if ABBOT_BEGGAR is unfired:
		fire ABBOT_BEGGAR;
	else:	
		say "[one of]The [as a noun]beggars[end as][or]The diseased [as a noun]wretches[end as][or]The [as a noun]unfortunates[end as] in front of me[at random] [one of]moan and grumble[or]plead and beg[or]hold out diseased hands[or]scratch their way across the dust towards me[at random][one of] pitifully[or], looking ill[or] with agonising slowness[at random].";

ABBOT_BEGGAR is a trigger. 

Rule for firing unfired ABBOT_BEGGAR:
		say "'Think of a spring,' the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] would say, talking about the likes of these beggars. 'Put something in, and you will receive. But Mechanics declares that only nothing can come of no work.'";

[This rule comes here so that the rule above won't fire when this one fires.]

Every turn when in the Cathedral Yard and BEGGAR_YARD1 is unfired:
	fire BEGGAR_YARD1;	

Rule for firing unfired BEGGAR_YARD2:
	now the poor wretches are scenery;
	say "Without a word, the creeping [as a noun]beggars[end as] lose interest in me and move away across the yard.";

Every turn when in the Cathedral Yard and BEGGAR_YARD2 is fired and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]beggars[end as] are baying and moaning softly.[or]One of the [as a noun]wretches[end as] is muttering about 'clockwerk men'. Poor man.[or]The [as a noun]wretches[end as] are tussling amongst themselves.[cycling]";

Chapter 2 - The Cathedral object

The Cathedral of Time is backdrop, in the Cathedral Space.  Understand "church" as the Cathedral of Time.

Understand "door/doors" as the Cathedral of Time when in the Cathedral Yard.
Understand "choir" as the Cathedral of Time when not in the Cathedral Choir.
Understand "nave" as the Cathedral of Time.
Understand "clerestory" as the Cathedral of Time when the location is not the Cathedral Choir.


Before doing something when the backdrop-walls are involved and the Cathedral of Time is visible:
	redirect the action from the backdrop-walls to the Cathedral of Time, and try that instead.

Rule for printing the description of the Cathedral of Time when in the Cathedral Yard:
	say "The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] door is open to the west, like a big mouth ready to swallow me whole."

Rule for printing the description of the Cathedral of Time:
	say "The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] walls rise all around me."	

Instead of entering the Cathedral of Time: 
	try going inside.

Instead of getting off the Cathedral of Time:
	try going outside.

Instead of listening when in the Cathedral Space:
	say "The soft sound of chanting drifts down the length of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as].";

Instead of listening when in the Cathedral Space during Return To The Cathedral:
	[[JON]: Up to you to provide some eerie quiet... ]
	say "The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] is soft eerie quiet and nothing more.";

Instead of listening when in the Cathedral Space and Calvin Cutscene is happening during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[one of]My breath is hammering in my ears.[or]I crouch as close as I can.[or]Their voices are quiet.[cycling]".

Part 2 - Great Door

Chapter 1 - Description

The Cathedral Entrance is west of the Cathedral Yard,  in the Cathedral Space. "Imagine an ant crawling up inside a daffodil trumpet. That's what it's like to stand in here, and this is just the Entry to the massive [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] further north (I guess in there you'll need to imagine an ant inside a church bell).[paragraph break]Let me try and do better. The main doors to the street are south and about as tall as the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] itself, so they need a lot of space just to fit the hinges in (those are about as big as me, I think). I came in from the Yard by the east door; there's another similar door west but it's locked right now - the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] is closed to the public most of the time.[paragraph break]Looking northwards I can see the long Nave, and the altar beyond. Carved on the floor just by my feet is the first of the aisle's [as a noun]Great Seal[end as]s." The printed name is "Great Door".

Instead of making to leave when in the Cathedral Entrance: try going east instead.
Instead of going inside in the Cathedral Entrance: try going north instead.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Great Seal


The entry seal is a Great Seal, in the Cathedral Entrance. "The [as a noun]first seal[end as] is made of [as a noun]steel[end as] cast directly onto the stone. It's so big that it's hard to see what it is, but – a long rod, tilting a large boulder – it's got to be a [as a noun]lever[end as]." Understand "lever", "steel", "boulder", "rod", "first", "great" as the entry seal. The printed name is "Great Seal".

Instead of pulling the entry seal:
	say "It's a carved [as a noun]lever[end as]. It doesn't <Italic>do</Italic> anything[one of]. (But is a [as a noun]lever[end as] that doesn't do anything a [as a noun]lever[end as]? I'm too young for philosophy!)[or].[stopping]";



Section 2 - Great Doors

Some Great Doors are a closed door, scenery, south of the Cathedral Entrance. "The [as a noun]doors[end as] are covered in [as a noun]carvings[end as] and [as a noun]inscriptions[end as], some of the Mechanists, but ever a few from the older faiths of smoke and fire. The [as a noun]doors[end as] lead to the city but are rarely open (Last Newtonmass I was part of a parade of children that troop to get the homeless to do some proper work, but I'll be too old for that this year.)" Understand "huge", "door", "carving", "hinge", "hinges", "inscription", "carvings", "inscriptions" as the Great Doors.

Instead of opening the Great Doors:
	say "You're joking, I suppose.";

Instead of going south when in the Cathedral Entrance: try entering the Great Doors.

Instead of entering the Great Doors:
	say "I'd have to open the Great Doors. [run paragraph on]";
	try opening the Great Doors;

Section 3 - West Door

The West Door is a closed door, locked, scenery, west of the Cathedral Entrance, east of the Public Yard. "A smaller [as a noun]door[end as] leading west to another yard, and the city. It's locked, of course."

Instead of opening the locked West Door:
	say "The West Door is locked.";

First before going through the locked West Door:
	try opening the West Door instead;

Section 4 - East Door

The East Door is scenery, in the Cathedral Entrance. "That's the [as a noun]door[end as] back to the Cathedral Yard."

Instead of opening or entering the East Door when the implicitly opening activity is not going on:
	try going east;

Instead of opening the East Door when Upper Nave is not visited:
	say "The [as a noun]East Door[end as] is open already - I just came in that way, remember?"

Instead of entering the East Door:
	try going east instead;

Instead of closing the East Door:
	say "It's not for me to go round shutting doors in this place.";



Part 3 - Lower Nave

Chapter 1 - Description

The Lower Nave is north of the Cathedral Entrance, in the Cathedral Space. "The Nave of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] is a quiet place, quieter than the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] because the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] only allows study here and no workshops. [as a noun]Pews[end as] on either side are enough to seat a thousand easily, with more further north. I can't look at the ceiling (it's so high) without lying down across the [as a noun]Second Great Seal[end as], and the last time I did that a monk tripped up on me and had me thrown out.[paragraph break]East and west are smaller [as a noun]shrines[end as], and back south are the Cathedral's [as a noun]Great Doors[end as]."

Instead of making to leave when in the Lower Nave: try going south instead.
Instead of going inside in the Lower Nave: try going north instead.

Instead of smelling in the Lower Nave:
	say "There's a smell of burning oil from the west.";

Section 2 - Idle actions

Every turn when in the Lower Nave and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds and not looking and not going and not smelling and the heat is not the noun and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "A little heat licks in from the west.";

Instead of sleeping in the Lower Nave:
	say "I'm not trying that again."

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 0 - Heat

The heat is scenery in the Lower Nave. Understand "little/gentle heat", "warmth", "hot/warm air/gust/wind" as the heat. "The heat licks in from the west."

Before doing something when the heat is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "It's just a wisp of heat." instead.

Before touching the heat:
	say "It's only gentle, almost nothing at all."

Before approaching the heat:
	try going east instead.

Before smelling the heat:
	say "The hot air smells of wax and oil." instead.


Section 0b - View of Doors

The glimpse-doors are a glimpse backdrop in the Lower Nave, in the Upper Nave, in the Cathedral Altar, identifying the Great Doors. "The [as a noun]Doors[end as] frame the entrance to the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] in shadow." Understand "doors", "great" as the glimpse-doors.

Section 1b - Shrines

Some shrines are a glimpse backdrop in the Lower Nave, in the Upper Nave, in the Cathedral Entrance, in the Cathedral Altar, in the East Apse, in the West Apse. Understand "shrines" as the shrines. "There are two [as a noun]shrines[end as] - the Calendar Shrine and the Shrine of the Saints."

Instead of approaching or entering the shrines when the location is the Lower Nave:
	say "There are two [as a noun]shrines[end as] - east and west of here."

Instead of approaching or entering the shrines:
	let the way be the best route from the location to the Lower Nave, using doors;
	if the way is a direction, try going the way instead;
	continue the action;

Section 1 - Lower Seal

The Lower Seal is a Great Seal, in the Lower Nave. "This one is made of [as a noun]brass[end as], all wound up into a [as a noun]coil[end as]. It's either a [as a noun]spring[end as] or one of Reloh's mechanical serpents."

Understand "spring", "brass", "coil", "second", "great" as the lower seal. The printed name of the Lower Seal is "Great Seal".

Section 2 - Pews

Some lower pews are a pew, in the Lower Nave. "Rows of varnished oak [as a noun]pews[end as]. I'm glad I'm not a [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] Wood Polisher! That'd take <Italic>forever</Italic>!" The printed name is "pews".

Part 4 - Upper Nave

Chapter 1 - Description

The Upper Nave is north of the Lower Nave, in the Cathedral Space. "This is the north end of the Nave, where the more important people sit during services – you can tell that because the [as a noun]pews[end as] either side are wider and have more legroom, and each has a little cup-holder carved into its armrest. Of course, it's also closer to the [as a noun]Altar[end as] to the north (and further from the draught coming up from the [as a noun]doors[end as] to the south).[paragraph break]A group of [as a noun]monks[end as] are standing around the altar, singing softly[if East Clerestory is not visited]. The Archbishop's office will be beyond and up the stairs, I think[end if].[paragraph break]The floor is taken up with the [as a noun]Third Great Seal[end as]."

Instead of making to leave when in the Upper Nave: try going south instead.
Instead of going inside in the Upper Nave: try going north instead.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Upper Seal

The Upper Seal is a Great Seal, fixed in place, in the Upper Nave. The description is "This one is a fine [as a noun]gold[end as] gilt, depicting an enormous ornate [as a noun]head[end as] with stone-block [as a noun]teeth[end as]. A [as a noun]winding-key[end as]."

Understand "third", "gold", "golden", "gilt", "ornate", "enormous", "head", "teeth", "winding key", "winding-key", "key", "great" as the upper seal. The printed name is "Great Seal".

Instead of turning the Upper Seal:
	say "I can't wind a [as a noun]winding key[end as] that isn't in a clock. And this one is just in the floor!"

Section 2 - Pews

Some upper pews are a pew, in the Upper Nave. "Rows of varnished oak [as a noun]pews[end as]. I'm glad I'm not a Cathedral Wood Polisher! That'd take <Italic>forever</Italic>!" The printed name is "pews". Understand "cupholder", "cup holder", "cup-holder" as the upper pews.



Part 5 - Altar

Chapter 1 - Description

The Cathedral Altar is north of the Upper Nave, in the Cathedral Space. "On a [as a noun]dais[end as] is the main [as a noun]altar[end as], representing the Celestial Workbench itself. Hanging over it as large as the setting sun is the incredible machinery of the [as a noun]Cathedral Clock[end as] itself.[paragraph break]Around the [as a noun]dais[end as], a group of [as a noun]monks[end as] are celebrating and blessing something on the altar. I can slip away – quickly, please! – in any of the four main directions, although I might have to scurry to get around the [as a noun]dais[end as][if East Clerestory is not visited] (the Archbishop's room is north of here, I think, above the [as a noun]Choir[end as].)[else].[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when in the Cathedral Altar: try going south instead.
Instead of going inside in the Cathedral Altar: say "I'm in the heart of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] right here."

Instead of singing when in the Cathedral Space and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "I don't think the [as a noun]monks[end as] would appreciate my joining in."
	
Instead of hiding from view when in the Cathedral Altar and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "I'm already crouched as low as I can go."

Chapter 2 - Scenery

TRIG_NOINTERFERE is a trigger.


Rule for firing TRIG_NOINTERFERE:
	say "[one of]There's no way I'm interfering with a ceremony as serious as this.[or]I'm serious. I'll look, but I won't touch.[stopping]"

Section 1 - Altar

The iron altar is scenery, in the Cathedral Altar. "The [as a noun]altar[end as] is a fine workbench of iron, with gold tracings to mark out the Measures and Main Angles of work. But that's nothing compared to what's on the [as a noun]Altar[end as] – a [as a noun]mechanism[end as] of fine gold and shimmering brass. It's not a clock but it's restlessly moving – to what end I can't see, though, because of all the monks clustered around." The printed name is "altar".

Instead of doing something when the iron altar is physically involved:
	say "[fire TRIG_NOINTERFERE]";

Section 2 - Dais and Canopy

The dais is scenery, in the Cathedral Altar. "The [as a noun]dais[end as] is the fourth seal, raised up from the stone floor. It's right below the [as a noun]Cathedral Clock[end as] and the light from above bathes the iron trim in dull light." Understand "canopy" as the dais.

Instead of doing something when the dais is physically involved:
	say "[fire TRIG_NOINTERFERE]";

Section 3 - Clock Machinery

The cathedral clock machinery is a backdrop, in the Cathedral Altar and the Choir. "The [as a noun]machinery[end as] of the [as a noun]Clock[end as] disappears out of sight overhead. It's hard to see in detail because of the light that streams through, cut to ribbons as it comes by the enormous cog-teeth. I can just about see a gear the size of a horse and an escapement like the mast of tall ship. And both are moving, with eternal slowness."

Instead of doing something when the cathedral clock machinery is physically involved:
	say "It's far too far above me.";

Section 4 - Monks

Some chanting monks are men, scenery, in the Cathedral Altar. "This is why the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] is so quiet, then. All the brothers are here, singing and chanting at the altar." Understand "monk", "lead", "group of" as the chanting monks.

Instead of doing something when the chanting monks are physically involved:
	say "[fire TRIG_NOINTERFERE]";

Some glimpsed monks are a glimpse backdrop, in Cathedral Entrance, in Lower Nave, in Upper Nave, in East Apse, in West Apse, in North Clerestory, in Cathedral Choir, in East Clerestory, in West Clerestory. "The [as a noun]monks[end as] are gathered around some [as a noun]gleaming object[end as] on the [as a noun]altar[end as]." The printed name is "monks". Understand "monk/monks", "chanting", "singing", "group of" as the glimpsed Monks.

The glimpsed monks identifies the chanting Monks.

Instead of listening to something when the player can see the glimpsed Monks:
	say "The [as a noun]monks[end as]['] song echoes off the [as a noun]walls[end as], one syllable at a time, one second at a time.";

Section 5 - Perpetuum Mobile

The real Perpetuum Mobile is scenery, portable, privately-named, on the iron altar. The description is "The [as a noun]machine[end as] on the [as a noun]altar[end as] is a finely-wrought [as a noun]case[end as] of [as a noun]dark wood[end as] topped by a [as a noun]tilting board[end as]. Standing on tip-toes I can see a [as a noun]ball-bearing[end as], wearily wending its way along a [as a noun]grooved path[end as] in the surface of the [as a noun]board[end as]. As it reaches one side, there is a loud cheer from the [as a noun]monks[end as] – the [as a noun]board[end as] tilts – and the [as a noun]ball[end as] continues back the other way. The effect is mesmerizing and the [as a noun]mechanism[end as] almost totally silent.[paragraph break]I think it's the most beautiful – most expensive thing – that I've ever seen. This isn't just a relic, clearly. This is something else."

The printed name of the real perpetuum mobile is "Perpetuum Mobile".

Understand "case", "dark", "wood", "tilting", "board", "ball-bearing", "groove", "grooved", "path", "track", "gold", "brass", "ball", "ball bearing", "mechanism", "machine", "gleaming", "object" as the real Perpetuum Mobile.

Understand "perpetuum", "mobile" as the real perpetuum mobile.

Understand "real", "one" as the real perpetuum mobile when the player has handled the decoy perpetuum mobile.


Rule for printing the name of the real Perpetuum Mobile when the Bishop's Library is unvisited:
	say "gleaming object"

Instead of doing something when the real Perpetuum Mobile is physically involved and the location is the Cathedral Altar:
	say "[fire TRIG_NOINTERFERE]";

Section 6 - Perpetuum Mobile Ceremony

Every turn when the player can see the Chanting Monks:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]monks[end as] gather in close to the [as a noun]Altar[end as], as the [as a noun]mechanism[end as] they watch makes a sudden movement. 'In Perpetuum, we Observe,' a [as a noun]lead monk[end as] cries.[or]'In Perpetuum, we Observe,' the other [as a noun]monks[end as] reply, raising their hands and making the sign of the Penduluum in the air. 'A-tempus, a-fugit,' they chant.[or]'A-tempus, a-fugit,' repeats the [as a noun]lead monk[end as]. He steps up to the [as a noun]dais[end as], and reaches with one finger to touch the side of the mechanism. All is quiet.[or]The [as a noun]machine[end as] makes an almost imperceptible <Italic>click</Italic>. The [as a noun]lead monk[end as] steps down and is lost in the crowd of [as a noun]monks[end as]. A great chant goes up: 'In Perpetuum, In Mobilis, Forever and Ever, Happen! Happen!'[or]A new [as a noun]monk[end as] steps up from the group. He looks a tiny bit younger than the previous [as a noun]lead monk[end as]. 'In the name of the Lever,' he declares.[or]'The Lever!' cry the other [as a noun]monks[end as].[or]'In the name of the ...' the lead monk continues. Whatever he's about to say is smothered by a booming <Italic>clonk</Italic> from the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] clock machinery above. The other monks seemed unfazed by this.[or]'And in the presence of the Winding Key,' the [as a noun]monk[end as] finishes, before making the sign of Winding. The other monks repeat the gesture. 'In Perpetuum, we Observe,' they chant.[cycling]"

Instead of listening to the location when the player can see the Chanting Monks:
	say "The [as a noun]monks[end as] are working through their ritual.";

Part 6 - Choir

Chapter 1 - Description

The Cathedral Choir is north of the Cathedral Altar, in the Cathedral Space. "To the north of the [as a noun]altar[end as], this is a semi-circular space with staircases to east and west leading up to the gallery overhead. When they sing from up there – I've only heard it once – they fill the whole [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] with their beating rhythmic music: songs that echo round and around the dome overhead like the movement of the Greater Rotation itself." The printed name is "Choir".

Instead of making to leave when in the Cathedral Choir: try going south instead.

First before going inside in the Cathedral Choir:
	unless the player can see the open secret panel:
		try going south instead;
	continue the action;

[ Rule for implicitly opening the secret panel:
	do nothing; ]

Instead of going up in the Cathedral Choir:
	say "East or west?"

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Windows

Some window panels are scenery, in the Cathedral choir. "There are [as a noun]windows[end as] here and more above in the Clerestory. What those show I can't make out from down here, but around this space are a series of panels depicting the Twelve Devotions, as visited upon St. Newton in pursuit of his great discoveries.[paragraph break]The last shows an [as a noun]object[end as] much like the one on the altar back south: beneath it is written 'In Infinituum, Momentuum Grant Motion Infinituum.'"

Understand "stained-glass", "stained glass", "stained", "windows" as the window panels.

The printed name is "windows".

Understand "windows", "devotions", "twelve", "colour", "colours", "color", "colors", "brilliant" as the window panels.

Instead of doing something when the window panels are physically involved:
	say "They're out of reach, above the Clerestory."

Section 2 - East and West Stairs

The East Stairs are an open door, not openable, scenery, east from the Cathedral Choir, down from the East Clerestory. "[if the location is the Cathedral Choir]A winding stone staircase up to the east side of the Clerestory above.[otherwise]Stone stairs leading down.[end if]"

The West Stairs are an open door, not openable, scenery, west from the Cathedral Choir, down from the West Clerestory. "[if the location is the Cathedral Choir]A winding stone staircase up to the west side of the Clerestory above.[otherwise if Doric is awake]The stairs spiral downwards.[otherwise]A spiral stone staircase leading down.[end if]"

Understand "stair", "staircase", "staircases", "spiral" as the East Stairs.
Understand "stair", "staircase", "staircases", "spiral" as the West Stairs.

Instead of climbing the East Stairs:
	try going the East Stairs;

Instead of climbing the West Stairs:
	try going the West Stairs;

After going through the east stairs from the Cathedral Choir:
	say "Two rotations to reach the top. Everything in this place is designed to be correct, as carefully set as new teeth on a dog-screw.";
	continue the action;

After going through the east stairs from the East Clerestory when BISH_DOOR_EVENT1 is fired and EAST_STAIR_EVENT1 is unfired:
	fire EAST_STAIR_EVENT1;
	continue the action;

After going to the North Clerestory from the East Clerestory when BISH_DOOR_EVENT1 is fired and EAST_STAIR_EVENT1 is unfired:
	fire EAST_STAIR_EVENT1;
	continue the action;

EAST_STAIR_EVENT1 is a trigger. 

Rule for firing unfired EAST_STAIR_EVENT1:
	if Bishop's Library is unvisited:
		say "'I'll be back,' I promise the [as a noun]guard[end as].[paragraph break]'Sure you will. With an Army, I suppose,' he laughs.";

Instead of going through the east stairs from the East Clerestory when Archbishop's Meeting has happened:
	unless Calvin's Patrol has happened:
		say "There's definitely something happening on the balcony to the northwest. I'd better investigate, hadn't I?" instead;
	otherwise:
		continue the action;

After going through the east stairs from the East Clerestory when Calvin's Patrol has not happened:
	say "I climb downstairs. The [as a noun]guard[end as] watches me closely, on the lookout for any tricks.";
	continue the action;

After going through the west stairs from the Cathedral Choir when Calvin's Patrol has not happened:
	say "Two rotations to reach the top. Everything in this place is designed to be correct, as carefully set as new teeth on a dog-screw.";
	continue the action;

After going through the west stairs from the West Clerestory when Calvin's Patrol has not happened:
	say "I climb back down.";
	continue the action;

Section 3 - Choir Stalls

Some choir stalls are a supporter, enterable, in the Cathedral Choir. "During normal services the [as a noun]choir[end as] sit in the stalls on the ground level by the east staircase. They're covered in carvings just like those on the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] doors, but lit up in brilliant colours from the [as a noun]stained glass[end as] all around."

Before inserting something into the choir stalls:
	try putting the noun on the choir stalls instead.

Understand "carving", "carvings" as the choir stalls.

The description is "The [as a noun]stalls[end as] are several levels deep and covered in [as a noun]carvings[end as], of everything from the file-makers craft to the really dull business of picking enough potatoes to feed the Watchmaker's Guild."

Instead of standing on, sitting on, hiding inside, or entering the choir stalls:
	say "I'm no chorister!";

Instead of pushing, pulling, opening or turning the choir stalls:
	say "The stalls are a massive wooden construction. Bit big for me to move, I think.";

Instead of singing in the Cathedral Choir:
	say "I doubt the monks would want to hear me. I doubt I want them to.";

Instead of touching the choir stalls:
	say "The wood is soft and old."

Instead of looking under the choir stalls:
	say "The stalls are flush with the floor."

Section 4 - Balcony above

The balcony-Clerestory is a glimpse backdrop in the Cathedral Choir. "The [as a noun]balcony[end as] is overhead, encircling this space."

Understand "balcony", "gallery", "clerestory" as the balcony-Clerestory. The printed name is "Clerestory".

Instead of approaching or entering the balcony-Clerestory:
	try going up.


Part 7 - Shrine Of The Saints

Chapter 1 - Description

The Shrine of the Saints is east from the Lower Nave, in the Cathedral Space. "A small shrine but a packed one. Where the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]'s got three [as a noun]niches[end as] for the Major Three Saints, the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] has about a hundred niches for the Minor Everybody Else. Little [as a noun]busts[end as] and [as a noun]statuettes[end as], some holding the tools of their trade, and all glaring at me for skulking around in here. The Nave is back west.[paragraph break]In one corner is a stand of [as a noun]candles[end as], for use in devotions (and for the poor to pinch for lighting)."

Instead of making to leave when in the Shrine of the Saints: try going west instead.
Instead of going inside in the Shrine of the Saints: try going west instead.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Statuettes

Some statuettes are scenery, in the Shrine of the Saints. The printed name is "statues". Understand "statue", "statues", "niche", "niches", "bust", "busts", "saint", "saints" as the statuettes.

Rule for printing the description of the statuettes:
	let x be a random miniature statuette;
	say "There's one: [The x], with his [tool of x].";

Instead of taking or pushing the statuettes:
	say "The [as a noun]statues[end as] are firmly bolted into place. The Church don't want parishioners feeling tempted. Or Clock Polishers, either.";

A miniature statuette is a kind of thing. Some miniature statuettes are defined by the table of minor saints.


