Allan			"hexagonal key"		"clutches"	"earnestly"
Ykea			"design manuals"		"seems to glare at"	"in frustration"

When play begins:
	now every miniature statuette is part of the statuettes;

Before printing the name of a miniature statuette:
	say "St ";

Understand the tool property as describing a miniature statuette.

[Carefully avoiding a disambiguation message for 'examine saint']
Understand "st/saint wainwright", "compass" as Wainwright.
Understand "st/saint maxwell" as Maxwell.
Understand "st/saint cauchy", "rod" as Cauchy.
Understand "st/saint descartes" as Descartes.
Understand "st/saint allan", "allan/hexagonal key", "allan's key", "st allan's key" as Allan.
Understand "st/saint ykea", "manuals" as Ykea.

Rule for printing the description of a miniature statuette:
	say "[The item described] [stance of the item described] his [tool of the item described] [expression of the item described].";

Instead of doing something when a miniature statuette is physically involved:
	say "These holy relics are not to be interfered with.";

Section 2 - Brass Stand

The brass stand is scenery, in the Shrine of the Saints.

The description is "The stand holds dozens of [as a noun]candles[end as] for devotions by laymen. They come in three colours: [as a noun]red[end as] for metal-workers and craftsmen, [as a noun]blue[end as] for fishermen and market-trades, and [as a noun]yellow[end as] for architects and draughtsmen. None are lit."

[Understanding performance problems. --JM]

[Understand "stand of candles" as the brass stand.

Understand "candle" as the brass stand when the player's command matches the text "stand", case insensitively.]

Understand "stand of candles", "candle stand" as the brass stand.

Instead of searching the brass stand:
	say "There are three colours of [as a noun]candle[end as] on the [as a noun]stand[end as]: [as a noun]red[end as], [as a noun]blue[end as] and [as a noun]yellow[end as]."

Instead of taking the brass stand:
	say "I'd look pretty conspicious walking around with it: it's easily as big as I am.";

Section 3 - Many different-colored candles

A single-red-candle is a candle. The wax is red.  The printed name is "red candle".
A single-blue-candle is a candle. The wax is blue.  The printed name is "blue candle".
A single-yellow-candle is a candle. The wax is yellow.  The printed name is "yellow candle".

Some red-candles are a candle dispenser, part of the brass stand. The wax is red. The representative item is the single-red-candle.
Some blue-candles are a candle dispenser, part of the brass stand. The wax is blue. The representative item is the single-blue-candle.
Some yellow-candles are a candle dispenser, part of the brass stand. The wax is yellow. The representative item is the single-yellow-candle.

[ Does the player mean examining the yellow-candles: it is likely. ]

Understand "cluster", "cluster of", "red", "candle", "candles" as the red-candles.
Understand "cluster", "cluster of", "blue", "candle", "candles" as the blue-candles.
Understand "cluster", "cluster of", "yellow", "candle", "candles" as the yellow-candles.

Understand "red candle" as the red-candles when the player can not see the single-red-candle.
Understand "blue candle" as the blue-candles when the player can not see the single-blue-candle.
Understand "yellow candle" as the yellow-candles when the player can not see the single-yellow-candle.

Rule for asking which do you mean when at least two candle dispensers are matched by the parser:
	say "There are [as a noun]red[end as], [as a noun]blue[end as] and [as a noun]yellow[end as] candles here - which do you mean?";

Instead of taking a candle dispenser:
	if the representative item of the noun is on-stage:
		say "I've already got [a representative item of the noun].";
	otherwise:
		move the representative item of the noun to the player;
		set pronouns from the representative item of the noun;
		say "I pluck [a representative item of the noun] from the [as a noun]stand[end as].";
		
Instead of pushing, pulling or turning a candle dispenser:
	say "Laying out the [as a noun]candles[end as] isn't my job. I deal with the <Italic>clocks</Italic>."

Check melting a candle on:
	say "I can't melt [the noun] on [the second noun]!" instead.

Instead of removing a candle dispenser from the brass stand:
	try taking the noun instead.

Instead of removing a candle from the brass stand:
	if the noun is carried:
		say "I've already got [the noun].";
	else:
		try taking the noun instead.

Part 8 - East Apse

Chapter 1 - Description

The East Apse is east of the Cathedral Altar, in the Cathedral Space. "More [as a noun]pews[end as], facing the [as a noun]altar[end as] back west. This is the women's corner, because they aren't allowed to sit with the men (especially women with children, who have to sit at the back). Much more interesting, is the [as a noun]iron grate[end as] to the northeast that covers – so they say – the door to the Cathedral crypt.[paragraph break][if the Orrery Hall is not visited]There's also a door to the southeast, and in the corner of my eye, I keep catching movement from somewhere inside[otherwise]The door to the southeast leads back to the Orrery Hall and strange [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as][end if]."

Instead of making to leave when in the East Apse: try going west instead.
Instead of going inside in the East Apse: try going southeast instead.

Chapter 2 - Crypt Grate

The crypt grate is a door, closed, locked, scenery, northeast of the East Apse, southwest of the Crypt Stairs. "The [as a noun]iron grate[end as] is made of metal a foot thick and it's locked with a gigantic [as a noun]padlock[end as]. I doubt I could manage to turn the lock even if I had the key for it, which I don't. Behind this is the Cathedral Vault: the one Gubbler was talking about breaking into! I guess they haven't tried yet..."

Does the player mean unlocking the crypt grate with the iron crypt key:
	it is likely.

Understand "metal", "iron", "padlock", "lock", "chain", "blackened", "black", "gate", "gigantic" as the crypt grate.

Instead of opening or pulling or attacking the crypt grate when the player does not have the old iron crypt key:
	say "The [as a noun]grate[end as] is locked with a blackened [as a noun]chain[end as] and [as a noun]padlock[end as] that's about the size of my head.";

Instead of unlocking the crypt grate with something when the second noun is not the old iron crypt key:
	say "The [as a noun]grate[end as] is locked with a blackened [as a noun]chain[end as] and [as a noun]padlock[end as] that's about the size of my head. And believe it or not, [the second noun] [is-are] not the right key.";

Before going through the crypt grate from the East Apse when Return To The Cathedral has not happened:
	if the crypt grate is examined:
		say "Did you read the bit about the [as a noun]padlock[end as]?" instead;
	else:
		try examining the crypt grate instead.

Chapter 3 - Orrery Hall Door

The hall door is a door, open, lockable, scenery, southeast of the East Apse. "A heavy [as a noun]door[end as], carved over with [as a noun]ellipses[end as] and [as a noun]stars[end as]." Understand "ellipse", "ellipses", "star", "stars" as the hall door.

Chapter 4 - Pews

The women's pews are a pew, in the East Apse. "The [as a noun]women's pews[end as] have armrests all along, partly so they're more comfortable, and partly to stop them talking to each other during the services. (So Drake says)."

Part 9 - West Apse

Chapter 1 - West Apse

The West Apse is west of the Cathedral Altar, in the Cathedral Space. "There are more [as a noun]pews[end as], though there's clearly one row missing, which Drake says is the one they took and chopped up to make the floorboards of my room. I don't believe it – why would anyone waste such a good bit of wood on me?[paragraph break]Anyway, the [as a noun]altar[end as] is back east and there's another [as a noun]archway[end as] in the southwest corner of the room. Silence emerges through it like noise from Calvin's mouth when he's eating. It must be a [as a noun]library[end as]."

Instead of making to leave when in the West Apse: try going east instead.
Instead of going inside in the West Apse: try going southwest instead.

Instead of listening when in the West Apse:
	say "Chanting from the east meets silence from the southwest."

Chapter 2 - Archway

The library archway is an open door, scenery, not openable, southwest of the West Apse. "[if in the West Apse]Through the [as a noun]archway[end as] I can see the grand [as a noun]library[end as].[otherwise]The [as a noun]archway[end as] leads back to the body of the Cathedral.[end if]". Understand "door" as the library archway.

Chapter 3 - Pews

The pews for the elderly are a pew, in the West Apse. "These [as a noun]pews[end as] are reserved for the elderly and infirm. Why they get the ones furthest from the Cathedral's doors I don't know." Understand "missing row", "row" as the pews for the elderly.

Part 10 - Calendar Shrine

Chapter 1 - Description

The Calendar Shrine is west of the Lower Nave, in the Cathedral Space. "[if we have not examined the Calendar]This [as a noun]shrine[end as] is centred around a big brass [as a noun]table[end as] with a glass [as a noun]top[end as], which doesn't seem nearly important enough for the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] of St Philip! Perhaps the important thing is the [as a noun]brazier[end as] in the corner: a shallow [as a noun]dish[end as] held over a low, flickering [as a noun]flame[end as]. The Nave is back east, should any of this prove dull.[otherwise]This shrine contains the [as a noun]Calendar Unlimited[end as], which I'm pretty sure is the thing St Philip built to get a city a named after him. It's ticking away quietly next to the brass [as a noun]brazier[end as]. The Nave is back east.[end if][if the number of things in the brazier is not zero][paragraph break]In the [as a noun]brazier[end as], bubbling slightly, [is-are a list of things in the brazier].[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when in the Calendar Shrine: try going east instead.
Instead of going inside in the Calendar Shrine: try going east instead.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Calendar

The Calendar Unlimited is scenery, in the Calendar Shrine. "[one of]The top of the [as a noun]table[end as] is [as a noun]glass[end as], beneath which is a mess of [as a noun]dials[end as] and [as a noun]pointers[end as] filling the whole surface. This is the <Italic>[as a noun]Calendar Unlimited[end as]</Italic>! The ultimate [as a noun]device[end as] that declares where in Time we are. Right now, for instance, the date is the 11th, the season is Autumn, the political climate is Restless, the next major disaster is Bakery Fire, the person currently looking is Wren – good Grease! IT CAN SEE ME![or]Somewhere on the panels of this horrible thing is marked the day I was born and the day I'll die. I hope it isn't too soon, that's all, and has nothing to do with any Figures in Grey.[stopping]"

Understand "top of the", "top of", "case", "dome", "brass", "table", "glass", "dials", "pointers", "device", "ultimate", "panels", "top", "device" as the Calendar Unlimited.

Instead of doing anything when the Calendar Unlimited is physically involved:
	say "They don't want dust getting in. They certainly don't want me getting in. So the whole case is tightly sealed.";

Instead of looking under the Calendar Unlimited:
	say "If you're hoping to see a short man curled up operating the [as a noun]dials[end as] you'd be disappointed. The whole thing is just one box (full of very, very small cogs)."

Section 2 - Parts of the Calendar

A calendar-widget is a privately-named, kind of thing. Every calendar-widget is part of the Calendar Unlimited.

Some calendar-widgets are defined by the Table of Calendar Widgets.

Rule for printing the description of a calendar-widget:
	say "The entry called [printed name] reads: '<Italic>[description-text]</Italic>'." instead.

Table of Calendar Widgets
calendar-widget			printed name		description-text		
season-widget			"Season"				"Autumn"			
date-widget				"Date"				"11th"		
disaster-widget			"Next Major Disaster"		"Bakery Fire"
person-widget			"Person Currently Looking"	"Wren"
political-widget			"Political Climate"		"Restless"
time-widget				"Time"					"Now"


Understand "season/month" as the season-widget.
Understand "date" as the date-widget.
Understand "disaster", "next", "major" as the disaster-widget.
Understand "person", "currently", "looking", "observer", "me", "wren" as the person-widget.
Understand "political", "climate" as the political-widget.
Understand "time" as the time-widget.


Instead of consulting the Calendar Unlimited about something:
	if the topic understood matches "season/month":
		try examining the season-widget instead;
	if the topic understood matches "date":
		try examining the date-widget instead;
	if the topic understood matches "disaster/next/major":
		try examining the disaster-widget instead;
	if the topic understood matches "person/currently/looking/me/wren":
		try examining the person-widget instead;
	if the topic understood matches "political/climate":
		try examining the political-widget instead;
	if the topic understood matches "time":
		try examining the time-widget instead;



Before researching when in the Calendar Shrine:
	try consulting the Calendar Unlimited about the topic understood instead.


Chapter 3 - The Brazier

Section 1 - Description

The brazier is a container, open, not openable, scenery, lit, in the Calendar Shrine. "A brass [as a noun]dish[end as] set over a little [as a noun]tripod[end as], in the centre of which a quiet [as a noun]flame[end as] burns. It's meant for incense and holy oils, that kind of thing. Doesn't seem very mechanistic to me: must be a hangover from the past, like shoelaces and Laundry."

Understand "tripod", "dish", "brass dish", "flame", "quiet flame" as the brazier.

Instead of touching the brazier:
	say "It's not <Italic>too</Italic> hot. Perhaps the fire underneath is like the spring in the Refectory Clock: delivering a tiny amount of power for a very very long time.";

Instead of melting a candle on the brazier:
	try inserting the noun into the brazier instead.

Before putting something on the brazier:
	try inserting the noun into the brazier instead.

Instead of taking or pushing or pulling or turning the brazier:
	say "It's an iron [as a noun]tripod[end as] the size of me; I can't lift it!"

Instead of extinguishing the brazier with:
	say "[one of]I think dowsing a Holy Flame is the sort of thing that makes them throw you down a well to see how <Italic>you</Italic> like it[or]I don't think that's a good idea[stopping]."

Section 2 - Wax Mixing

Before inserting something into the brazier:
	if the noun is not a candle and the noun is not a wax lump,
		say "The [as a noun]brazier[end as]'s pretty hot. I don't want to damage [the noun]." instead;

Instead of burning something when the brazier is visible and the noun is not a candle and the noun is not a wax lump:
	try inserting the noun into the brazier.

Instead of inserting something into the brazier:
	consider the wax-mixing rules for the noun;

The wax-mixing rules are an object-based rulebook.

A wax-mixing rule for a candle (called c) when the brazier does not contain a wax lump:
	let color be the wax of c;
	remove c from play;
	let r be a random wax lump in the Beehive;
	change the color of r to the color;
	change the size of r to 1;
	change the indefinite article of r to "a"; [in case we get a colossal one]
	move r to the brazier;
	say "The [as a noun]candle[end as] melts in seconds, leaving a thick pool of [as a noun][color] wax[end as] in the [as a noun]brazier[end as].";
	rule succeeds;

A wax-mixing rule for a candle (called c) when the brazier contains a wax lump:
	let r be a random wax lump in the brazier;
	let color be the wax of c;
	remove c from play;
	change the color of r to what you get when you mix color with color of r;
	increment the size of r by 1;
	if the size of r is greater than 10,
		now the indefinite article of r is "probably about half the church's wealth's worth of [as a noun]wax[end as] melted down into a";
	move r to the brazier;
	say "The [as a noun]candle[end as] melts in seconds, leaving a thick pool of [as a noun][color of r] wax[end as] in the [as a noun]brazier[end as].";
	rule succeeds;

A wax-mixing rule for a wax lump (called w) when the brazier does not contain a wax lump:
	move w to the brazier;
	say "The [as a noun]lump[end as] of [as a noun]wax[end as] re-melts in seconds.";
	rule succeeds;

A wax-mixing rule for a wax lump (called w) when the brazier contains a wax lump:
	let r be a random wax lump in the brazier;
	change the color of r to what you get when you mix color of w with color of r;
	increment the size of r by the size of w;
	if the size of r is greater than 10,
		now the indefinite article of r is "probably about half the church's wealth's worth of [as a noun]wax[end as] melted down into a";
	move w to the Beehive;
	say "The [as a noun]lump[end as] of [as a noun]wax[end as] re-melts in seconds, mixing and swirling together with what's already in the [as a noun]brazier[end as], to form a larger pool that's [color of r]-coloured.";
	rule succeeds;

Section 3 - Knife-scraping

Does the player mean scraping a molten wax lump which is in the brazier:
	it is likely.

Instead of scraping a molten wax lump when the player carries the knife:
	say "(using the [as a noun]knife[end as])[command clarification break]";
	try scraping the noun with the knife instead;

Instead of taking or scraping a molten wax lump:
	say "I try to scrape up the [as a noun]wax[end as] but the brazier's too hot for my fingers. I'll need some kind of tool to scrape the [as a noun]wax[end as] with.";

Instead of scraping a molten wax lump with something unsuitable for scraping:
	say "That's hardly a good tool for getting the [as a noun]wax[end as] off the [as a noun]brazier[end as]!";

Instead of scraping a molten wax lump with something suitable for scraping:
	let r be a random wax lump in the brazier;
	move r to the player;
	say "I scrape the [as a noun]wax[end as] up into a ball using [the second noun] and take it.";

Section 4 - Wax-squishing

After taking a wax lump:
	let c be the color of the noun;
	repeat with lump running through wax lumps carried by the player begin;
		if the lump is not the noun and the color of the lump is the color of the noun begin;
			increment the size of the noun by the size of the lump;
			move the lump to the Beehive;
			if the size of the noun is greater than 10,
				now the indefinite article of the noun is "probably about half the church's wealth's worth of [as a noun]wax[end as] melted down into a";
			say "I squidge the two lumps of [as a noun][color of the noun] wax[end as] together to make a larger [as a noun]lump[end as]." instead;
		end if;
	end repeat;
	continue the action;
	
Part 11 - Orrery Hall

Chapter 1 - Description

The Orrery Hall is southeast of the hall door, in the Cathedral Space. "[one of]Something metal clicks and whirrs past my head, like a slow metal wasp. I barely notice it in time to dodge because of all the other levers and [as a noun]rods[end as] and wires and lines. Each has a little [as a noun]ball[end as] on the end and the whole thing is called an [as a noun]Orrery[end as] - I've seen pictures of it in books, but never a real life-size one like this[or]This hall is mostly filled by a huge machine, of perfect bearing and a spooky silent grace[one of] (it reminds me of the candle tracks in the Abbey.) [or]. [stopping]It's called the '[as a noun]Orrery[end as]' and it's meant to depict the movements of the heavens, though to me it looks more like the badly-mangled bicycles they dredge from the river and sell on Saturday's for scrap, and how anyone's meant to read the whirls of [as a noun]discs[end as], [as a noun]balls[end as], wire hoops and spindles is beyond me[stopping]. The door is past a whipping meteorite to the northwest."

Instead of making to leave when in the Orrery Hall: try going northwest instead.
Instead of going inside in the Orrery Hall: try going northwest instead.

Section 2 - Event on Entry

SAATEVENT1 is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired SAATEVENT1:
	say "The [as a noun]brother[end as]'s attention twitches toward me.[paragraph break]'Ah, <Italic>good</Italic>! Someone's come along, I knew <Italic>someone</Italic> would, in the <Italic>end</Italic>.' As he speaks, he winds his hands together as if washing them under a tap. 'A <Italic>job</Italic>. I need this <Italic>map from the Society of Astronomers. <Italic>Tight-fisted</Italic> ogres, they always demand a sheet of my parchment in return for <Italic>every one</Italic> of theirs.'[paragraph break]He grins, as if he's just made a joke, and holds up a [as a noun]sheet of paper[end as] – it's a [as a noun]work order[end as] of some description, but I can't read more before he flaps it down on the table again. 'Run along with it, then.' He returns his gaze to the [as a noun]orrery[end as].";

After firing SAATEVENT1:
	move the work order to Brother-Sa'at's Desk;

Every turn when the player has been in the Orrery Hall for at least one turn and SAATEVENT1 is unfired:
	fire SAATEVENT1;

Rule for speaking with Brother Sa'at when SAATEVENT1 is unfired:
	say "I open my mouth a little: maybe five degrees. [run paragraph on]";
	fire SAATEVENT1 instead.

After looking in the Orrery Hall when the tome is uncut and we have examined the tome and the knife is off-stage:
	move the knife to the Cyclical Library;
	now the tome is cut open;
	continue the action;

Chapter 2 - Brother Sa'at

Section 1 - Description

Brother Sa'at is a monk, in the Orrery Hall. "[if sat at his desk][as a noun]Sat[end as] behind a [as a noun]desk[end as] in the corner, only just out of the way of a particularly far-off comet, is strange [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as]. He watches his machine with unblinking eyes.[otherwise if inspecting his Orrery][as a noun]Sa'at[end as] paces restlessly around the rim of the [as a noun]machine[end as], looking to fix whatever it was I did.[end if]". Understand "saat" as Brother Sa'at.

The description of Brother Sa'at is "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] is the oldest-looking [as a noun]monk[end as] I've ever seen and there's not a lot of him left to base that on. His body seems to be made of the same [as a noun]thin wires[end as] as the [as a noun]Orrery[end as] he's obsessed by. His skin is so pale I'm not sure he's ever been outside.[paragraph break]He's wearing a [as a noun]ring[end as], bearing the Cathedral's [as a noun]seal[end as], though it's too small to see what the [as a noun]seal[end as] is. As Drake once said to me, 'That ring gives him the right to demands things off monks same as I demand things off <Italic>you</Italic>.'[paragraph break][if sat at his desk][as a noun]Sa'at[end as] sits behind his [as a noun]desk[end as], regarding the [as a noun]orrery[end as] through cool eyes.[otherwise][as a noun]Sa'at[end as] paces around the [as a noun]orrery[end as], trying to work out what needs fixing.[end if]"

Brother Sa'at can be sat at his desk or inspecting his Orrery. Brother Sa'at is [initially] sat at his desk.

Brother Sa'at has a number called the letter state. The letter state of Brother Sa'at is 0.

Brother Sa'at wears a ring. Understand "sa'at's/saats/saat's/sa'ats ring", "seal", "bronze", "nugget", "small ring/nugget" as the ring. The description of the ring is "Sa'at's [as a noun]ring[end as] is a small [as a noun]bronze nugget[end as] stamped with a [as a noun]seal[end as]." 

Instead of doing something when the ring is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] snatches his hand away[one of]. 'Don't touch me,' he snaps. 'Don't even pretend to touch me!'[or].[stopping]"

Section 2 - Conversation

CT_SAAT_CREED is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[one of]'[as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as],' I ask. 'What's the [as a noun]creed[end as] of the Church?'[or]'Please, [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as]. I really need to know the [as a noun]creed[end as].'[or]'The [as a noun]creed[end as]?' I ask.[stopping]". The response text is "[one of]'[as a noun]Creed[end as]? Who has time for [as a noun]creed[end as]s? [as a noun]Creeds[end as] and passwords are the same thing, that's all a [as a noun]creed[end as] is. In the name of the [as a noun]Radish[end as], the [as a noun]Cartwheel[end as] and the [as a noun]Winding Key[end as], or something like that, it's all hopelessly out of date.' He shrugs. 'You'll probably hear them chanting about it. Back <Italic>there</Italic>.'[or]'[one of]Maybe it was[or]Or was it[or]Then again[or]How about[or]You could try[at random]... in the Name of,' [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as] begins, '[one of][as a noun]the Radish[end as][or][as a noun]the Rabbit[end as][or][as a noun]the Lever[end as][or][as a noun]the Monkey[end as][or][as a noun]the Lion[end as][or][as a noun]the Lighthouse[end as][at random], [one of][as a noun]the Cartwheel[end as][or][as a noun]the Carpet[end as][or][as a noun]the Spring[end as][or][as a noun]the Serpent[end as][or][as a noun]the Shipwreck[end as][at random] and [one of][as a noun]the Winding Key[end as][or][as a noun]the Whistling Womble[end as][or][as a noun]the Whittering William[end as][or][as a noun]the White Wallaby[end as][at random].'[stopping]"

CT_SAAT_ALTAR is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'The [as a noun]monks[end as] singing around the [as a noun]altar[end as],' I ask. 'What's that thing they've got?'" The response text is "'That thing?' he snaps. 'That thing is only the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]. And all those idiots can do with it is dogma and ritual! Checking to see it's still going, isn't that pathetic? Like they've not seen the best [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] is the one they're standing on. Where's their faith, I'd ask? Where? In a ritual? Or in a machine?' He waves an angry hand at the great Orrery, then snatches it back to start winding his fingers together."

CT_SAAT_BUSINESS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What's this [as a noun]work order[end as] for?' I ask." The response text is "'They're not about to let just any old ragamuffin barging into the Society of Astronomers,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] says, in disbelief. 'It's not a school, you know. It's a Society. Of Astronomers. They don't want just anyone barging in and saying, [']Will my crops fail?['], [']What about little Billy's hypothermia?['] You've no idea what a hassle people are. And they're everywhere! You go outside, you can't <Italic>move</Italic> for people!' He stops suddenly, and goes quiet."

CT_SAAT_CATHEDRAL is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Do you like it here?' I ask." The response text is "'Where?' he replies, quite genuinely. 'I could use a cushion, perhaps.'"

CT_SAAT_ABBOT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I think the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s going to do something dreadful,' I try to tell him." The response text is "'I'm sure he is,' he dismisses. 'They always do.'"

CT_SAAT_FIGURE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I saw this [as a noun]Figure[end as]...' I begin." The response text is "'I'm not interested in your pubescent ramblings!' he exclaims sharply. 'See that or shut up!' A sharp finger points at the [as a noun]Orrery[end as]."

CT_SAAT_ARCHBISHOP is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'How can I get to see the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as]?' I ask." The response text is "'Be more important than you are,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] snaps. 'And no, I won't take you on as an assistant. I don't need assistants. [if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 1]Did you get my [as a noun]book[end as] yet?[end if]'"

CT_SAAT_MONKS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "He's unlikely to know them, I think."

CT_SAAT_DOOR is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] doors are very impressive,' I tell him." The response text is "'Oh, yes,' he replies sarcastically. '<Italic>Very</Italic> impressive. You pull them and they open. It's hardly great mechanics, is it? Hardly the intricate engineering of the cosmos?'"

CT_SAAT_SAAT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What's your role here, [as a noun]Brother[end as]?' I ask." The response text is "He glares back at me. 'Babysitter, it would seem.'"

CT_SAAT_CLOCKWORK is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'But how does Clockwork work?' I ask." The response text is "'Time,' he declares. 'It's all predicated – that means, built on – the Universal solidity of Time. As long as there's no going back, no cutting short-cuts, all this will be quite alright.' Delicately, he pats one wheel of the [as a noun]Orrery[end as]. 'I built it, you know,' he adds."

CT_SAAT_SAINTS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What does one have to do to be a Saint?' I ask." The response text is "'Build one little trinket people think is <Italic>useful</Italic>,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] replies bitterly."

CT_SAAT_BOOKS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 0]'Do you read a lot of books?' I ask.[otherwise if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 1]'Which [as a noun]book[end as] was it again?' I ask.[otherwise]'Is it a good [as a noun]book[end as]?' I ask, thinking of [as a noun]Planetaria[end as].[end if]". The response text is "[if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 0]'No. [as a noun]Book[end as]s are for looking things up in,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] says firmly. 'Not for reading. Start-to-finish is best left for writers and writers alone.'[otherwise if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 1]'[as a noun]Principia Planetaria[end as]. [as a noun]Newton[end as]. Now get it quickly, before I calcify on the spot,' he says.[otherwise]'The good-est,' he scowls, sarcastically. 'Best thing [as a noun]Newton[end as] ever did. All this nonsense with [as a noun]penduluum[end as]s.' He shakes his head, then rounds on me. 'Now, don't listen to me, or you'll get into a lot of trouble, understand? And you'll only get out of it if you're very, very clever. I was, but I should think you're not. So just don't listen to me at all.'[end if]"

CT_SAAT_COORDINATES is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What are the co-ordinates for this [as a noun]book[end as]?' I ask.". The response text is "'Co-ordinates?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] demands, incredulously. 'For a <Italic>[as a noun]book[end as]</Italic>? Co-ordinates for a <Italic>planet</Italic>, yes. For a <Italic>moon</Italic>. For a comet anytime between now and next century, then you should be asking me, no-one else knows a thing about it. But a <Italic>book</Italic>? You don't find books in the heavens! You find books in <Italic>[as a noun]catalogue[end as]s</Italic> and <Italic>[as a noun]indices[end as]</Italic>!' He is almost red in the face, except he probably doesn't have the blood in him.".

CT_SAAT_LIBRARY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[if Cyclical Library is not visited]'How do I find the [as a noun]Library[end as]?' I ask.[otherwise]'I don't understand how to use the [as a noun]Library[end as],' I tell him.[end if]"

The response text is "[if Cyclical Library is not visited]'You found this place, didn't you?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] replies, with an impatient wave of his hand. 'Just do the same thing the other way!'[else]'Really!' he exclaims, contemptuously. 'Don't they <Italic>teach</Italic> you anything? Well!' He harrumphs. 'I'm certainly not about to, anyway!'[end if]".

CT_SAAT_orrery is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[if Brother Sa'at is inspecting his orrery]'Is the [as a noun]orrery[end as] all right?' I ask.[otherwise]'Did you build this [as a noun]orrery[end as]?' I ask.[end if]". The response text is "[if Brother Sa'at is inspecting his orrery]'I don't know what you did,' he replies menacingly. 'But when I fix it I don't expect to see it go wrong again. It's most upsetting.' He winds his hands in despair.[otherwise]'Every gear and trace,' he says proudly. 'Years ago now and I still don't fully understand it. It's a marvel. A <Italic>mystery</Italic>. I spend night after night,' – his eyes are filling with tears now – 'just contemplating it. You have to look at the right <Italic>times</Italic>, you see.' He's shaking. 'Have to get the times right otherwise it's no <Italic>good</Italic>...'[end if]"

CT_SAAT_WAX is a conversation topic. 

The enquiry text is "'Where can I get [as a noun]wax[end as] from?' I ask.".
The response text is "'Where does [as a noun]wax[end as] come from?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] repeats, seemingly absolutely astonished. 'Where do you <Italic>think</Italic> [as a noun]wax[end as] comes from, idiot? Where else <Italic>could</Italic> [as a noun]wax[end as] come from? [as a noun]Wax[end as] comes out of the back-end of <Italic>bees</Italic>, obviously!' He scratches his nose. 'Of course, it's not purple yet then. Gets turned into candles and I don't know what else.' Thinks. 'I don't know what else. Hmph. More [as a noun]bees[end as], probably. Typical.'".

CT_SAAT_HELP is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'[as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as],' I ask. 'Can you help me?'" 
The response text is "'I think you'll find <Italic>you</Italic> are helping <Italic>me</Italic>,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] replies archly. Then he steeples his fingers and adds, 'of course, what goes <Italic>around</Italic> often comes <Italic>back</Italic>, does it not? I'm sure you'll learn a lot running my errands for me.'"

CT_SAAT_WORKORDER is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What needs to be done for this [as a noun]work order[end as] to be finished?' I ask."
The response text is "'<Italic>Bureaucracy</Italic> is never <Italic>finished</Italic>. Oh, no. If it was <Italic>finished</Italic> maybe some of us could get on <Italic>without</Italic> all this looking-up-coordinates, and stamping-things-with-[as a noun]wax[end as], and all this sort of <Italic>rot</Italic>. I mean, what good is a <Italic>[as a noun]stamp[end as]</Italic>? We've got whole rooms of <Italic>machines</Italic> stamping out everything from <Italic>books</Italic> to <Italic>rats</Italic>, it's not like stamping is <Italic>difficult</Italic>. It's simply fiddly.' He pokes me in the shoulder. 'When you're bigger, you should do something <Italic>about</Italic> it, do you understand?'"

CT_SAAT_NEBULA is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What's this [as a noun]work order[end as] for?' I ask [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as]."
The response text is "'Well, it's so I can <Italic>work</Italic>,' he explains, with barely-concealed contempt. 'I know it's <Italic>hard</Italic> to believe but this [as a noun]orrery[end as] of mine isn't <Italic>complete</Italic>. How am I supposed to understand the <Italic>universe</Italic> when I don't know where everything <Italic>is</Italic>?'"

CT_SAAT_ASTRONOMERS  is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Who are the astronomers?' I ask [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as]."
The response text is "'They're idiots,' he replies, briskly. 'They spend all day staring into telescopes at things far away and never <Italic>noticing</Italic>, oh no, when someone steals their <Italic>sandwiches</Italic>. And they never see the <Italic>whole</Italic> picture.' He sighs, and looks at his [as a noun]Orrery[end as] with love.";

CT_SAAT_ME is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Do you know who I am?' I ask." The response text is "'Of course I don't,' he snaps. 'I don't have the <Italic>faintest</Italic> interest or clue. You're a <Italic>small</Italic> one.' Suddenly, a thought seems to strike him. His face pulls back in horror. 'Oh, Good <Italic>Grease</Italic>, you're no the child of someone <Italic>important</Italic>, are you, from the Council of <Italic>Scientists</Italic>?' He peers. 'Oh, <Italic>no</Italic>, of course not. Someone like that wouldn't be nearly as <Italic>dirty</Italic> as you are. Never mind, then.'";

