CT_SAAT_CREED is clustered with CT_SAAT_BUSINESS, CT_SAAT_CATHEDRAL, CT_SAAT_ABBOT, CT_SAAT_FIGURE, CT_SAAT_ARCHBISHOP, CT_SAAT_MONKS, CT_SAAT_DOOR, CT_SAAT_SAAT, CT_SAAT_CLOCKWORK, CT_SAAT_SAINTS, CT_SAAT_BOOKS, CT_SAAT_orrery, CT_SAAT_ALTAR, CT_SAAT_COORDINATES, CT_SAAT_LIBRARY, CT_SAAT_WAX, CT_SAAT_HELP, CT_SAAT_WORKORDER, CT_SAAT_NEBULA, CT_SAAT_ASTRONOMERS, CT_SAAT_ME.

Rule for firing a fired conversation topic that is clustered with CT_SAAT_CREED:
	say "I've already asked about that." instead;

Last after firing CT_SAAT_CREED:
	now CT_SAAT_CREED is unfired; [We can ask this one multiple times]

Last after firing CT_SAAT_BOOKS:
	now CT_SAAT_BOOKS is unfired; [We can ask this one multiple times]

Section 3 - Idle Actions

The Sa'atCounter is a counter. The top end is 4. The internal value is 2. [We have the 'work order' event on the first turn we enter.]

Every turn when the player can see Brother Sa'at:
	if Brother Sa'at is not inspecting his Orrery:
		increment the Sa'atCounter;

Rule for firing the Sa'atCounter:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]orrery[end as] quietly revolves.[or]Something the [as a noun]orrery[end as] does – though I can't see a thing different – makes [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] clap his hands in delight.[or][as a noun]Sa'at[end as]'s eyes dart across the wheel of his [as a noun]orrery[end as] like he was reading a book.[or]The [as a noun]gears[end as] on the orrery slowly tug at the distant [as a noun]comets[end as] over the doorway.[or][as a noun]Sa'at[end as] jots a note to himself, very quickly, almost without seeing what it says.[at random]"

Chapter 3 - Work Order

A work order is carried by Brother Sa'at. The description is "The order outlines the need for a map of a Nebula, whatever that is[if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 4]. It's official: stamped in [as a noun]purple wax[end as] with the Cathedral icon[end if]."

Understand "sheet", "paper", "of paper" as the work order.
Understand "seal", "sealed", "wax", "icon" as the work order when the work order is sealed.

Definition: the work order is sealed:
	if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 4, yes;
	no;

Definition: the work order is not sealed:
	if the work order is sealed, no;
	yes;

Before asking Brother Sa'at for the work order:
	try taking the work order instead.

Before asking Brother Sa'at for the Principia Planetaria:
	try taking the Principia Planetaria instead.

Instead of dropping the sealed work order when the Dank Alley is not visited:
	say "Better hang onto it. You never know when something with the [as a noun]seal[end as] on it might come in handy." instead;

Instead of putting the sealed work order on something when the Dank Alley is not visited:
	say "Better hang onto it. You never know when something with the [as a noun]seal[end as] on it might come in handy." instead;

Instead of inserting the sealed work order into something when the Dank Alley is not visited:
	say "Better hang onto it. You never know when something with the [as a noun]seal[end as] on it might come in handy." instead;

Instead of dropping the work order in the Orrery Hall:
	try putting the work order on the desk instead.

Chapter 4 - Desk

The Brother-Sa'at's desk is a supporter, in the Orrery Hall. The description is "The desk is covered in paper, covered in scribbles." Understand "table" as Brother-Sa'at's desk.

The printed name is "desk".

Rule for writing a paragraph about Brother-Sa'at's desk:
	say "On [as a noun]Sa'at[end as]'s [as a noun]desk[end as] is a pile of papers[if the Work Order is on Brother-Sa'at's Desk], including [a work order][end if].";

Instead of putting something on Brother-Sa'at's desk:
	try giving the noun to Brother Sa'at instead;



Chapter 5 - Papers

Brother-Sa'at's papers are on Brother-Sa'at's desk. The description is "I can't make the slightest sense of any of them."

Understand "pile", "pile of", "note", "Sa'at's papers", "Brother Sa'at's papers" as Brother-Sa'at's papers.

The printed name is "Sa'at's papers".

Instead of taking Brother-Sa'at's papers:
	say "It all looks like junk to me[if BISH_DOOR_EVENT1 is fired] It certainly wouldn't convince a member of the Swiss Watch that I was working for anyone. Anyone sane, anway[end if].";

Instead of putting something on Brother-Sa'at's papers:
	try giving the noun to Brother Sa'at instead;

Instead of inserting something into Brother-Sa'at's papers:
	try giving the noun to Brother Sa'at instead;

Chapter 6 - Orrery

The Orrery Machine is a thing, scenery in the Orrery Hall. "The [as a noun]machine[end as] is centred around a large gold ball that's meant to look like the [as a noun]sun[end as] (but I've seen the [as a noun]sun[end as], and not only does it change size it changes colour too – much redder after dinner, much like Brother Horloge's cheeks). Around that centre are wide hoops of [as a noun]thin wire[end as] which are slowly revolving, sliding and spinning over one another. Fixed to them are coloured balls – [as a noun]planets[end as] – and pressed brass [as a noun]discs[end as] bearing the symbols of the major constellations. Further off, in the very corners of the chamber, are [as a noun]comets[end as], asteroids and a few [as a noun]galaxies[end as]."


Instead of doing something when the Orrery Machine is physically involved and Brother Sa'at is inspecting his Orrery:
	say "'Get away,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] insists, and pushes me back, so hard that it hurts.";

Instead of doing something when the Orrery Machine is physically involved:
	now Brother Sa'at is inspecting his Orrery;
	Brother Sa'at fixes the problem in three turns from now;
	say "This'll distract the old bat, surely. I grab one of the wheels of the [as a noun]orrery[end as] and push it onwards, just a bit.[paragraph break][as a noun]Sa'at[end as]'s on his feet in seconds, knowing something's wrong just by the <Italic>sound</Italic>. He starts to pace around the edge of the machine, peering at it and fretting. Strangely, he doesn't tell me off. It's like he can't see me at all.";

At the time when Brother Sa'at fixes the problem:
	now Brother Sa'at is sat at his desk;
	if the player can see Brother Sa'at begin;
		say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] suddenly chortles with delight. With one hand he tweaks one of the wheels of the machine: nothing seems to change, but he's immediately satisfied and returns to his desk, beaming.";
	end if;

Section 2 - Parts of the Orrery machine

Some parts of the Orrery are privately-named, part of the Orrery Machine. The description is "[one of]All the parts of the [as a noun]Orrery[end as] are lovingly crafted.[or][as a noun]Sa'at[end as] must have spent a lifetime polishing these.[or]No detail is left undetailed.[cycling]".

Understand "space", "ball", "balls", "brass", "disc", "discs" as the parts of the Orrery.

Instead of doing something when the parts of the Orrery are physically involved and the action is physical:
	try taking the Orrery Machine instead.

An Orrery-part is a kind of thing. Every orrery-part is part of the Orrery Machine. An orrery-part is privately-named.

Instead of doing something when an orrery-part is physically involved and the action is physical:
	try taking the Orrery Machine instead.
Instead of doing something when an orrery-part is physically involved:
	say "The parts of the [as a noun]orrery[end as] are polished brass."

Some orrery-parts are defined by the Table of Orrery Components.

Table of Orrery Components
orrery-part			printed name		description
op-galaxy			"galaxies"				"The galaxies are pressed discs like coins."
op-sun				"sun"				"The sun is a solid gold ball."
op-comet			"comets"				"The Orrery has three comets, built on strange elliptical axes."
op-planet			"planets"				"Each planet is lovingly etched with land-masses, volcanoes and seas."
op-wire				"thin wires"			"The wires hold the planets and galaxies on their axes"
op-gear				"gears"				"The Orrery is run by finally wrought gears."

Understand "galaxy", "galaxies" as op-galaxy.
Understand "sun", "gold/golden ball", "large gold/golden ball" as op-sun.
Understand "comet", "comets", "meteor", "meteors", "meteorites", "whipping" as op-comet.
Understand "planet", "planets", "earth", "mars", "venus", "jupiter", "saturn", "mercury", "neptune", "pluto", "uranus", "land-masses", "land masses", "land", "volcano", "volcanoes", "sea", "seas", "coloured", "ball", "balls" as op-planet.
Understand "wire", "wires", "thin", "axis", "axes" as op-wire.
Understand "gear", "gears" as op-gear.

The op-galaxy, op-comet, op-wire, op-gear and op-planet are plural-named.


Part B - Bureaucracy puzzle (work order stamping)

Understand "clean ear" as a mistake ("[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] watches me. 'No good. Honestly. I mean, honestly.'") when in the Orrery Hall.

Understand "clean out/my/your ear" as a mistake ("[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] watches me. 'No good. Honestly. I mean, honestly.'") when in the Orrery Hall.

Instead of taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 0 and Brother Sa'at is sat at his desk:
	change the letter state of Brother Sa'at to 1;
	if BISH_DOOR_EVENT1 is fired:
		say "A [as a noun]work order[end as], I'm thinking: <Italic>perfect</Italic>. I can think of somewhere handy to wave this! But then [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as] ";
	otherwise:
		say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] ";
	say "snatches the [as a noun]order[end as] back out of my hands.[paragraph break]'Oh, <Italic>cantilever</Italic>,' he swears, coarsely, looking it over. 'I've <Italic>forgotten</Italic> the galactic axis that I want, haven't I?' He begins hunting around for something but evidently can't find it. 'You!' he declares, waving an unruly finger. 'You, go get a [as a noun]Principia[end as], will you? From the <Italic>library</Italic>. A [as a noun]Planetaria[end as], mind you now, not one of those [as a noun]Mathematica[end as]s, they're no use to me. Good! Well, <Italic>then</Italic>! <Italic>Off</Italic> you go!' He puts the [as a noun]order[end as] back down on his desk and proceeds to ignore me.";

Instead of taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 1 and Brother Sa'at is sat at his desk:
	say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] snatches it back from me. 'I told you to get my [as a noun]book[end as]!' he exclaims. 'This [as a noun]order[end as]'s no use without the co-ordinates. How would they know which map to provide? Think, child. <Italic>Think![no line break]</Italic>'[paragraph break]";

Instead of taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 2 and Brother Sa'at is sat at his desk:
	change the letter state of Brother Sa'at to 3;
	say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] snatches back the [as a noun]order[end as] to check it over. 'Coordinates – map – Crab Nebula – yes, yes, we know. As the cog said to the butcher's wife. What? Oh. Oh, <Italic>cam</Italic>.' He scowls at me. 'Seal and sign. They always say, seal and sign. I've signed, but oh, no, not good enough, is it?' He begins the process of hunting around again.[paragraph break]'Do you need another [as a noun]book[end as]?' I ask.[paragraph break]'No. Idiot,' he says. 'I need some [as a noun]wax[end as] for sealing with. Official business, has to be [as a noun]purple wax[end as], none of this empty-your-ear business that was perfectly good enough a few years ago. Oh no. The world's gone mad, I tell you.' He winds his hands together quite unexpectedly. 'Mad.'"

Instead of taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3 and Brother Sa'at is sat at his desk:
	say "'Without a seal,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] says, 'this parchment you're so busy trying to steal is worse than useless. It's a waste of my time <Italic>and</Italic> it's useless.'"

After taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is less than 4:
	say "I slip the [as a noun]work order[end as] off his desk while he's distracted with the [as a noun]orrery[end as]. 'You!' he boils. Not distracted enough. But he doesn't snatch it back. 'Don't do that!' He's too wrapped up watching his machine to actually do anything – just shakes his fist, and then gets back to work." instead;

After taking the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 4:
	say "[as a noun]Sa'at[end as] snatches it back off me, to check it over. 'Sign, seal, coordinates, Crab Nebula, <Italic>[as a noun]Henric Sa'at[end as]</Italic>, that's me, all good. Good!' He seems satisfied and puts the [as a noun]order[end as] into my hands again. 'Now run along. The <Italic>Society</Italic> is in Paris, as I'm sure you know, so you'll <Italic>have</Italic> to get the boat from Port Hauter, it's about three days in one of those <Italic>wagon-things</Italic> they let children ride. Take a <Italic>pigeon</Italic> and send me a note if... when... you make it. Best of luck!' With a poor attempt at affection, he pats me on the shoulder." instead;

Understand "henric" as Brother Sa'at.

Instead of giving the Work Order to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is not 4:
	move the work order to Brother-Sa'at's desk;
	say "I hand [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] back his [as a noun]work order[end as]. 'Good for you,' he says. 'And let that be a lesson to you <Italic>not</Italic> to steal!' He's grinning, for no reason. Then he returns his <Italic>attention</Italic> to the [as a noun]orrery[end as]." instead;

Instead of giving the Work Order to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 4:
	say "'Well, I don't want it back, it's too late for that,' he says. 'You've promised. Now get on with it. To Paris! It'll only take about a month, I don't know what you're worried about.'" instead;

Instead of giving the Principia Planetaria to Brother Sa'at when Brother-Sa'at's desk does not enclose the work order:
	say "'Well, that's very kind,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] replies testily. 'But I'd like the [as a noun]work order[end as] I spent all morning preparing first, I think. How about that? Then you can give me the [as a noun]book[end as] I want too.'" instead;

Instead of giving the Principia Planetaria to Brother Sa'at when Brother-Sa'at's desk encloses the work order:
	remove the Principia Planetaria from play;
	change the letter state of Brother Sa'at to 2;
	say "'Is this the [as a noun]book[end as]?' I ask, offering the [as a noun]Principia Planetaria[end as] to [as a noun]Sa'at[end as].[paragraph break]'Well </Italic>done</Italic>,' he replies, insincerely. 'Well done. You managed to <Italic>not</Italic> bring me the [as a noun]Principia Mechanistica[end as], [as a noun]Principia Mathematica[end as] or the [as a noun]Principia Copiomatrix[end as]. Or <Italic>any</Italic> of the other dubious volumes I'm always pulling down.' He takes the book I have brought and quickly flicks through the pages. Seems he knows exactly what he's after...[paragraph break]'There we are. <Italic>Coordinates</Italic>...' He scribbles something down onto the work order on the desk. 'Done. Coordinates to find coordinates, all to find <Italic>coordinates</Italic>,' he says with a smile. The [as a noun]book[end as] he puts away in his [as a noun]desk[end as]. 'I'll keep that,' he mutters, winding his hands around one another. 'Yes, why <Italic>not</Italic>?'" instead;

To decide if we have provided Sa'at with the book:
	if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is at least 2, yes;
	no.

Instead of putting a wax lump on the work order when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is less than 4:
	say "I've no way of stamping it, even if I could get the [as a noun]wax[end as] to stick. Might as well let the [as a noun]Brother[end as] do it for me."

Instead of putting a wax lump on the work order:
	say "I don't need to do that any more."

Instead of giving a wax lump to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is less than 3:
	say "'What are you waving that at me for?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] demands. 'And I hope you didn't steal it from my desk.'";

Instead of giving a candle to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is not 3:
	say "'What are you waving that at me for?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] demands. 'I'm not a bad smell.'";


Instead of giving a candle to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3:
	say "'What am I supposed to do with that?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] demands. 'Chew it down?'";


Definition: a wax lump is unpurple-colored:
	if the color of it is purple, no;
	yes;

Definition: a wax lump is purple-colored:
	if the color of it is purple, yes;
	no;

Instead of giving an unpurple-colored wax lump to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3:
	say "'[as a noun]Purple wax[end as]?' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] demands, looking at me like I'm an idiot. 'Did something go wrong with your eyes? Or didn't they teach you colours when they explained the use of your opposable thumb?' He snickers to himself, then returns to his work.";

Instead of giving a purple-colored wax lump to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3 and Brother-Sa'at's desk does not enclose the work order:
	say "'Will this do?' I ask, holding out the lump of [as a noun]purple wax[end as]. [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] doesn't look closely.[paragraph break]'Yes, but I rather <Italic>think</Italic> you'll need to give me back my [as a noun]work order[end as] <Italic>first</Italic>, don't you? Before I have the <Italic>Swiss Watch</Italic> down here, very angry <Italic>indeed</Italic>.'";

The wax-task been-and-returned flag is a truth state that varies.

Every turn when the location is not the Orrery Hall and the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3:
	now the wax-task been-and-returned flag is true;

Instead of giving a purple-colored wax lump to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 3 and Brother-Sa'at's desk encloses the work order:
	change the letter state of Brother Sa'at to 4;
	remove the noun from play;
	say "'Will this do?' I hold out the lump of [as a noun]purple wax[end as].[paragraph break]He looks at it curiously. 'Did you get this from the Cathedral <Italic>store cupboard</Italic>?' he asks. ";
	if wax-task been-and-returned flag is true:
		say "'You took long enough <Italic>about</Italic> it, it's hardly far away. And it's";
	otherwise:
		say "'I don't think you did. It's";
	say " a pretty disgusting <Italic>colour</Italic>, isn't it? Look at all the streaks. Never mind. I'd rather have this done <Italic>now</Italic> than in another two months, or however long it'll <Italic>take</Italic> you to find a simple cupboard.'[paragraph break]With that he produces a small metal box, into which he drops the [as a noun]wax lump[end as]. Winding the box up with a key, it starts to rattle and shake and eventually smoke. Once it's run down, the [as a noun]wax[end as] inside has melted enough for [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] to dribble a few drops onto the [as a noun]work order[end as] and then stamp it with his [as a noun]ring[end as].[paragraph break]'Good,' he announces. 'That's that. <Italic>Bureaucracy</Italic> appeased. No need to [as a noun]wax[end as] <Italic>lyrical</Italic> about it.' He rubs his hands, chortles to himself, then abruptly stops and returns to his work. I'm roundly ignored in all this, of course."

Instead of giving a wax lump to Brother Sa'at when the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 4:
	say "'Yes, very good,' [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] says with a sigh. 'It's already sealed, so you can put your nasty [as a noun]wax[end as] away.'"

Part 12 - Cyclical Library

Chapter 1 - Description

The Cyclical Library is southwest of the Library Archway, in the Cathedral Space. "[one of]The Library of the [as a noun]Cathedral of Time[end as] is about as much like the one in the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] as the mould on the floorboards of my room is like the oak tree standing outside the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] gate. [once only]This room is massive, and mechanised. Little blocks of shelving, holding about twenty books each, tile the cylindrical wall in a kind of mosaic, but they're all fixed up to winches, pulleys, and the whole room can revolve, so instead of you going to a find a [as a noun]book[end as]... well, you get the idea."

Instead of making to leave when in the Cyclical Library: try going northeast instead.
Instead of going inside in the Cyclical Library: try going northeast instead.

Section 2 - Event on Entry moved to examining shelves and things

LIBRARYEVENT1 is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired LIBRARYEVENT1:
	say "(You might be wondering where all the power for these big machines comes from. The story goes that when St Philip founded the city, he made sure to build the cathedral on a spring.)";

After examining the contraption when LIBRARYEVENT1 is unfired:
	fire LIBRARYEVENT1;

After examining the bookshelves when LIBRARYEVENT1 is unfired:
	fire LIBRARYEVENT1;

After examining the controls when LIBRARYEVENT1 is unfired:
	fire LIBRARYEVENT1;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Initiates

Some initiates are people, in the Cyclical Library. "Between where I am, and the [as a noun]monks[end as] around the [as a noun]altar[end as] back in the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], comes a long period with a shaved head but no dining rights. The boys here are studying hard because they're getting hungry."

Rule for writing a paragraph about the initiates:
	now the initiates are mentioned.

Understand "monk", "monks", "reader", "readers", "initiate", "boys", "kids", "guys" as the initiates.

Instead of doing something when the initiates are physically involved:
	say "I'd better not disturb them.";

Before asking the initiates about:
	say "I'm not meant to disturb them. They're deep in study." instead.
Before telling the initiates about:
	say "I'm not meant to disturb them. They're deep in study." instead.
Before shouting at the initiates:
	say "I'm not meant to disturb them. They're deep in study." instead.

Section 2 - Library Tables

Some library tables are a supporter, scenery, in the cyclical library. "A few long [as a noun]tables[end as] for working."

Understand "table", "desks", "desk", "long tables" as the library tables.

Instead of searching the library tables when the gigantic tome is visible:
	say "On one of the tables is a gigantic [as a noun]Tome[end as]."

Section 3 - Bookshelves

Some bookshelves are scenery, in the Cyclical Library. "The [as a noun]bookshelves[end as] form a giant's jigsaw on the wall, right the way around. They're all rigged and pulleyed so the order can be changed at will. The whole process is controlled by an oddly-small [as a noun]contraption[end as] in the centre of the room, that dangles down from the ceiling."

Understand "books", "shelf", "shelves" as the bookshelves.

Instead of doing something when the bookshelves are physically involved:
	say "Even the nearest one is far above my reach. They have to be, otherwise anytime a [as a noun]book[end as] was ordered using the [as a noun]controls[end as] in the centre of the room, someone might be crushed by a flying bookcase.";

Section 4 - Contraption

A contraption is in the Cyclical Library. "This is all controlled by a gold-panelled [as a noun]contraption[end as] that dangles from the ceiling like a chandelier they forgot to hoist. It's got three main [as a noun]controls[end as]. Occasionally, one of the [as a noun]initiates[end as] going through the studying phase of their education goes up and takes a look, but they seem nervous to touch it, for some reason."

Understand "chandelier", "machine" as the contraption.

The description is "Suspended from the centre of the ceiling the [as a noun]contraption[end as] is covered in [as a noun]controls[end as]. Somehow what you select down here on ground level gets fed into the mechanism that controls the walls. There must be wires running up inside the tubes and turning cogs overhead. "

Instead of doing something when the contraption is physically involved:
	if attacking, say "I'd get in trouble if I did that." instead;
	say "You'd need to be specific on which [as a noun]control[end as] I should use, and how.";

Instead of entering or climbing the contraption:
	say "[one of]I'd have half the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] down on me in seconds if I tried that![or]No chance. I'm not looking to make a distraction, here![cycling]";

Some controls are part of the contraption. The description is "The [as a noun]controls[end as] consist of three [as a noun]crank[end as]s (one [as the X crank]steel[end as], one [as the Y crank]brass[end as] and one [as the Z crank]gold[end as]), beneath which are three rotating cylindrical [as a noun]dials[end as] engraved with numerals and the letters [as the X meter]X[end as], [as the Y meter]Y[end as] and [as the Z meter]Z[end as], showing <Italic>[value of X control], [value of Y control], [value of Z control]</Italic>. Next to all this is an [iron chain] that runs right back up to the ceiling."

Understand "tumblers", "numbers", "values", "numerals", "cranks", "cylindrical", "dials", "meters", "main controls", "three controls" as the controls.

Instead of doing something when the controls is physically involved:
	if attacking, say "I'd get in trouble if I did that." instead;
	say "You'd need to be specific on which [as a noun]control[end as] I should use, and how.";

Instead of using or switching on the controls:
	say "If I just mess about at random with these things I'll probably turn the whole library inside out. Best to be specific. Which [as a noun]control[end as], which way?";

The X control is a Cartesian Control. The metal of the X control is "steel". The letter is "X". The coordinate is 5.7.
The Y control is a Cartesian Control. The metal of the Y control is "brass". The letter is "Y". The coordinate is 1.1.
The Z control is a Cartesian Control. The metal of the Z control is "gold". The letter is "Z". The coordinate is 6.8. 

The X meter is a privately-named Cartesian Meter, part of the controls. The model of the X meter is the X control. 
The Y meter is a privately-named Cartesian Meter, part of the controls. The model of the Y meter is the Y control.
The Z meter is a privately-named Cartesian Meter, part of the controls. The model of the Z meter is the Z control.

Understand "steel meter", "x meter", "meter" as the X meter.
Understand "brass meter", "y meter", "meter" as the Y meter.
Understand "gold meter", "z meter", "meter" as the Z meter.

Understand "cylindrical", "control", "dial" as a Cartesian Meter.

The X crank is a privately-named Cartesian Crank, part of the controls. The model of the X crank is the X control. The meter of the X crank is the X meter. The printed name is "steel crank".

The Y crank is a privately-named Cartesian Crank, part of the controls. The model of the Y crank is the Y control. The meter of the Y crank is the Y meter. The printed name is "brass crank". 

The Z crank is a privately-named Cartesian Crank, part of the controls. The model of the Z crank is the Z control. The meter of the Z crank is the Z meter. The printed name is "gold crank".

Understand "x crank", "steel crank", "steel", "x", "crank" as the X crank.
Understand "y crank", "brass crank", "brass", "y", "crank" as the Y crank. 
Understand "z crank", "gold crank", "gold", "z", "crank" as the Z crank.

Instead of turning a Cartesian Crank (called the handle):
[we rotate as many times as specified by the player, or by a random amount. We tell the player which we're doing, and the random one will never go past a correct answer! ]
	let the turn-count be the number of turns to make;
	if the turn-count is 0, let the turn-count be a random number between 1 and 4;
	let n be the coordinate of the model of the handle;
	repeat with turn-number running from 1 to the turn-count:
		if the units part of n is 9:
			decrease n by 0.9;
		otherwise:
			increase n by 0.1;
		if the number of turns to make is 0: [ ie. player didn't specify] 
			if units part of n is 3 or the units part of n is 2 or the units part of n is 5:
				let the turn-count be the turn-number;
				break;
	change the coordinate of the model of the handle to n;
	say "I turn [the handle] clockwise [turn-count times][one of], and the second tumbler of [the meter of the handle] slowly winds on, so it now[or]. [The meter of the handle][stopping] reads [tens part of n][units part of n].";

Instead of turning a Cartesian Crank (called the handle) backwards:
[we rotate as many times as specified by the player, or by a random amount. We tell the player which we're doing, and the random one will never go past the tens column of a correct answer! ]
	let the turn-count be the number of turns to make;
	if the turn-count is 0, let the turn-count be a random number between 1 and 4;
	let n be the coordinate of the model of the handle;
	repeat with turn-number running from 1 to the turn-count:
		if the tens part of n is 9:
			decrease n by 9.0;
		otherwise:
			increase n by 1.0;
		if the number of turns to make is 0: [ ie. player didn't specify] 
			if the tens part of n is 4 or the tens part of n is 1 or the tens part of n is 9:
				let the turn-count be the turn-number;
				break;
	change the coordinate of the model of the handle to n;
	say "I turn [the handle] anticlockwise [turn-count times][one of], and the first tumbler of [the meter of the handle] slowly winds on, so it[or]. [The meter of the handle][stopping] now reads [tens part of n][units part of n].";

Instead of number-setting a Cartesian Crank to:
	try number-setting the meter of the noun to the number understood instead

Instead of number-setting a Cartesian Meter to:
	say "I can't turn the numbers directly, but I could turn the [as a noun]crank[end as]s clockwise or anticlockwise.";

Section 4b - Plural objects for cranks and dials

Some group-cranks are privately-named, part of the controls. The printed name is "cranks". Understand "cranks" as the group-cranks. 

Before doing something with the group-cranks:
	if examining, say "Three [as a noun]cranks[end as] - [as the X crank]steel[end as], [as the Y crank]brass[end as] and [as the Z crank]gold[end as]. They're attached by piston-arms to the [as the X meter]X[end as], [as the Y meter]Y[end as] and [as the Z meter]Z[end as] [as a noun]meters[end as] in that order." instead;
	say "You'll have to pick a [as a noun]crank[end as]: [as the X crank]steel[end as], [as the Y crank]brass[end as], or [as the Z crank]gold[end as]?" instead.

Some group-meters are privately-named, part of the controls. The printed name is "meters". Understand "meters", "dials" as the group-meters. 

Before doing something with the group-meters:
	if examining, say "The dials read [value of X control] (attached to the [as a noun]steel crank[end as]), [value of Y control] (attached to the [as a noun]brass crank[end as]) and [value of Z control] (attached to the [as a noun]gold crank[end as], which I can't help noticing is a little more securely attached)." instead;
	say "You'll have to pick a dial: [as the X meter]X[end as], [as the Y meter]Y[end as] or [as the Z meter]Z[end as]?" instead.

Section 5 - Book Pulling

The iron chain is scenery, in the Cyclical Library. "A long [as a noun]iron chain[end as] that leads up to the [as a noun]contraption[end as] on the ceiling. I shouldn't say this, but it looks a lot like the toilet flush that Brother Armitage has been designing." 

Understand "links" as the iron chain.

Instead of pulling the chain:
	say "I pull hard on the [as a noun]chain[end as][one of] and very slowly, the mechanism overhead comes to life. The walls themselves start to move! Sections of [as a noun]shelf[end as] seem to float upwards, creating gaps that others sidle into. It's like a basket of crabs kicked over in a market; all the [as a noun]books[end as] scuttling this way and that. Noisy, though. [as a noun]Shelves[end as] collide with thumps and groans and scrapes. The [as a noun]initiates[end as] look very annoyed indeed.[paragraph break][or]. [as a noun]Shelves[end as] move and glide around the walls of the library with colossal noise! [stopping]Eventually, everything comes to a halt, and a metal plate extends down from a position above my head with a single [as a noun]book[end as] in place.";
	abide by the book-pulling rules;

The book-pulling rules are a rulebook.

To decide which indexed text is the library code:
	let x be the coordinate of the X control;
	let y be the coordinate of the Y control;
	let z be the coordinate of the Z control;
	decide on "[the tens part of x][the units part of x][the tens part of y][the units part of y][the tens part of z][the units part of z]";

A book-pulling rule:
	if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is not 1 begin;
		say "The [as a noun]book[end as] is nothing I want though, so I release the [as a noun]chain[end as] and it disappears back into the stacks." instead;
	end if;

A book-pulling rule when the library code is "431295":
	if the Principia Planetaria has been handled:
		say "The [as a noun]book[end as] turns out to be a dummy! Just a block of wood with 'BOOK ON LOAN' painted on the cover. It won't come free; and disappears into the [as a noun]shelf[end as] once more." instead;
	otherwise:
		now the player carries the Principia Planetaria;
		say "It's the [as a noun]Principia[end as]! I snatch it down and the plate slides back into the [as a noun]shelf[end as]." instead;

A book-pulling rule when the library code is "930428" or the library code is "951024":
	[Easter egg based on the serial numbers for releases of Curses!.]
	say "Sadly, it's not the [as a noun]Principia[end as]. I release the chain and the [as a noun]book[end as] disappears into the stacks. Curses!" instead;

A book-pulling rule:
	say "Sadly, it's not the [as a noun]Principia[end as]. I release the chain and the book disappears into the stacks." instead;

Section 5 - Card Catalogue

A card catalogue is in the Cyclical Library. "By the [as a noun][as a noun]door[end as] to the northeast is a  card catalogue[end as]. That'll be gone as soon as they work out how to do something more useful with one of Reloh's keyboards than just type letters onto paper."

Understand "index", "indices", "cards", "catalog", "box of" as the card catalogue.

The description is "It's a box of [as a noun]index cards[end as], filed alphabetically. Easy to use, unlike the [as a noun]contraption[end as] that controls the [as a noun]shelves[end as]! I just need to look up whatever title I'm after."

Instead of opening the card catalogue:
	say "I could look something up in the [as a noun]catalogue[end as], if I wanted to."

Before researching when in the Cyclical Library:
	try consulting the card catalogue about the topic understood instead.

Instead of consulting the card catalogue about:
	if the topic understood is a topic listed in the table of index cards begin;
		say "[response entry][paragraph break]";
	otherwise;
		say "I don't find that in the [as a noun]catalogue[end as]. It might be listed under a different name or it might not be here at all.";
	end if;

Table of index cards
topic		response
"principia"	"I find one, then another, then a third [as a noun]Principia[end as]... seems to be a popular name.[if the letter state of Brother Sa'at is 1] Which one was [as a noun]Sa'at[end as] after?[end if]"
"planetaria" or "principia planetaria/planeteria" or "saats/sa'ats/saat's/sa'at's book/volume/request/principia" or "astronomy" or "stars" or "planets" or "planetarium" or "planeteria"	"There are a lot of cards labelled '[as a noun]Principia[end as]', but after a while I pick out [as a noun]Newton[end as]'s <Italic>[as a noun]Planetaria[end as]</Italic>. The card reads: '['][as a noun]Principia Planetaria[end as], the apocryphal astronomical writings of St. Isaac [as a noun]Newton[end as].['] Cartesian Call Number (43, 12, 95).' I put it back in the [as a noun]catalogue[end as]."
"newton"	"[one of]Nothing: all the cards are indexed by work name, not author. But can I remember the work name? It was the [as a noun]Participant[end as], or the [as a noun]Particular[end as], or something like that.[or]Still nothing. Looks like they haven't improved the system yet, then.[stopping]"

Section 6 - Tome

The gigantic tome is in the Cyclical Library. "On one of the [as a noun]desk[end as]s, without a reader, sits a gigantic [as a noun]Tome[end as]."

The description is "[if uncut]A large volume, fresh from the Gutenberg in the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]. I can tell it's new by the freshness of the leather, and smell of burnt ink and the way some of the [as a noun]pages[end as] are still uncut – each [as a noun]leaf[end as] is printed double and folded over before being stitched in, so the [as a noun]book[end as] needs to be sliced open. Whichever monk was reading it probably went off to find a [as a noun]knife[end as].[otherwise]The book is very new and not very evenly cut.[end if]"

Understand "book", "page", "pages", "leaf", "leaves" as the gigantic tome.
Understand "uncut" as the gigantic tome when the tome is uncut.

The tome can be cut open or uncut. The tome is uncut.

Instead of taking the tome:
	say "The book's out for one of the [as a noun]initiates[end as]. I've no good reason to steal it from him.";

Instead of opening the tome:
	try reading the tome;

Instead of reading the cut open tome when the knife is on-stage:
	say "‘On the pattern of flora in the water of shallow ponds. Many investigations have been made into this fascinating subject using the latest close-viewing Opticks and the researches of Maxwell and Newton into the properties of lens-glass. However...'[paragraph break]This doesn't read like a church [as a noun]book[end as] at all.";

Instead of reading the uncut tome when the knife is on-stage:
	say "The [as a noun]book[end as] isn't readable yet because the pages haven't been cut open.";

Instead of reading the tome when the knife is off-stage:
	move the knife to the Cyclical Library;
	say "A [as a noun]knife[end as] falls out from inside the [as a noun]book[end as]. Presumably the absent-minded monk had been using it as a bookmark <Italic>before</Italic> going off to find a paper [as a noun]knife[end as].";

Instead of slicing the uncut tome with the knife:
	now the tome is cut open;
	say "I slit open the [as a noun]pages[end as] of the [as a noun]Tome[end as] with the [as a noun]knife[end as]. What a helpful child I am!";

Section 7 - Knife

A knife is a thing. The description is "A short steel [as a noun]letter-opener[end as], sharp enough to slice through [as a noun]paper[end as] but probably not much good for fending off Calvin."

Understand "short", "sharp", "knife" as the knife.
Understand "letter-opener", "letter opener" as the knife when the knife is not a garden-knife.
Understand "gardener's", "gardeners" as the knife when the knife is a garden-knife.

After taking the knife:
	say "I pick up the [as a noun]knife[end as]." instead;

Before dropping the knife:
	say "A [as a noun]knife[end as] like this might come in useful. If I run into any [as a noun]Figure[end as]s... or any Drakes...[paragraph break]" instead.

Before putting the knife on something:
	try dropping the knife instead.

Before inserting the knife into something (this is the can't insert knife rule):
	try dropping the knife instead.

Does the player mean slicing with the knife:
	it is likely.

The knife can be a garden-knife. The knife is not a garden-knife.

Rule for printing the description of the garden-knife knife:
	say "A short gardener's [as a noun]knife[end as], sharp enough for slicing bramble-stems but probably not much good for fending off Calvin."

Section 6b - The Tome disappears and the Knife is left behind

[ If the player fails to find the Knife and has finished with the Planteria puzzle so he won't go back, we drop the knife in his view so he can continue the game ! ]

MONK_DROPS_KNIFE is a trigger.

Every turn when the location is the Upper Nave and the knife is off-stage and the knife is not handled and we have provided Sa'at with the book [so we aren't going back to the Library and we don't have the knife ]: 
	fire MONK_DROPS_KNIFE;

Rule for firing unfired MONK_DROPS_KNIFE:
	move the knife to the location;
	remove the gigantic tome from play;
	move backdrop-initiate to the Upper Nave;
	say "A [as a noun]hassled initiate[end as] dashes through the Nave, knocking me to one side with the corner of the gigantic Tome he's carrying in both arms. The collision makes [as a noun]something[end as] clatter to the floor, but he doesn't see it - and then he's gone."

Understand "clatter", "something", "thing" as the knife when MONK_DROPS_KNIFE is fired and the knife is not handled.

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-floor when in the Upper Nave and MONK_DROPS_KNIFE is fired and the knife is not handled:
	say "On the floor is a long paper [as a noun]knife[end as]."

The backdrop-initiate is a man, scenery, privately-named. Understand "tome", "gigantic", "initiate", "monk", "hassled" as the backdrop-initiate. The printed name is "hassled initiate". Before doing something with the backdrop-initiate, say "The [as a noun]initiate[end as] is long gone." instead.

Section 8 - The Principia Planetaria

The Principia Planetaria is a thing. The indefinite article is "The". The description is "It's a slim volume – surprisingly. The title is '[as a noun]Principia Planetaria[end as]: The Apocryphal Astronomical Writings of St. Isaac [as a noun]Newton[end as].'"

Understand "book", "tome", "tables", "co-ordinates", "apocrypha", "diagrams", "planeteria" as the Principia Planetaria.

Instead of reading the Principia Planetaria:
	say "Why anyone would want to read this is beyond me. After a hundred pages of numerical tables listing the table-co-ordinates of co-ordinates of stars, there follows a hundred pages of diagrams of the night skies and everything beyond, all joined up by watch escapements, rods, springs, levers and releases. <Italic>[as a noun]Newton[end as]'s Clock Universalis</Italic>, it's called. It's like someone sketched the Milky Way and their child doodled all over it. (It's an <Italic>Apocrypha</Italic>. I'm pretty sure that means it's rubbish).";

Part 13 - West Clerestory

The West Clerestory is a room, in the Cathedral Space. "[if Archbishop's Meeting has not happened]This [as a noun]balcony[end as] has a good view down over the [as a noun]choir stalls[end as] and the [as a noun]altar[end as] where the [as a noun]monks[end as] are still clustered. The sun has just come round and I'm standing, bathed in colourful light from the enormous [as a noun]stained glass window[end as]. Spiral [as a noun]stairs[end as] lead down, and the [as a noun]balcony[end as] curves around to the northeast.[otherwise]This is where the [as a noun]Figure[end as] went – and it's not hard to see where he went next either. Part of the west wall has disappeared to reveal a [as a noun]ladder[end as] built into a space in the [as a noun]brickwork[end as]. No problem, though. I'm <Italic>good</Italic> with [as a noun]ladder[end as]s.[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when in the West Clerestory: try going down instead.
Instead of going inside in the West Clerestory: try going down instead.

Instead of facing west in the west clerestory when the player can see the secret construction ladder:
	try examining the secret construction ladder instead;

Chapter 1 - Window of the Lever

The window of the lever is a stained glass window, scenery, in the West Clerestory. "This pane of [as a noun]glass[end as] depicts an enormous [as a noun]lever[end as], being operated from very far off by a [as a noun]man[end as] wearing a [as a noun]bed-sheet[end as]. (Or that's what it looks like to me: he's probably meant to be an [as a noun]angel[end as])." Understand "big", "beautiful", "windows", "enormous", "gigantic", "man", "wearing", "wearing a", "wearing the", "bed-sheet", "bed sheet", "bedsheet", "sheet", "angel" as the window of the lever.

Chapter 2 - Ladder

[Begins life out of play]

A secret construction ladder is scenery. "There's a [as a noun]flue[end as] hidden in the wall, with this old construction [as a noun]ladder[end as] leading away upwards. One of the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s six secret doors!"

Understand "old", "flue", "bricks", "brickwork", "gap", "space" as the secret ladder.

Instead of going up in the presence of the secret ladder:
	try climbing the secret ladder;

Instead of going west in the presence of the secret ladder:
	try climbing the secret ladder;

Instead of going inside in the presence of the secret ladder:
	try climbing the secret ladder;

Instead of climbing or entering the secret ladder:
	say "I scurry up the rungs of the [as a noun]ladder[end as]. A few squeak and bend but none of them break.[paragraph break]And then I'm out on a narrow ledge, high up near the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]'s roof! Mustn't look down!";
	move the player to Among the Gargoyles;

Instead of closing the secret ladder:
	say "I can't see any mechanism for it (the [as a noun]Figure[end as] must have been in quite a hurry not to do it himself. So I've scared him, then!)";

Instead of taking the secret ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] is carved into the wall[if the caretaker's ladder is handled]. There's no wandering off with this one[end if]."

After looking in the West Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has ended:
	move the escaping Figure backdrop to all rooms that are the West Clerestory;

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in West Clerestory:
	try climbing the secret ladder.
	
Chapter 3 - Balcony

The Clerestory-balcony is a privately-named backdrop. It is in the West Clerestory, East Clerestory and North Clerestory. The printed name of the Clerestory-balcony is "balcony". Understand "balcony", "rail", "railing" as the Clerestory-balcony. 

The description of the Clerestory-balcony is "The [as a noun]balcony[end as] curves right around the Clerestory."

Instead of climbing or jumping on or jumping off or jumping over or entering the Clerestory-balcony: say "No thanks. I'd rather take the stairs."

Instead of jumping when the Clerestory-balcony is visible:
	try jumping over the Clerestory-balcony.

Part 14 - North Clerestory

Chapter 1 - Description

The North Clerestory is a room, northeast of the West Clerestory, northwest of the East Clerestory, in the Cathedral Space. "[if Archbishop's Meeting has not happened]To the south the whole cathedral stretches away, all [as a noun]monks[end as] and [as a noun]pews[end as] and the three [as a noun]Great Seals[end as] on the floor. The [as a noun]balcony[end as] - which is the only thing to stop me breaking my neck - curves from southwest to southeast.[otherwise]The north end of the [as a noun]cathedral[end as]. There's the big [as a noun]stained window[end as] overhead and also the enormous [as a noun]statues[end as] of [as a noun]Babbage[end as] and [as a noun]Breguet[end as]. The [as a noun]balcony[end as] continues to the southwest.[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when in the North Clerestory: try going southwest instead.
Instead of going inside in the North Clerestory: try going southeast instead.

Section 2 - Glimpses of Great Seals

The great-seals is a glimpse backdrop in the North Clerestory. "The [as a noun]three seals[end as] run up the length of the Church: I can see the nearest, depicting the winding key, the others are less clear from here."

Understand "three seals", "seals", "great seals", "three great seals", "seal", "first seal", "second seal", "third seal", "first great seal", "second great seal", "third great seal" as great-seals. 

The printed name of great-seals are "three Great Seals".

Instead of approaching the great-seals:
	try going southeast; 

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Statues

A thing called the statues of St Breguet and St Babbage is plural-named, in the North Clerestory. 
"In one corner stand two enormous [as a noun]statues[end as] of [as a noun]St Breguet[end as] and [as a noun]St Babbage[end as]. They're huddled together like conspirators and [as a noun]Babbage[end as]'s enormous head gets in the way of the beautiful [as a noun]stained glass[end as] behind him."

The description is "[if Archbishop's Meeting has not happened]The [as a noun]statues[end as] are twice life-size (probably exactly), and carved down to the tiniest detail. [as a noun]Breguet[end as] has fluff on his jacket and [as a noun]Babbage[end as]'s wig seems to have a few [as a noun]lice[end as]. Their enormous faces are turned toward each other, and down, as though they were muttering something about me. In between their feet is [a shadowy gap].[otherwise]Between the [as a noun]statues[end as] is a pool of [as the shadowy gap]deep shadow[end as].[end if]"

The printed name is "statues of St. Breguet and St. Babbage"

Instead of taking the statues of St Breguet:
	say "[if Archbishop's Meeting has not happened]Sure. And when I've done that I'll go play football with the Moon, shall I?[otherwise]They're huge.[end if]";

Section 2 - Shadowy Gap

The shadowy gap is part of the statues of St Breguet. Understand "shadows", "shadow" as the shadowy gap. The description is "Between the toes of [as a noun]St Breguet[end as] and [as a noun]St Babbage[end as] is a patch of [as a noun]deep shadow[end as], perfect for hiding in!"

Understand "fluff", "lice" as the shadowy gap.

Instead of hiding from view when in the North Clerestory:
	try hiding inside the shadowy gap instead.

Instead of entering the shadowy gap:
	try hiding inside the shadowy gap instead;

Instead of hiding inside the shadowy gap when the number of entries in the current script is not 0 and entry 1 of the current script is CHASE1:
	now Doric is fooled;
	say "I slip into the [as a noun]shadowy gap[end as] between the [as a noun]statues[end as]['] legs.";

Instead of hiding inside the shadowy gap when Doric is not in the East Clerestory: [ was current script not {} but this doesn't seem to fire. ]
	say "[as a noun]Doric[end as]'s already passed me by. No need to waste time!";

Instead of hiding inside the shadowy gap:
	say "That'd make a great hiding place, except that nobody's following me.";
	
Instead of searching the shadowy gap:
	say "There's nothing but darkness in there."


Section 3 - Windows

The window of the steel snake is a stained glass window, scenery, in the North Clerestory. "This [as a noun]window[end as] depicts a gigantic coiled [as a noun]snake[end as] made of steel."

The printed name of the window of the steel snake is "window".

Understand "big", "beautiful", "coiled", "spring", "enormous", "gigantic", "windows" as the window of the steel snake.

Section 4 - Distant Doric

DistantDoric is a man, in the North Clerestory. "Along the [as a noun]balcony[end as] to the southeast I can see a [as a noun]guard[end as], standing duty." The description is "A [as a noun]Protectorate[end as], of the Archbishop's personal guard. He'll be tough, strong <Italic>and</Italic> stupid." The printed name is "Doric".

Instead of approaching DistantDoric when in the North Clerestory:
	try going southeast;

Understand "guard", "man", "Doric", "protectorate", "watchman", "uniform" as DistantDoric.

Instead of asking DistantDoric about something when in the North Clerestory:
	try shouting at DistantDoric instead;

Instead of shouting at DistantDoric when in the North Clerestory:
	start the chase;
	say "'Hey, [as a noun]Watchman[end as]!' I yell. 'How come a man as fat as you isn't on marches?'[paragraph break]'Why, you...' he growls. Quick: he's coming this way!";

Instead of throwing something at DistantDoric when in the North Clerestory:
	say "[The noun] wouldn't make a great missile. There might be something better around here somewhere - for what good making a member of the Swiss Watch angry will do."

Instead of throwing a wax lump at DistantDoric when in the North Clerestory:
	start the chase;
	move the noun to the East Clerestory;
	say "I lob [the noun] squarely at the side of the [as a noun]guard[end as]'s helmet. He turns around, startled. And spots me.[paragraph break]'Why, you...' he growls. Quick, Wren: he's coming this way!"

Instead of attacking DistantDoric:
	say "He's all the way along the balcony - but I could probably throw something at him, if I dared..."

Before attacking DistantDoric with something:
	try throwing the second noun at DistantDoric instead.


Section 5 - Chase Event

Doric is a man. Doric can be fooled or unfooled. Doric is unfooled.

CHASE0 is a scripted event.
CHASE1 is a scripted event.
CHASE2 is a scripted event.
CHASE3 is a scripted event.
CHASE4 is a scripted event.

CHASE0 is clustered with CHASE1, CHASE2, CHASE3, and CHASE4.	

To start the chase:
	now Doric is not fooled;
	change the current script to {CHASE0, CHASE1, CHASE2, CHASE3, CHASE4};

Rule for firing CHASE1:
	if the player is not in the North Clerestory:
		say "[as a noun]Doric[end as] races along the balcony to the North Clerestory.";
	move Doric to the North Clerestory;

Rule for firing CHASE1 when Doric is fooled:
	now Doric is not fooled;
	remove Doric from play;
	remove CHASE2 from the current script, if present;
	say "[as a noun]Doric[end as] races along the balcony and straight past my hiding place, and over to the west side! Perfect!";

Rule for firing CHASE2:
	move Doric to the West Clerestory;
	if the player is not in the West Clerestory:
		say "The [as a noun]guard[end as] races around the balcony to the west.";

Rule for firing CHASE3:
	if the player is not in the Cathedral Choir:
		say "I can see [as a noun]Doric[end as] hurrying past the [as a noun]choir stalls[end as] and over to the east staircase. Headed back to his post - he's a smart one.";
	move Doric to the Cathedral Choir;

Rule for firing CHASE4:
	move Doric to the East Clerestory;

First after firing a scripted event that is clustered with CHASE1:
	if the player can see Doric begin;
		move Doric to the East Clerestory;
		clear the current script;
		say "[as a noun]Doric[end as] catches up with me, puffing and panting a little. [one of]'Why, in the name of the Lever and...' - then he stops himself short and[or]He[stopping] boxes my ears very skilfully, demanding I apologise. I apologise. [paragraph break]He grunts and returns to his door[one of], muttering, 'almost gave t'away there, [as a noun]Doric[end as], almost gave t'away.'[or].[stopping]";
	end if;

Instead of going to the East Clerestory from the North Clerestory when the current script is  {CHASE1, CHASE2, CHASE3, CHASE4}:
	run straight into Doric;

Instead of going to the North Clerestory from the West Clerestory when the current script is  {CHASE2, CHASE3, CHASE4}:
	run straight into Doric;

Instead of going to the West Clerestory from the Cathedral Choir when the current script is  {CHASE3, CHASE4}:
	run straight into Doric;

Instead of going to the Cathedral Choir when the current script is {CHASE4}:
	run straight into Doric;



To run straight into Doric:
	move Doric to the East Clerestory;
	clear the current script;
	say "I run straight into [as a noun]Doric[end as], who catches me and duffs me a up a little. 'In the name of Lever,' he exclaims, panting. 'You little swine.' Then he turns on a heel and marches back to his post.";

Chapter 3 - Event on Entry - Overheard Conversation

Section 1 - Beginning

Overheard Conversation is a scene. Overheard Conversation begins when the location is the North Clerestory and Archbishop's Meeting has happened. Overheard Conversation ends when SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT3 is fired.

After looking in the North Clerestory when Archbishop's Meeting has happened and SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2 is unfired:
	change the current script to { SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2, SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2B , SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT3 };

Section 2 - Scripted events

SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2 is a scripted event. The display text is "[as a noun]Gubbler[end as] and the [as a noun]Figure[end as], in the [as a noun]shadows[end as] between the [as a noun]statues[end as]! I duck behind [as a noun]Babbage[end as]'s coat-tails to listen.[paragraph break]'I'm not, erm, not happy, no,' [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] is saying. 'This isn't right.'[paragraph break]'It's vital. Give me the [as a noun]key[end as],' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] replies. The voice is a quiet murmur, like the sound of a mead barrel rolling over flagstones. 'You've got your money.'[paragraph break]'Here.' [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] sounds reluctant. 'It's an exact copy of, erm, the one, in the desk. Yes. And there's a [as a noun]guard[end as] too, but I'll well – I'll – well. It doesn't do to say.'";

SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2B is a scripted event. The display text is "'And the final lock?' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] demands.[paragraph break]'That's your problem,' [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] says, sounding almost like an [as a noun]Abbot[end as] for once. 'Nothing to do with me, no, oh no. Look: do you even know your way around this [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]?'[paragraph break]'Intimately,' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] replies smoothly. 'All six secret doors.'[paragraph break]'Six?' [as a noun]Gubbler[end as] demands. His voice is sweating. 'The only one I know is the one from the Choir to the Bishop's Library! Where there's a <Italic>drill</Italic> and all... Look. I'm going to, erm, go. Good luck. I hope you get caught.'[paragraph break]With that, the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] shuffles away southeast."

After firing SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2:
	move the Figure in Grey to the North Clerestory;
	move the Abbot-in-meeting to the North Clerestory;

After firing SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT2B:
	remove the Abbot-in-meeting from play;
	move the glimpsed-Abbot backdrop to all rooms that are the North Clerestory;

SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT3 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] glides out of the shadows – right over to where I'm standing. 'I see you, little one,' he says softly. The face is nothing but darkness below his hood. His tight leather costume makes him look like a snake. 'You should stop turning up where you aren't wanted,' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] advises menacingly. 'You're playing with fire. You are warned.'[paragraph break]Then, in a flash of silver like a fish slipping the bait, the [as a noun]Figure[end as] wheels and darts away southwest."

After firing SE_NC_FIGUREEVENT3:
	remove the Figure in Grey from play;
	move the escaping Figure backdrop to all rooms that are the North Clerestory;

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in the North Clerestory:
	try going southwest instead;

Instead of approaching the glimpsed-Abbot when in the North Clerestory:
	try going southeast instead;

Section 3 - The Figure In Grey, Again

The Figure in Grey is a man, scenery. "I take a peek, but can't make out much beyond the smooth grey cloak." The figure in grey wears a smooth grey cloak.

Instead of doing something when the Figure in Grey is physically involved during Overheard Conversation:
	do nothing;

Instead of searching or approaching or entering or examining or hiding behind the statues of St Breguet and St Babbage when the player can see the Figure in Grey:
	say "I stay pressed into the [as a noun]shadows[end as]...";

Instead of searching or approaching or entering or examining or hiding inside the shadowy gap when the player can see the Figure in Grey:
	say "I stay pressed into the [as a noun]shadows[end as]...";

Instead of approaching the Figure in Grey:
	say "I stay pressed into the [as a noun]shadows[end as]...";

Section 3b - The Abbot, in meeting

Abbot-in-meeting is a privately-named, man. Understand "abbot/abbott", "gubbler", "man" as Abbot-in-meeting. The printed name is "Abbot Gubbler". The description is "I can't see him clearly, but I know that voice anywhere. He sounds even more afraid than last time.";

[Understand "hold breath" as listening when in the presence of the Abbot-in-meeting.
Understand "hold my/your breath" as listening when in the presence of the Abbot-in-meeting.]

Instead of listening in the presence of the Abbot-in-meeting:
	say "I hold my breath tight...";

Instead of doing something when the Abbot-in-meeting is physically involved:
	do nothing;

Section 4 - Constrained Exits

Instead of going southeast from the North Clerestory when the player can see the Figure in Grey:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] might see me!";

Instead of going southeast from the North Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has happened and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "Look - no-one else is going to stop this. So I better had. I need to get after the [as a noun]Figure[end as], and he went the other way!";
Instead of going down from the West Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has happened and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "Look - no-one else is going to stop this. So I better had. I need to get after the [as a noun]Figure[end as], and he went the other way!";
Instead of going the West Stairs in the West Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has happened and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "Look - no-one else is going to stop this. So I better had. I need to get after the [as a noun]Figure[end as], and he went the other way!";
Instead of going northwest from the West Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has happened and Return to the Cathedral is not happening:
	say "Look - no-one else is going to stop this. So I better had. I need to get after the [as a noun]Figure[end as], and he went the other way!";

Instead of going southwest from the North Clerestory when the player can see the Figure in Grey:
	do nothing;

After going southwest from the North Clerestory when Overheard Conversation has happened:
	unless Return To The Cathedral has happened:
		say "I chase after the [as a noun]Figure[end as].";
		try looking instead;
	otherwise:
		continue the action;

Part 15 - East Clerestory

Chapter 1 - Description

The East Clerestory is a room, in the Cathedral Space. "[if Doric is in North Clerestory]I'm legging it from the [as a noun]watchman[end as], who's only a pace behind. I could jump the [as a noun]rail[end as] or take the [as a noun]stairs[end as] down - I know which I'd prefer![otherwise if Archbishop's Meeting has not happened]This [as a noun]balcony[end as], which curves to the northwest, is built right above the [as a noun]choir stalls[end as]. I could probably jump down onto them if I felt really brave, but it'd be easier to take the [as a noun]stairs[end as], built against the east wall, just below the enormous [as a noun]stained glass window[end as].[otherwise]I'm back on the [as a noun]balcony[end as] on the east side of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], underneath the [as a noun]gigantic window[end as] depicting a [as a noun]winding key[end as]. [as a noun]Stairs[end as] lead down and the [as a noun]balcony[end as] curves away to the northwest.[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when in the East Clerestory: try going down instead.
Instead of going inside in the East Clerestory: try going north instead.

Instead of facing east when in the East Clerestory:
	try examining the window of the winding-key.

Section 1 - Glimpse of Choir stalls

The glimpsed Choir is a glimpse backdrop, in the East Clerestory, in the West Clerestory, in the North Clerestory. "The choir stalls are empty." The printed name is "choir". Understand "choir", "stall", "stalls" as the glimpsed Choir.

The glimpsed Choir identifies the choir stalls.

Chapter 2 - Events on Entry

Section 1 - Event on Entry - Doric

DORICEVENT1 is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired DORICEVENT1:
	say "Excellent. I can get on and warn him about [as a noun]Gubbler[end as]'s plan to have something stolen from the Vaults!";

After looking in the East Clerestory when DORICEVENT1 is unfired:
	fire DORICEVENT1;

Section 2 - Event on Entry - Figure

FIGUREEVENT1 is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired FIGUREEVENT1:
	say "What's the point of adults, exactly? They sit around complaining about how rotten everything is – you've heard them – but when a real problem comes along they don't do anything. It's not good. I'll just...[paragraph break]Wait a minute. Noises, to the northwest. Whispers. A [as a noun]glimpse[end as] of Grey...[paragraph break]";

After looking in the East Clerestory when Archbishop's Meeting has happened and FIGUREEVENT1 is unfired:
	move the glimpse of the Figure to the East Clerestory;
	fire FIGUREEVENT1;

The glimpse of the Figure is a scenery thing. Understand "grey" as the glimpse of the Figure. "I catch just a [as a noun]glimpse[end as] of a Grey in the shadows between the two statues to the northwest. If I crept that way, I might be able to catch what's being said..."

Instead of approaching the glimpse of Figure:
	say "I creep closer.";
	try going northwest instead;
	
Instead of examining the glimpse of the Figure for the second time:
	try approaching the glimpse of the Figure instead.

Instead of facing northwest when the glimpse of the figure is in the location:
	try examining the glimpse of the Figure instead;

Before listening when the glimpse of the Figure is visible:
	say "I can't make it out from here, but there are murmurs... words... perhaps if I snuck up a little closer..." instead;

Chapter 3 - Doric

Section 1 - Description

Doric is a man, in the East Clerestory. "[if Doric is awake]There's another door to the north; solid oak. It may not look like much, but judging by the [as a noun]guard[end as] in front, that's the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as]'s door[one of]. The [as a noun]guard[end as]'s name-badge declares him to be [as a noun]Doric[end as] of the Swiss Watch[once only].[otherwise][as a noun]Doric[end as] is still standing guard, but his eyes seem to be closed.[end if]"

Understand "guard", "man", "Doric", "protectorate", "uniform" as Doric.

Instead of approaching Doric:
	say "The [as a noun]guard[end as] isn't going anywhere. I mean, seriously. He's not going <Italic>anywhere</Italic>."

The description of Doric is "[DoricDescription]"

To say DoricDescription:
	if Doric is awake:
		say "The [as a noun]Guard[end as] is a member of the [as a noun]Protectorate[end as], hired from the ranks of the Swiss Watch and trained specially for Church service. This one wears an elaborate uniform of straps and buckles that clink whenever he moves. More menacingly, he carries a large [as a noun]halberd[end as]. ";
	otherwise:
		say "For a highly-trained [as a noun]guard[end as] I'm not impressed. He seems to have fallen asleep on his feet. His brain must have wound down... [If CHASE0 is fired]Either that or all the running around has worn him out.[end if] ";

Doric has a truth state called the faith flag.
Doric has a truth state called the business flag.
The faith flag of Doric is false. The business flag of Doric is false.
							  
CHASE_COUNTER is a counter. 

Doric carries a halberd. The description of the halberd is "It's like an axe on a stick.". Understand "large" as the halberd.

Instead of doing something when the halberd is physically involved:
	say "I don't think he'll let me play with it.";

Section 2 - Atmospheric events

The DoricCounter is a counter. The internal value is 2. The top end is 4.

Every turn when the player can see awake Doric:
	if Doric is not the noun [to avoid when we've talked to him, hopefully]:
		increment the DoricCounter;

Rule for firing the DoricCounter when in the East Clerestory:
	say "[one of][as a noun]Doric[end as] stares me down. Not fair: he's trained in this sort of thing.[or][as a noun]Doric[end as] shines his [as a noun]halberd[end as] on his sleeve.[or][as a noun]Doric[end as] resolutely does not adjust his feet.[or][as a noun]Doric[end as] glares straight ahead; with a little sideways so he can see me.[at random]"

Section 2b - Interaction

Instead of showing something to Doric:
	say "[as a noun]Doric[end as] sniffs. 'Don't mean much to me, rat,' he remarks."

Instead of showing the knife to Doric:
	say "[one of]'What you goin' to do with that?' [as a noun]Doric[end as] smirks. 'Pick your nose?'[or]'Put it away, rat,' he says, and very gently taps his [as a noun]halberd[end as] on the stone floor.[stopping]"
	
Instead of attacking Doric:
	say "Have you not noticed his [as a noun]halberd[end as]?"

Instead of kissing Doric:
	say "No way!"

Instead of attacking Doric with something:
	say "Have you not noticed his [as a noun]halberd[end as]?"

Section 3 - Conversation

CT_DOR_FAITH is a conversation topic.
The enquiry text is "[if the faith flag of Doric is false]'I know the [as a noun]creed[end as],' I tell him.[otherwise]'I knew the [as a noun]creed[end as], didn't I?'[end if]".
The response text is "[if the faith flag of Doric is false]'Go on, then.' He waits for me to supply it.[otherwise]'Eventually,' he concedes. 'Like you'd not been in the church a good long while.'[end if]".

Last after firing CT_DOR_FAITH when the faith flag of Doric is false:
	now CT_DOR_FAITH is unfired; [We can ask this one multiple times]
	test the player's creed;

CT_DOR_BUSINESS is a conversation topic.
The enquiry text is "[if the business flag of Doric is false]'What kind of proof do you want?' I ask him.[otherwise]'Looks like I am here on business, doesn't it?' I say, smugly. 'Official Church business.'[end if]".
The response text is "[if the business flag of Doric is false]He shrugs. 'Look, kid. Either you're a tourist, or you're a thief, or you've got some kind of reason to see the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as]. If you'll be able to see him over his desk, seeing as you're not that big.'[paragraph break]'I've got a very important reason!' I protest.[paragraph break]'Then show the paper what says so,' [as a noun]Doric[end as] replies. 'Else you'll be out here feeling important all day.'[otherwise]'Good for you,' he says, without interest. 'You going to just stand there or are you going to go in?'[end if]".

CT_DOR_ABBEY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I've come from the [as a noun]Abbey[end as],' I tell him.". The response text is "'Really.' He narrows his eyes suspiciously. 'We've been hearing quite a racket from there just now. Like a pack of wild animals hit the bell room.'[paragraph break]'Really?' I reply, innocently. 'Probably a clock – slipped.'[paragraph break]'Clocks don't slip,' he says firmly. 'And if you're <Italic>really</Italic> a good Church kid you'd know that.'".
CT_DOR_CATHEDRAL is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'You must get bored of standing around here,' I say.". The response text is "'No chance. No way,' he replies. 'You aren't getting me to go wandering off. No way.'".
CT_DOR_ABBOT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'You don't understand,' I tell him. 'The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]'s in league with this [as a noun]Figure[end as] and they're going to...'". The response text is "'Look, kid,' [as a noun]Doric[end as] says, not unkindly. 'Either we'll get you through this door or you can get lost. I don't mind either way. Don't appeal to my better nature because I don't have one. I've just got <Italic>my</Italic> nature, and my nature says block the door, [as a noun]Doric[end as], and don't let no kid go slipping by you. Clear on that?'".
CT_DOR_ARCHBISHOP is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Is the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] in?' I ask, cannily.". The response text is "'Oh, he's in,' [as a noun]Doric[end as] says. 'If he's in for you – well. That's an open question, as you fear-logians might say.'".
CT_DOR_PEOPLE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "He's not likely to know them, standing round here all day.".
CT_DOR_DOOR is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Can I go through your [as a noun]door[end as]?' I ask.". The response text is "'Why don't you try?' he sneers in reply.".
CT_DOR_SAAT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Do you know anything about [as a noun]Brother Sa'at[end as]?' I ask.". The response text is "'If he's not trying to come through this door then I don't know a thing,' he says. 'So don't think you can name-drop your way into my confidence.'".
CT_DOR_CLOCKWORK is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Are you interested in clockwork?' I ask.". The response text is "'I'm a pious man,' he says, abruptly, affected by your question. 'Not a holy one, maybe, and not clever enough for mysteries and that. But I pay my duties once a week.'".
CT_DOR_SAINTS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Who's your favourite saint?' I ask.". The response text is "'I dunno.' He shrugs. '[as a noun]Newton[end as] was a good one, wasn't it? All that slogging around being hit by apples. Good training, they say.'".
CT_DOR_BOOKS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I need to find a [as a noun]book[end as],' I tell him.". The response text is "'So what y['] asking me for? Like I can read, a [as a noun]Protectorate[end as] like me? I ought to chuck you over t' [as a noun]balcony[end as] for suggestin' it. But I won't.' He tips his head at the [as a noun]door[end as]. 'Boss might hear it. Doesn't like violence inside.' Suddenly he stiffens. 'Less it's necessary, don't think about rushing me neither.'".

CT_DOR_SEAL is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Do I need this proof of yours sealed or stamped?' I ask." The response text is "'First of all,' [as a noun]Doric[end as] answers patiently, 'if you was a proper Abbey type, you'd already know. Second of all, without a [as a noun]seal[end as] a piece of paper's just a piece of paper. Third of all, you can't get [as a noun]seal[end as]s just anywhere so you can get your hopes down already. Got it?'"

CT_DOR_ME is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Listen, I'm from the Abbey,' I begin, 'and...'". The response text is "'I don't much care if you're from the Moon,' [as a noun]Doric[end as] replies smartly. 'Either you're not comin' in or you're not comin' in, that's how I see it. And in the rare occasion that you <Italic>are</Italic> comin' in, you'll have something good enough to make me step aside. Won't you, rat?'"

CT_DOR_DORIC is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'So, do you like being a [as a noun]guard[end as]?' I ask."
The response text is "[as a noun]Doric[end as] puffs out his chest. 'Yeah. I mean, yeah. It's alright. The hours are good. Pension's okay. Good for the soul, all this standing around. Yeah.'[paragraph break]He doesn't look too convinced."

CT_DOR_HALBERD is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What's that you're carrying?' I ask, nonchalantly." The response text is "'Pain in the rear-side of anyone tryin' to slip their way past,' he replies with a toothy smile. 'You get me?'"

The conversation table of Doric is the Table of Doric's Conversation.

