section 2 - the Upper reaches region

The Upper Reaches of the Cathedral are a region. Among the Gargoyles, Lower Gears, Upper Gears, Rafters, Behind the Clock Face are in the Upper Reaches of the Cathedral.

Part 5 - Among the Gargoyles


Among the Gargoyles is a room. "Looking down – oh, no... - there's the floor of the [as a noun]choir[end as], far below. I'm really, really high up – higher than my little bedroom – and this ledge is a lot narrower than my bed... And there's nothing to hold onto - nothing out there but space, and the gigantic workings of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] clock, like a hornet's nest suspended from the centre of the dome."

Instead of going down in Among the Gargoyles:
	say "I've got to follow him... somehow...[paragraph break]";

Instead of going a direction in Among the Gargoyles:
	say "There's no way off this ledge at all!";

Instead of facing down when in Among the Gargoyles or in Lower Gears:
	say "A million metres below me are [as a noun]monks[end as] as small as black ants crawling round the [as a noun]altar[end as] below the clock. They'll get the shock of their lives when I fall on them..."

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in Among the Gargoyles:
	say "He's climbing the clock: how I'm supposed to get over <Italic>there</Italic>?"


Chapter 2 - Scripted Events

After looking in Among the Gargoyles when SE_GARGOYLES1 is unfired:
	change the current script to { SE_GARGOYLES1, SE_GARGOYLES2 };
	move the escaping Figure to the Upper Reaches of the Cathedral;
	update backdrop positions;

SE_GARGOYLES1 is a scripted event. The display text is "Steady, Wren. You're good with heights, aren't you? But maybe not with [as a noun]pendulums[end as]! I duck to avoid the massive [as a noun]penduluum[end as] of the clock as it almost knocks me clear of the ledge! I cling onto a [as a noun]gargoyle[end as] and I don't... look... down...".

SE_GARGOYLES2 is a scripted event. The display text is "I spot the [as a noun]Figure[end as], clinging to the clock's [as a noun]gears[end as] like a spider. The hood is turned to look back at me. For a moment he's still – then an escapement above him turns and he's scuttled upwards, deep into the turning wheels.".

Instead of doing something with the escaping Figure when SE_GARGOYLES2 is not fired and SE_GARGOYLES1 is fired:
	[ we're in Amongst the Gargoyles and we've not yet seen him. But we're about to! ]
	do nothing;

Chapter 3 - Pendulum

The cathedral clock penduluum is scenery, in Among the Gargoyles. "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] weight is the size of a Grandfather clock and the rod on which it swings is the size of a tree. If that thing hits me it'd be like Drake stepping on a beetle."

The printed name is "penduluum". Understand "pendulum" as the cathedral clock penduluum.

The PenduluumCounter is a Busy Counter. The top end is 4. The internal value is 4.

Every turn when in Among the Gargoyles and SE_GARGOYLES2 is fired:
	increment the PenduluumCounter;

Rule for firing the PenduluumCounter when the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 1:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] hangs still in space for a full second at the far point of its swing.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] coasts to a stop inside the clock.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is dead still, at the far point of its swing.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is far off inside the clock.[stopping]";

Rule for firing the PenduluumCounter when the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 2:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] careers towards me, coming closer every second![or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is picking up speed, heading right for me![or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] charges towards me![stopping]";

Rule for firing the PenduluumCounter when the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 3:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] swings up to ledge, almost knocking me down.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] slows to the edge of the ledge and waits for a moment, before starting to swing away.[or]For a moment the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is still beside me, then it seems to float backwards off the ledge.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] almost hits me as it turns.[stopping]";

Rule for firing the PenduluumCounter when the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 4:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] sails away in slow-motion through the machinery of the great Clock.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] soars back into the depths of the clock's workings.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] swings away.[stopping]";

Instead of jumping in Among the Gargoyles:
	try taking the cathedral clock penduluum instead;

Instead of climbing or climbing up or jumping on the Cathedral Clock penduluum:
	try taking the cathedral clock penduluum instead;

Before taking the Cathedral Clock penduluum when the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 1 or the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 2:  [ that is, "sailed away" or "far point of swing" ] 
	say "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as]'s at the far end of its swing. <Italic>You can't fly, young Wren</Italic>." instead;

Instead of entering or approaching or taking the Cathedral Clock penduluum: 
	if the internal value of the PenduluumCounter is 3: [ this is, "approaching fast" ]
		say "Until the last moment I don't know whether I can do it... and as the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] slows to a halt, I wrap my arms around it. A moment later my feet are in mid-air and";
	else:  [ this is, "up to the ledge" ]  
		say "Every wire in my body is telling me it's too late, and that makes it even later. At the last possible moment I jump into space... I'm over the vast hall of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]... and then with an awkward crash I hit the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] and wrap my arms around it![paragraph break]The whole clock above seems to shiver from the extra weight and then";
	say " I'm away, floating over space, faster and faster...[paragraph break]At the lowest point when I'm whipping through the air, I let go and drop onto a platform, surrounded by [as a noun]gears[end as] and windings. The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is gone in a moment.";
	move the player to Lower Gears;


Chapter 4 - Scenery

Section 1 - Gargoyles

Some gargoyles of vice are a backdrop, in Among the Gargoyles and Parapet. "The ledge is fitted with [as a noun]gargoyles[end as] showing the Vices of Laxity. Everything's here, from sleeping on the job (poor [as a noun]Doric[end as]!) through to eating with your mouth open." Understand "gargoyle" as the gargoyles of vice.

The printed name of the gargoyles of vice is "gargoyles".

Instead of climbing the gargoyles of vice:
	say "I'm not going to let go of them, am I? I don't fancy keeping my balance up here.";

Instead of touching the gargoyles of vice:
	say "The [as a noun]gargoyles[end as] are as crisply carved as the day they were made."

Section 2 - Choir Floor

The choir floor is a backdrop, in Among the Gargoyles, Lower Gears and Upper Gears. "[if the location is not Upper Gears]The [as a noun]floor[end as] looks hard and very far away. Great.[otherwise]I can't see the [as a noun]floor[end as] any more from this height.[end if]"

Instead of doing something when the Choir Floor is physically involved:
	say "[if the location is not Upper Gears]No thanks.[otherwise]I can't see the [as a noun]floor[end as] any more from this height.[end if]";

Section 2b - Singing Monks

Some singing monks is a backdrop, in Among the Gargoyles, Lower Gears and Upper Gears. "[if location is not Upper Gears]The shining spots far below are the bald heads of the [as a noun]monks[end as] around the [as a noun]altar[end as]. The [as a noun]machine[end as] on the [as a noun]altar[end as] itself is a single gleaming gold dot.[else]I can't see the [as a noun]monks[end as] any more, only hear the whisper of their song.[end if]". Understand "altar", "gold", "dot", "gleam", "bald", "head", "heads", "chant", "chanting", "song" as the singing monks.

Include (- has animate, -) when defining the singing monks.

Before asking the singing monks about:
	say "If I cried for help now they'd probably think it was a miracle: the Clock talking back to them." instead;

Before shouting at the singing monks:
	say "If I cried for help now they'd probably think it was a miracle: the Clock talking back to them." instead;

Instead of listening when the singing monks are visible:
	say "The song is soft and rhythmic and very, very far away."

Instead of doing something when the singing monks are physically involved:
	say "[if the location is not Upper Gears]No thanks.[otherwise]I can't see the monks any more from this height.[end if]";

Every turn when the singing monks are visible and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds and SE_GARGOYLES2 is fired:
	if the location is the Upper Gears:
		say "[one of]The chanting is barely audible now.[or]The [as a noun]monk[end as]'s song is lost in the tick-tock of the clock.[cycling]";
	else:
		say "[one of]Thin singing drifts up from the floor far below.[or]The stone walls echo with soft chanting, regular and low, like the murmur of a shifting counterweight.[or]The [as a noun]monks[end as] below are just bright spots in black circles on the distant [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] floor.[cycling]"; 
	
Section 3 - Gears

Some gearwheels are a backdrop, in Among the Gargoyles, Lower Gears, Upper Gears, and the Rafters. "[if the location is Among The Gargoyles]The workings of the clock are a shipwreck of [as a noun]cogs[end as], [as a noun]wheels[end as] and [as a noun]traces[end as], their [as a noun]teeth[end as] and [as a noun]struts[end as] and [as a noun]scaffolds[end as] moving with the same slow purpose as [as a noun]Sa'at[end as]'s Great [as a noun]Orrery[end as]. Up above them I can just see the enormous face of the [as a noun]rose window clock face[end as].[otherwise if the location is Lower Gears]It's a maze of [as a noun]gears[end as], [as a noun]chains[end as] and [as a noun]pinions[end as], all in constant movement. A few have [as a noun]rungs[end as] and [as a noun]ladder-holds[end as] beside them, others have [as a noun]struts[end as] across their diameters.[otherwise if the location is Upper Gears]There are [as a noun]pulleys[end as] and [as a noun]chains[end as] everywhere, all turning the massive [as a noun]shaft[end as] of the clock overhead.[otherwise if the location is Rafters]The [as a noun]gears[end as] are across the room from where I am. The [as a noun]clock-face[end as] itself is nothing but a glass wall and the gigantic [as a noun]hands[end as] whose shadows I can just see.[end if]"

The printed name of the gearwheels is "gears".

Understand "gear", "gear-teeth", "gear-tooth", "teeth", "gears", "working", "workings", "[clockwork]", "cog", "cogs", "wheel", "wheels", "trace", "traces", "teeth", "strut", "struts", "pinion", "pinions", "scaffold", "scaffolds", "mechanism" as the gearwheels.

Understand "clock", "cathedral clock" as the gearwheels when in Lower Gears or in Upper Gears or in Rafters.

Instead of climbing the gearwheels when in Among the Gargoyles:
	say "They're a jump of twenty feet away or more, suspended over thin air and the [as a noun]choir[end as] below!";

Instead of doing something when in the Rafters and the gearwheels are physically involved:
	say "They're back across the space from me.";

Instead of turning or pushing or pulling or taking or touching the gearwheels:
	say "They'd take my hand off without a second thought."


Part 6 - Lower Gears

Lower Gears is a room. "I'm sandwiched between [the (remainder after dividing the times-moved of the Lower Gears by 4) + 2 in words] [as a noun]gears[end as] of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] clock, on a [by times-moved]narrow[or by]rickety[or by]unsteady[or by]worm-ridden[end] wooden [by times-moved]platform[or by]ledge[or by]plank[end] that [by times-moved]winds in and out between[or by]runs to the left of[or by]passes to the right of[or by]runs underneath[or by]bridges a gap between[end] the [as a noun]workings[end as]. [one of]This noise is louder than [as a noun]Reloh[end as]'s [as a noun]duplicator[end as] – like an army of cooks all banging saucepans. I can barely keep straight which way is up and which way is down.[paragraph break][once only][as a noun]Platforms[end as] lead away to the [available-platforms][if times-moved of the Lower Gears is greater than 6], and a line of [as a noun]rungs[end as] disappears up[end if]."

The Lower Gears have a number called the times-moved. The times-moved of the Lower Gears is one.

Index map with Lower Gears mapped north of Among the Gargoyles.

Before going a planar direction in Lower Gears:
	if the noun is listed in the available-platforms begin;
		change the available-platforms to {north, northeast, east, southeast, south, southwest, west, northwest};
		sort the available-platforms in random order;
		truncate the available-platforms to 3 entries;
		say "I worm my way between the [as a noun]gears[end as].";
		increase the times-moved of the Lower Gears by a random number between one and three;
		move the player to Lower Gears instead; [illusion of true movement.]
	end if;
	say "There's no way to go that way, except into the grinder of [as a noun]gear-teeth[end as] themselves." instead;

After going up from Lower Gears:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] went up so I will too. And never mind if the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] clock loses a few seconds from me moving the [as a noun]gears[end as] as I climb. If the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] loses its relics that'd be worse!";
	continue the action;

Understand "chain", "chains", "rung", "rungs", "ladder", "ladders", "hold", "holds", "ladderhold", "ladderholds", "ladder-hold", "ladder-holds", "line of" as the gearwheels when the location is Lower Gears.

Instead of climbing the gearwheels when in Lower Gears:
	try going up;

Instead of approaching the Escaping figure when in the Lower Gears:
	try going up.

Instead of going down when in Lower Gears:
	try jumping instead.

Instead of jumping when in Lower Gears:
	say "There's nothing but mashing [as a noun]gear-teeth[end as] beneath me.";

Section 1 - Platforms underfoot

Some Clock platforms are scenery in Lower Gears. "The [as a noun]platforms[end as] are held together by old iron nails." Understand "platform", "ledge", "ledges", "walkway", "walkways", "rafters" as the Clock platforms.

Instead of approaching or entering the Clock platforms:
	say "Which way? [as a noun]Platforms[end as] lead away to the [available-platforms].";

Instead of getting off the Clock platforms:
	try going down.

Instead of climbing the Clock platforms:
	try going up.

Instead of taking the Clock platforms:
	say "I could cling on for dear life, but it wouldn't help me catch the [as a noun]Figure[end as]!"

Instead of looking under the clock platforms: 
	try facing down.

Instead of facing down when in the Lower Gears:
	say "There's a long drop that way..."



Part 7 - Upper Gears

Upper Gears is a room, up from Lower Gears. "[one of]Further up now, and hanging onto a [as a noun]scaffold[end as] beam for dear life. Below – quite a lot of space, and the rest of the [as a noun]mechanism[end as]. Around, [as a noun]cogs[end as] grinding the rust off other [as a noun]cogs[end as]. And in front: a void of empty space. A gap – and then the beautiful crystal light of the [as a noun]rose window[end as]: the [as a noun]clock face[end as] itself, filling my eyes with light.[paragraph break]Way overhead, higher even still, is the [as a noun]shaft[end as] that turns the [as a noun]clock hands[end as], reaching out from the gigantic [as a noun]mechanism[end as] and through the [as a noun]window[end as]. There's no way I can climb there. The [as a noun]gears[end as] here are smaller and moving too fast.[or]I'm on a [as a noun]beam[end as] bathed in the light of the [as a noun]rose window[end as]. High overhead is the thick metal [as a noun]beam[end as] that turns the [as a noun]clockhands[end as], reaching through the [as a noun]window[end as], but there's no way I can climb there.[stopping]"


Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in Upper Gears:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] must have climbed the gear-teeth: I can't do that! I'm just a child!"

Chapter 2 - Event on entry

UG_FIGURE1 is a trigger.

After looking in Upper Gears when UG_FIGURE1 is unfired:
	fire UG_FIGURE1;

Rule for firing UG_FIGURE1:
	say "Clinging to the [as a noun]shaft[end as] overhead is the [as a noun]Figure[end as]. He must have climbed the [as a noun]gear-teeth[end as] themselves.[paragraph break]'Why do you follow me, child?' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] demands, his hooded face turning towards me. 'You can't stop me from stealing the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]. There are greater wheels turning here that you don't understand!'[paragraph break]Then swiftly and smoothly, like a cat, he grabs onto a passing length of [as a noun]chain[end as] and soaring up into space. But I'm thinking one thing: he's planning to steal the <Italic>[as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]!</Italic> [paragraph break]";

Chapter 3 - Objects

Section 1 - Barrel

A barrel is fixed in place, in the Upper Gears. "[if thick chain is gripped]The [as a noun]chain[end as] I'm holding onto runs out of the [as a noun]barrel[end as] beside me[else]Right beside me is a [as a noun]barrel[end as] with a [as a noun]crank[end as], from which a thick iron [as a noun]chain[end as] runs up to a [as a noun]pulley[end as] near the top of the [as a noun]window[end as][end if]. [if iron weight is low]The [as a noun]counterweight[end as] has fallen and the [as a noun]barrel[end as] must be empty of [as a noun]chain[end as][else]A enormous [as a noun]counterweight[end as] hangs up there[end if]."

The description of the barrel is "The [as a noun]barrel[end as] feeds out a long iron [as a noun]chain[end as] to a [as a noun]pulley[end as] above the [as a noun]rose window[end as]. The [as a noun]barrel[end as] has a [as a noun]crank[end as] on front and also a [as a noun]release lever[end as]. [if iron weight is low]The [as a noun]barrel[end as] is almost empty: the [as a noun]counterweight[end as] has spooled out plenty of [as a noun]chain[end as].[else]The [as a noun]chain[end as] is stiff and taut thanks to the [as a noun]counterweight[end as], high up by the [as a noun]beams[end as].[end if]"

Instead of entering the barrel:
	say "That'd be a precarious place to stand."

Instead of standing on the barrel:
	say "That'd be a precarious place to stand."

Section 2 - Crank

The crank handle is part of the barrel. The printed name is "crank". The description is "The [as a noun]crank[end as] handle on the [as a noun]barrel[end as] probably winds the [as a noun]chain[end as]."

Instead of turning the crank handle when the iron weight is high:
	say "The [as a noun]chain[end as] is as taut as it'll go, since the [as a noun]weight[end as] is hoisted right to the top.";

Instead of turning the crank handle when the iron weight is low:
	now the iron weight is high;
	if the thick chain is gripped:
		say "I let go of the [as a noun]chain[end as] first. ";
		now the thick chain is not gripped;
	say "The [as a noun]crank[end as] turns easily, and very slowly the [as a noun]weight[end as] rises up towards the [as a noun]pulley[end as]. Who's meant to use this machine I don't know: maybe it's left over from construction.[paragraph break]After several minutes of work, the [as a noun]weight[end as] is as high as it'll go. The [as a noun]release lever[end as] clicks closed.";

Section 3 - Lever

The release lever is part of the barrel. The printed name is "lever". The description is "The [as a noun]release lever[end as] on the [as a noun]barrel[end as] is currently [if the iron weight is low]open[else]closed[end if]."

Instead of pulling or opening or attacking the release lever when the iron weight is low:
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as]'s already open, so nothing happens.";

Instead of pulling or opening or attacking the release lever when the thick chain is not gripped and the iron weight is high:
	now the iron weight is low;
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] unlocks and the [as a noun]chain[end as] begins to spool away instantly, as the [as a noun]weight[end as] over the [as a noun]pulley[end as] plummets downwards. The [as a noun]links[end as] beside me wind away towards the [as a noun]window[end as].";

Instead of pulling or opening or attacking the release lever:
	say "Clinging on tight – I must have gone crazy – I kick the [as a noun]release[end as] with my shoe. There's a sudden jerk and then I'm shot up and across. Stomach in throat, air beneath my feet, arms screaming for a clear explanation of exactly what do I think I'm doing? Hurtling towards the [as a noun]pulley[end as]. How much chain is there in this thing? I've no idea. What if I hit? I'm frozen as the [as a noun]pulley[end as] comes up fast – I'm going to be knocked down – [paragraph break]- and I've no choice but to let go and I <Italic>fall</Italic> – [paragraph break]- onto a [as a noun]rafter[end as] below. Sweet Grease! I can barely move I'm so busy shaking!";
	move player to Rafters;

Instead of pushing or knocking on the release lever:
	try pulling the release lever;

Instead of closing the release lever when the iron weight is high:
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] is closed. I could pull it open."

Instead of closing the release lever:
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] won't close, presumably because the [as a noun]counterweight[end as] is now so low."

Chapter 4 - Scenery

Section 1 - Gearwheels

Instead of climbing or jumping on the gearwheels when in Upper Gears:
	say "It's all vertical now, and the bars of the [as a noun]scaffold[end as] and struts of the [as a noun]cogs[end as] are more spaced out than the rungs of my [as a noun]ladder[end as]. I can't get higher.";

Understand "shaft", "hands", "beam", "metal beam" as the gearwheels when the location is Upper Gears.

Understand "rung", "rungs", "ladder", "ladders", "hold", "holds", "ladderhold", "ladderholds", "ladder-hold", "ladder-holds", "vertical", "pulley", "pulleys", "chains" as the gearwheels when the location is Upper Gears.

Section 2 - Rose Window

A rose window is a backdrop, in the Upper Gears, Rafters, Behind the Clock Face, Minute Hand, and Parapet.

Rule for printing the description of the rose window when in Upper Gears:
	say "The [as a noun]window[end as] is above and beyond. Its stained glass covers the clock's workings in fractured colours. It's beautiful here. Light and clockwork in perfect harmony.[paragraph break]A [as a noun]pulley[end as] hangs at its top, attached to the long [as a noun]chain[end as] that feeds into the [as a noun]barrel[end as] by my feet.";

Understand "clock", "face", "clockface", "clock-face", "crystal", "light", "stained", "glass" as the rose window.

Before doing something when the rose window is physically involved:
	if the location is Upper Gears or the location is Rafters:
		say "I can't reach the window from here." instead;
	continue the action.

Instead of attacking the rose window:
	say "It's enormous. Even if I could push a fingerhole through, no-one would ever even see. And it wouldn't help me keep up with the [as a noun]Figure[end as]."

Instead of touching the rose window:
	say "The [as a noun]glass[end as] is cold and warped; roughly made."

Instead of searching or looking through the rose window:
	say "Nothing is visible through the thick syrup colours of the [as a noun]glass[end as]."

Section 2b - Twelve

The XII is part of the rose window. Understand "numeral", "numerals", "number" as the XII.

Rule for printing the name of XII when in Behind the Clock Face:
	say "IV".

Understand "xii", "twelve" as the XII when in the Rafters.
Understand "iv", "four" as the XII when in Behind the Clock Face.

Instead of doing something when the XII is physically involved:
	if the location is the Rafters:
		say "Almost. Not quite.";
	else:
		say "I can't reach the [as a noun]window[end as] from here."

Rule for printing the description of the XII:
	say "The numerals are the size of my body."


Section 3 - Chain

A thick chain is a backdrop, in the Upper Gears, the Rafters, and Behind the Clock Face. "[if the location is the Rafters]The [as a noun]chain[end as] runs down from the [as a noun]pulley[end as] overhead to the [as a noun]weight[end as] below. It passes more [as a noun]rafters[end as] on the way.[otherwise]The thick [as a noun]chain[end as] extends both up and down from here.[end if]"

Understand "links" as the thick chain.

Understand "pulley", "weight", "iron" as the thick chain when the location is not Upper Gears.

Rule for printing the description of the thick chain when in the Upper Gears:
	say "The thick [as a noun]links[end as] of the [as a noun]chain[end as] run up to a [as a noun]pulley[end as] by the window. Over the [as a noun]pulley[end as] is a [as a noun]weight[end as], [if the iron weight is high]hoisted as high as it can go[otherwise]which dangles far below[end if]. The end near me feeds into the [as a noun]barrel[end as][if the thick chain is gripped].[paragraph break]I'm holding on to the [as a noun]chain[end as] for dear life![else].[end if]";

Instead of climbing the thick chain when in Upper Gears:
	say "I could grip my arms around the [as a noun]chain[end as] but the angle's too steep – and the distance, it's pretty far! I can't just climb out. I really can't.";

Before swinging the thick chain:
	try climbing the thick chain instead.

Instead of climbing the thick chain [elsewhere]:
	try going down instead;

The thick chain can be gripped. The thick chain is not gripped.

Instead of taking the thick chain when the thick chain is not gripped:
	now the thick chain is gripped;
	say "I grab hold of the [as a noun]chain[end as].";

Instead of taking the thick chain when the thick chain is gripped:
	say "I'm holding the [as a noun]chain[end as] already."

Instead of dropping the thick chain when the thick chain is gripped:
	now the thick chain is not gripped;
	say "I let go of the [as a noun]chain[end as].";

Instead of dropping the thick chain when the thick chain is not gripped:
	say "I'm not holding the [as a noun]chain[end as]."

Instead of pulling the thick chain:
	say "[if the iron weight is high]The [as a noun]chain[end as] is taut from here to the [as a noun]pulley[end as], since the [as a noun]weight[end as] is hoisted right to the top[otherwise]The [as a noun]chain[end as] is far too heavy to move[end if].";

Section 4 - Counterweight

An iron weight is scenery, in the Upper Gears. The iron weight can be high or low. The iron weight is high. The description of the iron weight is "A solid chunk of iron [if high]near the [as a noun]pulley[end as][otherwise]hanging far, far below the gigantic [as a noun]rose window[end as][end if]." Understand "counterweight", "counter-weight" as the iron weight. 

Instead of doing something when the iron weight is physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]weight[end as] is far, far out of my reach, on the other end of the [as a noun]chain[end as].";

Part 8 - Rafters

Chapter 1 - Description

Rafters is a room. "I'm clinging to a [as a noun]rafter[end as]. Beside me is the long [as a noun]chain[end as] from the [as a noun]pulley[end as], down past the [as a noun]rose window[end as] that lights up everything in vivid red, blue and green. My foot could almost reach the [as a noun]XII[end as] on the clock face... Below is nothing but a few more [as a noun]rafters[end as], presumably used for placing and replacing the [as a noun]glass[end as]. And then there's nothing but a long drop for a long time, and the chanting of the [as a noun]monks[end as] far below.[paragraph break]By one of the [as a noun]rafters[end as] near the bottom of the [as a noun]window[end as] is a little [as a noun]door[end as] in the clock itself – a pane of [as a noun]glass[end as], swung outward. <Italic>The [as a noun]Figure[end as].</Italic>" 

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in the Rafters:
	try going down;

Instead of making to leave in the Rafters: 
	try going down.

Instead of taking the thick chain when in the Rafters:
	try going down.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Instead of going up in the Rafters:
	say "Up is nothing but the gigantic [as a noun]pulley[end as] and I can't scramble onto that even if I wanted to.";

Instead of going a planar direction when in the Rafters:
	say "I can't go anywhere from here. I'm out on a limb, with just the [as a noun]chain[end as] for company.";

After going down from the Rafters:
	say "Gingerly, I clamber down the [as a noun]chain[end as] and onto the [as a noun]rafters[end as] behind the clock face, lower down.";
	continue the action;

Section 1a - rafters

Some rafter beams are a backdrop. They are in the Rafters, in the Upper Gears, in Behind the Clock Face. "The [as a noun]beams[end as] are so narrow I have to go one foot after the next. And they're old, and a little bent in the middle too!". Understand "rafters", "beam", "beams" as the rafter beams.

Instead of dropping the rafter beams:
	say "No way. I'm happier having something underfoot, at least!"

Instead of taking the rafter beams:
	say "Don't worry, there's no chance I'm letting go.";

Instead of approaching or entering the rafter beams in Upper Gears:
	say "The [as a noun]rafters[end as] can't take me anywhere from here. I need to go up!"

Instead of approaching or entering the rafter beams in Rafters:
	try going down;

Instead of approaching or entering the rafter beams in Behind the Clock Face:
	try going outside;

Section 1 - Rose Window

Instead of doing something when the Rose Window is physically involved and the location is the Rafters:
	say "It's sheer [as a noun]glass[end as]. I couldn't do anything.";

Rule for printing the description of the rose window when in Rafters:
	say "The [as a noun]window[end as] is close enough to touch – but so large it fills everything I can see. Somewhere below a panel is missing, creating a small [as a noun]door[end as].";

Part 9 - Behind the Clock Face

Chapter 1 - Description

Behind the Clock Face is a room, down from the Rafters. "Like an unlucky symbol I can see the giant [as a noun]clock face[end as] in front of me, in reverse. The [as a noun]hands[end as] are turning widdershins, very slowly. Enough to make me shudder. The [as a noun]rafter[end as] I'm on extends right up to the [as a noun]glass panel[end as] set in the [as a noun]clock face[end as], where a small square of normal light breaks the green and silver of the stained glass, near the [as a noun]IV[end as].[paragraph break]The [as a noun]chain[end as] extends beside me, up and down."

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in Behind the Clock Face:
	try going outside;

Instead of going up when in Behind the Clock Face:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] is within reach. This is where the [as a noun]Figure[end as] went.";

Instead of going down when in Behind the Clock Face:
	try going up instead;

Understand "small", "sky", "blue", "door", "panel", "square" as the Rose Window when the location is Behind the Clock Face.

Instead of entering the Rose Window when the location is Behind the Clock Face:
	try going outside instead;

Instead of exiting when the location is Behind the Clock Face:
	try going outside instead;

After going outside from Behind the Clock Face:
	say "I crawl over to the [as a noun]door[end as] and slip through...[paragraph break]";
	end the chapter;
	continue the action;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Rose Window

Understand "door", "glass", "pane", "pane of glass", "little/small door", "blue", "sky" as rose window when the location is Behind the Clock Face or the location is the Rafters.

Rule for printing the description of the rose window when in Behind the Clock Face:
	say "Jewelled light blinds me. Only the square of blue sky - the [as a noun]door[end as] - looks safe. But that's the way the [as a noun]Figure[end as] went, so I know it's not...";

Book 5 - The Roofs of the City

Cathedral Roof is a region. The Minute Hand, Parapet, Buttress are in Cathedral Roof.

Roofs of the City is a region. Rooftop 1, Rooftop 2,  Rooftop 3, Rooftop 4, Rooftop 5, Sloping Roofs, Weather Station, Observation Platform are in the Roofs of the City.

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-sky when in the Roofs of the City:
	if TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired:
		say "Somewhere up there is the [as a noun]Figure[end as] in his [as a noun]ornithopter[end as].";
	else:
		say "The sky is gathering with clouds."

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-clouds when in the Roofs of the City:
	say "The skies are darkening. Growing grey."

Part 0 - the Figure

After looking in the Minute Hand:
	move the escaping Figure to the Cathedral Roof;

After looking in Rooftop 1:
	move the escaping Figure to the Roofs of the City;

Rule for printing the description of the escaping Figure when in Roofs of the City and TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] is still beating its heavy bulk up into the sky."

Rule for printing the description of the escaping Figure when in Roofs of the City:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] is getting away. I've got to stop him somehow!"

Instead of shouting at the escaping Figure in Roofs of the City:
	say "Save your breath for running and jumping, Wren, that seems to be what's needed around here!"

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in Roofs of the City:
	say "[one of]Problem is, I'm not sure which way to turn any more[or]Maybe if I look around I'll find some clue[stopping]."

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure in Roofs of the City when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired:
	say "He's escaped in an [as a noun]ornithopter[end as]! Either I <Italic>find</Italic> a way of following him or I give up here in despair."

[Added separate backdrop for these instead. ]
[Understand "ornithopter", "helipcopter" as the escaping Figure when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired.]


Rule for printing the description of the escaping Figure when in Roofs of the City and balloon spotted is true:
	say "The [as a noun]Figure[end as] is a dot in the sky to the south. I need to get up there!";

Part 1 - Minute Hand

Minute Hand is a room, exterior, outside from Behind the Clock Face. "It's a good thing I'm good with heights, because this is the highest I've ever been. My back's pressed against the cool [as a noun]glass[end as] of the [as a noun]rose window[end as], and I'm standing on a wide metal [as a noun]girder[end as]. The [as a noun]<Italic>long</Italic> hand[end as]. The one that tells you minutes. Luckily it's between the III and the IV - almost level. For now.[paragraph break]And stretched out before me is the most incredible view: the whole of the [as a noun]city[end as] of [as a noun]St Philip[end as], a mess of buildings like it washed up when the rivers flooded. Only the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] looks like it was actually built instead of dropped."

Instead of going inside from the Minute Hand:
	try entering the clock face door.

Instead of making to leave when in the Minute Hand:
	try jumping.

Before going up when in the Minute Hand:
	say "If you want me to jump, just say so..." instead.

Instead of going nowhere when in the Minute Hand:
	try jumping.

Instead of approaching the escaping Figure when in the Minute Hand:
	say "I can't begin to understand how he got there..."

Index map with Minute Hand mapped east of Behind the Clock Face.

After looking in Minute Hand when SE_MINHAND0 is unfired:
	change the current script to { SE_MINHAND0, SE_MINHAND1, SE_MINHAND2, SE_MINHAND3 };
	continue the action;

SE_MINHAND0 is a scripted event. 

SE_MINHAND1 is a scripted event. The display text is "There's a parapet of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] a good way below, but no ladders, chains or cogs this time. But somehow – that's where the [as a noun]Figure[end as] is, looking my way, almost as though wondering if he should stay to help. But he doesn't, and then he's gone.".

SE_MINHAND2 is a scripted event. The display text is "The metal underfoot shivers a little...".

SE_MINHAND3 is a scripted event. The display text is "From behind the [as a noun]window[end as] there's an enormous groan like a giant waking up, and then the [as a noun]hand[end as] underneath me simply disappears. Five-eighteen, Wren. Too steep to stop myself from sliding...[paragraph break]Woomph!".

After firing SE_MINHAND3:
	move the player to the Parapet;

Instead of going down in Minute Hand:
	try jumping.

Instead of jumping in Minute Hand:
	say "If the [as a noun]Figure[end as] can do it, then so can I. And if the [as a noun]Figure[end as] can <Italic>survive</Italic> it, well, maybe I can too... I step off the [as a noun]metal bar[end as]...";
	change the current script to {};
	move the player to the Parapet;
[	say "No chance. It's too far. I'd have my neck in pieces before my feet hit the stone.";]

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Clock face / Rose Window

Rule for printing the description of the rose window when in Minute Hand:
	say "The gigantic [as a noun]clock face[end as]. It has no handholds, no rungs. Nothing.";

Instead of entering the rose window when in Minute Hand:	
	try entering the clock face door instead.

Section 2 - City View

A thing called the mh_distant_city_view is scenery, privately-named, in Minute Hand. "I can see the whole [as a noun]city[end as], from the [as a noun]River Tempus[end as] on side to the quicker [as a noun]River Fugit[end as] on the other. It all seems small enough to pick up and fix – but only if I reached a little further out..." The printed name is "city".

Understand "city", "view", "whole", "river", "rivers", "tempus", "fugit", "building", "buildings" as the mh_distant_city_view.

Instead of doing something when the mh_distant_city_view is physically involved: say "The [as a noun]city[end as] is spread out before me like a painting, but it's far, far away."


Section 3 - Minute hand

An iron bar is scenery, in Minute Hand. "The [as a noun]minute hand[end as] is a thick bar of iron about the size of one of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] pews. But it's also smooth, and steep, and not a safe place to stay..."

Understand "minute", "hand", "girder", "long", "metal", "underfoot" as the iron bar.
The printed name of the iron bar is "minute hand".

Instead of dropping the iron bar:
	try jumping instead;

