Part 4 - Rooftop 1 (Lightning Rods)

A rooftop-area is a kind of room. A rooftop-area is usually exterior. The printed name of a rooftop-area is "Rooftop".

After facing towards a rooftop-area:
	say "The rooftops continue towards [the noun].";

The Rooftop 1 is a exterior rooftop-area. "[one of]If the [as a noun]city[end as] I saw from the clock-face is a sprawl then its [as a noun]roofs[end as] are worse: sloping and slanting this way and that, a mess of slate and lead, like someone kicked over a house of cards. Some parts look flat and others impossibly steep, or blocked with tall [as a noun]chimneys[end as], extra walls or sharp lightning [as a noun]rods[end as] plugged into [as a noun]vegetable patches[end as] way below.[or]The [as a noun]roofs[end as] are a mess of [as a noun]chimneys[end as], [as a noun]lightning rods[end as], ways forward and sudden sharp drops.[stopping][paragraph break]From here, I could scramble down to the south, or hop across small gaps to the southeast or southwest. The Cathedral itself is back east."

Instead of making to leave in Rooftop 1:
	try going east.

Instead of going down in Rooftop 1:
	say "The [as a noun]street[end as] is too far to fall!"

Instead of going east in Rooftop 1:
	say "The [as a noun]buttress[end as] is too steep to climb back.";

Instead of jumping in Rooftop 1:
	try going down instead;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Chimneys & Rods

Some chimneys are a backdrop, in Rooftop 1, Rooftop 3, Rooftop 4, Sloping Roofs. "Who would have thought people kept so much rubbish up here, hidden out of sight? This isn't <Italic>precision engineering</Italic>, all these [as a noun]chimneys[end as], this is a <Italic>great big mess of stuff</Italic>. It's not Right or Proper at all." Understand "chimney", "stuff", "tall chimneys" as the chimneys.

Instead of attacking or pushing or taking the chimneys:
	say "I couldn't pull this stuff apart, the [as a noun]lightning rods[end as] and [as a noun]chimneys[end as] are all solid – even if they are a mess.";

Instead of climbing or entering the chimneys when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired:
	say "I can't get as high as [as a noun]Figure[end as] did that way."

Instead of climbing or entering the chimneys:
	say "They'd probably fall over."

Does the player mean doing something with the chimneys: it is very unlikely.

Section 1b - Rooftops

Some tiled rooftops are a backdrop, floor-substitute, in Roofs of the City. Understand "roofs", "roof" , "rooves", "tile", "tiles", "gutter", "tiled", "gutters", "level", "levels", "rooftop" as the rooftops. "The [as a noun]roofs[end as] are a mismatch of [as a noun]tiles[end as] and [as a noun]levels[end as] and different directions. At any moment I might be tipped off into the [as a noun]street[end as]!"

Instead of taking the tiled rooftops:
	say "I can't go wandering off with the [as a noun]rooftops[end as], can I?"

Does the player mean doing something with the tiled rooftops: it is unlikely.
Does the player mean jumping off the tiled rooftops: it is likely.
Does the player mean getting off the tiled rooftops: it is likely.

Instead of jumping off or getting off the tiled rooftops:
	try going down instead.




Section 2 - Lightning Rods

Some lightning rods are a backdrop, in the Roofs of the City. "The [as a noun]lightning rods[end as] run straight into gardens and [as a noun]vegetables patches[end as]. Something to do with the rattling of the metal feeding the plants. I don't think I understood it when I heard it explained to me."

Instead of touching or taking the lightning rods:
	say "Touch that and you might burn: the vibrations can be that strong. Especially in thunderstorms."

Understand "rod", "vegetable patches", "patch/patches", "rods", "lightning rods", "lightning rod" as the lightning rods.

Section 3 - streets

Some streets below are a backdrop, in Roofs of the City. "There are [as a noun]streets[end as] below on all sides, fairly quiet now as the evening draws in." Understand "street" as the streets below.

Instead of doing something when the streets below are physically involved:
	say "I'm not at [as a noun]street[end as]-level and I plan to <Italic>stay</Italic> that way."

Understand "alley", "alleys", "alleyway", "alleyways", "alley-ways", "alley-way" as the streets below when the location is not Rooftop 4.

Instead of throwing something at the streets:
	say "I'd have the City Guard out with bows and arrows if I started doing that."

Part 5 - Rooftop 2 (Fallen Chimney)

The Rooftop 2 is a rooftop-area, southwest of Rooftop 1. "[one of]This [as a noun]roof[end as] is almost flat, which is good, because there's empty space to the north and west of it. I could slip south, through the remains of a [as a noun]brick chimney[end as] collapsed into a whole heap of [as a noun]bricks[end as]. It seems to have been barged out the way to make room for a flat wide [as a noun]turret-top[end as], on top of which is a glossy [as a noun]platform[end as] with a brass [as a noun]railing[end as]. Overlapping [as a noun]roofs[end as] create a second path to the northeast.[or]A corner of a [as a noun]roof[end as], with nothing to north or west. There's a safe way back northeast. South is a fallen-down [as a noun]brick chimney[end as] topped by a big glossy [as a noun]platform[end as] that's elbowed itself some more space up here.[stopping]"

Instead of jumping in Rooftop 2:
	say "The [as a noun]platform[end as]'s not that high, but it's too high to jump up and grab it.";

Instead of going down in Rooftop 2: 
	say "It's too far to fall to the [as a noun]street[end as] below."

Instead of making to leave in Rooftop 2:
	try going northeast.

