	try taking the huge chunk of brick;

Instead of climbing or jumping on or entering the fallen chimney:
	say "[one of]I climb halfway up the [as a noun]brick[end as], but then skitter down again.[or]The [as a noun]chimney[end as] has fallen into a huge [as a noun]pile[end as] of [as a noun]rubble[end as] and sections of wall, pretty much unclimbable unless I was a cat. If I was on the [as a noun]platform[end as], I could probably slide down it safely enough.[stopping]".


Instead of attacking the fallen chimney with something:
	redirect the action from the fallen chimney to the mortar, and try that instead;

Instead of scraping the fallen chimney with something:
	redirect the action from the fallen chimney to the mortar, and try that instead;

Instead of slicing the fallen chimney with something:
	redirect the action from the fallen chimney to the mortar, and try that instead;


Section 4 - Huge Chunk

A huge chunk of brick is a heavy thing. The description is "A[if the item described is held] stupid irritating miserable no-good wind-down cockeye[end if] heap of [as a noun]bricks[end as], almost too heavy for me to carry at all."

Understand "bricks" as the huge chunk of brick.

Rule for printing the drop event of the huge chunk of brick:
	say "I can't keep carrying the [as a noun]bricks[end as] as well. I put them down.[paragraph break]";

Rule for printing the take event of the huge chunk of brick:
	say "I can just about pick it up. It's heavy, though... worse than carrying both [as a noun]Drake[end as] and [as a noun]Calvin[end as]'s laundry baskets![paragraph break]";


Section 5 - Mortar

Some mortar is scenery, not plural-named, in Rooftop 2. "If all the [as a noun]mortar[end as] in these buildings is as dry and crumbled as the [as a noun]mortar[end as] in the broken [as a noun]chimney[end as], then I'd better tread carefully. A curious pigeon could scrape this stuff away."

The mortar can be firm or loose. The mortar is firm.

Understand "crumbled", "crumbling", "dry" as the mortar.

Definition: a thing is scrapable if it is the knife or it is the lucky clock key or it is the small gear.

Instead of attacking or scraping or taking the mortar:
	say "My fingers are too big – and a bit too soft, even after all the cleaning I've done – to break the [as a noun]mortar[end as]. I'll need to use a tool of some sort."

Instead of slicing the mortar with something:
	try scraping the mortar with the second noun.

Instead of scraping the mortar with something scrapable:
	now the mortar is loose;
	say "Using the [if the second noun is the knife][as a noun]knife[end as] point [else]edge of the [second noun] (and wearing it down horribly!) [end if]I work some of the [as a noun]mortar[end as] loose, powder some more, and free up a small [as a noun]chunk[end as] of [as a noun]brick[end as] that I can probably just about carry.";

Instead of attacking the mortar with something:
	try scraping the mortar with the second noun instead;

Part 6 - Sloping Roofs

The Sloping Roofs is a rooftop-area, south of Rooftop 1. "[one of]It's hard to keep my bearings here: below, several [as a noun]streets[end as] are coming together and their overhanging [as a noun]roofs[end as] – good for keeping the rain off – create a patchwork of [as a noun]tiles[end as], [as a noun]chimneys[end as], gaps and different [as a noun]levels[end as]. I can scramble up a few [as a noun]levels[end as] to the north, or I could probably slip away west and south, but I might get turned around in either of those directions.[or]Several [as a noun]roofs[end as] meet here in a mess of [as a noun]tiles[end as]. East is impassable, but I could climb roof[as a noun]tiles[end as] to north, west or south.[stopping]"

The printed name of the Sloping Roofs is "Sloping Roofs". [rather than "rooftop"]

Section - Bendy directions

South of Rooftop 2 is west of Sloping Roofs. 
South of Sloping Roofs is west of Rooftop 4. 
[West of Sloping Roofs is Rooftop 2.
West of Rooftop 4 is Sloping Roofs.]


Index map with Sloping Roofs mapped west of Rooftop 3.

Section - Moving in the Sloping Roofs

Instead of going down in Sloping Roofs: 
	say "There's no way down from here, I'm in the middle of a meeting of [as a noun]roofs[end as]."

Instead of jumping in Sloping Roofs:
	try going down instead.

Instead of making to leave in Sloping Roofs:
	say "Which way - north, east or south?"

Instead of going east in Sloping Roofs:
	say "[one of]East is impassable[or]When I said impassable, I meant, I'm at an impasse[stopping]."

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Chimneys & Rods

Part 7 - Rooftop 3 (Pipe and Board)

The Rooftop 3 is a rooftop-area, southeast of Rooftop 1. "[one of]Half of this [as a noun]roof[end as] has collapsed, and whoever was repairing it clearly ran out of money only partway into the job. A [as a noun]pile[end as] of [as a noun]construction materials[end as] has been left behind[pipes-and-boards]. Some look like they could easily fall off the [as a noun]roof[end as] onto the [as a noun]street[end as] to the east. Safer ground is a short hop down to the northwest, and a bit of a scramble to the southwest.[or]This [as a noun]roof[end as] is half-repaired and littered with [as a noun]construction materials[end as][pipes-and-boards]. There's a [as a noun]street[end as] to the east, but I could inch along the [as a noun]roof[end as] to the northwest or southwest.[stopping]"

To say pipes-and-boards:
	if the pile supports the length of piping or the pile supports the solid wooden board,
		say " -";
	if the pile supports the length of piping,
		say " [as a noun]pipes[end as]";
	if the pile supports the length of piping and the pile supports the solid wooden board,
		say " and";
	if the pile supports the solid wooden board,
		say " [as a noun]boards[end as]";
	if the pile supports the length of piping or the pile supports the solid wooden board,
		say ", that kind of thing";

Instead of going east in Rooftop 3: say "The [as a noun]street[end as] is a little too far below for me to safely fall."

Instead of going down in Rooftop 3: 
	try going east instead.

Instead of jumping in Rooftop 3:
	try going east instead.

Instead of making to leave in Rooftop 3:
	say "Which way - northwest or southwest?"


