Section 3 - Wooden board

A solid wooden board is a heavy supporter, portable, on the pile. The description is "A solid wooden [as a noun]board[end as], about seven feet long."

The printed name is "board".

Understand "plank", "long", "seven foot", "seven feet" as the solid wooden board.

Rule for writing a paragraph about the solid wooden board:
	say "Lying on the [as a noun]tiles[end as] is a length of wooden [as a noun]board[end as]";
	if the length of piping is in the location:
		say " and a [length of piping]";	
	say "."

Rule for printing the drop event for the solid wooden board:
	say "The [as a noun]board[end as] is too heavy to carry alongside anything else. It slaps down onto the roof by my feet.[paragraph break]";

Rule for printing the take event for the solid wooden board:
	say "I grab one end of the [as a noun]board[end as], ready to drag it along behind me.[paragraph break]";

Instead of putting something on the solid wooden board:
	say "That wouldn't make carrying [the noun] any easier, would it?";

After going when the solid wooden board is carried:
	say "[one of]I drag the [as a noun]board[end as] along behind me[or]I pull the [as a noun]board[end as] along[at random].";
	continue the action;

Instead of pulling the solid wooden board:
	if the solid wooden board is not carried:
		try taking the solid wooden board instead;
	say "That's exactly what I am doing."

After looking when the solid wooden board is carried:
	say "I'm still holding this wooden [as a noun]board[end as]."

Section 4 - Pipe

The length of piping is a heavy thing, on the pile. The description is "A curious bit of metal [as a noun]piping[end as]: it's about ten feet long, with two right-angle bends at either end. Shaped like a gigantic staple, you might say."

The printed name is "pipe".

Instead of entering a length of piping:
	say "It's only a handspan wide. I'm not a real Wren, you know!"

Instead of climbing the length of piping:
	say "It's doesn't go anywhere.";

Instead of inflating the length of piping:
	say "I blow across the top. It makes a deep noise, like a groan."

Rule for printing the name of length of piping when taking inventory:
	say "long and cumbersome length of [as a noun]piping[end as]";

Rule for writing a paragraph about the length of piping:
	say "Lying on the [as a noun]tiles[end as] is a length of [as a noun]piping[end as]";
	if the solid wooden board is in the location:
		say " and a [solid wooden board]";	
	say "."

After looking when the length of piping is carried:
	say "I'm still holding this length of [as a noun]piping[end as]."

Understand "pipe", "curious", "bit", "long", "ten foot", "ten feet", "bent", "bend", "bends", "gigantic", "staple" as the length of piping.

Rule for printing the drop event for the length of piping:
	say "This [as a noun]pipe[end as]'s too much to handle as well. I put it down.[paragraph break]";

After going when the length of piping is carried:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]pipe[end as] skitters along behind me[or]The [as a noun]pipe[end as] booms as it rattles along[or]I drag the [as a noun]pipe[end as] along behind me[at random].";
	continue the action;

Instead of searching the length of piping:
	say "The other end's in there."

Instead of pulling the length of piping:
	if the length of piping is not carried:
		try taking the length of piping instead;
	say "That's exactly what I am doing."

Part 8 - Rooftop 4 (Ornithopter & Tarp)

Chapter 1 - Description

The Rooftop 4 is a rooftop-area, south of Sloping Roofs, southwest of Rooftop 3. North of Rooftop 4 is nowhere. "[one of]I'm on the south side of a group of buildings, with other [as a noun]roofs[end as] I could scramble onto east, west, and northeast. To the south, there's a low wall and then a [as a noun]gap[end as] - an [as a noun]alleyway[end as] - before the next [as a noun]roof[end as] along. And that [as a noun]roof[end as] looks interesting: covered in [as a noun]machinery[end as], though it's hard to say for sure.[or]There are other [as a noun]roofs[end as] east, west and northeast. To the south is a low wall, a twelve-foot [as a noun]gap[end as], and then another [as a noun]roof[end as] that's covered in [as a noun]machinery[end as].[stopping]"

Instead of jumping in Rooftop 4:
	if the plank bridge is in location or the wooden board is in the notch:
		say "The [as a noun]street[end as] is too far to fall." instead;
	try going south instead.

Instead of going down in Rooftop 4: 
	try jumping instead;

Instead of making to leave in Rooftop 4:
	say "Which way - east, west, or northeast?";

Instead of facing south when the plank bridge is on-stage and in Rooftop 4:
	say "There's my handy [as a noun]bridge[end as] over the [as a noun]alley[end as]."

Instead of facing south in Rooftop 4:
	say "There's a twelve foot [as a noun]gap[end as] over an [as a noun]alley[end as], and then a [as a noun]roof[end as] covered in [as a noun]machinery[end as]."
	
Section 2 - Wisp of hot smoke

Every turn when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4B is fired [ don't get in the way of the Figure leaving ] and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds and the chimney pipe is in the Rooftop 5 [ turn messages off when we've elongated the chimney ] and not looking and not going [ try to avoid long text streams ]:
	say "[one of]A breath of [as a noun]hot air[end as] touches me from the east.[or]Steam drifts over the [as a noun]rooftops[end as] from the east.[or]A [as a noun]smell[end as] of wet [as a noun]smoke[end as] drifts in from the east.[cycling]";

The hot_wisp is privately-named, scenery in Rooftop 4. The printed name is "wisp of steam". Understand "wisp of", "steam", "smoke", "hot", "air", "wet", "smell", "smell of", "breath", "breath of" as the hot_wisp. The description is "A [as a noun]breath[end as] of [as a noun]hot air[end as], dragging itself across the [as a noun]rooftops[end as] from the east."

Instead of smelling when the hot_wisp is visible:
	say "I can smell [as a noun]hot air[end as], blowing in from the east."
	
Instead of doing something when the hot_wisp is physically involved and the action is physical:
	say "It's just a breath of air, nothing more."


Chapter 2 - Event on entry

TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is a trigger. 
TRIG_ROOFTOP_4B is a trigger. 

Rule for firing TRIG_ROOFTOP_4:
	say "Something moves high above, and in a grey flash the [as a noun]Figure[end as] is before me. He nods once. 'Relentless,' he remarks. 'I admire your courage. Your persistence. You could almost be a machine.' I'm barely a foot away, and I still can't see his face. Nothing but that sleek grey suit.[paragraph break]'But this is where I leave you.' He whips away the [as a noun]tarpaulin[end as], to reveal an [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] - something I've only seen in lithographs before. Pausing only to wind the key beneath one of the craft's gigantic wings, he leaps inside and in a moment, has taken to the sky! But the [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] is slow-moving: like a drifting leaf, drifting upwards...";

Rule for firing TRIG_ROOFTOP_4B:
	say "So, what now? There's no way I'm letting him get away; I'm going to be in so much trouble after all I've done, I'll need something to show for it. But I can't fly either. There's got to be a way. Something I can do. If the Universe is clockwork the way the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says it is, then it can't have left me stranded, can it?"

After looking in Rooftop 4 when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is unfired:
	fire TRIG_ROOFTOP_4;

Every turn when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 was fired and TRIG_ROOFTOP_4B is unfired:
	fire TRIG_ROOFTOP_4B;

Rule for choosing notable locale objects of Rooftop 4 when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is unfired:
	set the locale priority of the notch to 1;
	set the locale priority of the large tarp to 2;

Section	2b - Ornithopter object

The ornithopter is a glimpse backdrop in Roofs of the City. Understand "ornithopter" as the ornithopter when TRIG_ROOFTOP_4 is fired. "The [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] is still large in the sky. It moves like a slow, fat moth - with the [as a noun]Figure[end as] aboard!" Understand "helicopter" as the ornithopter.

Instead of approaching or entering the ornithopter: 
	say "I need a way to get myself airborne!"

Instead of doing something when the ornithopter is physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] is moving through the [as a noun]clouds[end as]!"


Chapter 3 - Objects


Section 2 - Notch

A notch is a container, fixed in place, in Rooftop 4. The description is "The ['][as a noun]notch[end as]['] is a [as a noun]gap[end as] in the [as a noun]brickwork[end as] of the low [as a noun]south wall[end as] overlooking the [as a noun]alley[end as][one of]. Maybe the missing bricks fell out down below: that would have been pretty bad for whoever was below![or].[stopping]".

Rule for writing a paragraph about the notch:
	say "[if the notch contains the solid wooden board]There's a wooden [as a noun]board[end as] set into the [as a noun]notch[end as] in the [as a noun]wall[end as] and pushed out over the [as a noun]street[end as].[else]The low [as a noun]south wall[end as] is missing a few bricks creating a [as a noun]gap[end as] – well, I'll call it a <Italic>[as a noun]notch[end as]</Italic>.[end if]". 

Rule for writing a paragraph about the notch when the plank bridge is on-stage:
	say "The wooden [as a noun]board[end as] makes a nice plank [as a noun]bridge[end as], weighed at this end with [as a noun]bricks[end as]."

Understand "low", "south", "wall", "gap", "wall", "brickwork", "brick-work" as the notch.

Instead of putting something on the notch:
	try inserting the noun into the second noun;

Instead of putting something on the solid wooden board when the notch contains the solid wooden board:
	try inserting the noun into the notch instead;

After inserting the solid board into the notch:
	say "The [as a noun]board[end as] slides in – not perfectly, it's no clockwork, but it'll do. The [as a noun]plank[end as] extends out over the [as a noun]alley[end as] to the south, almost all the way!";

Instead of inserting the huge chunk of brick into the notch when the notch contains the solid wooden board:
	remove the solid wooden board from play;
	remove the huge chunk of brick from play;
	say "I unload the [as a noun]bricks[end as] onto the end of the [as a noun]board[end as], weighing it down nicely.";
	move the plank bridge to Rooftop 4;

Instead of inserting the huge chunk of brick into the notch:
	say "I don't have the tools to repair this [as a noun]wall[end as], and anyway, whyever would I want to?";

Instead of inserting the length of piping into the notch when the notch contains the solid wooden board:
	move the length of piping to the location;
	say "I dump the [as a noun]pipe[end as] on the end of the board to weigh it down. It stays for a moment, and then slides away."

Instead of inserting the length of piping into the notch:
	say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as] would probably reach from this [as a noun]roof[end as] over the [as a noun]alley[end as] to the next [as a noun]roof[end as], but I wouldn't like to try and walk across it. I'm a child, not an ant!"

Instead of inserting something that is not the solid wooden board into the notch:
	say "That wouldn't do me any good, would it?"

Instead of inserting something into the notch when the solid wooden board is in the notch:
	say "That wouldn't weigh the board down by much."

Instead of inserting the large tarp into the notch when the solid wooden board is in the notch:
	say "The [as a noun]tarpaulin[end as] is surprisingly light - not enough to counterbalance the board."

Instead of fastening something to the solid wooden board when the solid wooden board is in the notch:
	try inserting the noun into the notch instead.

Instead of entering or jumping over or climbing the notch:
	if the wooden board is in the notch or the plank bridge is on-stage:
		say "It's a twelve-foot [as a noun]gap[end as]. I can't jump that!" instead;
	try going south instead.


Section 1 - Tarp

A large tarp is in Rooftop 4. "[one of]A large [as a noun]tarp[end as] is thrown over something in the middle of this space[or]A large [as a noun]tarp[end as] lies discarded here[stopping]." The description is "[one of]This is expensive stuff: a fabric that's lighter and stronger and less itchy than the stuff they make my bed-sheets from, the way sunlight is different than the beady glow in a rat's eye. The [as a noun]Figure[end as] must be pretty wealthy to leave something like this behind! Perhaps he meant it as a present: something to buy me off the chase.[paragraph break]Not going to happen. I'm going to catch him, stop him, save the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] and go collect my reward from the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as].[or]This is expensive stuff: a fabric that's light and strong. The [as a noun]Figure[end as] must be pretty wealthy to leave something like this behind![stopping]"

Understand "tarpaulin", "sheet", "roll", "roll of" as the tarp. The printed name of the tarp is "tarp".

Instead of touching the tarp:
	say "It's smooth as metal."

Instead of wearing the tarp:
	say "It's just a big square of [as a noun]tarpaulin[end as], there are no holes or anything."

Instead of slicing the tarp with the knife:
	say "The [as a noun]tarp[end as] is pretty tough: the [as a noun]knife[end as] probably couldn't get through it (and I don't see a reason to try!)"

After taking the tarp:
	say "Cumbersome, but that said – it rolls up pretty small!";

Rule for printing the name of the large tarp when taking inventory:
	say "[as a noun]roll of tarp[end as]"
	
Instead of opening the tarp:
	say "Unrolling it would make it harder to carry, and not much else. Right now it fits neatly under my arm."

Instead of opening the tarp when the elongated chimney pipe is wrapped:
	try taking the tarp instead.

