Section 3 - 12-foot gap

A twelve-foot gap is a door, scenery, privately-named, open, not openable, south of Rooftop 4. "The [as a noun]alley[end as] below is far enough that a fall might knock my knees from their sprockets, and it's too wide to jump.". Through the twelve-foot gap is the weather station. Understand "alley", "alley way", "alley-way", "alleyway", "twelve-foot gap", "twelve foot gap", "12 foot gap" as the twelve-foot gap. 

After going through the twelve-foot gap:
	say "I inch out over my makeshift [as a noun]bridge[end as]. The weight of the [as a noun]bricks[end as] shudders – if it slips off that board then I'm dead, in a second – but it doesn't. And from the end, it's a short <Italic>spring</Italic> to the [as a noun]roof[end as] beyond.";
	continue the action;

Instead of going through the twelve-foot gap when the notch contains the solid wooden board:
	say "[one of]I inch a little way out on the [as a noun]board[end as], but it quickly starts to tip over, into the [as a noun]street[end as]. I freeze, and inch my way back. Without something to weigh the other end down, this [as a noun]bridge[end as] is useless![or]I'll need to weigh the other end down with something first.[stopping]";

Instead of going through the twelve-foot gap when the plank bridge is off-stage:
	say "It's a twelve-foot [as a noun]gap[end as]. I can't jump that!";
	
Last instead of going through the twelve-foot gap when the player is carrying the length of piping:
	say "With this [as a noun]pipe[end as] in tow I'd be too heavy to get across the plank [as a noun]bridge[end as][one of]. (Even though it might help my balance!)[or].[stopping]"

Instead of climbing or entering or standing on the wooden board when the wooden board is in the notch:
	try going south.

Instead of jumping over the twelve-foot gap:
	if the wooden board is in the notch or the plank bridge is on-stage:
		say "It's a twelve-foot [as a noun]gap[end as]. I can't jump that!" instead;
	try entering the twelve-foot gap;

Section 4 - Plank bridge

A plank bridge is scenery. "The wooden [as a noun]board[end as] passes over the [as a noun]notch[end as] in the brick [as a noun]wall[end as]. The near end is held down by the weight of the [as a noun]bricks[end as]."

Understand "brick", "bricks", "huge", "chunk", "of", "solid", "wooden", "board", "long", "seven foot", "seven feet" as the plank bridge.

Instead of taking the plank bridge:
	say "No thanks. I never want to lift that weight again.";

Instead of entering or climbing or approaching the plank bridge:
