Section 1 - Chimney Pipe

A thing can be a hot air vent. A thing is seldom a hot air vent.

A chimney pipe is fixed in place, hot air vent, in Rooftop 5. "By the [as a noun]alley[end as] is a squat [as a noun]iron chimney[end as] that's belching out fat [as a noun]slugs[end as] of [as a noun]smoke[end as]. The kitchen down there must be getting ready for a feast!"

Understand "squat", "iron", "smoke", "slugs", "steam", "hot air" as the chimney pipe.

The description is "A narrow [as a noun]iron chimney[end as]. [as a noun]Smoke[end as] and [as a noun]steam[end as] puff out of it. This might not even be a kitchen, it might be one of those [as a noun]steam[end as] boilers the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] sometimes mutters about."

Instead of smelling a hot air vent:
	say "The [as a noun]smoke[end as] is hot, but not greasy."

Instead of putting the length of piping on the chimney pipe:
	remove the length of piping from play;
	remove the chimney pipe from play;
	remove the hot_wisp from play;
	move the elongated chimney to Rooftop 5;
	say "With a bit of hefting and hauling – and a couple of burns on my arms – I get the [as a noun]pipe[end as] slotted over the [as a noun]chimney[end as]. The new, longer [as a noun]chimney[end as] points north, and starts getting hot and belching out [as a noun]steam[end as] pretty quickly.";


