Part 10 - Observation Platform

Chapter 1 - Description

The Observation Platform is an exterior room. "[one of]I'm standing on a [as a noun]platform[end as] overlooking a [as a noun]roof[end as] covered in broken bricks to the north. A flight of metal [as a noun]steps[end as] lead down to the southeast, back to the [as a noun]weather station[end as]. A brass [as a noun]railing[end as] runs around the edge to stop anyone falling (I could use one of those for my bedroom).[or]The railed [as a noun]platform[end as] overlooks a littered [as a noun]roof[end as] to the north and [as a noun]steps[end as] lead southeast back to the [as a noun]weather station[end as].[stopping]"

North from the Observation Platform is Rooftop 2.
Down from the Observation Platform is the Weather Station.

Instead of jumping in Observation Platform:
	try going north;

After going north from the Observation Platform:
	say "I swing myself over the [as a noun]railing[end as], and land neatly on the [as a noun]roof[end as] below.";
	try looking;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Brass Railing

The OP_brass-railing is scenery, privately-named, in the Observation Platform. "The brass [as a noun]rail[end as] is well-polished and set about shoulder-height. I could easily climb over it to the [as a noun]roof[end as] below." The printed name is "brass railing".

Understand "rail", "brass", "railing" as the OP_brass-railing.

Instead of climbing or jumping over or entering the OP_brass-railing:
	try going north;

Instead of taking or pulling or attacking the OP_brass-railing:
	say "It's pretty firmly built into the [as a noun]platform[end as] (and if I could get it free, the whole place might unspiral like a sprung watch.)";


Section 2 - Northern Roof

The northern roof is scenery, in the Observation Platform. "The [as a noun]roof[end as] to the north is gently sloped and lead-lined. It's been littered with clumps of brick by whoever built this tower."

Understand "rooftop", "roof-top", "roof top", "sloped", "lead", "lead-lined", "lined", "littered" as the northern roof.

Instead of jumping on or entering or approaching the northern roof:
	try going north;

Section 2b - Platform

The ob_platform is privately-named, floor-substitute, scenery, in the Observation Platform. Understand "platform", "observation" as the ob_platform. The printed name is "platform". "The [as a noun]platform[end as] is surrounded by a [as a noun]rail[end as] and fitted with a large, rather fine [as a noun]telescope[end as]."

Instead of getting off or jumping off the ob_platform:
	say "I could climb the [as a noun]steps[end as] back to the [as a noun]weather station[end as] - or I could try my luck jumping the [as a noun]railing[end as]!"

Section 3 - Glimpse of Weather Station

The glimpse of the weather station is a glimpse backdrop in the Observation Platform. It identifies the zephyrgraph. The printed name is "weather station". "The [as a noun]weather station[end as] is southeast of the [as a noun]platform[end as], down some [as a noun]steps[end as]." Understand "step", "steps", "weather", "station" as the glimpse of the weather station. Instead of entering or climbing the glimpse of the weather station, try going down.

Chapter 3 - Telescope Mechanism

Section 1 - The Telescope Itself

The telescope is a pointer, eyeable, in the Observation Platform. "At the [as a noun]platform[end as]'s centre is a metal [as a noun]telescope[end as] on a [as a noun]tripod[end as] that points [way of the telescope]. The [as a noun]tripod[end as] has a [as a noun]crank[end as] at its base[one of], much like the ones in the library and the one inside the clock's working. There must be a blacksmith somewhere who does nothing but make these [as a noun]crank[end as]s. I might be able to see him from here, too, since I've got a perfect view of the whole [as a noun]city[end as][or], and the thing is set up to give a great view of the [as a noun]city[end as][stopping]."

The description of the telescope is "The [as a noun]telescope[end as] is made of finely-moulded brass stamped with the crossed lightning of the Weather Guild. [one of]Often accused of non-mechanism – weather's just too temperamental - they tend to build machines that are overly complicated, like the ratchet-and-piston umbrella they sell in the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] Yard market for nine hours a pop. So, the [as a noun]telescope[end as][or]The[stopping] [as a noun]tripod[end as] has a [as a noun]crank[end as] and [as a noun]compass[end as] on the base, which presumably do the business of pointing.[paragraph break]Still, the view must be fantastic. I could look through the [as a noun]telescope[end as] to see. (It's currently pointing [way of the telescope])."

Understand "tripod", "mechanism" as the telescope.

The way of the telescope is east.

Instead of turning the telescope:
	say "No good trying to move it by hand. You can't back a gear against a gasket, as they say."

Instead of turning the telescope forwards: try turning the telescope.
Instead of turning the telescope backwards: try turning the telescope.

Instead of direction-setting the telescope to a direction:
	try turning the telescope;

Observing through the telescope is a rulebook.

Before searching the telescope:
	try looking through the telescope instead.
	
Instead of looking through the telescope:
	consider the observing through the telescope rules;

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points north:
	say "Looking north, [as a noun]St Philip[end as] stretches away to the mountains beyond. [run paragraph on]";

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points west:
	say "The huge [as a noun]clockface[end as] of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] – or a tiny portion of it – fills the eyepiece. [run paragraph on]";

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points northwest or the telescope points southwest:
	say "The [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]'s spires cut the view of the [as a noun]city[end as] beyond. [run paragraph on]";

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points east or the telescope points southeast:
	say "Sunlight sparkles from the flickering surface of the [as a noun]Fugit[end as], running down from the mountains towards the sea, far to the south. [run paragraph on]";

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points northeast:
	say "The [as a noun]Abbey[end as] roof looks weathered and beaten this close up. [run paragraph on]";

Rule for observing through the telescope when the telescope points south:
	change balloon spotted to true;
	say "[one of]At first I think it's a fly on the lens, but when it doesn't move away I realise it's the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s [as a noun]ornithopter[end as]! Flying south, is he? [or]The [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s [as a noun]ornithopter[end as] is clearly visible to the south. [stopping][run paragraph on]";

Balloon spotted is a truth state that varies. Balloon spotted is false.

Last observing through the telescope:
	if balloon spotted is false:
		say "But no sign of the [as a noun]Figure[end as] in this direction.";
	otherwise:
		say paragraph break;

Section 2 - Compass

The compass is a pointer, part of the telescope. The description is "The [as a noun]compass[end as] is a steel [as a noun]pointer[end as] riveted over the engraving of a [as a noun]compass[end as] on a brass [as a noun]disc[end as] at the telescope's base. Currently the [as a noun]pointer[end as] is set to [way of the compass][if the way of the compass is the way of the telescope]; the [as a noun]telescope[end as] points the same way[otherwise], while the [as a noun]telescope[end as] points [way of the telescope][end if]."

Understand "steel", "pointer", "brass", "disc", "engraving", "engraved" as the compass. The printed name is "compass".

The way of the compass is west.

Instead of turning the compass:
	try turning the compass backwards.

Instead of turning the compass backwards:
	try direction-setting the compass to anticlockwise of the way of the compass;

Instead of turning the compass forwards:
	try direction-setting the compass to clockwise of the way of the compass;

Instead of direction-setting the compass to the way of the compass:
	say "The [as a noun]compass[end as] points [way of the compass] already.";

After direction-setting the compass to a direction:
	say "I flip the [as a noun]compass[end as]-[as a noun]pointer[end as] round to [way of the compass][one of]. It moves easily: well-oiled is well-placed, as they say.[or].[stopping]";

Section 3 - Crank

The large crank is part of the telescope. The description is "The large [as a noun]crank[end as] is attached to one leg of the [as a noun]tripod[end as]. There's presumably an Archimedes screw inside or something connecting it to the [as a noun]compass[end as] and the [as a noun]telescope[end as] up top."

Understand "handle" as the large crank.

Instead of turning the large crank when the way of the telescope is the way of the compass:
	say "The [as a noun]crank[end as] won't turn. Must be something wrong with the rest of the mechanism[one of].[or]. Something to do with the [as a noun]compass[end as], maybe?[stopping]";

Instead of turning the large crank:
	now the way of the telescope is the way of the compass;
	say "[one of]Am I turning the [as a noun]crank[end as] or is the [as a noun]crank[end as] turning me? After a few rotations of whichever-it-is, I find I'm on a different side of the tower with both me and [as a noun]telescope[end as] facing [way of the telescope].[or]The [as a noun]crank[end as] turns and I find myself looking [way of the telescope].[or]This time, I turn the [as a noun]crank[end as] and pay attention to what's going on. It's not the [as a noun]telescope[end as] turning – it's the whole tower itself. That's the Weather Guild for you. The only thing that doesn't turn is the [as a noun]compass[end as]. Now we're pointing [way of the telescope], same as it.[or]The [as a noun]tower[end as] rotates so the [as a noun]telescope[end as] is now pointing [way of the telescope].[stopping]";

Section 4 - City View

A thing called the op_distant_city_view is scenery, privately-named, in Observation Platform. "The view isn't quite as incredible as from the minute hand of the clock, but I can still see the jumble of [as a noun]buildings[end as], dipping slightly down as it approaches the [as a noun]River Tempus[end as] to one side, and the [as a noun]River Fugit[end as] on the other." The printed name is "city".

Understand "city", "whole", "river", "rivers", "tempus", "fugit", "building", "buildings" as the op_distant_city_view.

Instead of doing something when the op_distant_city_view is physically involved: say "The [as a noun]city[end as] is a lot, lot bigger than I thought it was - and it's a lot, lot bigger than me."

Part 11 - Weather Station

The Weather Station is an exterior room. "[one of]Good Grease! This roof is like a museum of [as a noun]meters[end as]! [once only][as a noun]Thermometers[end as], [as a noun]barometers[end as], [as a noun]precipitometers[end as] (these are just [as a noun]glass tubes[end as] open to catch the rain) and a [as a noun]zephyrgraph[end as] attached to a flight of metal [as a noun]stairs[end as] that lead up to a [as a noun]platform[end as] to the northwest. I can't see any other way off this [as a noun]roof[end as], although there is a closed [as a noun]hatch[end as] underfoot. The wooden [as a noun]plank[end as] I used to get here is further off than I thought!"

Index map with Weather Station mapped south of Rooftop 4.

