[ only once ]
Rule for firing a fired conversation topic that is clustered with CT_COV_5_MYSELF:
	say "I've already asked about that." instead;

CT_COV_5_MYSELF is clustered with CT_COV_5_DIFFERENCEENGINE, CT_COV_5_ABBEY, CT_COV_5_CATHEDRAL, CT_COV_5_ABBOT, CT_COV_5_FIGURE, CT_COV_5_ARCHBISHOP, CT_COV_5_PEOPLE, CT_COV_5_COVALT, CT_COV_5_RAVENS, CT_COV_5_CLOCKWORK, CT_COV_5_SAINTS, CT_COV_5_SORRY, CT_COV_5_PERPETUUM, CT_COV_3_HERESY, CT_COV_3_SHOP, CT_COV_5_ADDRESS.
.

Rule for choosing the conversation table of Covalt when the state of Covalt is 5:
	change the chosen conversation table to the table of enthusiastic Covalt conversation;

CT_COV_5_MYSELF is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'So what should I do now?' I ask.". The response text is "'Go and find out,' he replies, cheerfully.".

CT_COV_5_DIFFERENCEENGINE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I don't understand about this [as a noun]Engine[end as].'". The response text is "'Of course you don't. Clockwork and High Mathematik all in one. It's like understanding being on fire when you're drowning underwater.' He grimaces. '[as a noun]St. Babbage[end as] – he was a genius. That's how it works. But it's for what it does – that's what you're needing it for. So how doesn't really matter.'".

CT_COV_5_ABBEY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Maybe I should go back to the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]?'". The response text is "'If you've got any sense you'll stay out of there for the next twenty years at least,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says. 'No place of a mind, working chores and all that copying out.'".

CT_COV_5_CATHEDRAL is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What about guarding the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]?'". The response text is "'Design before you do,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] growls. 'No point rushing in and standing around. That won't bring your Uncle back to life, as they say.'".

CT_COV_5_ABBOT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I think the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] was scared,' I say.". The response text is "'Should be,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] smiles, 'with you on his heels. Taking to the air in a [as a noun]balloon[end as]! Mad as a monkey-wrench!'".

CT_COV_5_FIGURE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'How will I catch the [as a noun]Figure[end as]? He's faster – and probably smarter, too...'". The response text is "'Like I've told you. Like this [as a noun]Difference Engine[end as] will tell you. Time for some decent advising, that's what it is. Right now, if you look at it, you've got nothing but my demented ramblings and your own half-baked ideas,' – he holds up a palm – 'no offence, but you're shorter than my navel so you can't be that well-read. So.' He shrugs.".

CT_COV_5_ARCHBISHOP is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Shouldn't we warn the [as a noun]Archibishop[end as]?'". The response text is "'You did already,' he says, impatiently. 'Anyway, if we start trying to do things the Church way we'll be going round and round a clockface when we should be going up in the air.' He grins, suddenly. 'I meant that as a figure of speech, but I guess that's what you did, there, in your [as a noun]balloon[end as]. So fancy that.'".

CT_COV_5_PEOPLE is a conversation topic. The response text is "He won't know them.".

CT_COV_5_COVALT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What will you do?'". The response text is "'Me? Keep my head down and fix my skylight. There's nothing else for me to do. I'm not having me suspected of theft, heresy is quite a charge enough.'".

CT_COV_5_RAVENS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I like your [as a noun]ravens[end as].'". The response text is "'Well, they must like you,' he replies, 'or they'd have had your eyes out by now.'".

CT_COV_5_CLOCKWORK is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'So you know all about clockwork, then.'". The response text is "'My fingers know.' He waves one. It's about the size of a small dog. 'I'm a doer, not so much a thinker. I find things out instead of dreaming them up. That's my way. Half-bothered.'".

CT_COV_5_SAINTS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'You know about the [as a noun]Saints[end as]?'". The response text is "'Inventors, designers. Good, mind. Very good. But morality, well, there's another thing. I don't know such.' He lowers his voice. 'I got my doubts on [as a noun]Babbage[end as], say. And [as a noun]Newton[end as]? They say he lost it once his fame got out and it wound up with him locked in a cell somewhere, raving loony.' He winks. 'But don't tell ‘em I told you when you tell the other sprats, eh?'".

CT_COV_5_SORRY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'I'm sorry about dragging you into this,' I tell him.". The response text is "'Not a grit-grime, don't worry. I'm dragging myself straight back out again the second you leave.'".

CT_COV_5_PERPETUUM is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Is the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] important?'". The response text is "'Well,' and he scratches his chin with a noise like a cart over cobbles. 'Depends. Thing is, that right now, it isn't any use. Just for looking at. Powers itself and nothing else. I think that's right. The universe does its grind and the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] does it other. Or maybe they're related.' He shakes his head. 'I don't know. I get fuzzy about Book VIII of [as a noun]Euclid[end as], you don't want to be asking me.'".

CT_COV_5_ADDRESS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'How do I get to this Counting House?' I ask." The response text is "'That's easy,' he replies - then rattles off a long list of directions I don't remember at all. But I nod and pretend to listen anyway - exactly as though I was back in the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]."

Rule for choosing the conversation topic when we are enquiring of Covalt about something and the state of Covalt is 5:
	change the chosen conversation topic to the conversation corresponding to a hiding place of the second noun in the table of Covalt's Interesting Items;

Chapter 5 - Idle actions

COVALT_COUNTER is a counter. The top end is 5. The internal value is 2.

After speaking with Covalt:
	defer firing COVALT_COUNTER;

After looking during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	[defer firing the counter]
	if the internal value of COVALT_COUNTER is 1,
		change the internal value of COVALT_COUNTER to the top end of COVALT_COUNTER;

Every turn when the player can see Covalt:
	increment COVALT_COUNTER;

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 1 and no conversation topic that is clustered with CT_COV_1_MYSELF is fired:
	say "The [as a noun]huge man[end as] in the doorway is waiting for me to tell him my story.";

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 1:
	say "[one of]'So?' the [as a noun]giant[end as] demands.[or]The big [as a noun]man[end as]'s face is curled up into a growl.[or]'Well? [']Plain yourself,' the [as a noun]giant[end as] demands. 'Make yourself plain.'[or]The [as a noun]giant[end as] shifts his weight, making the floor creak.[or]'How'd you end up here, eh?' he demands.[in random order]";

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 2:
	say "[one of]'So? What's a [as a noun]Figure[end as] to do with you?'[or]'How'd you get started on this?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands. 'Eh?'[or]'What's the story here?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands. 'All the story.'[or]'What's the great plot, eh?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands.[or]'You'd better explain and quick,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] growls.[or]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] swings a little. He's clearly not a patient angry [as a noun]giant[end as].[or]'You tell me what that [as a noun]Figure[end as] was after,' he demands.[in random order]";

[State 3: see Rummaging Puzzle, below.]

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 4:
	say "[one of]'I've got a <Italic>different</Italic> idea forming,' he muses.[or]'We got to do something else,' he murmurs. 'Something <Italic>different</Italic>...'[or]'There's an idea on the tip of my...' he begins, looking puzzled. 'Something. No. <Italic>Different</Italic>...'[or]'It'll come to me, I'm sure,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says.[or][fire CT_COV_4_DIFFERENCE][paragraph break][stopping]";

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 5:
	if examining Covalt:
		now the internal value of the COVALT_COUNTER is the top end of the COVALT_COUNTER;	
	else if the number of entries in the hurry-up script is not 0:
		say entry 1 of the hurry-up script, paragraph break;
		remove entry 1 from the hurry-up script;


The hurry-up script is a list of texts that varies. The hurry-up script is { "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] looks pleased with himself.", "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] is waiting for me to go.", "It's time I got on.", "The [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s out there, right now! I need to hurry up!" }.

When play begins:
	sort the hurry-up script in random order;

Part 2 - Clock Shop

Covalt's Shop Hunt is a scene. Covalt's Shop Hunt begins when the location is the Clock Shop. Covalt's Shop Hunt ends when the location is the Street. 

Understand "find diagram" as a mistake ("[look for diagram]") when Covalt's Shop Hunt is happening and the state of Covalt is 3.
Understand "look for diagram" as a mistake ("[look for diagram]") when Covalt's Shop Hunt is happening and the state of Covalt is 3.
Understand "search for diagram" as a mistake ("[look for diagram]") when Covalt's Shop Hunt is happening and the state of Covalt is 3.
Understand "hunt for diagram" as a mistake ("[look for diagram]") when Covalt's Shop Hunt is happening and the state of Covalt is 3.

To say look for diagram:
	say "[one of]There's lots of places to look - [if location is the Clock Shop]the [as a noun]desk[end as], the [as a noun]shelves[end as], the [as a noun]papers[end as] - I'll just have to start somewhere[else]in the main shop, as well as here[end if][or]You're going to have to think of somewhere to look, I can't see it.[stopping]. "

The sunlight-shadows are scenery, privately-named, in the Clock Shop. The printed name is "sunlight". Understand "sunlight", "shadow", "shadows" as the sunlight-shadows. The description is "[as a noun]Sunlight[end as] makes brass gleams, [as a noun]shadows[end as] are for things to get lost in."

Instead of doing something when the sunlight-shadows are physically involved:
	say "[as a noun]Sunlight[end as] and [as a noun]shadows[end as] are intangible."



Chapter 1 - Description

The Clock Shop is north of Covalt's Bedroom. "[if the state of Covalt is at least 4]The afternoon [as a noun]sunlight[end as] comes in from the [as a noun]door[end as] to the street, casting [as a noun]shadows[end as] across the [as a noun]junk-piles[end as] and [as a noun]scrap[end as].[else][one of]The first thing I see is the [as a noun]door[end as] onto the street, southwest. Escape! But there's nothing to escape from - [as a noun]Covalt[end as] is just a clockmaker and this is his workshop: a tiny room stuffed with [as a noun]springs[end as] and [as a noun]rods[end as] and [as a noun]pistons[end as], the [as a noun]workbench[end as] and [as a noun]shelves[end as] and [as a noun]floor[end as] almost invisible under a layer of [as a noun]brass teeth[end as] and [as a noun]penduluums[end as].[or]When I say I'm in [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s shop I'm mean I'm <Italic>in</Italic> it, knee-deep. It's everywhere. It's got more bits than one of Cook's soups, and there's not a scrap of space that isn't filled with [as a noun]clutter[end as]. It'd be like standing inside an intricate mechanism - if there was any Good Order or Precision at all, which there isn't. Somewhere in this mess is a [as a noun]table[end as], and the [as a noun]door[end as] to the street is southwest, but even that's hung with a rack of [as a noun]shelves[end as].[stopping][end if]"

Instead of making to leave in the Clock Shop: try going southwest.
Instead of going inside in the Clock Shop: try going south.


Chapter 2 - Rummaging puzzle

Section 1 - Examining clutter


After printing the description of a clutter thing (called the junk) that is remembered by Covalt when the state of Covalt is 3:
	say paragraph break;
	have Covalt inspect the junk;
	[ drop the extra line break cause by the last rule in this activity. We don't need it! ]
	now the final-paragraph-break is false;

After printing the description of uninspected clutter thing (called the hiding-place) when the player remembers at least three things and the state of Covalt is 3:
	say " And here's his [as a noun]diagram[end as] too";
	if the hiding-place is the toffee, say ", underneath";
	say ". I pull it out and hand it over to him.[paragraph break]";
	fire TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND instead;

After printing the description of uninspected clutter when the state of Covalt is 3:
	say " [one of]No sign of the [as a noun]diagram[end as]. [or]No luck on the [as a noun]diagram[end as] though. [or]Still no trace of [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s [as a noun]diagram[end as]. [or]No [as a noun]diagram[end as] there. [stopping]";

Section 2 - Covalt can look

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the state of Covalt is 3:
	have Covalt inspect the item Covalt inspects;

To have Covalt inspect (the curio - a thing):
	if the curio is remembered by Covalt:
		say "[one of]'Not there,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says. 'I looked.'[or]'I tried there,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] remarks.[or]'No good,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says.[or]'I already tried there,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] remarks.[cycling]";
	else if at least 10 things are inspected:
		say "'But, ah-ha! Here's the snake from its hollow log!' He pulls out a folded sheet of paper from behind [the curio].[paragraph break]";
		fire TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND;
	else if 3 things are inspected and COVALT_REMARKS_ON_DESIGNS is unfired:
		fire COVALT_REMARKS_ON_DESIGNS;
	else:
		now Covalt remembers the curio;
		say "[one of][as a noun]Covalt[end as] rummages [if the curio is plural-named]through[else]around[end if] [the curio].[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] looks over [the curio], hoping to find his [as a noun]diagram[end as], but having no luck.[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] straightens up from inspecting [the curio]. 'Nothing there,' he reports.[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] is shaking his head. 'It's not with [the curio].'[in random order]";

To decide which thing is the item Covalt inspects:
	let L be a list of objects;
	let L be the list of uninspected clutter in the Clock Shop;
	remove the clock shop door from L, if present;
	if the number of entries in L is 0, decide on Covalt;
	sort L in random order;
	decide on the entry 1 of L.

COVALT_REMARKS_ON_DESIGNS is a trigger. 

Rule for firing COVALT_REMARKS_ON_DESIGNS:
	say "'Problem is,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] remarks as he hunts around the room, 'I have to keep hiding all my [as a noun]design[end as]s from prying eyes. So I end up <Italic>losing</Italic> my [as a noun]design[end as]s, and then it's a right trouble to dig them back up again.'";

Section 2b - Covalt will look where you ask him to

After firing a clutter topic (called the gas):
	let the curio be the hiding place corresponding to a conversation of the gas in the table of Covalt's Interesting Items;
	if the curio is not inspected:
		now the internal value of COVALT_COUNTER is 2;
		have Covalt inspect the curio;
		
Instead of asking Covalt to try examining or searching clutter:
	now the internal value of COVALT_COUNTER is 2;
	have Covalt inspect the noun;

Section 3 - Finding the Diagram

TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] spreads the paper out. 'The [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as],' he whistles. 'Built by [as a noun]St. Babbage[end as]. Not drawn, you understand.' His voice has taken on a deep, reverent quality, more solemn and rich than the crackly old [as a noun]Abbot[end as] ever manages in any of his sermons. It's like the page itself was radiating the light of moving gear-teeth, throwing sparkles into [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s eyes.[paragraph break]'Drawing it came after,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] continues. '[as a noun]Babbage[end as], they say, just sat down and made the machine. Cog-only knows how. Can't stop, you see. Can't start. Must have been running <Italic>while</Italic> he was putting it together. The [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] is self-winding. Runs and runs without stopping. Forever. Look,' and he points to parts of the [as a noun]diagram[end as]. Cogs attached to other cogs that attach back to themselves. 'No spring at all,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says, wistfully. 'Nowhere for a spring to even <Italic>go</Italic>. A universe in miniature, all of its lonesome, so the books tell it. Got to keep it separate, and all is well. But your [as a noun]Figure[end as], if he gets one. Well, I don't know.'[paragraph break]'What do you think the [as a noun]Figure[end as] wants with it?' I ask.[paragraph break]'Batters me. I don't know. I wondered that ever since he turned up asking me to build one – like I even <Italic>could</Italic> build one. I mean, this [as a noun]diagram[end as], isn't a <Italic>full</Italic> [as a noun]diagram[end as]. It's like an aspect of the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]. Like, if you shone a light at the machine and sketched the outline of the shadow. Good for the soul of a clockworker, but your [as a noun]Figure[end as]? I guess he wants it [']dapted. For something else. Something quite else...' He trails off, quite suddenly, lost in thought.";

After firing TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND:
[	now the player carries the Perpetuum Mobile diagram; ]
	now Covalt carries the Perpetuum Mobile diagram;
	change the state of Covalt to 4;
[ and we speed up his every-turn messages ] 
	change the top end of COVALT_COUNTER to 2;
	change the internal value of COVALT_COUNTER to the top end of COVALT_COUNTER;

Instead of taking the the Perpetuum Mobile diagram when Covalt is carrying the the Perpetuum Mobile diagram:
	fire TAKE_DIAGRAM;

Instead of examining the the Perpetuum Mobile diagram when Covalt is carrying the the Perpetuum Mobile diagram:
	fire TAKE_DIAGRAM;
	continue the action.

TAKE_DIAGRAM is a trigger.

Rule for firing TAKE_DIAGRAM:
	now the player carries the Perpetuum Mobile diagram;
	say "I pick the [as a noun]diagram[end as] out of [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s hands to take a look myself."

Every turn when TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND was fired and TAKE_DIAGRAM is unfired:
	fire TAKE_DIAGRAM;

Section - the Perpetuum Mobile diagram

The Perpetuum Mobile diagram is a thing. The printed name is "diagram of the Perpetuum Mobile". The description is "It's really a series of [as a noun]diagram[end as]s: the first, the rack for the ball-bearing just like I saw in the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]. But below, well, then it gets <Italic>really</Italic> complicated. Cogs forming snake-like chains across several levels, all intersecting and then re-intersecting with themselves. It looks like, if it wasn't running, it'd be locked up solid and tight. But <Italic>because</Italic> it's running, there's always a gap for the next tooth to take." Understand "plan/diagram/blueprint", "perpetum", "diagram/ of" as the Perpetuum Mobile diagram.

After printing the description of the Perpetuum Mobile diagram when the player can see Covalt during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	fire TRIG_DIAGRAM;

TRIG_DIAGRAM is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_DIAGRAM:
	say "[paragraph break]'It's not the full picture,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] remarks. 'You can't get it from just one angle. You got look, lots of ways. So, you know. This picture's more for <Italic>inspiration</Italic> than to actually <Italic>build</Italic> one. That's what I told the [as a noun]Figure[end as], see.'[run paragraph on]";

Instead of putting the Perpetuum Mobile diagram on something:
	try dropping the Perpetuum Mobile diagram.
Instead of inserting the Perpetuum Mobile diagram into something:
	try dropping the Perpetuum Mobile diagram.
Instead of giving the Perpetuum Mobile diagram to someone:
	try dropping the Perpetuum Mobile diagram.

Instead of dropping the Perpetuum Mobile diagram:
	say "Come on, Wren, you can’t go leaving [as a noun]diagram[end as]s of Holy Relics lying around the place now can you?";

Instead of giving the Perpetuum Mobile diagram to Covalt:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] shakes his head. He's deep in thought.";

Chapter 3 - Clutter

The junk is privately-named, scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "[as a noun]Clutter[end as] is assorted [as a noun]stuff[end as] filling up space. It'd be better to say this room has '[as a noun]gaps[end as]', one of which I'm standing in and another I'm using to breathe. The rest is filled by the [as a noun]workbench[end as], the wall of [as a noun]pendulums[end as] of all shapes and sizes, and, everywhere else, [as a noun]clocks[end as] and clock [as a noun]parts[end as]."
Understand "assorted", "clutter", "junk", "stuff", "mess", "things", "gaps", "parts", "shop", "workshop", "room", "junk-pile", "junk-piles" as the junk.

Understand "scrap" as the junk when the scrap of paper is not visible.

Instead of searching the junk:
	say "Somewhere amongst all the [as a noun]parts[end as] and [as a noun]tools[end as] there's a [as a noun]workbench[end as] and the solid wooden [as a noun]table[end as]."

Instead of taking the junk:
	say "I'd need several carts to carry all this away."

A rack of shelves is scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "A rack of [as a noun]shelves[end as] hang on the back of the [as a noun]door[end as] to the street. They mostly hold [as a noun]clocks[end as] (mainly whole [as a noun]clocks[end as]), although I can see a [as a noun]monkey head[end as] sticking up at the back." The printed name is "shelves".

Instead of searching the rack of shelves:
	try examining the rack of shelves.

The solid table is a supporter, scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "The solid [as a noun]table[end as] is covered with piles and piles and <Italic>piles</Italic> of [as a noun]paper[end as], rather than the [as a noun]tools[end as] littering the [as a noun]workbench[end as]." The printed name is "table". Understand "desk", "wooden" as the table.

Instead of searching the solid table:
	try examining the solid table.

Instead of looking under the solid table:
	say "There's more stuff down there - more [as a noun]papers[end as], mostly."


Some assorted clocks are scenery, privately-named, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "The [as a noun]clocks[end as] are every shape and size (including one with an oval dial whose hands extend and shrink as they go round; it's creepy to watch). A [as a noun]water clock[end as] keeps time by dripping water through a tiny hole. And at the back, there's a small [as a noun]mechanical monkey[end as] and a [as a noun]strange device[end as] made of wires and metal plates." Understand "clock", "clocks" as the assorted clocks. The printed name is "clocks".

Instead of taking or pushing or pulling or attacking the assorted clocks:
	say "There are about a hundred of them, but only a few really stand out."

Instead of turning the assorted clocks:
	say "There are about a hundred, with a hundred winding keys."

The workbench is scenery, a supporter, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "The [as a noun]workbench[end as] is fitted with [as a noun]tools[end as], with more [as a noun]tools[end as] piled on top. It's then dusted over with clock [as a noun]parts[end as], some so careful and delicate they deserve individual felt cushion – this [as a noun]Covalt[end as] is clearly a lunatic, scattering them about like this. In one corner of the [as a noun]workbench[end as], there's also a strange brown [as a noun]lump[end as]." Understand "bench" as the workbench.

Instead of searching the workbench:
	try examining the workbench.

Instead of looking under the workbench:
	say "There's more [as a noun]tools[end as] down there."

Some tools are scenery, clutter, on the workbench. "[as a noun]Tools[end as] of every size and denomination. This is a parliament of [as a noun]tools[end as]. An army. A great big heap. One is particularly odd: a smooth [as a noun]metal box[end as] with two flat pins sticking out the top. They're labelled + and -."

Some clock parts are scenery, privately-named, clutter, on the workbench. "[as a noun]Springs[end as], [as a noun]pistons[end as], [as a noun]rods[end as], [as a noun]screws[end as] – there's a whole litany of [as a noun]parts[end as] in here." Understand "spring", "springs", "piston", "pistons", "rod", "rods", "screw", "screws", "clock parts", "clock part", "teeth", "brass", "cog", "cogs", "trace", "traces", "clockwork" as the clock parts.

The water clock is scenery, privately-named, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "It works by droplets, falling onto a pan that gradually drags down on a rotating wooden tumblers. No winding and no hands!" Understand "water clock" as the water clock.

The battery is scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "This is a riddle. What's a [as a noun]box[end as] that has no lid? Two pins but no teeth for them to meet? Doesn't look like it does anything, but solidly built and carefully sealed up?" Understand "box", "metal", "smooth", "pins", "+", "-", "batteries" as the battery. The printed name is "smooth metal box".

Instead of taking or touching or tasting the battery:
	say "[one of]'Don't touch that!' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] barks.[or]I don't want to make him angry.[stopping]";

The well-thumbed Bible is scenery, clutter, on the solid table. "A copy of the [as a noun]Bible[end as]. Leafing through, it looks fairly standard."

Instead of searching or opening the Bible: try examining the Bible instead.

Instead of taking the Bible:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] probably needs it - it lists all the Laws of Physik, all the motions of gear-wheels, and a lot of useful Constants besides."
	
Instead of consulting the Bible about:
	say "I don't have time to read all the Laws right now. Besides, they won't have changed since the <Italic>last</Italic> time I read them."

Some wall of pendulums is scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] has [as a noun]penduluums[end as] in a rack on his wall like a sword-fighter might have swords." Understand "penduluum", "penduluums", "pendulum", "stubby", "brass" as the wall of pendulums.

Instead of doing something when the wall of pendulums is physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]penduluums[end as] look too heavy to shift and they're a little high up for me."

Instead of searching the wall of pendulums:
	say "Standing on tip-toes I can peer between the [as a noun]penduluums[end as][check in case we've found it in the noun]";

The mechanical monkey is scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "It's a [as a noun]toy[end as], or a [as a noun]pet[end as], like Gubbler's salamander (and about the same size). Right now it's wound down." Understand "small", "toy", "pet", "head" as the mechanical monkey.

Instead of taking the mechanical monkey:
	say "[one of]'Leave that,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] snaps. 'It'll take your fingers off, it's mean.'[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] shakes his head. 'I'm not joking.'[stopping]";

Instead of turning the mechanical monkey:
	say "No key.";

The strange device is scenery, clutter, in the Clock Shop. "Hard to say. It looks like a cross between a plant and a box of screwdrivers. Hard to believe it does anything." 

Instead of turning or switching on the strange device:
	say "I can't really see how!";

Instead of taking the strange device:
	say "If I tried to lift it it would probably fall to pieces."

The butter toffee is scenery, clutter, on the workbench. "It's [as a noun]toffee[end as]. Butter [as a noun]toffee[end as]." Understand "brown", "lump" as the butter toffee.

Instead of pushing or looking under or pulling the butter toffee:
	say "[one of]There's nothing underneath it, if that's what you're thinking.[or]I don't know what you're thinking.[cycling]";

Before eating or tasting the butter toffee:
	say "[one of]I nibble a corner. Wren, I'm thinking, you're hungry...[or]I take another bite, while [as a noun]Covalt[end as] isn't looking.[stopping]" instead;
	
Instead of slicing the butter toffee with the knife:
	say "I shave off a slice of [as a noun]toffee[end as] and eat it. Sugar. Food. I'm hungrier than I realised!" instead.
	
Instead of taking the butter toffee:
	say "'Don't take all of it,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] grumbles. 'I need it to think.' So, [run paragraph on]";
	try eating the butter toffee instead.

Some sketch papers are scenery, clutter, on the solid table. "The [as a noun]desk[end as] is covered in [as a noun]papers[end as] – few complete blueprints, though; they're mostly [as a noun]sketches[end as]. In the middle of the pile is a well-thumbed [as a noun]Bible[end as], marked with several more bookmarks (more papers)." The printed name is "papers". Understand "sketches" as the papers. Understand "piles", "paper", "piles of paper/papers", "bookmark", "bookmarks" as the sketch papers.

Instead of searching the sketch papers:
	try examining the sketch papers.

Instead of taking or looking under the sketch papers:
	say "Under the [as a noun]papers[end as] are more papers and under those... is the [as a noun]table[end as][check in case we've found it in the noun]";

To say check in case we've found it in (t - a thing):
	if the player remembers at least six things and the state of Covalt is 3:
		say ". And there's his [as a noun]diagram[end as]. I pull it free and hand it over.[paragraph break]";
		fire TRIG_DIAGRAM_FOUND;
	else:
		if the t is uninspected, now the player remembers the t;
		say ".";

Chapter 4 - Door

The clock shop door is a closed door, privately-named, clutter, scenery, southwest of the Clock Shop, northeast of Escapement St. The description is "[if the location is the Clock Shop]On the back of the [as a noun]door[end as] to the street is a series of [as a noun]shelves[end as], each bristling with all manner of [as a noun]clocks[end as].[otherwise]The sign reads [as a noun]Covalt[end as] & Son, Chronometers Secular, Sacred and Second-Sprung. The word Son has been scratched out.[end if]".

Understand "door" as the clock shop door. The printed name is "door".

Understand "covalt", "covalt's", "chronometers", "scratched", "out", "and", "son", "word", "clock", "shop", "door", "sign" as the clock shop door when the location is Escapement St.

Before making to leave in the Clock Shop during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	try opening the clock shop door instead.

Instead of approaching the clock shop door:
	try entering the clock shop door.

Before going southwest in the Clock Shop during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	try opening the clock shop door instead.

Before entering the clock shop door in the Clock Shop during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	try opening the clock shop door instead.

Instead of opening the clock shop door when the scrap of paper is off-stage during Covalt's Shop Hunt:
	if the state of Covalt is at least 4:
		say "'Hold up,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] insists. 'I'm trying to think. Don't go doing something <Italic>different</Italic> and distracting me.'"; 
	else:
		say "[one of]'Not so fast,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] growls. 'You can't go running off with no idea else what's next or you'll wind up dancing in the circus or something and what kind of <Italic>story</Italic> would that be, eh? So settle down, quit with your legging it, and let's get thinking.'[or]This [as a noun]giant[end as]'s my only hope of working out what to do next, I think. I'm not going anywhere until he says so.[stopping]";

Instead of opening the clock shop door when Return to Covalt's has not happened:
	say "'That's the place,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] insists, tapping the address with a finger like a roofing mallet. 'The [as a noun]Difference Engine[end as]'ll tell you all you want. I hope. If you can get a clear idea what you're wanting and – well, anyway.' He puffs his cheeks. 'One dead air [as a noun]balloon[end as] says you'll do your best.'[paragraph break]'Thanks,' I say. The first person to be nice to me – well, ever really. 'I appreciate your help.'[paragraph break]'Well, nothing's too good for a rat,' he snarls. 'And do pop back here later if you need to. I don't think. Bringing the Heretic police and that. Go on! Get out of it.' And with that, he boots me through the [as a noun]door[end as] and slams it with relish.";
	end the chapter;
	say "The streets of [as a noun]St Philip[end as] are narrow and winding, closer to the veins on a leaf than anything mechanical. Their only Holy Function is to get me completely and totally lost, and they do that so well that it takes a good couple of hours to find the way to the steps of the Counting House.[paragraph break]If anyone from the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] saw me out here they'd have my guts for wheel-belts. Lucky then that this is a [as a noun]side-street[end as] off a [as a noun]side-street[end as], the kind of place full of urchins who look the same as me. I'd feel right at home - if I wasn't further from it than I'd ever been.";
	move the player to The Street;

