Chapter 3 - Guards

Some Counting House guards are a person, scenery, privately-named, in the Counting House Steps. "The [as a noun]guards[end as] are dressed in full livery: bright red [as a noun]uniforms[end as] with [as a noun]pendulums[end as] hanging from both shoulders, embroidered [as a noun]cog-links[end as] on their elbows, knee-high black [as a noun]boots[end as] tipped with metal [as a noun]pointers[end as]. Each one carries a flat [as a noun]spear[end as]: the one carried by the [as a noun]guard[end as] on the left is longer than the one carried by the [as a noun]guard[end as] on the right." The printed name is "guards".

Understand "wide",  "uniformed",   "first guard/one/man", "second guard/one/man",  "guard", "guards", "man", "men", "on the", "left", "right"   as the Counting House Guards.

