The glimpse-machine identifies the sprung platform.



Section 4 - Desk

A large oak desk is a supporter, in the Grand Foyer. "To the north a pair of [as a noun]doors[end as] of beveled glass are filled by broken fragments of light and clockwork. To the south is a large [as a noun]oak desk[end as] and the [as a noun]brass doors[end as] back onto the street." The description is "A sturdy [as a noun]oak table[end as], probably made from the timbers of a sunken Spanish ship or something[if the number of things on the oak desk is not zero]. On the [as a noun]table[end as] is [a list of things on the large oak desk][end if]."

Rule for writing a paragraph about the large oak desk:
	say "To the north are two [as a noun]doors[end as] of beveled glass. To the south is a large [as a noun]oak desk[end as] just next to the [as a noun]brass doors[end as] back onto the street.";

Understand "sturdy", "table", "wood", "wooden", "timbers" as the oak desk. The printed name is "oak desk".

Instead of looking under the large oak desk:
	say "There's nothing very interesting down there. The floor, mostly."

Instead of opening the large oak desk:
	say "The [as a noun]desk[end as] doesn't have a drawer."

Section 5 - Folder

A leather folder is on the large oak desk. The description is "A [as a noun]leather folder[end as] containing a few documents. It's stamped with the [as a noun]Parliamentary seal[end as] of a spiked wheel."

The reading matter is "Inside are a few documents stamped with [as a noun]Parliament seal[end as]s. <Italic>'In the Absence of his Lordship the honourable and worthy minister etc. etc. Raffles du Mer, fighting on the Main for cog and country, her Ladyship the honourable and austere etc. etc. Duchess has been sanctioned to undertake War Office administrative responsibilities. We hope and pray for her husband's swift return...'</Italic>[paragraph break]The next sheet has a hand-written note attached: <Italic>'Questions approved. Most unusual? Politically motivated?'</Italic> Beneath that is a [as a noun]sheet of numbers[end as]: must be an accounts list or something."

Instead of opening or searching the leather folder:
	try reading the leather folder instead;

The Parliamentary documents are part of the leather folder. Understand "paper", "papers", "note", "handwritten note", "hand-written note", "hand written note" as the Parliamentary documents.

Instead of examining or reading the Parliamentary documents:
	try reading the leather folder instead.

Instead of taking the Parliamentary documents:
	say "They look boring. I don't need them."

The Parliamentary seal is part of the leather folder. Understand "spiked", "wheel", "stamp" as the Parliamentary seal. 

Instead of examining the Parliamentary seal:
	say "It's a cog, predictably: but a really spiky one. The kind you could roll over your enemies."

The sheet of numbers is part of the Parliamentary documents. Understand "account", "accounts", "list", "action/actor control", "action/actor column",  "columns" as the sheet of numbers.

Understand "actor" as the sheet of numbers when the location is not the Western Platform.
Understand "action" as the sheet of numbers when the location is not the Eastern Platform.

Instead of examining the sheet of numbers:
	say "Two columns of five-digit numbers. One is headed Action Control, the other Actor Control[one of].[or]. But they're just numbers - lines and lines and lines of numbers that don't mean a thing![stopping]"

Instead of taking the sheet of numbers:
	say "It's no use to me."

