Part 15 - Eastern Platform

Section 2b - flash of red

[ We see this if we try to turn the abacus having not yet found the key ]

To provide key-hint:
	if the ruby key is off-stage:
		move the ruby key to the small golden dials;
		move flash-red to the location;
		say "[line break]From somewhere in the depths of the [as a noun]machine[end as], I catch a flash of red.";

Carry out taking the ruby key:
	remove the flash-red from play;

The flash-red is a scenery, privately-named, thing. The description is "Something, gleaming, in the depths of the [as a noun]machine[end as]."  Understand "flash", "flash of", "red" as the flash-red. The printed name is "flash of red".

Instead of approaching the flash-red:
	let the way be the best route from the location to the Engine Room, using doors;
	if the way is a direction, try going the way instead;
	say "It's somewhere in the [as a noun]machine[end as]."

Instead of doing something when the flash-red is physically involved:
	say "The flash was somewhere deep in the [as a noun]machine[end as]."

Chapter 1 - Description

The Eastern Platform is a major mirror-room, east of the Main Platform. The hemisphere is east. The printed name is "East Platform". The Eastern Platform mirrors the Western Platform.

Instead of making to leave when in the  Eastern Platform: try going west.
Instead of going inside when in the  Eastern Platform: try going west.


The first description is "This is a small [as a noun]platform[end as] to the [west with mirroring] of the central dais. It surrounded on most sides by a [as a noun]brass rail[end as], hooked onto which is a steel [as a noun]abacus[end as] that in turn is connected to the [as a noun]Engine[end as] by delicate silver [as a noun]chains[end as] like the spider-webs of my room after the rain has got in.[paragraph break]Hanging above the [as a noun]platform[end as] is a large wooden [as a noun]signboard[end as][if the model-at-hand is not phrase-viewed].[else][board display][end if]"

To decide which difference-engine model is the model-at-hand:
	decide on the viewed model of a random visible display board.

The second description is "This [as a noun]platform[end as] is the mirror-image of the one to the [east with mirroring], except that the words on the painted [as a noun]signboard[end as] above the [as a noun]abacus[end as] are different[if the model-at-hand is not phrase-viewed].[else][board display][end if]"

To say board display:
	let m be the model-at-hand;
	say ": <Italic>'[stored meaning of m]'[if m is not the action-model]. [end if]</Italic>";


Chapter 2 - Action Display

The action display is a display board, scenery, in the Eastern Platform. The printed name is "signboard". The viewed model of the action display is the action-model.

Chapter 3 - Action Control

An abacus called the action-control is scenery, in the Eastern Platform. 

There is a difference-engine model called the action-model.
The controlled model of the action-control is the action-model.

The stored value of the action-model is 32149;
The stored meaning of the action-model is "How much cost today immortality?";
The word-table of the action-model is the table of action words.

[ for actions we accept:
	first - 7			- where
	second - 8, 4, 9 - wicked, in St Phillip, lost
	third - 3, 5 - is planning, chose
	fourth - 2, 4 - to go, today
	fifth - 3, 5 - next, ambition
or

Where in St. Phillip live today property
Where in St. Phillip is planning to steal property
Where wicked is planning to steal property
	first - 7 - where
	second - 4, 8 - wicked, in St Phillip
	third - 3, 9 - is planning, live
	fourth - 3 - to steal
	fifth - 7 - property
]

To decide what number is the action test score for (n - a number):
[ complicated, because we accept two questions "where's the figure going?" and "how will he steal..."? ]
	let first-test-score be 0;
	let test-score be 0;
	let init-n be n;
	assert that n is at least 11111 and n is at most 99999 issuing "Difference engine value out of range.";
	repeat with i running from 1 to 5:
		let l be the remainder after dividing n by 10;
		[l is now the least significant digit of n]
		if i is: 
			-- 5: 
				if l is 7, increase first-test-score by 1;
			-- 4:
				if l is 8 or l is 4 or l is 9, increase first-test-score by 1;
			-- 3: 
				if l is 3 or l is 5, increase first-test-score by 1;
			-- 2: 
				if l is 2 or l is 4 or l is 5, increase first-test-score by 1;
			-- 1: 
				if l is 3 or l is 5, increase first-test-score by 1;
		let n be n minus l;
		let n be n divided by 10; [ -- hence dropping the final digit from n]
	let n be init-n;
	repeat with i running from 1 to 5:
		let l be the remainder after dividing n by 10;
		[l is now the least significant digit of n]
		if i is: 
			-- 5: 
				if l is 7, increase test-score by 1;
			-- 4:
				if l is 8 or l is 4, increase test-score by 1;
			-- 3: 
				if l is 3 or l is 9, increase test-score by 1;
			-- 2: 
				if l is 3, increase test-score by 1;
			-- 1: 
				if l is 7, increase test-score by 1;
		let n be n minus l;
		let n be n divided by 10; [ -- hence dropping the final digit from n]
	if the first-test-score is greater than the test-score, decide on the first-test-score;
	decide on test-score.

A correctness appraisal rule for the action-model:
[	if the stored value of the action-model is 78325 or the stored value of the action-model is 78525:]
	if the action test score for the stored value of the action-model is 5:
		say "My heart skips a beat. <Italic>It’s my question</Italic>.";
		rule succeeds;

The last correctness appraisal rule for the action-model:
	let l be the action test score for the stored value of the action-model;
	if l is at most 1:
		say "[one of]Of course, I'm meant to be asking about the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s plans.[or]Of course, I'm meant to be trying to track down the [as a noun]Figure[end as].[cycling]";
	else if l is at most 3:
		say "But [one of]I can feel I'm close to knowing where to look for the [as a noun]Figure[end as], but I'm not quite there[or]I don't need a machine to tell me this isn't the quite question [as a noun]Covalt[end as] wanted me to ask[or]I want to know where the [as a noun]Figure in Grey[end as] is going in his [as a noun]ornithopter[end as], and I know this isn't quite going to help with that[or]it doesn't quite feel right[at random].";
	else if l is 4:
		say "That seems very close - but not quite it!";

An unlocking rule for an abacus:
	say "I’d need some kind of [as a noun]key[end as], surely." instead;

