	say "[one of]I couldn't get a grip on the wet [as a noun]brick[end as].[or]There's no way to go that way.[stopping]"


Chapter 2 - Door

A door called the huge iron door is locked, not open, west of the Front Door. "This side of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] is right up against the side wall of another: I could slip around to north or south."

Understand "thick", "metal", "front", "door" as the huge iron door.

The description of the huge iron door is "[one of]The [as a noun]door[end as] is made of thick metal – it's amazing the [as a noun]boards[end as] below don't break from the weight. It's locked with a huge [as a noun]iron bar[end as] and a [as a noun]padlock[end as]. It's almost like it's saying to me, Wren, you need to find another way in.[or]The [as a noun]door[end as] is closed, locked and impenetrable.[stopping]"

Instead of entering the warehouse walls when in the Front Door:
	try going west.

Instead of entering the huge iron door when in the Front Door:
	try going west.

Before going west when in the Front Door:
	try examining the huge iron door instead;

Instead of opening, pushing, pulling or turning the huge iron door:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] is locked.";

Instead of unlocking the huge iron door with the wrench:
	try attacking the huge iron door with the wrench;

Instead of unlocking the huge iron door with something:
	say "I don't have a key anything like big enough for this [as a noun]door[end as]." instead;
	
Instead of climbing or entering or standing on the huge iron door:
	say "The [as a noun]iron bar[end as] across the door is level with my neck - I can't even get started.";

Instead of attacking the huge iron door:
	say "[one of]If I had a thousand years to scratch away at that [as a noun]padlock[end as] with my [as a noun]clock key[end as] – or maybe only a hundred with something better – I might be able to get through that [as a noun]iron bar[end as]. But what's forged lasts, as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says, and I wasn't forged.[or]The [as a noun]door[end as] is too solid for me to batter, or even dent.[or]I can't.[stopping]";

Instead of attacking the huge iron door with the wrench:
	say "It might be a good [as a noun]wrench[end as] but I can hardly batter my way through [as a noun]iron[end as] with it, can I? A boot's no good for a butter-knife, as they say.";

Instead of attacking the huge iron door with something:
	try attacking the huge iron door;

Rule for supplying a missing second noun when the noun is the huge iron door and unlocking:
	say "I don't have a key anything like big enough for this [as a noun]door[end as]." instead;

The solid iron padlock is part of the huge iron door. The description is "The [as a noun]padlock[end as] is built of old anchors and the funnels of the great spring-powered Ocean liners. It would take a key the size of my arm to open it."

Instead of taking or pulling or attacking or pushing the solid iron padlock:
	try attacking the huge iron door.
	
Instead of unlocking the solid iron padlock with:
	try unlocking the huge iron door with the second noun.

Instead of opening the solid iron padlock:
	say "It's locked, as firmly as a crocodile's jaw once it's gotten a child by the leg."

The massive iron bar is part of the huge iron door. The description is "The [as a noun]bar[end as] is fixed with a [as a noun]padlock[end as] the size of a bull's head (and probably just as thick).". Understand "huge bar", "huge iron bar" as the massive iron bar.

Instead of taking the massive iron bar:
	say "I can't lift it - it's locked down with a [as a noun]padlock[end as]. And even if it wasn't, I'm sure I still couldn't lift it."
	
Instead of pulling or attacking or pushing the massive iron bar:
	try attacking the huge iron door.

Instead of climbing the massive iron bar:
	try climbing the huge iron door.

Instead of unlocking the massive iron bar with the wrench:
	say "I can't lever the [as a noun]bar[end as] - or I could, but I'd need a lever as long as the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] itself, and a pivot made of diamond-crusted granite!"

Part 4 - North Side

Chapter 1 - Description

North Side is a room. East of the North Side is north of the Front Door. "The wooden boards are gone - this is just an [as a noun]island[end as] of rubble and sheet metal, as though the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] to the south had been built from the ruins of some older, larger building. Rats skim across the pools of water. Skirting round the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] to east or west will mean picking my way over unsteady piles of [as a noun]brick[end as] and in the scrappy moonlight I can barely see my own feet.[paragraph break]Which all means, if they unleash the dogs on me, this'd be a terrible place to try and run." The printed name is "North Side of Warehouse".

Understand "swim" as jumping when the location is the North Side.

Before running outside when in the North Side:
	say "A terrible place." instead.

Instead of going north when in the North Side:
	say "The [as a noun]rubble[end as] gets thicker and higher in that direction[one of][or]. There's no way past[stopping].";

Instead of going south in the North Side:
	try entering the warehouse walls.

Instead of making to leave when in the North Side:
	try going east.

Understand "swim" as jumping when the location is the North Side.
Understand "dive" as jumping when the location is the North Side.
Understand "swim in/into [something]" as entering when the location is the North Side.
Understand "dive in" as jumping when the location is the North Side.
Understand "dive in/into [something]" as entering when the location is the North Side.


Instead of jumping when in North Side:
	say "[one of]I might dash myself on the [as a noun]bricks[end as] if I tried.[or]If I wanted to swim - which I <Italic>don't</Italic> - then there might be clearer water to the west.[stopping]"

Instead of entering or approaching the river_glimpse when in the North Side:
	try jumping.

Instead of going down when in the North Side:
	try jumping.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Junk

The rubble is scenery in the North Side. "'Without order there is tardiness,' they say: well, this place is so tardy that by the time it got here it was a ruin. Dark shadows and broken [as a noun]bricks[end as][if the Wrench is not handled]. And a gleam of metal – looking closer I spy a good quality [as a noun]wrench[end as][end if]."  

Understand "brick", "bricks", "unsteady", "pile/piles of", "rubble-pile", "pile/piles", "sheet", "metal",  "rock", "rocks", "pool", "pools", "pools of water", "island/islands of", "island", "islands" as the rubble.

Instead of searching or looking under or pushing or pulling or turning or attacking the rubble:
	say "There's nothing but more [as a noun]rubble[end as] in the [as a noun]rubble[end as], and then there's the [as a noun]water[end as]."

Instead of taking the rubble:
	say "I'm not here to go wandering off with the ruins of an old [as a noun]warehouse[end as]."

Instead of searching or looking under or pushing or pulling or turning or attacking the rubble when the Wrench is not handled:
	now the wrench is not scenery;
	say "In the midst of the [as a noun]rubble[end as] is a good-looking [as a noun]wrench[end as]. I pick it out. [run paragraph on]";
	try taking the wrench.

Every turn when the Wrench is not handled and the location is the North Side and the player has been in the North Side for exactly two turns and the rubble is unexamined:
	say "There's a flash of metal from the [as a noun]rubble-pile[end as]."

Instead of entering or climbing or approaching the rubble:
	say "[one of]I'd twist my ankle, and for what? The [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s not out there, is he?[or]There's nothing for me that way.[stopping]"

Understand "flash/gleam", "metal", "flash/gleam of" as the Wrench when the Wrench is not handled.

Section 2 - Wrench

The wrench is portable scenery, not fixed in place, privately-named, in the North Side. "A [as a noun]wrench[end as]. Not a holy tool but a pretty good one all the same. Why anyone would leave it out here to rust is beyond me. I can't help checking the top for bloodstains but of course it's been out in the rain..."

Understand "good", "quality", "weighty", "wrench", "metal", "tool", "mighty", "spanner" as the wrench when we have examined the rubble or the wrench is not scenery.

After taking the wrench:
	now the wrench is not scenery;
	say "[one of]It's a good weighty [as a noun]wrench[end as], as satisfying to hold as a thigh-joint of lamb.[or]It's a [as a noun]wrench[end as], but I take it.[or]Taken.[stopping]";

Part 5 - Dock

Chapter 1 - Description

The Dock is a room. North of the Dock is west of the North Side. South of the Dock is the Ledge. "The west side of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] is built out over the [as a noun]water[end as], where a few plank [as a noun]boards[end as] form a small [as a noun]dock[end as] for boats. The [as a noun]warehouse[end as] has a[if Front Door is visited]nother[end if] [as a noun]door[end as] here but of course it's closed – solid iron and locked with a [as a noun]metal bar[end as].[paragraph break]I could skirt the building to the north and south so long as I kept my balance. The [as a noun]water[end as] looks like ink and it's bubbling slightly. There's probably a [as a noun]crocodile[end as] down there, watching me right now."

Instead of going inside when in the Dock:
	try going east;
Instead of making to leave when in the Dock:
	try going south;

Before going down when in the Dock:
	try jumping instead.

The River Thymes is a backdrop, in the Dock and the Ledge. "The wide [as a noun]River Thymes[end as] disappears into the darkness like someone had knocked a water-glass over a map of the town. On the other side are bright [as a noun]lights[end as] and fine [as a noun]buildings[end as]: that's [as a noun]Palatine Hill[end as] built right by the springs[if the location is the Dock]. The [as a noun]river[end as] is bubbling slightly here[end if]."

Understand "bubbling", "bubbles", "bubble" as the River Thymes when in the Dock.

Instead of tasting or drinking the River Thymes:
	say "I'm not drinking river-water! I'm not quite a drowned rat yet!"
	
Instead of touching the River Thymes:
	say "The [as a noun]water[end as] is cold."

Understand "wet", "dark water", "water", "wide river" as the River Thymes.

Before going inside when in the Dock:
	if the player has been underwater:
		try going west instead;
	now jump-jump-now is true;
	say "Which did you mean - open the [as a noun]door[end as] - or (gulp) jump in the [as a noun]water[end as]?" instead.

Section 1 - swimming

Before entering the River Thymes when the player has not been underwater and jump-jump-now is false:
	now jump-jump-now is true;
	if the player's command includes "jump/swim", continue the action;
	say "You don't <Italic>really</Italic> mean jumping in the river, do you?" instead.

Jump-jump-now is a truth state that varies. [for you b5 fans out there.]
Before reading a command when jump-jump-now has been true for exactly two turns:
	now jump-jump-now is false.


Instead of saying yes when jump-jump-now is true:
	try going west.

Instead of saying no when jump-jump-now is true:
	now jump-jump-now is false;
	say "Good. I'm glad to hear it!"


Instead of entering the River Thymes:
	change the internal value of the BREATH_COUNTER to 1;
	say "[one of]What a terrible idea. I take a deep breath and do it anyway.[or]I throw myself back into the horrible grimy [as a noun]water[end as].[stopping]";
	if the location is the Dock:
		move the player to River 2;
	if the location is the Ledge:
		move the player to River 1;




Section 2 - Synonyms for swimming

Understand "swim" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "dive" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "jump in" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "dive in" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "jump back in" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "dive back in" as jumping when the River Thymes is visible.

Understand "swim in/into/through [something]" as entering when the River Thymes is visible.
Understand "dive in/into [something]" as entering when the River Thymes is visible.

Before jumping on the River Thymes:
	try jumping instead.

Before jumping when the River Thymes is visible:
	try entering the River Thymes instead.

Before going a direction in the presence of the River Thymes when the noun is due west:
	try entering the River Thymes instead;

Instead of undressing in the presence of the River Thymes:
	say "What's the point? If I'm going to go swimming I might as well stay as warm as I can."


Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Loading Bay Door

[Hyphenated to avoid conflict with the Loading Bay room, below]

The loading-bay door is a locked door, privately-named, scenery, closed, east of the Dock, west of Loading Bay. "The [as a noun]door[end as] is made of heavy [as a noun]iron plates[end as]. It might slide up towards the roof if it wasn't locked down and about as heavy as the crescent moon."

The printed name of the loading-bay door is "massive door". Understand "heavy", "massive", "solid", "large", "huge", "metal", "iron", "plates", "plated", "sliding", "door", "lock", "bar", "padlock" as the loading-bay door.

Instead of opening, pushing, pulling or turning the loading-bay door when in the Dock:
	say "The [as a noun]iron door[end as] is firmly locked.";

An unlocking rule for the loading-bay door when in the Dock:
	say "I don't have a key. A key for this door would be massive. I probably couldn't even carry the key if I had it." instead;

Instead of attacking the loading-bay door:
	say "If I had a thousand years to scratch away at that padlock with my [as a noun]clock key[end as] – or maybe only a hundred with something better – I might be able to get through that [as a noun]iron bar[end as]. But what's forged lasts, as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says, and I wasn't forged.";

Instead of attacking the loading-bay door with the wrench:
	say "It might be a good [as a noun]wrench[end as] but I can hardly batter my way through iron with it, can I? A boot's no good for a butter-knife, as they say.";

Instead of attacking the loading-bay door with something:
	try attacking the loading-bay door;

Section 2 - Quay

Some mooring posts are scenery, not plural-named, in the Dock. "A small loading dock – just some [as a noun]mooring posts[end as] on the [as a noun]planking[end as] and a [as a noun]ramp[end as] going into the water." The printed name is "loading dock".

Understand "dock", "loading", "ramp", "planking", "small" as the mooring posts.

Instead of putting something on the mooring posts:
	try dropping the noun instead;

Section 3 - Crocodile

A crocodile is scenery in the Dock. The description is "I can't see him, but I'm sure that <Italic>he</Italic> can see <Italic>me</Italic>."

Before doing something when the crocodile is physically involved:
	try examining the crocodile instead.

Section 4 - Palatine Hill

Palatine Hill is a backdrop, in the Dock, in the Ledge. "[as a noun]Palatine Hill[end as] is where the gentry live, the kind that come to the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] once a year for Newtonmass, pass a few gold coins around and then pat you on the head. The [as a noun]lights[end as] shine out across the river, brighter than the stars." Understand "lights", "buildings" as Palatine hill.

Instead of doing something when Palatine Hill is physically involved:
	say "It's far, far away, across the [as a noun]river[end as]."


Part 6 - Ledge

The Ledge is south of the Dock. South of the Ledge is west of South Side. "This is a narrow plank walkway on the west side of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as], that gives me barely a hand-span between the [as a noun]brick wall[end as] and the dark water of the [as a noun]River Thymes[end as]. I could scurry north or south like a rat, but hanging around here might be a bad idea."

After assembling available exits when in the Ledge:
	remove {west} from the viable directions, if present;
	continue the action;

Instead of making to leave when in the Ledge:
	try going south.

Before going down when in the Ledge:
	try jumping instead.

Before going inside when in the Ledge:
	say "There's no door in the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] this side... unless you meant jumping in the [as a noun]water[end as]...";

Understand "brick", "side", "wall", "walls" as the backdrop-walls when in the Ledge.

Rule for printing the name of the backdrop-walls when in the Ledge:
	say "brick walls"

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-walls when in the Ledge:
	say "The [as a noun]brick wall[end as] rises several times my height above me."

Instead of climbing the backdrop-walls when in the Ledge:
	say "[one of]I couldn't get a grip on the wet [as a noun]brick[end as].[or]There's no way to go that way.[stopping]"


Part 7 & 8 - Two Rivers

An underwater room is a kind of room.

Definition: a room is overground:
	if it is an underwater room, no;
	yes;

Instead of smelling the location when the location is an underwater room:
	say "That'd make for an easy way to drown.";

Understand "swim" as jumping when the location is an underwater room.

Instead of jumping when in River 1:
	try going north;
Instead of jumping when in River 2:
	try going south;

Instead of dropping something when the location is an underwater room:
	say "I'd only lose [if the noun is plural-named]them[otherwise]it[end if].";

To decide whether the player has been underwater:
	if an underwater room is visited, yes;
	no;

To decide whether the player has not been underwater:
	unless the player has been underwater, yes;
	no;

Rule for printing the description of the Perpetuum Mobile diagram when the player has been underwater:
	say "The [as a noun]diagram[end as]’s bedraggled but still clear enough to read.";

Section - the Water

The freezing cold water is a backdrop, in the River 1, in the River 2. "The [as a noun]water[end as] is ice-cold." 

Before touching the freezing water:
	try examining the freezing water instead.

Instead of doing something when the freezing cold water is physically involved:
	say "I'm trashing around in it pretty good already!";

Instead of eating or drinking or tasting or smelling the freezing cold water:
	say "I keep getting mouthfuls, I don't need any more!"

Instead of entering the freezing cold water when in River 1:
	try going north.
Instead of entering the freezing cold water when in River 2:
	try going north.

Understand "river", "thymes", "dark water" as the freezing water.

Instead of getting off the freezing cold water:
	try going east.

[Understand performance problems. --JM]

[Understand "swim in/through [something]" as entering when the freezing cold water is visible and the player's command matches the text "water/river".]

Understand "swim in/through [the freezing cold water]" as entering.

Understand "my/your breath", "breath" as "[breath]".

Understand "hold [breath]" as a mistake ("What do you think I'm doing?") when the freezing cold water is visible.


Chapter 1 - River 1 Description

River 1 is an underwater room, west of the Ledge. The printed name is "Underwater". "I feel like a drowning dog[one of], splashing around in the freezing cold [as a noun]water[end as]. No-one teaches clock-polishers to swim! But at least all that oil teaches you to hold your breath, and[or]. But[stopping] whatever it is I'm doing with my legs seem to be keeping me just below the surface.[paragraph break]I could clamber back onto the [as a noun]planks[end as] to the east, or follow them northwards. But I'd better not put my feet down because who knows what's down there..."

Understand "put feet down" as a mistake ("No, I insist. I'm not doing it.") while the location is River 1.

Instead of making to leave when the location is River 1:
	try going east.
Instead of going inside when the location is River 1:
	say "I'm already in it as far as I can go!";

After going north in River 1:
	say "I paddle my way along.";
	continue the action;

Instead of going in River 1 when the noun is west or the noun is northwest:
	say "That would take me out into the river. I can't swim, remember?";

Instead of going in River 1 when the noun is due south or the noun is northeast:
	say "That way would take me under the [as a noun]dock[end as], which doesn't sound safe at all.";

Chapter 2 - River 2 Description

River 2 is an underwater room, west of the Dock, north of River 1. The printed name is "Underwater, by the Dock". "This is like being in one of Chef's trifles: black jelly sheet above and mucky custard below. I'm holding my breath and trying to see through the muck. The [as a noun]ramp[end as] back up to the [as a noun]dock[end as] is to the east, which is a relief: below it, a fat [as a noun]pipe[end as] sticks out from somewhere and disappears into the depths of the [as a noun]river[end as]. Beside it is some kind of [as a noun]drain[end as] built into the earth under the [as a noun]dock[end as]."

Instead of making to leave when the location is River 2:
	try going east.
Instead of going inside when the location is River 2:
	try entering the drain entrance.

After going south in River 2:
	say "I paddle my way through the [as a noun]water[end as].";
	continue the action;

Instead of going in River 2 when the noun is due west or the noun is north:
	say "That would take me out into the [as a noun]river[end as]. I can't swim, remember?";

Instead of going southeast in River 2:
	say "That way would take me under the [as a noun]dock[end as], which would hardly be safe, would it?";

Section 1 - Planks from Underwater

The dock planks are a backdrop, in River 1, in River 2. Understand "ramp", "board", "boards", "plank" as the dock planks. "The wooden [as a noun]boards[end as] are just shadows above the [as a noun]water[end as]."

Instead of doing something when the dock planks are physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]planks[end as] are out of the [as a noun]water[end as]."

Before approaching or climbing or standing on or entering the dock planks:
	try exiting instead.


Chapter 3 - Exiting to Dock

Instead of going up in an underwater room:
	try going east instead;

After going east from an underwater room:
	fire TRIG_RIVER;
	continue the action;

TRIG_RIVER is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_RIVER:
	say "Grabbing onto one of the [as a noun]poles[end as] supporting the [as a noun]dock[end as] I pull myself back up.";
	destroy wet things, with leading break;


Rule for firing TRIG_RIVER:
	say "I pull myself back out, [one of]teeth chattering[or]dripping[or]spitting water[or]shivering[at random].";
	destroy wet things, with leading break;;


Chapter 4 - Breathing difficulties

BREATH_COUNTER is a busy counter. The top end is 4. The internal value is 1.

After going to an underwater room from an overground room:
	change the internal value of the BREATH_COUNTER to 1;
	continue the action;

Every turn when the location is an underwater room:
	increment BREATH_COUNTER;

Rule for firing BREATH_COUNTER when the internal value of BREATH_COUNTER is the top end of the BREATH_COUNTER minus one:
	say "It's getting harder to hold my breath down here...", paragraph break;

Rule for firing BREATH_COUNTER when the internal value of BREATH_COUNTER is the top end of the BREATH_COUNTER:
	say "[one of]My lungs are getting fully-wound: kicking and flailing I get up to the surface and drag myself out.[or]I need to breathe again, so I pull myself back up.[stopping]";
	if the location is River 1, move the player to Ledge;
	if the location is River 2, move the player to Dock;
	[  we subtly extend the player's breathing time every time this happens, so it doesn't get too annoying... ]
	increase the top end of the BREATH_COUNTER by 4;
	destroy wet things;

To destroy wet things, with leading break:
	if the player is carrying water-destructible things:
		if with leading break, say line break;
		say "[The list of water-destructible things carried by the player] ";
		[ yuk to the following: yeah, I7 can do better, but for now I'm going down a minimum-research route! ]
		if the number of water-destructible things carried by the player is greater than 1:
			say "have turned to pulp - I throw them";
		else:
			say "has turned to a pulp - I throw it";
		say " away into the [as a noun]water[end as][if the player is carrying the Perpetuum Mobile diagram]. The [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] [as a noun]diagram[end as] still seems readable, however[end if].";
		repeat with item running through water-destructible things carried by the player:
			remove item from play;

Instead of examining a water-destructible thing when the player is in an underwater room:
	say "It's looking pretty ruined by the [as a noun]water[end as]."

Chapter 5 - Bubbling pipeline

The bubbling pipeline is scenery, in River 2. "The [as a noun]pipe[end as] emerges from the under the [as a noun]dock[end as] somewhere and disappears off into the [as a noun]river[end as]. There's a hairline [as a noun]crack[end as] in it bubbling [as a noun]gas[end as] into the [as a noun]water[end as]."

Understand "fat", "cracked", "broken", "pipe", "hairline", "bubbling", "bubbles", "bubble", "gas", "of gas", "crack", "fracture", "fractured", "gas pipe/pipeline/line/bubble/bubbles", "line" as the bubbling pipeline.

Before smelling or tasting the bubbling pipeline:
	say "It'll be [as a noun]gas[end as], not air!" instead.

Instead of approaching the pipeline:
	say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as] disappears into the muck in one direction, and into the [as a noun]building[end as] to the other."

Instead of opening or entering the pipeline:
	say "It's just a hairline [as a noun]crack[end as], nothing more."

Instead of searching the pipeline:
	say "[one of]It's the thinnest of [as a noun]crack[end as]s, and the [as a noun]bubbles[end as] get in the way. But then, I suppose t[or]T[stopping]here's nothing in it but [as a noun]gas[end as]."

Instead of slicing the pipeline with the knife:
	say "I open the [as a noun]crack[end as] a fraction but nothing good comes of it - a few more [as a noun]bubbles[end as] escaping, maybe, that obscure my view of the [as a noun]drain[end as] below."

Instead of unlocking the pipeline with the knife:
	try slicing the pipeline with the knife.

Instead of unlocking the pipeline with something:
	say "[The second noun] isn't going to lever the [as a noun]crack[end as]."

Chapter 6 - Drain cover

The drain entrance is a door, scenery, privately-named, closed, not openable, locked, not lockable, northeast of River 2, southwest of the Drain Pipe.

Rule for printing the description of the open drain entrance:
	say "The [as a noun]drain[end as] is open and hardly looks welcoming. It's a dark hole leading northeast into the [as a noun]embankment[end as] under the [as a noun]dock[end as].";

Rule for printing the description of the drain entrance:
	say "The drain leads into the [as a noun]embankment[end as] under the [as a noun]dock[end as]. The [as a noun]cover[end as] is held in place by [count of the bolts in words] [if the count of the bolts is one]last [end if][as a noun]bolt[s][end as].";

The printed name is "drain[if closed] cover[end if]".

Understand "drain", "entrance", "embankment", "embankment under the dock" as the drain entrance.
Understand "cover", "closed" as the drain entrance when the drain entrance is closed.
Understand "hole", "open" as the drain entrance when the drain entrance is open.

Some bolts are part of the drain entrance. The bolts have a number called the count. The count of the bolts is 5.

Understand "bolt" as the bolts.

Instead of going through the closed drain entrance:
	say "The [as a noun]drain cover[end as] is closed.";

To turn bolts:
	if count of the bolts is less than 5 and the wrench is visible:
		try unlocking the drain entrance with the wrench;
	else:
		say "I can't turn [as a noun]bolts[end as] with my bare hands!";

Instead of turning the bolts:
	turn bolts;

Instead of opening the closed drain entrance:
	turn bolts;

Instead of unlocking the drain entrance with something when the second noun is not a key:
	try unscrewing the bolts with the second noun;

Rule for supplying a missing second noun while unscrewing the bolts with:
	if count of the bolts is less than 5 and the wrench is visible:
		change the second noun to the wrench;
	else:
		say "I can't turn [as a noun]bolts[end as] with my bare hands!";

Rule for supplying a missing second noun while unscrewing the drain entrance with:
	if count of the bolts is less than 5 and the wrench is visible:
		change the second noun to the wrench;
	else:
		say "I can't turn [as a noun]bolts[end as] with my bare hands!";

Instead of unscrewing the bolts with the wrench:
	decrease the count of the bolts by 1;
	if the count of the bolts is:
		-- 4: 
			say "Using the [as a noun]wrench[end as] underwater is like trying to run with pins-and-needles in your feet. But I do get the [as a noun]bolt[end as] loose. It sinks down out of sight into the mud.";
		-- 3:
			say "Another of the [as a noun]bolts[end as] comes free.";
		-- 2:
			say "That's half the [as a noun]bolts[end as] undone now. I only hope this [as a noun]drain[end as] goes somewhere useful!";
		-- 1:
			say "One more to go!";
		-- 0:
			say "That's it! The last [as a noun]bolt[end as] falls into the muck, and with a little encouragement the [as a noun]drain cover[end as] follows.";
			now the drain entrance is open;
			now the drain entrance is unlocked;

Instead of unscrewing the bolts with the wrench when the count of the bolts is 0:
	say "The [as a noun]bolts[end as] are all unscrewed and the [as a noun]drain[end as] is open.";

Instead of unscrewing the bolts with something:
	say "I can't get a good grip on those [as a noun]bolts[end as] with [the second noun].";

Instead of entering the open drain entrance when in River 2:
	try going northeast;

After going through the drain entrance from River 2:
	say "I squirm my way inside. It's narrow, not enough room to turn round. That doesn't seem like a problem until I remember that I'm underwater and the second hand's ticking till I run out of air. I push forwards: there's just me, the metal walls and the pounding of my heart like a massive drum.[paragraph break]Luckily the [as a noun]drain[end as] tilts up a little. My lips break the surface and I'm so relieved I feel like crying.";
	continue the action;

After going through the drain entrance from the Drain Pipe:
	say "I squirm my way back into the [as a noun]river[end as].";
	continue the action.

Part 8 - Drain

Chapter 1 - Description

The Drain Pipe is a room. The printed name is "Drain". The description is "The [as a noun]drain[end as]'s heading upwards and I've found air above me, a little pocket of it, like I was an ant trapped inside a spirit level. [if the air pocket is unexamined]But I'm breathing, so either there's some kind of [as a noun]grate[end as] above me or the air will eventually turn as stale as prisoner's bread.[otherwise]Above me is a [as a noun]grate[end as].[end if]"

Instead of making to leave when the location is the Drain Pipe:
	try going southwest. 

Instead of going inside when in the Drain Pipe:
	try going up;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Air pocket, Grate, Drain

The air pocket is privately-named, scenery, a door, open, not openable. "[if the location is the Drain Pipe]There's some kind of [as a noun]grate[end as] above me![otherwise]I suppose if they load things from boats into here at high tide then the floor might get pretty wet. That's why they needed such a large [as a noun]drain[end as].[end if]"

The air pocket is up from the Drain Pipe. The air pocket is down from the Loading Bay.

Understand "grate" as the air pocket.

Understand "air", "pocket" as the air pocket when the location is the Drain Pipe.
Understand "drain", "hole" as the air pocket when the location is the Loading Bay.

Every turn when the location is the Drain Pipe:
	now the printed name of the air pocket is "air pocket".
Every turn when the location is the Loading Bay:
	now the printed name of the air pocket is "drain hole".

Instead of opening the air pocket, try going the air pocket.

After going through the air pocket from the Drain Pipe:
	if the air pocket is unexamined:
		say "There's some kind of [as a noun]grate[end as] above me. I push it open and haul myself up into a wide room.";
	otherwise:
		say "I push my way up through the [as a noun]grate[end as] and out of the [as a noun]water[end as].";
	now the air pocket is examined;
	continue the action;

After going through the air pocket from the Loading Bay when the warehouse door is closed:
	say "I suppose the water-rats didn't get a chance to eat my toes last time. I drop ";
	if the player is carrying the long ladder:
		move the long ladder to the Loading Bay;
		say "the [as a noun]ladder[end as] and head ";
	say "back into the [as a noun]water[end as] of the [as a noun]drain[end as].";
	continue the action;

Instead of going through the air pocket from the Loading Bay when the warehouse door is open:
	say "I can’t. The [as a noun]crate[end as] is completely blocking the [as a noun]drain[end as]. Next high tide, this place is going to <Italic>flood</Italic>.";

Part 10 - Loading Bay

Chapter 0 - Inside the Warehouse rooms

Definition: a room is warehouse-interior:
	if it is the Loading Bay or it is the Storage Bay or it is the Gas Platform or it is the Warehouse Basement, yes;
	no;

Instead of listening when the location is  a warehouse-interior room:
	say "Drip... drip... drip... (but don't mind that, it's just me.)";

Instead of smelling when the location is a warehouse-interior room:
	say "The dank air reeks of fish and clock oil[one of] (what do they keep in this place, mechanised sharks?)[or].[stopping]"

Instead of examining the backdrop-walls when the location is warehouse-interior:
	say "The [as a noun]walls[end as] are wet up to a foot or so of depth."

Chapter 1 - Description

The Loading Bay is a room. "[one of]I'm inside the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] but there's no sign of the [as a noun]Figure[end as]. Or anyone else. Or anything. [once only]This [as a noun]warehouse[end as] may be massive but it seems completely empty, [if the warehouse door is open]just that [as a noun]crate[end as] blocking the [as a noun]drain[end as],[otherwise]just one huge [as a noun]crate[end as] wrapped all round with thick iron [as a noun]chains[end as],[end if] and some piles of [as a noun]debris[end as] like those outside. There's enough floor space continuing off to the south for a game of clockball.[paragraph break]About halfway down is the large metal [as a noun]door[end as] I saw from the [as a noun]dock[end as]. Above it a fat [as a noun]pipe[end as] crosses from wall to wall. The [as a noun]drain[end as] I came up from is by the north wall.". The printed name is "Loading".

Instead of making to leave when in the Loading Bay:
	try going down.
Instead of going north when in the Loading Bay:
	try going down.

Instead of going inside when in the Loading Bay:
	try going south.

Understand "play clockball" as a mistake ("I'd need a team of ten. But it's just me here right now. Just Wren... and maybe the [as a noun]Figure[end as], too.") when the location is the Loading Bay.

Section 1 - glimpses of the ladder

After going south from the Loading Bay to Storage Bay when the long ladder is scenery:
	now the long ladder is not scenery;
	say "I trip over the rung of a [as a noun]ladder[end as] lying on the floor, pick myself up, and continue walking.";
	continue the action.

The ladder-glimpse is a privately-named glimpse backdrop, in the Loading Bay, in Storage Bay. The printed name is "long ladder". Understand "long ladder", "ladder" as the ladder-glimpse when the ladder is over there.

Before approaching the ladder-glimpse when in the Loading Bay:
	try going south instead.

Before approaching the ladder-glimpse when in the Storage Bay:
	try going north instead.

Instead of doing something with the ladder-glimpse:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] is in the [if location is Loading Bay]south[else]north[end if] end of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as]."

To decide if the ladder is over there:
	if the location is Storage Bay and the long ladder is in the Loading Bay, yes;
	if the location is the Loading Bay and the long ladder is in the Storage Bay, yes;
	no.


Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Drain

[ See 'Air Pocket', above. ]

Section 2 - Junk

Some debris [we have 'junk' in the clock shop and 'rubble' outside the warehouse...] is scenery, privately-named, in the Loading Bay.

The printed name of the debris is "junk". Understand "broken", "ceramic", "wood", "wooden", "junk", "debris", "rubble", "stone/stones", "beam/beams",  "pile/piles", "pile/piles of", "tile/tiles", "guttering", "gutter/gutters" as the debris. 

Rule for printing the description of the debris:
	say "Broken [as a noun]stone[end as], wooden [as a noun]beams[end as], piles of [as a noun]ceramic tile[end as] and [as a noun]guttering[end as], things like that. ";

After printing the description of the debris when the north end of the rope is in the Loading Bay and the middle of the rope is not handled:
	say "A massive coil of [as a noun]rope[end as]. ";

After printing the description of the debris when the long ladder is in the Loading Bay and the long ladder is scenery:
	now the long ladder is not scenery;
	say "A long [as a noun]ladder[end as]. ";

Last after printing the description of the debris:
	say paragraph break;

Before searching or pushing or pulling or looking under the debris:
	try examining the debris instead.

Instead of taking or turning the debris:
	say "Clearing up in here isn't going to help me stop the [as a noun]Figure[end as].";

Section 3 - A Long Ladder

A long ladder is portable scenery, in the Loading Bay. "A long wooden [as a noun]ladder[end as], the sort of thing I could carry around so long as there was no-one about to crash into."

The long ladder can be placed. The long ladder is not placed.
The long ladder can be stuck. The long ladder is not stuck.

Instead of pushing or pulling or turning the long ladder:
	if the long ladder is handled:
		say "There's plenty of manhandling when I carry this thing around; it's enormous.";
	else:
		say "They'll be plenty of manhandling if I carry this thing around; it's enormous.";

After printing the description of the placed long ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as]'s propped underneath the [as a noun]pipe[end as]. ";

After printing the description of the stuck long ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] leads down from the [as a noun]platform[end as]. ";

Rule for writing a paragraph about the long ladder:
	say "[if placed]A long [as a noun]ladder[end as] leans against the [as a noun]wall[end as] below the [as a noun]pipe[end as].[otherwise if stuck]The [as a noun]ladder[end as] leads from the [as a noun]platform[end as] to the earth floor below.[otherwise]There's a long [as a noun]ladder[end as] here.[end if]";

Instead of climbing the long ladder when the long ladder is not placed and the long ladder is not stuck:
	if the player is carrying the long ladder:
		say "But I'm carrying it!" instead;
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] doesn't exactly go anywhere at the moment.";

[
Instead of climbing the placed long ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] puts me in reach of the [as a noun]pipe[end as].";
]

Before going up when the long ladder is placed and the long ladder is visible:
	try climbing the long ladder instead.

Instead of taking the stuck long ladder:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] has dropped almost out of reach. There's no way I could get it back up here.";

After taking the long ladder:
	now the long ladder is not scenery;
	now the long ladder is not placed;
	say "[one of]I stick the [as a noun]ladder[end as] under my arm. I must look like a rat stealing a baguette stick.[or]I take hold of the [as a noun]ladder[end as] again.[stopping]";

TRIG_LADDER_PIPE is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_LADDER_PIPE:
	say "Waving this [as a noun]ladder[end as] around is like trying to repair a differentiator in a pocket watch with nine foot wooden arms. I'm all over the place and only after making a noise louder than the midday chimes do I get it against the wall, by the [as a noun]pipe[end as].[paragraph break]If the [as a noun]Figure[end as] is here somewhere then I might as well have pulled the doorbell.";

Rule for firing fired TRIG_LADDER_PIPE:
	say "I get the [as a noun]ladder[end as] into place under the [as a noun]pipe[end as].";

Instead of putting the long ladder against the backdrop-walls when a warehouse pipe (called the pipe-aimed-for) is visible:
	try putting the long ladder against pipe-aimed-for.

Before putting the long ladder on a warehouse pipe (called the pipe-aimed-for): [ a before rule, to natch the instead rule for the pipes! ]
	try putting the long ladder against pipe-aimed-for instead.

Instead of putting the long ladder on a scenery thing:
	try putting the long ladder against the second noun.

Section 3b - Standing on the Ladder

Yourself can be atop the ladder. Yourself is not atop the ladder.

After printing the name of the location when the player is atop the ladder and we are looking:
	say ", up the [as a noun]ladder[end as]".

Rule for writing a paragraph about the long ladder when the player is atop the ladder and the long ladder is placed:
	say "I'm standing on top of the [as a noun]ladder[end as], which is leaning against the [as a noun]pipe[end as]."

Definition: a thing is below the ladder:
	if it is enclosed by the player, no;
	if it is the long ladder:
		if taking, yes;
		if the player is atop the ladder, no;
		yes;
	if it is a warehouse pipe, no;
	yes;

Definition: a thing is above the ladder:
	if it is enclosed by the player, no;
	if it is the long ladder:
		if taking, no;
		if the player is atop the ladder, yes;
		no;
	if it is a warehouse pipe, yes;
	no;

Instead of climbing the placed long ladder when the player is not atop the ladder:
	now the player is atop the ladder;
	say "I scramble [one of]to the top of the [as a noun]ladder[end as]. I'm now in reach of the [as a noun]pipe[end as][or]back up the [as a noun]ladder[end as][stopping].";

Instead of climbing the placed long ladder:
	say "I'm up here already, aren't I?"

Instead of doing something when something below the ladder is physically involved and the player is atop the ladder:
	auto-go down the ladder;
	continue the action.

Before going when the player is atop the ladder:
	auto-go down the ladder;
	continue the action;

To auto-go down the ladder:
	say "I climb back down the [as a noun]ladder[end as].";
	now the player is not atop the ladder;

To auto-go up the ladder:
	say "I climb back up the [as a noun]ladder[end as].";
	now the player is atop the ladder;

Before getting off when the player is atop the ladder:
	auto-go down the ladder instead.
Before going down when the player is atop the ladder:
	try getting off the long ladder instead.
Before going outside when the player is atop the ladder:
	try getting off the long ladder instead.

Instead of doing something when something above the ladder is physically involved and the player is not atop the ladder and the long ladder is placed and the long ladder is visible:
	auto-go up the ladder;
	continue the action.

Before dropping something when the player is atop the ladder:
	auto-go down the ladder;
	continue the action.

[ What if one thing is up the ladder and the other is down? We catch this and cope with it first. ]

First instead of doing something when something above the ladder is physically involved and something below the ladder is physically involved and the long ladder is placed and the long ladder is visible:
	let the top-thing be a random above the ladder thing which is physically involved;
	let the bottom-thing be a random below the ladder thing which is physically involved;
	if the top-thing is a warehouse pipe and the bottom-thing is a rope segment:
		[ in this case, does the player need to go get the rope from below? ]	
		if fastening something to:
			if the player is atop the ladder, auto-go down the ladder;
		continue the action;
		[ because the actual "rope & pipe" instead routines will kick in before the auto-up/down rules above by law of Specificity ] 
	else:
		say "But [the top-thing] [is-are] up [if the player is atop the ladder]here with me[else]there[end if] and [the bottom-thing] [is-are] down [if the player is atop the ladder]there[else]here with me[end if], so I don't see how I could.";

Section 4 - North Pipe

A warehouse pipe is a kind of thing. A warehouse pipe is usually scenery, privately-named. The printed name of a  warehouse pipe is usually "pipe". Understand  "fat", "pipe" as a warehouse pipe.

Before entering a warehouse pipe:
	say "That'd be a dangerous place to stand." instead.

Instead of jumping when the location contains a warehouse pipe:
	say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as] is too high to reach like that, if that's what you're thinking";
	if the long ladder is not handled and the location is the Loading Bay:
		say " - but the [as a noun]ladder[end as] sticking out of the pile of [as a noun]debris[end as] might help";
	say "."

The north pipe is a warehouse pipe, in the Loading Bay. "[if the player is atop the ladder]The [as a noun]pipe[end as] sticks out of the [as a noun]wall[end as] just level with the top of the [as a noun]ladder[end as][otherwise]The [as a noun]pipe[end as]'s a fair few feet up the wall[end if]. What it's for is anyone's guess."

Section 4a - Ladder and ropes

Instead of putting the long ladder against a warehouse pipe:
	try standing the long ladder up instead;

Before inserting the long ladder into a warehouse pipe:
	try standing the long ladder up instead;

Instead of standing the long ladder up when the player can see a warehouse pipe:
	fire TRIG_LADDER_PIPE;
	move the long ladder to the location;
	now the long ladder is placed;

Before throwing a rope segment at a warehouse pipe:
	try fastening the noun to the second noun instead;

Before wrapping a warehouse pipe with  a rope segment:
	try fastening the second noun to the noun instead;

Before putting a rope segment on a warehouse pipe:
	try fastening the noun to the second noun instead;

Before removing a rope segment from a warehouse pipe:
	try untying the noun from the second noun instead;


Section 4b - Auto-climbing the ladder

Before doing something when a warehouse pipe is physically involved:
	unless the long ladder is placed and the long ladder is in the location:
		unless we are putting the long ladder against the second noun or we are fastening a rope segment to the second noun or we are untying a rope segment from the second noun:
			if we are putting a rope segment on the second noun:
				say "[one of]I toss the end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] up towards the [as a noun]pipe[end as], but don't even get close.[or]I lob the [as a noun]rope[end as] again, but it's too heavy to get anywhere near the [as a noun]pipe[end as].[or]I can't do it from down here - it's too far![stopping]" instead;
			say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as]'s out of my reach." instead;

Section 4c - Non-interactions

Before entering or jumping on or climbing a warehouse pipe:
	say "I could probably scramble around on the [as a noun]pipes[end as] if I had a death-wish, except they don't go anywhere, just straight from one side of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] to the other." instead.

Before looking under or searching a warehouse pipe:
	say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as]s go across the ceiling of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as], in one side and out the other." instead.

Last instead of touching a warehouse pipe:
	say "The [as a noun]pipe[end as]s are cold."

Section 5 - Sliding Door

Rule for printing the description of the loading-bay door when in the Loading Bay:
	say "The huge metal [as a noun]door[end as] is made of solid iron that could be lifted up if, say, I was a twelve-foot Hotlands warrior.";

An unlocking rule for the loading-bay door when the location is the Loading Bay:
	say "I don't have a key for that [as a noun]door[end as]." instead;

Instead of unlocking the loading-bay door with something when the location is the Loading Bay:
	say "I don't have a key for that [as a noun]door[end as]." instead;

Instead of opening, pushing, pulling or turning the loading-bay door:
	say "Do you think I'd let [as a noun]Drake[end as] and [as a noun]Calvin[end as] hit me so hard if I was strong enough to lift this [as a noun]door[end as]?";

Instead of attacking the loading-bay door when the location is the Loading Bay:
	say "I don't stand a chance.";

Instead of fastening a rope segment to the loading-bay door:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] is sheer - there's nothing to tie the [as a noun]rope[end as] onto."

Section 6 - Crate

An enormous packing crate is scenery, in the Loading Bay. "An enormous [as a noun]packing crate[end as]. Whatever’s inside must be important because its wrapped around with iron security [as a noun]chains[end as], almost like they were afraid of whatever’s inside breaking out[if the north end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] is tied to the packing crate]. The [as a noun]rope[end as] is tied to the crate[end if]."

Understand "huge", "thick", "chains", "chain", "iron chain/chains", "security", "iron security chain/chains" as the enormous packing crate.

Rule for printing the description of the enormous packing crate when the warehouse door is open:
	say "The [as a noun]crate[end as] has dropped into the [as a noun]drain[end as], blocking my way out!";

The printed name of the enormous packing crate is "crate".

Instead of opening the enormous packing crate:
	say "There's no way I could get that open. It's locked up tighter than [as a noun]Brother Reloh[end as]'s sympathy."

Instead of unlocking the enormous packing crate with something:
	try opening the noun.

Instead of climbing or standing on the enormous packing crate:
	say "The packing [as a noun]crate[end as]'s too high to climb easily and too low to get me near anything useful. I'm staying put.";

Instead of entering the enormous packing crate when the player is clambering:
	try climbing the enormous packing crate instead.

Instead of attacking the enormous packing crate:
	say "I couldn't break into it[one of]. It'd be like a mayfly trying to break straight through the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] walls[once only]."

Instead of slicing the enormous packing crate with the knife:
	say "The [as a noun]knife[end as] can't cut the [as a noun]chains[end as], any more than I could chew through them."

Instead of eating the enormous packing crate:
	say "I'd be here for years, several thousand or so."

Instead of taking the packing crate:
	say "The [as a noun]crate[end as] is far too big and heavy for me to carry, I’d need to be a giant to even get my arms around it! But I could probably push it along the floor a little if I needed to.";
	
Before wrapping the packing crate with a rope segment:
	try fastening the second noun to the noun instead.

Instead of pushing the packing crate:
	try pushing the noun to north. [ helping hand! ] 

Rule for supplying a missing second noun when pushing the packing crate to:
	say "Where should I push the [as a noun]crate[end as]?";

Understand "push [enormous packing crate] down/into/through/in/to [air pocket]" as pushing it to. [Not a totally smart thing to do - i.e. the parser never normally generates such a command - but good enough for what we need]
Understand "push [enormous packing crate] down/through/in/to through/into/in/to [air pocket]" as pushing it to. [e.g. > push crate down into drain]
[ Understand "push [enormous packing crate] down/through in to [air pocket]" as pushing it to. [probable overkill] ]

Before inserting the enormous packing crate into the air pocket:
	try pushing the enormous packing crate to the air pocket instead.

Instead of pushing the enormous packing crate to a direction (called d):
	if d is down or d is north:
		try pushing the noun to the air pocket;
	otherwise if d is south:
		say "I put my shoulderblades to the side of the [as a noun]crate[end as] and dig my heels in and get it to move maybe an inch... not much further. Another look at the room is enough to convince me there’s no way I can push the [as a noun]crate[end as] all the way over there." instead;
	otherwise:
		say "[one of]I give the [as a noun]crate[end as] a little shove and it moves, but t[or]T[stopping]here's nowhere for it to go in that direction.";

Instead of pushing the enormous packing crate to the air pocket:
	abide by the crate pushing rules;
	assert that the south end of the rope is tied to the warehouse door issuing "Rope is tied to door.";
	assert that the north end of the rope is tied to the enormous crate issuing "Rope is tied to crate.";
	assert that exactly two rope segments are coiled issuing "Two rope segments coiled.";
	say "Putting my shoulderblades to the side of the [as a noun]crate[end as] and digging my heels into the floorboards, I manage to move the [as a noun]crate[end as] a tiny inch towards the [as a noun]drain[end as]. Every inch after that is easier as more and more of the [as a noun]crate[end as] is out of contact with the floor, until suddenly it’s moving and I nearly fall backwards with it.[paragraph break]The [as a noun]crate[end as] tumbles away into the [as a noun]drain[end as]. The [as a noun]rope[end as] goes taut. And across the hall there’s the sound of moving metal...";
	now the warehouse door is open;

The crate pushing rules are a rulebook.

A crate pushing rule when the enormous packing crate is unsuspended:
	say "I could push the [as a noun]crate[end as] down the [as a noun]drain[end as] but then it’d be gone and it’d probably block my way out." instead;

A crate pushing rule when the enormous packing crate is suspended:
	let r be the other end of the rope nearby;
	if r is off-stage or r is unanchored:
		say "I’ve attached this [as a noun]rope[end as] to the [as a noun]crate[end as] but it’s not tied off anywhere, so surely I’d lose the whole thing if I pushed the [as a noun]crate[end as] down the [as a noun]drain[end as]?" instead;

A crate pushing rule when no rope segment is coiled:
	say "Even if the [as a noun]rope[end as] was taut – which it isn’t, not by a long way – I don’t think this [as a noun]crate[end as]’s heavy enough to rip the [as a noun]door[end as] right out of the wall." instead;

A crate pushing rule when exactly one rope segment is coiled:
	say "There’s still so much slack in the [as a noun]rope[end as] even with it passing over one of the [as a noun]pipe[end as]s, that I’d just lose the [as a noun]crate[end as] down the hole if I did that." instead;

Section 7 - Rope

A rope segment is a kind of thing.

Interconnection relates one rope segment to another (called the other end). The verb to be terminated at implies the interconnection relation. 

Attachment relates one thing (called the anchor) to one rope segment. The verb to be tied to implies the reversed attachment relation.

A rope segment can be coiled. A rope segment is usually not coiled.

Definition: a rope segment (called the segment) is anchored:
	if the anchor of the segment is nothing, no;
	yes;

Definition: a rope segment (called the segment) is unanchored:
	unless the segment is anchored, yes;
	no;

Definition: a thing is suspended:
	if a rope segment is tied to it, yes;
	no;

Definition: a thing is unsuspended:
	unless it is suspended, yes;
	no;

To decide which rope segment is the rope nearby:
	if the location is the Loading Bay:
		decide on the north end of the rope;
	otherwise if the location is the Storage Bay:
		decide on the south end of the rope;

The north end of the rope is a rope segment, privately-named, in the Loading Bay. 

Rule for printing the description of the north end of the rope:
	say "An enormous coil of [as a noun]rope[end as], maybe enough to cross the [as a noun]Thymes[end as] and back."

Understand "rope", "end", "end of", "end of the" as the north end of the rope. The printed name of the north end of the rope is "rope".

Rule for printing the name of the rope while taking inventory:
	say "part of the rope";

Section 6.0 - Printing Rope

Rule for printing the description of a rope segment (called the segment):
	say "An enormous coil of [as a noun]rope[end as], maybe enough to cross the [as a noun]Thymes[end as] and back. ";
	unless the noun is held:
		say the room description details of the segment;
	otherwise:
		say paragraph break;

Rule for writing a paragraph about a rope segment (called the segment):
	if the middle of the rope is held:
		say "I'm holding ";
		if the segment is tied to nothing or the other end of the segment is off-stage:
			say "one end ";
		otherwise:
			say "the middle ";
		[end if]
		say "of a long [as a noun]rope[end as]. ";
	otherwise:
		say "There's a length of [as a noun]rope[end as] [if the player is atop the ladder]on the floor [end if]here. ";
	[end if]
	say the room description details of the segment;

To say the room description details of (the segment - a rope segment):
	say "It";
	if the segment is tied to something (called the anchor):
		say "'s tied to [the anchor] and";
	if the segment is coiled:
		say " is looped over a [as a noun]pipe[end as] near the ceiling and";
	if the middle of the rope is not held and the other end of the segment is off-stage and the segment is unanchored and the segment is not coiled:
		say " is coiled up in loops and";
	[end if]
	if the other end of the segment is on-stage:
		if the segment is coiled:
			if the other end of the segment is coiled:
				say " passes over the second [as a noun]pipe[end as] to the [if the segment is the north end of the rope]south[otherwise]north[end if]. It";
			else:
				say " slopes down to the [if the segment is the north end of the rope]south[otherwise]north[end if] at a sharp angle. It";
		else if the other end of the segment is coiled:
			say " disappears to the [if the segment is the north end of the rope]south[otherwise]north[end if] at a sharp angle up towards the roof. It";
		otherwise:
			say " snakes away to the [if the segment is the north end of the rope]south[otherwise]north[end if]. It";
		[end if]
	[end if]
	if the other end of the segment is tied to something and the segment is tied to something:
		say " is tied tightly at both ends.";
	else if the number of handled rope segments is zero:
		say " looks far too heavy for me to carry, but I could probably drag it around.";
	else:
		say " is too heavy for me to lift the whole thing[if the player is carrying a rope segment], but I'm managing to drag it around[end if].";

The south end of the rope is a rope segment, privately-named. The south end of the rope is terminated at the north end of the rope. The printed name is "rope". Understand "rope", "end", "end of", "end of the" as the south end of the rope.

The middle of the rope is a rope segment, privately-named. The printed name is "rope". Understand "rope", "middle", "middle of", "part", "part of" as the middle of the rope.

Rule for printing the name of a rope segment (called r) while clarifying the parser's choice of something:
	if r is the middle of the rope:
		say "part I'm holding";
	otherwise if the player holds the middle of the rope:
		if r is tied to something (called the anchor):
			say "end tied to [the anchor]";
		otherwise if r is coiled:
			say "end hanging down from the [as a noun]pipe[end as]";
		otherwise:
			say "length coiled up on the ground";

Rule for implicitly taking a rope segment (called r):
	say "(first taking [the r])[command clarification break]";
	try taking r;
	assert that the player carries the middle of the rope issuing "Player segment carried.";
	redirect the action from r to the middle of the rope;

Before dropping a rope segment when the player is carrying the middle of the rope:
	if the noun is the middle of the rope, continue the action;
	try dropping the middle of the rope instead.

Section 6.05 - Non-interactions with Rope

Instead of slicing a rope segment with the knife:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as]'s too thick to get through with this [as a noun]knife[end as]."

Instead of slicing a rope segment with the knife when the warehouse door is open:
	say "That'd cut off my last chance to get inside this place. I won't do it. There's too much at stake."

Instead of climbing a rope segment:
	say "[one of]I've never been much good with [as a noun]rope[end as]s - [as a noun]ladder[end as]s, yes, [as a noun]rope[end as]s no. I wouldn't make it as a sailor.[or]I'm not a monkey.[stopping]".

Instead of attacking a rope segment:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as]'s too fat to tear into pieces."
	
Instead of burning a rope segment:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] is [one of]soaked to its core from every time this place has flooded. It [once only]wouldn't burn even if I <Italic>had</Italic> something to light it with."

Instead of pushing a rope segment to a direction:
	if the player is not carrying a rope segment:
		carry out the implicitly taking activity with the noun;
	try going the second noun.

Instead of turning a rope segment:
	try pulling the noun instead;

Instead of touching a rope segment:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] is thick and fibrous."
		

Section 6.1 - Taking Rope

Instead of taking a rope segment when the player has the middle of the rope and the noun is unanchored and the noun is not coiled and the other end of the noun is off-stage:
	say "I couldn't carry the whole [as a noun]rope[end as]. It's the size of two of me!";

Instead of taking a rope segment when the player has the middle of the rope:
	say "I'm already holding the [as a noun]rope[end as] (I could give it a yank to coil it up, if that's what you meant.)";
[	try pulling the middle of the rope instead;]

Carry out taking a rope segment:
	now the player carries the middle of the rope instead;

Report taking a rope segment when the noun is unanchored and the other end of the noun is off-stage:
	say "I pick up one end of the long [as a noun]rope[end as]." instead;

Report taking a rope segment when the noun is anchored and the other end of the noun is off-stage:
	say "I've picked up the other end of the long [as a noun]rope[end as]." instead;

Report taking a rope segment:
	say "I take hold of the [as a noun]rope[end as]." instead;

Section 6.2 Going with Rope

The main warehouse is a region. The Loading Bay and Storage Bay are in the main warehouse.

Check going when the player carries the middle of the rope:
	unless the room gone to is in the main warehouse:
		try dropping the middle of the rope;
 		continue the action;

After going when the player carries the middle of the rope:
	consider the rope movement rules;
	continue the action;

The rope movement rules is a rulebook.

A rope movement rule:
	if the room gone to contains a rope segment and the room gone from contains an rope segment (called r):
		if r is coiled or r is anchored:
			remove the middle of the rope from play;
			say "I let go of this part of the [as a noun]rope[end as]." instead;

A rope movement rule:
	if the room gone from contains an unanchored rope segment (called the loose end) and the room gone to contains a rope segment:
		remove the loose end from play;
		say "I haul the [as a noun]rope[end as] back along the floor, coiling it up as I go." instead;

A rope movement rule:
	if the room gone from contains a rope segment (called the coil) and the other end of the coil is off-stage:
		move the other end of the coil to the room gone to;
		say "I drag the [as a noun]rope[end as] out along the floor behind me." instead;

Section 6.3 - Dropping Rope

Instead of dropping a rope segment when the middle of the rope is not carried:
	say "I'm not holding the [as a noun]rope[end as]!";

Instead of dropping a rope segment when the location does not contain a rope segment:
	remove the noun from play;
	if the location is the Loading Bay:
		move the north end of the rope to the location;
	otherwise if the location is the Storage Bay:
		move the south end of the rope to the location;
	say "I drop the end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] to the floor.";

Carry out dropping a rope segment:
	remove the noun from play instead;
	assert that the location contains a rope segment issuing "Location contains a rope segment.";

Report dropping a rope segment:
	say "I let go of the [as a noun]rope[end as][if the player is atop the ladder] and it falls to the floor[end if]." instead;

Section 6.4 - Looping Rope

The rope-looping rules are a rulebook.

A rope-looping rule when the rope nearby is coiled (this is the rope already coiled rule):
	say "I’d gain nothing from winding the [as a noun]rope[end as] any further around the [as a noun]pipe[end as]: it'd only make it harder to pull, and it wouldn't really make it any shorter." instead;

A rope-looping rule when the rope nearby is anchored (this is the rope tied down rule): 
	if the other end of the rope nearby is anchored:
		say "Both ends are already tied down!" instead;
	otherwise if the other end of the rope nearby is on-stage:
		say "I’ve only got the middle of the [as a noun]rope[end as], not a free end." instead;

A rope-looping rule (this is the can't reach the pipe rule):
	unless the long ladder is placed and the long ladder is in the location:
		say "[one of]I try slinging the [as a noun]rope[end as] up in the air and over the [as a noun]pipe[end as] – no success. It’s too heavy for me to get it any distance up before it’s back down on my head again.[or]I can’t reach the [as a noun]pipe[end as] from down here![stopping]" instead;

Instead of fastening a rope segment to a warehouse pipe:
	abide by the rope-looping rules;
	assert that the long ladder is in the location and the long ladder is placed issuing "The player can reach the rope.";
	let x be the rope nearby;
	now x is coiled;
	if the player is not atop the ladder:
		now the player is atop the ladder;
		say "[one of]I scramble a few rungs up the [as a noun]ladder[end as] with the end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] looped around my neck – not very safe, I know, but I’m working as fast as I can. Once I’m up high enough I[or]I scramble up the [as a noun]ladder[end as] and[stopping]";
	else:
		say "I";
	say " [one of]chuck the [as a noun]rope[end as] over the [as a noun]pipe[end as]. Great. It’s now hanging down like a pulley![or]toss the [as a noun]rope[end as] over the piping.[stopping]";

Section 6.5 - Tying rope

Definition: a thing is a cleat:
	if it is the enormous packing crate, yes;
	if it is the warehouse door, yes;
	no;

Before fastening a rope segment to a long ladder:
	say "[one of]That sounds like it would make things <Italic>very</Italic> complicated, and won't achieve anything either![or]I can't usefully tie the [as a noun]rope[end as] to that.[stopping]" instead.

Before fastening a rope segment to the player:
	say "[one of]I don't fancy trussing myself up for the [as a noun]Figure[end as] to come find me[or]I don't think that's going to help me here[stopping]." instead.

Instead of tangling or turning a rope segment:
	say "I could tie it to something - and probably more usefully than just tying it to itself!"

The rope-fastening rules are an object-based rulebook.

A rope-fastening rule for something suspended:
	say "I’d gain nothing by tying the [as a noun]rope[end as] up in loops." instead;

A rope-fastening rule for something unsuspended when the rope nearby is anchored:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] is already tied to [the second noun]: I’d gain nothing by tying it there again." instead;

Instead of fastening a rope segment to a cleat:
	abide by the rope-fastening rules for the second noun;
	now the rope nearby is tied to the second noun;
[ 
	c8TODO: Depends - if the other end of the rope is free and with me, I should stay holding it.
]
	remove the middle of the rope from play;
	continue the action;

After fastening a rope segment to a cleat when no on-stage rope segment is coiled:
	let r be the other end of the rope nearby;
	say "I fasten the free end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] to [the second noun][if r is on-stage and r is anchored]. The other end is tied to [the anchor of r] across the room but there’s an enormous amount of slack in between[end if].";

After fastening a rope segment to a cleat when exactly one on-stage rope segment is coiled:
	let r be the other end of the rope nearby;
	say "I tie off the free end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] to [the second noun][if r is on-stage and r is anchored]. The other end is tied to [the anchor of r] across the room but despite passing over one of the ceiling [as a noun]pipe[end as]s there’s still plenty of slack in the system[otherwise]. The other end passes over one of the ceiling [as a noun]pipe[end as]s and dangles free on the other side. I’ve made a pulley system, but it might not be enough to shift [the second noun][end if].";

After fastening a rope segment to a cleat when at least two on-stage rope segments are coiled:
	let r be the other end of the rope nearby;
	say "[if r is not anchored]I tie this end of my pulley system off to [the second noun].[otherwise]I haul down on this end of the [as a noun]rope[end as], feeling the fibres stretch. When the whole system is as taut as I can get it, I loop it through [the second noun] and tie it tighter than one of [as a noun]Drake[end as]’s Viennese burns. As the system relaxes the [as a noun]door[end as] seems to move maybe a second’s hand from the floor...[end if]";

Section 6.6 - Untying rope

First for supplying a missing second noun when untying a rope segment from:
	if the player can see a cleat:
		if the rope nearby is tied to a cleat:
			change the second noun to the anchor of the noun;

Before untying the middle of the rope from something:
	redirect the action from the middle of the rope to the rope nearby;

Instead of untying a rope segment from something:
	if the second noun is not a cleat:
		say "[The noun] isn't tied to [that or those for the second noun]!" instead;
	abide by the rope-untying rules;
	now the noun is tied to nothing;	
	say "[one of]It takes a bit of jiggling, but I work the knot free from [the second noun][or]I work the knot free from [the second noun][stopping].";

The rope-untying rules are a rulebook.

A rope-untying rule when the rope nearby is not anchored:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] isn’t tied to anything here." instead;

A rope-untying rule when the warehouse door is open:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] system is held so tight I can’t begin to get the knots to move." instead;

Instead of untying a rope segment from a warehouse pipe:
	abide by the rope-unlooping rules;
	now the noun is not coiled;
	say "I pull the [as a noun]rope[end as] back down from the [as a noun]pipe[end as].";

Section 6.7 - Unlooping rope

The rope-unlooping rules are a rulebook.

A rope-unlooping rule when the rope nearby is not coiled:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] isn’t looped over the [as a noun]pipe[end as] here!" instead;

A rope-unlooping rule when the rope nearby is anchored:
	if the other end of the rope nearby is on-stage:
		if the other end of the rope nearby is anchored:
			say "But both ends of the [as a noun]rope[end as] are tied down!" instead;

A rope-unlooping rule when the rope nearby is anchored:
	if the other end of the rope nearby is on-stage:
		remove the other end of the rope nearby from play;
		now the other end of the rope nearby is not coiled;
		now the rope nearby is not coiled;
		say "Holding the [as a noun]rope[end as] just by where I’ve tied it, I haul it arm over arm until finally it falls free of the [as a noun]pipe[end as] overhead." instead;

Section 6.8 - Pulling Rope

Before pulling a rope segment:
	unless the noun is the middle of the rope:
		carry out the implicitly taking activity with the noun;

Instead of pulling a rope segment:
	abide by the rope-pulling rules;

The rope-pulling rules are a rulebook.

A rope-pulling rule when the warehouse door is open:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] is already as taut at it’ll go, holding that [as a noun]door[end as] open!" instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the south end of the rope is anchored and no on-stage rope segment is coiled:
	say "[one of]I can pull all I like, but I’m never going to just pull the [as a noun]door[end as] away from the wall. It’s supposed to move upwards, like someone was pulling it from above![or]I need to get that [as a noun]rope[end as] to pull upwards, not along the floor![stopping]" instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the south end of the rope is anchored:
	say "[one of]There’s a tiny moment when I think I’m actually doing some good, before I try just hanging with all my weight on the [as a noun]rope[end as]. And that does no good. So what more can I do?[or]I’m simply not heavy enough to get that [as a noun]door[end as] to shift. I need something heavier than it is. A battleship maybe, or a small moon.[stopping]" instead;

A rope-pulling rule when at least two on-stage rope segments are anchored:
	say "I tug one way and the other. Both ends of this [as a noun]rope[end as] are firmly tied - but there's no way I can pull hard enough to the lift that [as a noun]door[end as]. Not on my own, anyway." instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the rope nearby is anchored and the other end of the rope nearby is off-stage:
	say "I heave my weight on the [as a noun]rope[end as] to check the knot: quite firm." instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the other end of the rope nearby is off-stage and the rope nearby is coiled:
	now the rope nearby is not coiled;
	say "I pull the [as a noun]rope[end as] clear from the [as a noun]pipe[end as]. The end whips [if the player is atop the ladder]down to [end if]the floor with a vengeance." instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the other end of the rope nearby is off-stage:
	say "The [as a noun]rope[end as] uncoils a little." instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the other end of the rope nearby is anchored:
	say "I heave my weight on the [as a noun]rope[end as] to check the knots in the other end: firm." instead;

A rope-pulling rule when the other end of the rope nearby is coiled:
	now the other end of the rope nearby is not coiled;
	remove the other end of the rope nearby from play;
	say "There’s a thump as the other end of the [as a noun]rope[end as] uncoils from the [as a noun]pipe[end as]. I drag it back across the floor and gather it up." instead;

A rope-pulling rule:
	remove the other end of the rope nearby from play;
	say "I pull the [as a noun]rope[end as] back over from the other end of the long room." instead;

Part 11 - Storage

Storage Bay is a room, south of the Loading Bay. "The south end of the enormous room. There's another [as a noun]door[end as] to the east: less formidable than the others except for a sign that says KEEP OUT. Sounds promising[if the Gas Platform is unvisited] - perhaps this is the cupboard where the [as a noun]Figure[end as] sleeps[end if][if the warehouse door is open].[paragraph break]The enormous iron [as a noun]door[end as] has lifted just a couple of handspans from the floor.[end if]." The printed name is "Storage".

The south pipe is a warehouse pipe, in the Storage Bay. "The [as a noun]pipe[end as]'s a fair few feet up the wall. What it's for is anyone's guess."

Instead of making to leave when in the Storage Bay:
	try going north.
Instead of going inside when in the Storage Bay:
	try going east.

Section 1 - Drain scenery

The drain_glimpse is a glimpse backdrop in Storage. The printed name is "drain". Understand "drain" as the drain_glimpse. The description is "The wide [as a noun]drain[end as] is to the north."

Instead of approaching or entering the drain_glimpse: try going north.


Chapter 2 - Warehouse Door

The warehouse door is a door, not open, not openable, not locked, not lockable, east of the Storage Bay, west of Gas Platform. "The enormous [as a noun]warehouse[end as] continues back to the north towards the [as a noun]drain[end as] I came up by. There’s a second fat [as a noun]pipe[end as] crossing the room here." The description is "The [as a noun]door[end as] is designed to slide up into the ceiling and in the centre of it is an [as a noun]iron ring[end as] for lifting. Presumably the [as a noun]Figure[end as] can just slide it up, but I certainly can’t[If the Warehouse Door is suspended and the warehouse door is closed]. The [as a noun]rope[end as] I’ve got tied to it might help though[otherwise if the warehouse door is open]. Instead I’ve got the [as a noun]rope[end as] tied to it, and my pulley system has lifted the whole thing about two feet from the floor[end if]."

Understand "iron door", "enormous door" as the warehouse door.


Instead of opening, pulling or pushing the closed warehouse door:
	say "It’s far heavier than me. I tug as best I can on the [as a noun]iron ring[end as] in the centre of the [as a noun]door[end as], but there’s no way I can move it unaided.";

Instead of opening, closing, pulling or pushing the open warehouse door:
	say "It was hard enough getting it this far! I shouldn’t be ungrateful!";

After going through the warehouse door:
	say "[one of]Lying on my belly I pull myself through the gap into darkness. Halfway through I have a sudden horrible mental image: the [as a noun]Figure[end as], standing by the [as a noun]crate[end as] in the [as a noun]drain[end as] with a candle to the [as a noun]rope[end as] that’s holding the enormous [as a noun]door[end as] above me. As quick as I can I pull myself through[or]I crawl under the [as a noun]door[end as][stopping][if the player carries the long ladder]. Then I reach back through the gap and pull the [as a noun]ladder[end as] after me[end if].";
	continue the action;

Understand "gap" , "crack" as the warehouse door when the warehouse door is open.
Understand "crack of light", "light" as the warehouse door when the warehouse door is open and the location is the Gas Platform.


Rule for printing the description of the warehouse door when in the Gas Platform:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] is open a crack."

Section 2b - a Narrow Gap for passing things through

Instead of inserting a rope segment into the open warehouse door:
	say "There's no slack to push the [as a noun]rope[end as] anywhere."

Instead of inserting something carried by the player into the open warehouse door:
	move the noun to the Gas Platform;
	say "I push [the noun] through the crack under the [as a noun]door[end as]."

Instead of inserting something into the open warehouse door:
	say "I can't fit [the noun] through the gap under the [as a noun]door[end as]!"
	
The KEEP OUT sign is part of the warehouse door. The description is "It says KEEP OUT."

Instead of doing something when the KEEP OUT sign is physically involved:
	say "I can't reach the sign, and anyway, it's clear enough."
 
The iron ring is part of the warehouse door. The description is "The [as a noun]ring[end as] is set in the middle of the [as a noun]door[end as]."



Instead of pulling or taking the iron ring:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] is way too heavy for me to lift (maybe if I was an army of a hundred men, I could do it. Maybe.)"

Instead of fastening something to the iron ring:
	try fastening the noun to the warehouse door instead.

Before inserting a rope segment into the iron ring:
	try fastening the noun to the warehouse door instead.

Before untying a rope segment from the iron ring:
	try untying the noun from the warehouse door instead.


Part 12 - Gas Platform

Chapter 1 - Description

Gas Platform is a room.

Instead of going inside when in the Gas Platform:
	try going down.
Instead of making to leave when in the Gas Platform:
	try going west.

Gas Platform can be murky or bright. Gas Platform is murky.

Rule for printing the name of Gas Platform:
	say "[if Gas Platform is murky]Darkness[otherwise]Gas Platform[end if]";

Rule for printing the description of the Gas Platform:
	if the Gas Platform is murky:
		say "I’m on some kind of metal [as a noun]platform[end as] in the dark. The crack of light below the [as a noun]door[end as] is enough to make out a few controls beside me and a set of [as a noun]stairs[end as] leading down to the north. Down seems an odd way to be going since we’re above the river. There’s probably [as a noun]serpents[end as] down there, or [as a noun]octopuses[end as], or [as a noun]water-camels[end as].";
	otherwise:
		say "I’m on a metal [as a noun]platform[end as] that sticks out over an impossibly large space, at least twice the size of the [as a noun]warehouse[end as] back west. The room below is deep: the original staircase to the north gives up halfway down[if the long ladder is stuck]. Beyond that there’s the [as a noun]ladder[end as][end if].[paragraph break]In the light of a hundred gas-lamps I can see the carved earth walls. This whole place has been scooped clean out of the rock and it’s filled with rows upon rows of [as a noun]silent metal men[end as]: a whole [as a noun]army[end as] of [as a noun]statues[end as]. Perhaps somewhere amongst them is the [as a noun]Figure[end as].";

Rule for writing a paragraph about the warehouse door when in the Gas Platform:
	now the warehouse door is mentioned;
	do nothing;

Some monsters are animals, scenery, in the Gas Platform. Understand "octopus", "octopuses", "water-camels", "water camel/camels", "water-camel", "serpent", "serpents" as the monsters. 

Before doing something with the monsters:
	say "I can't see them, but I'm sure they're there." instead.

Instead of jumping when in the Gas Platform and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "I'd break my neck."
Instead of jumping when in the Gas Platform and the Gas Platform is bright:
	say "No chance! I'd twist my ankle at the bottom of this pit and I don't see those [as a noun]statues[end as] lifting me back up!"

Chapter 2 - Staircase

Section 1 - Staircase

The broken staircase is a door, open, not openable, scenery, down from the Gas Platform, up from the Warehouse Basement.

Before going north from the Gas Platform: try going down instead.

Understand "set of", "stair", "stairs", "step", "steps" as the broken staircase.

Rule for printing the name of the broken staircase when in the Gas Platform:
	say "staircase";

Rule for printing the description of the broken staircase when in the Gas Platform:
	if the Gas Platform is murky:
		say "I can’t see much beyond the first few steps.";
	otherwise:
		say "The [as a noun]staircase[end as] gives up halfway to the floor. It’s like the [as a noun]platform[end as] was sticking its tongue out at the [as a noun]army[end as] below. It’s probably them who chopped it in half."

The broken staircase can be fixed. The broken staircase is not fixed.

Instead of climbing the broken staircase:
	try going down.

Instead of going through the broken staircase from the Gas Platform when the broken staircase is not fixed:
	if the Gas Platform is murky:
		say "[one of]I feel my way down the first few steps: then the metal underfoot starts to rock and sway. Perhaps all this is built on the [as a noun]water[end as] and is floating – or perhaps the supports have rotted and the [as a noun]staircase[end as] is about to fall. Terrified, I race back up to the safety of the [as a noun]platform[end as].[or]I’m not going down that [as a noun]staircase[end as] in the dark. There’s something <Italic>wrong</Italic> about it.[stopping]";
	otherwise:
		say "The [as a noun]staircase[end as] ends halfway to the ground, in midair with nothing holding it up.";

After going through the broken staircase from the Gas Platform:
	say "There’s no way I’m turning my back on those [as a noun]metal men[end as], so I swing round to the back of the [as a noun]ladder[end as] and climb down that, even though there’s a moment when I’m hanging out over an enormous drop.[paragraph break]None of them move as I make my way down.";
	continue the action;
	
Instead of pulling or pushing the broken staircase:
	say "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] are quite secure - what there are of them, anyway."
Instead of attacking the broken staircase:
	say "They're broken already."
	
Instead of searching the broken staircase:
	say "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] lead halfway down to nowhere.";

Section 2 - Ladder

Instead of dropping the long ladder when in the Gas Platform:
	try standing the long ladder up;

Instead of putting the long ladder on the broken staircase when in the Gas Platform:
	try standing the long ladder up;

Instead of fastening the long ladder to the broken staircase when in the Gas Platform:
	try standing the long ladder up;

Instead of climbing the long ladder when the long ladder is in the Gas Platform:
	try going down.

Instead of going down when in the Gas Platform and the player is carrying the long ladder and the gas Platform is bright:
	try standing the long ladder up;
	continue the action;

Instead of standing the long ladder up when in the Gas Platform:
	if the Gas Platform is murky:
		say "It’s far too dark to go waving the [as a noun]ladder[end as] around in here. I’d probably knock myself out.";
	otherwise:
		move the long ladder to the location;
		now the long ladder is stuck;
		now the broken staircase is fixed;
		say "It takes a lot of awkwardness to get the [as a noun]ladder[end as] over the side and pointing down towards the floor. That's when I fumble it and let go. It lands with a crash - the top disappears into the dark - then swings back, settling against the [as a noun]platform[end as]. I exhale a long breath.[paragraph break]None of the [as a noun]metal men[end as] react.";

Chapter 3 - Gas controls

Section 1 - Control Panel

Some gas controls are scenery, in the Gas Platform. "The [as a noun]platform[end as] has a control box fitted to it. [if the Gas Platform is murky]There may be more controls out there in the dark but what’s visible is a [as a noun]button[end as], a large [as a noun]lever[end as], and a red-painted [as a noun]bolt[end as].[otherwise]These are the controls for the gas-lights. There must be a big tank under the [as a noun]platform[end as] or something.[end if]"

Understand "control", "box", "platform", "railing" as the gas controls.

Instead of switching on the gas controls:
	say "I don’t know what they do, let alone how to make them work. There’s nothing for it, Wren, but to guess and hope you don’t break anything!";
	
Instead of opening the gas controls:
	say "The gas [as a noun]controls[end as] are welded shut."
	
Instead of looking under the gas controls when the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "There's darkness down there."

Instead of looking under the gas controls when the Gas Platform is bright:
	say "The floor of the cavern is a nasty drop below me."
	
Instead of looking under the gas controls when the Gas Platform is bright and the long ladder is stuck:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as] leads down to the floor of the cavern."

Instead of pushing, pulling, turning the gas controls:
	say "The [as a noun]controls[end as] and [as a noun]platform[end as] are all welded to one another."

Rule for writing a paragraph about the gas controls when the ignition button is ignited and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "I'm surrounded by a ring of tiny blue [as a noun]stars[end as].";

Section 1b - Some fires, little and large

A ring of tiny stars is a thing. The description is "Tiny points of gentle [as a noun]blue light[end as], all around me, the size of pinheads. They're close enough to touch except for some reason I can't touch them (or they're infinitely far away, there's no way I can be sure.)". Understand "tiny", "points", "points of", "gentle", "blue", "light" as the ring of tiny stars. 

Instead of doing something when the ring of tiny stars are physically involved:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]stars[end as] seem to be just a fraction beyond wherever I think they are, like they were slowly growing and moving away at the same time.[or]I can't seem to reach them.[stopping]";

Some gas flames are a backdrop. The description is "Blue [as a noun]flames[end as] burn all around the walls of this cavernous space, gleaming off the sides of the [as a noun]men[end as][if the location is the gas Platform] below[end if]."  

Understand "gas-lamps", "lamp", "light", "lights", "gas-light", "gas-lights", "gaslamps", "gaslamp", "gaslight", "gaslights", "lamps", "carved/earth walls", "carved earth walls", "hundred" as the gas flames.

Before doing something when the backdrop-walls are involved and the gas flames are visible:
	redirect the action from the backdrop-walls to the gas flames, and try that instead.

Instead of doing something when the gas flames are physically involved:
	say "They're well out of my reach."

Before smelling the gas flames:
	say "I can't smell them now they're lit." instead.

Before listening to the gas flames:
	say "They make a low noise, like breathing." instead.

Section 2 - Ignition Bolt

An ignition bolt is part of the gas controls. The description of the ignition bolt is "[if the Gas Platform is bright or the ignition button is ignited]Around the [as a noun]bolt[end as] is engraved HI, OFF and IGN. Whatever that means. There's not even an arrow to say which way it's pointing.[otherwise]The [as a noun]bolt[end as]’s just a [as a noun]bolt[end as], but knowing my luck I’ll turn it and find it was the only thing holding this [as a noun]platform[end as] up.[end if]".

Understand "red", "red-painted", "red painted", "painted", "bolt" as the ignition bolt.

The ignition bolt has a number called the setting. The setting of the ignition bolt is 1.

Instead of smelling when in the Gas Platform and the setting of the ignition bolt is greater than 1 and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "There's a strong smell, almost metallic, filling the air."
	
Instead of smelling when in the Gas Platform and setting of the ignition bolt is 1 and setting of the ignition bolt has been 2 and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "That strange smell has gone now."

Instead of listening when in the Gas Platform and the setting of the ignition bolt is greater than 1 and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "Hissing: steady, incessant, deadly."

Instead of listening when in the Gas Platform and setting of the ignition bolt is not 1 and setting of the ignition bolt has been 1 and the Gas Platform is murky:
	say "The room is quiet again."

Instead of pushing or pulling the ignition bolt:
	say "[as a noun]Bolts[end as] are for turning, not for [if pushing]pushing[else]pulling[end if][one of] (<Italic>To each its function, to none diversity</Italic>, as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] would say)[once only]."

CANT_TURN_WITH_HANDS is a trigger. 

Instead of turning or opening the ignition bolt when CANT_TURN_WITH_HANDS is unfired:
	fire CANT_TURN_WITH_HANDS;

Rule for firing CANT_TURN_WITH_HANDS:
	say "I can’t turn it with just my hands. Which might even be lucky.";

Instead of turning or opening the ignition bolt when the player is not carrying the wrench:
	fire CANT_TURN_WITH_HANDS.

Instead of turning or opening the ignition bolt:
	try unscrewing the ignition bolt with the wrench.

Instead of unscrewing the ignition bolt with the wrench:
	abide by the bolt-turning rules;

Instead of screwing the ignition bolt in with the wrench:
	try unscrewing the noun with the second noun instead;

The bolt-turning rules are a rulebook.

A bolt-turning rule when the Gas Platform is bright:
	say "I've already got the lights working. I don't want to be in the dark now I've seen what's down there." instead;

A bolt-turning rule when the long lever is not armed:
	say "The [as a noun]bolt[end as] doesn’t seem to turn: partly because the lever’s in the way of the [as a noun]wrench[end as] handle." instead;

A bolt-turning rule when the ignition button is not ignited:
	change the setting of the ignition bolt to the remainder after dividing the setting of the ignition bolt by 3; [0, 1, 2]
	increment the setting of the ignition bolt by 1; [1, 2, 3]
	if the setting of the ignition bolt is:
		-- 2:
			say "I heave the [as a noun]bolt[end as] around. The room is suddenly filled by a hissing sound, like a thousand baby cobras coiling and getting ready to strike! A strange smell comes with them." instead;
		-- 3:
			say "I turn the [as a noun]bolt[end as] further. The baby cobras grow up into hot-headed angry adult cobras the size of oak trees. I’m almost deafened by the noise." instead;
		-- 1:
			say "I heave the bolt further still. Abruptly, the room falls quiet." instead;

A bolt-turning rule when the ignition button is ignited:
	now the Gas Platform is bright;
	remove the monsters from play;
	remove the ring of tiny stars from play;
	move the gas flames backdrop to all gas-lit warehouse rooms;
	move glimpse_men to the Gas Platform;
	say "I heave the [as a noun]bolt[end as] around and the [as a noun]stars[end as] on the wall erupt into [as a noun]flame[end as], filling the chamber with light! And what a chamber – deep and wide. This is like standing inside the clock of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] all over again. The room below must be carved out of the rock below the riverbed, and its walls are crisscrossed with [as a noun]pipe[end as]s which are now providing light.[paragraph break]And the room is full of people. [as a noun]Men[end as], standing in rows and staring at me. I freeze myself to the spot. They do the same. It's like the room was filled with mirrors reflecting my image. I wave a hand but nothing happens. Nothing at all. Then I realise that they're [as a noun]statues[end as] - [as a noun]statues[end as] in full armour - gleaming metal [as a noun]statues[end as], more than I could count without growing thirty or forty more hands.[paragraph break]Whatever they are they must be connected to the [as a noun]Figure[end as]. There’s nothing for it, Wren, but to get a closer look somehow..." instead;

Definition: a room is gas-lit warehouse:
	if it is the Warehouse Basement, yes;
	if it is the Gas Platform, yes;
	no.

Section 3 - Lever

A long lever is part of the gas controls. The description of the long lever is "[if the Gas Platform is bright]A long metal [as a noun]lever[end as] labeled IGN in red letters.[otherwise]It feels like a metal [as a noun]lever[end as], not as heavy as the one on the [as a noun]Difference Engine[end as] but still needing both hands to pull.[end if]"

The long lever can be armed. The long lever is not armed.

Instead of pushing or turning the long lever:
	say "[as a noun]Levers[end as] are for pulling[one of] (well, levers like this one are, anyway.)[or].[stopping]";


Instead of pulling or taking or switching on the armed long lever:
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as]’s already pulled up as far as possible.";

Instead of pulling or taking or switching on the long lever:
	now the long lever is armed;
	say "[one of]Pulling the [as a noun]lever[end as] takes all the strength I’ve got left (which isn’t too much). In reply f[or]The [as a noun]lever[end as] moves more easily this time. F[stopping]rom [if the Gas Platform is bright]all around[else]the darkness[end if] comes the sound of a thousand tiny clicks.";

Section 4 - Ignition

An ignition button is part of the gas controls. The description of the ignition button is "[if the Gas Platform is bright]A large brass [as a noun]button[end as] labeled IGN.[otherwise]Just underneath the [as a noun]lever[end as] is a large brass [as a noun]button[end as]. I can just make out three letters, side-by-side: I, G and N.[end if]"

The ignition button can be ignited. The ignition button is not ignited.

Instead of pulling or turning the ignition button:
	say "[as a noun]Buttons[end as] are for pressing." 

Instead of pushing or switching on the ignition button:
	abide by the ignition rules;	

The ignition rules are a rulebook.

An ignition rule when the ignition button is ignited:
	now the long lever is not armed;
	say "The [as a noun]lever[end as] snaps back. There is that clicking sound again, but [if the Gas Platform is bright]nothing else happens[else]the tiny blue [as a noun]stars[end as] are unaffected[end if]." instead.

The first ignition rule when the long lever is not armed:
	say "I push the [as a noun]button[end as]. Huh. Doesn't seem to do anything." instead;

An ignition rule when the setting of the ignition bolt is 1:
	now the long lever is not armed;
	say "I push the [as a noun]button[end as]. The [as a noun]lever[end as] drops back suddenly almost taking my arm off! There's that clicking noise all around again." instead;

An ignition rule when the setting of the ignition bolt is 2:
	now the ignition button is ignited;
	move the ring of tiny stars to the Gas Platform;
	now the long lever is not armed;
	say "I push the [as a noun]button[end as]. The [as a noun]lever[end as] drops back suddenly almost taking my arm off! At the same time, the room is filled by a snapping noise and suddenly the darkness is full of [as a noun]stars[end as]! A whole sky of [as a noun]blue points[end as]. Where am I now? Outside? It's a huge space, wherever it is. The hissing noise has stopped, too." instead;

An ignition rule when the setting of the ignition bolt is 3:
	say "I push down on the [as a noun]button[end as] but it refuses to press. One of the cobras must have gotten stuck underneath it or something." instead;

Chapter 4 - New Wrench

The wrench can be rusty or shiny. The wrench is rusty.

After going to the Gas Platform when the wrench is rusty and the player does not have the wrench and the wrench is not in main warehouse:
	now the wrench is shiny;
	move the wrench to Gas Platform;
	continue the action;

Rule for printing the description of the shiny wrench:
	say "This must be why whoever-it-was threw away that other [as a noun]wrench[end as]. This one's lighter, stronger and shinier, the same way the [as a noun]Archbishop[end as]’s teeth compare to mine.";

Chapter 5 - Glimpse of the Men

Some glimpse_men are scenery, people. Understand "army of", "silent", "gleaming", "metal", "men", "man", "statue", "statues",  "army", "rows", "row" , "full", "in full", "armour" as the glimpse_men. The printed name is "gleaming metal statues". The description is "Row on row of [as a noun]metal men[end as], all staring straight ahead like unwound hour-hands."

Instead of entering or approaching the glimpse_men:
	try going down instead.

Instead of asking the glimpse_men about something:
	say "They are as silent as the dead."
Instead of talking to the glimpse_men:
	say "They are as silent as the dead."
Instead of telling the glimpse_men about something:
	say "They are as silent as the dead."

Instead of doing something when the glimpse_men are physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]men[end as] are below me, filling the floor of this cavernous room."

Part 13 - Warehouse

The Warehouse Basement is a room. "I’m standing in a forest of eyes, and arms, and hands. There are [as a noun]metal men[end as] all around me, on every side, all slightly different. The flickering [as a noun]gas-lights[end as] make it look and sound like they’re breathing. Any one might be about to reach out a hand and grab my shoulder… I need to work out the [as a noun]Figure[end as]’s plan as quickly as possible and then get out of here. In one piece!"

Instead of going inside when in the Warehouse Basement:
	say "I'm far enough in for my liking already!".
Instead of making to leave when in the Warehouse Basement:
	try going up.

The printed name of the Warehouse Basement is "Warehouse"

Rule for printing the name of the broken staircase when in the Warehouse Basement:
	say "ladder";

Rule for printing the description of the broken staircase when in the Warehouse Basement:
	say "The [as a noun]ladder[end as]’s my ticket out of here, back up to the [as a noun]platform[end as]. (I don’t want to think about how I’m going to get back down that [as a noun]drain[end as]!)";

Instead of going through the broken staircase from the Warehouse Basement:
	say "I’m not running till I’ve worked out what the [as a noun]Figure[end as]’s up to. Once I know that, <Italic>then</Italic> I’m running.";

Before listening when in the Warehouse Basement:
	say "The fuzz from the gas [as a noun]flames[end as] echoes between the [as a noun]soldiers[end as] like a whisper." instead.

The army of metal statues is scenery, in the Warehouse Basement. "Each [as a noun]man[end as] is made of fine steel and brass and stands upright, six foot tall. Their faces are bare [as a noun]faceplates[end as] with tiny [as a noun]glass eyes[end as], but they all look like they’re crying because of deep [as a noun]channels[end as] for rain run-off that run from forehead to chin. Each one has a small [as a noun]hatch[end as] in the middle of their [as a noun]chest[end as], a tiny fraction ajar."



Understand "steel", "brass", "mechanical", "men", "man", "chest", "hatch", "small hatch", "hatches", "figures", "faceplate", "channels", "hands",  "arms", "faces", "faceplates", "tiny", "glass", "eyes", "chests" as the army of metal statues.

Before smelling or listening to the army of metal statues:
	say "They are silent, lifeless metal. Aren't they?" instead.

Instead of doing something when the army of metal statues is physically involved:
	say "With shaking fingers I open the [as a noun]hatch[end as] on the front of the nearest [as a noun]man[end as]. No surprise maybe to find he’s full of clockwork, but it’s clockwork like I’ve never seen before: cogs so small it’s like his whole body is infested with woodlice and scrambling maggots. It’s like someone had taken the whole of the [as a noun]Difference Engine[end as] and shrunk it to fit.[paragraph break]Except there’s obviously something missing. All those cogs would need a spring about the size of the [as a noun]metal man[end as]’s head and it would need to be placed right in the middle where it could unwind to every part of his body. But in the perfect spot there’s nothing but a gap, like someone built this machine but forgot to give it a heart. What good are these [as a noun]men[end as] if all they do is stand in line?[paragraph break]That’s when I notice the [as a noun]seal[end as]. Something embossed on the inside of the [as a noun]chest hatch[end as]. I take a step back to see it more clearly – a winding key above an ocean wave, familiar from somewhere – and step into something firm and solid. A [as a noun]man[end as] out of line?[paragraph break]A hand grips my throat. 'Who sent you here?' demands a voice, smooth and icy. A voice I know well, that I should have expected. 'Who else knows about this place? Tell me before I crush every pinion in your neck.'[paragraph break]My toes are scrabbling on - off - the ground. I try to scream but nothing comes out beyond a whisper. I can see the outline of the [as a noun]Figure[end as]’s cowl and beyond that, for the first time, almost make out an unhappy face underneath.[paragraph break]'You’ll tell me or you’ll die here,' the [as a noun]Figure[end as] says ever-so-softly. 'You’ll die cold and alone like the rat you are.'[paragraph break]The room is getting darker. The tank for the [as a noun]gas lamps[end as] must be running low. Soon it’ll be me and the [as a noun]Figure[end as] and all these men, and no-one will ever find me or know where I’ve gone. Or is that someone? In the background? A shape, a shadow, my imagination or…[paragraph break]'[as a noun]Covalt[end as]?' I whisper, in desperation. '[as a noun]Covalt[end as], is that…'[paragraph break]Then the lights disappear completely. The heavy cord of the [as a noun]clock-key[end as] round my neck has become tangled while I was asleep. The [as a noun]stars[end as] are coming out. I’m underwater again.";
	end the chapter;
	fire SE_RETURN_1;
	move Covalt to Covalt's Bedroom;
	move the player to the feather bed;
	change the current script to {SE_RETURN_0, SE_RETURN_2}

Book 9 - Return to Covalt's

Return to Covalt's is a scene. Return to Covalt's begins when the player can see the army of metal statues. Return to Covalt's ends when the player carries the decoy Perpetuum Mobile.

[ we separate the states for the second section, to avoid rule conflicts. ]
Covalt has a number called the second-state. The second-state of Covalt is 1.

When Return to Covalt's begins:
	now the state of Covalt is 10.
	[ We turn off previous conversation topics!! ]

Part 1 - Bedroom

Chapter 1 - Description

Rule for printing the description of Covalt's Bedroom during Return to Covalt's:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s little back room. The [as a noun]skylight[end as]’s still got a Wren-shaped hole in it but at least the bits of [as a noun]balloon[end as] have been tidied away into a corner with all the rest of a clockmaker’s [as a noun]junk[end as].";

After going north from Covalt's Bedroom when Return to Covalt's is happening and Covalt is in Covalt's Bedroom: [horrible unnatural language]
	if the second-state of Covalt is:
		-- 1: say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] follows me, his expression anxious.";
		-- 2: say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] follows me. 'You looking for something, are you?'";
		-- 3: say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] follows me. 'Sit still, won’t you? You’re giving me frets.'";
		-- 4: say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] follows me.";
	try looking;
	move Covalt to Clock Shop;

Chapter 2 - Events

SE_RETURN_0 is a scripted event. [dummy]

SE_RETURN_1 is a scripted event. The display text is "There’s something pinching my nose. And again. Regular, like a second-hand – maybe he’s tied me to a clock, I’m thinking, maybe I’m going round and round the face on the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]…[paragraph break]I open my eyes and two black birds flutter away, startled.[paragraph break]'There you are. Still in there? That’s good enough.' The voice is old and crumbling like the stones of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] walls. [as a noun]Covalt[end as]. 'I found you on my doorstep. Hour ago, or more than that. Wasn’t sure you were breathing. Don’t know how people tick and you can’t wind them back up when they're gone.' Behind his thick brows I can see sympathy. 'I'm glad you're all right.'"

SE_RETURN_2 is a scripted event. The display text is "'Found this with you,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] adds, holding up a [as a noun]scrap of paper[end as] that looks like a postage stamp in his gigantic hand. 'Stay away – this is what it says, you see, not what I'm telling you. Stay away or the child will… Well.' The old man pauses, awkwardly. 'It says to stay away, whichever-wards.'"

After firing SE_RETURN_1:
	change the second-state of Covalt to 1;

After firing SE_RETURN_2:
	move the second-scrap to Covalt.

The second-scrap is a privately-named thing. The printed name is "scrap of paper". Understand "scrap", "scrap of", "paper" as the second-scrap. 

Before asking Covalt for the second-scrap:
	try asking Covalt about "note" instead.

Before taking or examining the second-scrap:
	try asking Covalt about "note" instead.

Instead of doing something when the second-scrap is physically involved:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s holding it." 

