Instead of doing something when the Perpetuum Mobile diagram is involved and the second-state of Covalt is 3:
	if we are not giving the perpetuum mobile diagram to Covalt:
		try giving the perpetuum mobile diagram to Covalt instead;

Instead of giving or showing the Perpetuum Mobile diagram to Covalt when the second-state of Covalt is less than 3:
	say "He doesn’t even look. He’s thinking about something else.";

Instead of giving or showing the Perpetuum Mobile diagram to Covalt when the second-state of Covalt is 3:
	remove the Perpetuum Mobile diagram from play;
	now the player carries the silver coins;
	change the second-state of Covalt to 4;
	say "'Wait now, what’s that? What are you doing with that?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s voice is shaking, unless I’m still feeling dizzy. He’s pointing at the [as a noun]diagram[end as] of the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] in my hands like it was a two-foot rat. 'Did I tell you could go walking off with that?'[paragraph break]'I...'[paragraph break]Then the grin breaks out across his face; like a sunrise through dirty windows. 'Because that’s a bloody brilliant thought from a brain as small as yours. Brilliant. I was saying I couldn’t build a [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as], wasn’t I? But I can always build a box. Doesn’t take nothing to build a box. Because a trap is just a nice box with something nasty inside. Here.'[paragraph break]Digging in a pocket he pulls out three screws, a key, a length of wire coiled into a helix, a plane file, a square of sandpaper and then finally, three shiny silver [as a noun]minutes[end as] that make my eyes light up.[paragraph break]'There’s an [as a noun]autopothecary[end as] over the street,' he says gleefully. 'You get yourself in there and buy the strongest [as a noun]sleeping drug[end as] you’re old enough to buy. Then we’ll spring-load it into a [as a noun]decoy[end as] [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] and next morning there’ll be a sleeping [as a noun]Figure[end as] in the Cathedral vaults ready to be locked up and forgotten.'[paragraph break]";
	if the location is the Clock Shop:
		say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] smiles a wicked smile and then turns to rummaging across his worktop. 'I’ll get some bits together. You get going with that [as a noun]money[end as].'";
	otherwise:
		say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] drags me through into the main workshop and starts rummaging across his worktop. 'I’ll get some bits together. Get yourself going with that [as a noun]money[end as].'";
		move Covalt to the Clock Shop;
		move the player to the Clock Shop;


