CT_COV_R4_MYSELF  is a conversation topic.  The enquiry text is "'What happens to me after we catch the [as a noun]Figure[end as]?'". The response text is "The old [as a noun]giant[end as] looks up at me with some sympathy. 'Since this plan of ours doesn’t have a wet flame’s chance of working,' he says very sensibly, 'we’d better not waste time worrying about that.' But I can see he’s not being honest.".

CT_COV_R4_BALLOON is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Breaking into the vaults will be nothing compared to that [as a noun]balloon[end as] ride.'". The response text is "'Idiot,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] replies. 'You’ll trip over your feet and that’ll be that.'".
CT_COV_R4_CATHEDRAL is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'What happens if I don’t make it back into the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]?'". The response text is "'You will,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says. 'That’s not the risky part of the plan, so you’ve got to.'".
CT_COV_R4_CLERGY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'If they catch me in there...'". The response text is "'Then don’t let them catch you,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] growls. 'The point is they catch <Italic>him</Italic>. So let’s make sure that’s how it happens.'".
CT_COV_R4_FIGURE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'And then when they find the [as a noun]Figure[end as], passed out...'". The response text is "'Holding what looks like the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]... It'll be a short sentence.' He draws one finger across his throat. 'And then we wind out what to do with the real [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as],' he sighs.".
CT_COV_R4_PEOPLE is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "He won’t know them.".
CT_COV_R4_COVALT is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'Can you really make something that’ll look convincing? The [as a noun]Figure[end as] is no idiot...'". The response text is "'I can,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] replies stiffly, 'and I’ll do it even better if you stop with your rusty talking.'".
CT_COV_R4_RAVENS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'If you can make something that looks like a [as a noun]raven[end as]...' I begin.". The response text is "He pauses for a moment, then smiles. 'Kind of you, street-rat. So.'".
CT_COV_R4_DECOY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "'How's the [as a noun]decoy[end as] going?' I ask.". The response text is "'All the better for not having questions about it,' he growls smartly.".
CT_COV_R4_MONEY is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[if the sleeping drug has been on-stage]'I hope this [as a noun]drug[end as] is worth all that [as a noun]money[end as],' I complain.[else]'This is too much [as a noun]money[end as],' I complain. 'I can't take it.'[end if]". The response text is "'A clockmaker of my calibre can afford to throw thre'pence to a rat,' he replies, gruffly offended. 'Now [if the sleeping drug has been on-stage]give that thing over and let's get this done.'[else]get moving and spend it how I told you.'[end if]".
CT_COV_R4_DRUGS is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[if the sleeping drug has been on-stage]'I hope I bought the right thing,' I say.[else]'What sort of [as a noun]drug[end as] should I make?' I ask.". The response text is "[if the sleeping drug has been on-stage]'There's only one way we'll know,' says [as a noun]Covalt[end as], holding his hand out to me.[else]'Something to knock that [as a noun]Figure[end as] out for a week,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says coldly. 'Don't spare the whip.'[end if]".

CT_COV_R4_CLOCKWORK is a conversation topic.
CT_COV_R4_SAINTS is a conversation topic.
CT_COV_R4_DIFFERENCEENGINE is a conversation topic.
CT_COV_R4_PERPETUUM is a conversation topic.

Rule for firing CT_COV_R4_CLOCKWORK:
	fire CT_COV_R3_CLOCKWORK;

Rule for firing CT_COV_R4_SAINTS:
	fire CT_COV_R3_SAINTS;

Rule for firing CT_COV_R4_DIFFERENCEENGINE:
	fire CT_COV_R3_DIFFERENCEENGINE;

Rule for firing CT_COV_R4_PERPETUUM:
	fire CT_COV_R3_PERPETUUM;

CT_COV_R4_MYSELF is clustered with CT_COV_R4_BALLOON, CT_COV_R4_CATHEDRAL, CT_COV_R4_CLERGY, CT_COV_R4_FIGURE, CT_COV_R4_PEOPLE, CT_COV_R4_COVALT, CT_COV_R4_RAVENS, CT_COV_R4_CLOCKWORK, CT_COV_R4_SAINTS, CT_COV_R4_DIFFERENCEENGINE, CT_COV_R4_PERPETUUM, CT_COV_R4_DECOY, CT_COV_R4_MONEY, CT_COV_R4_DRUGS.

Rule for firing a fired conversation topic that is clustered with CT_COV_R4_MYSELF:
	say "I've already talked to him about that." instead;

Chapter 7 - Idle actions

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the second-state of Covalt is 1 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "[one of][as a noun]Covalt[end as] paces the floor, waiting for me to explain.[or]'Well?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands.[or]'You’d better tell me what happened to you,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] growls.[once only]";

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the second-state of Covalt is 2 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "[one of][as a noun]Covalt[end as] looks stunned, like he’s been used as a bell-clapper.[or]The [as a noun]giant[end as]’s face is cracked with frowns.[or]'I don’t see the teeth on this,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] grumbles. 'I don’t see them at all.'[once only]";

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the second-state of Covalt is 3 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "[one of]'What we need,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] murmurs, 'is a plan.'[or]'Some kind of plan,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] grumbles. 'You can’t make eggs into an omelet if you don’t have a plan.'[or]'Can’t do anything without a plan, you see,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] says. 'Something to lay out what you do, when.'[or][fire TRIG_COV_DIAGRAM][stopping]";

TRIG_COV_DIAGRAM is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_COV_DIAGRAM:
	say run paragraph on;
	try giving the Perpetuum Mobile diagram to Covalt;
	say run paragraph on;

Rule for firing COVALT_COUNTER when the second-state of Covalt is 4 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "[one of][as a noun]Covalt[end as] has settled at his workbench and is turning the plan of the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] this way and that.[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] rummages across his workbench and comes up trumps with a  sheet of metal and a small brass pin.[or]'Just the surface stuff,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] murmurs to himself. 'None of them workings.'[or]'Are you still here?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands of me, suddenly. 'Where’s your side of all this?'[once only]";

Part 2 - Clock Shop


Rule for printing the description of the Clock Shop during Return to Covalt's:
	say "Looks like [as a noun]Covalt[end as] didn’t tidy up while I was out, then. From the bedroom door to the south round to the door to the southwest there are 11 hours of stuff – [as a noun]cogs[end as], [as a noun]traces[end as], all that clockwork the monks polish up and [as a noun]Covalt[end as] just tosses around his room like so much chickenfeed.";

Instead of opening the clock shop door when the second-state of Covalt is less than 4 and Return to Covalt's is happening:
	say "[one of]'Stop there,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] demands, blocking my way with one gigantic hand. 'You’ve dragged me up into this, now you’re going to help me out.'[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] stops me. 'Not till we’ve a plan, and I know what’s what.'[or][as a noun]Covalt[end as] stops me.[stopping]";

After going from the Clock Shop to the Bedroom when the second-state of Covalt is 4 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "'Where are you off to?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] bawls after me. 'Don't you have somewhere to go?'";
	continue the action.

After going to the Clock Shop from the Bedroom when the second-state of Covalt is 4 during Return to Covalt's:
	say "'Now, will you get off over the street?' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] grumbles.";
	continue the action.


After going through the clock shop door from the Clock Shop during Return to Covalt's:
	if the second-state of Covalt is 4:
		say "'Make it a knock-out, don’t go halves!' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] shouts after me. I clutch the three silver [as a noun]coins[end as] in my hand.";
	continue the action;

Instead of closing the clock shop door when in the Clock Shop during Return to Covalt's:
	say "I wouldn’t dare touch it. Who knows what mechanics its got to try and take my hand off if I did?";

Instead of doing something when a clutter thing (called the junk) is involved during Return To Covalt's:
	if the junk is not the clock shop door:
		say "This stuff isn’t going to help me now.";
	otherwise:
		continue the action;

Understand "sheet", "metal", "small", "brass", "pin", "sheet of" as the junk when Return To Covalt's is happening.

After going through the clock shop door from Escapement St when the player is dishonest:
	say "I go back inside the shop, one hand clutched round the stolen [as a noun]coin[end as]. If he sees I've got it he'll have me skinned, I know.";
	continue the action;

Part 3 - Escapement Street

Escapement St is a room. "[if the sleeping drug is off-stage]It’s night. [as a noun]Gaslight[end as] webs the shining edges of the [as a noun]cobblestones[end as]. All the shops are closed except for [as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s back northeast. The streets are empty of people, no boys running around or girls weaving cats-cradle between their hands.[paragraph break]But the [as a noun]Autopothecary[end as] to the northwest is still open. It never closes.[else]I'm standing on one edge of the street, holding the precious [as a noun]sleeping drug[end as] gingerly in my hands. There are no carriages about but if one swoops round the corner, hits me and smashes the bottle, that's it, all our plans are over! It's a short dash back to [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s, to the northeast.[end if]".

Instead of going inside when in Escapement St:
	if the player does not have the sleeping drug:
		try going northwest;
	else:
		try going northeast;

Instead of making to leave when in Escapement St:
	if the player does not have the sleeping drug:
		try going northwest;
	else:
		try going northeast;

The printed name of Escapement St is "Escapement Street".

The street-backdrop is a privately-named, proper-named, scenery, in Escapement St. Understand "street", "cobble", "cobbles", "boys", "boy", "girl", "horse", "horses" as the street-backdrop. The printed name is "Escapement Street". The description is "The street is empty now."

Instead of approaching or entering the street-backdrop:
	say "The [as a noun]autopothecary[end as] is northwest; [as a noun]Covalt[end as]'s is back northeast."

Instead of searching the street-backdrop:
	try examining the street-backdrop.

The autopothecary shopfront is a backdrop, in Escapement St, in the Autopothecary Booth. "The [as a noun]Autopothecary[end as] is a machine set behind some expensive plate glass. OPEN ALL NIGHT, reads the sign. FOR WINDING-UP AND WINDING-DOWN AT ALL HOURS OF THE WATCH." Understand "glass wall/walls", "plate glass wall/walls", "booth", "chemist", "chemists", "plate", "glass" as the autopothecary shopfront.

Before doing something when the backdrop-walls are involved and the autopothecary shopfront is visible:
	redirect the action from the backdrop-walls to the autopothecary shopfront, and try that instead.

Understand "sign" as the  autopothecary shopfront when the location is Escapement St.

Before throwing something at the autopothecary shopfront:
	try attacking the autopothecary shopfront instead.

Before inserting something into the autopothecary shopfront:
	try attacking the autopothecary shopfront instead.

Before putting something on the autopothecary shopfront:
	try attacking the autopothecary shopfront instead.

Instead of attacking the autopothecary shopfront:
	say "I'd bring the guard in seconds (glass this is expensive is <Italic>always</Italic> counterweighted to a whole array of bells and clappers)."

Instead of entering or approaching the autopothecary shopfront when the location is Escapement St:
	try going northwest;

Instead of going north in Escapement St:
	say "I don’t have the time – or the wind – to go running all over the city any more.";

Instead of going south in Escapement St:
	say "I don’t have the time – or the wind – to go running all over the city any more.";

Instead of going to the Clock Shop from Escapement St when the player does not have the sleeping drug:
	say "[as a noun]Covalt[end as] would eat my head if I went back empty-handed." instead;

Section - Scenery

Some gaslamps are a backdrop, in Escapement St, in the Autopothecary Booth.  "[one of]The [as a noun]lamps[end as] have all been lit by little wedge-shaped [as a noun]hammers[end as] that peel back on [as a noun]ratchets[end as] and strike a [as a noun]flint[end as] inside the... look, this doesn’t really matter, does it?[or]There’s enough light to see the [as a noun]Autopothecary[end as], northwest, and [as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s to the northeast, and what else is in the dark can stay there for all I care.[stopping]"

Understand "gas", "lamp", "gaslamp", "lamps", "light", "lights", "gaslight", "gaslights", "hammer", "hammers", "ratchet", "ratchets", "flint", "wedge-shaped", "wedge", "shaped" as the gaslamps.

Instead of doing something when the gaslamps are physically involved and the location is the autopothecary booth:
	say "The [as a noun]gaslamps[end as] are back outside on the street."

Instead of smelling when in Escapement St:
	say "The street smells of grease oil and horses."
Instead of listening when in Escapement St:
	say "Nothing. This place could as well be a graveyard."

Section - Covalt's locative backdrop

The Covalt-shop is a locative glimpse backdrop, in the Autopothecary Booth, localising the Clock Shop. Understand "covalts", "covalt", "covalt's" as the covalt-shop.

Chapter 8 - Silver minutes

Some silver coins are a proper-named thing. The silver coins have a number called the count. The count of the silver coins is 3.

Understand "1", "one" as the silver coins.
Understand "2", "two" as the silver coins when the count of the silver coins is at least 2.
Understand "3", "three" as the silver coins when the count of the silver coins is at least 3.
Understand "silver", "coin", "minute", "minutes", "money", "cash" as the silver coins.
Understand "last" as the silver coins when the count of the silver coins is 1.

Before printing the name of the silver coins:
	say "[count of silver coins in words] ";

Rule for printing the name of the silver coins:
	say "silver minute[if the count of the silver coins is not 1]s[end if]".

Rule for printing the description of the silver coins:
	say capitalized "[count of silver coins in words] silver coin[s]. [run paragraph on]";
	say "More money than I've ever held before[one of]. Certainly more than they give out at Newtonmass (and then take straight back again in the New Year’s Donations)[once only].";

Rule for printing the description of the silver coins when the player is dishonest:
	say "One silver [as a noun]minute[end as]. Mine, to cover the cost of the bumps, the scrapes, the knocking's out, the water, the running, the jumping and all the rest of it I've had today. It doesn't seem unfair, does it?"

Instead of turning the silver coins:
	say "I toss [if the count of silver coins is one][as a noun]the coin[end as][else]one of the [as a noun]coins[end as][end if]. [one of]Threads[or]Rails[at random]."

Instead of turning the silver coins when the player is dishonest and the player can see Covalt:
	say "In front of [as a noun]Covalt[end as]. No way.";

Section - And keeping hold of them

Instead of dropping the silver coins when the player is dishonest and the sleeping drug has been on-stage:
	say "This is my cut for all this. I'm not parting with it, no way."

Instead of dropping the silver coins:
	say "I can't lose them. They're too precious."

Before putting the silver coins on something:
	try dropping the silver coins instead.

Last Instead of inserting the silver coins into something:
	try dropping the silver coins.

Section - And Covalt

Instead of giving the silver coins to Covalt:
	say "'I'm not having them back,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] snaps, bitterly. 'Take 'em and go get that draught like I told you to!'";

Instead of showing the silver coins to Covalt:
	say "'What are you wavin' them around for? Don't go getting ideas about being <Italic>flash</Italic>,' he insists. 'You'll have spent them in five minutes. If you know what's good for you, that is.'"

Instead of giving the silver coins to Covalt when the player is dishonest:
	say "I can't. If he knows I didn't spend it he'd have me roasted. I've got no choice now but to hang on to it and hope he doesn't ever find out."	

Instead of showing the silver coins to Covalt when the player is dishonest:
	say "He'd crush me to a pulp. I'm already afraid he can see it somehow."



Part 4 - Autopothecary

After going from Escapement St to the Autopothecary Booth:
	say "I step through the door of the [as a noun]booth[end as].";
	continue the action;

The Autopothecary Booth is northwest of Escapement St. "This is a small tiled [as a noun]booth[end as], all gleaming white and washed down to make it look healthy and clean. A glass wall to the southeast lets in enough [as a noun]gaslight[end as] to see the controls of the machine: a series of little [as a noun]doors[end as], each with its own [as a noun]coin slot[end as] beneath.[paragraph break]Beside the machine is a neat little [as a noun]press[end as] for pulping the fresh ingredients into a medicine. Above that is a [as a noun]parts-list[end as] chalked up like an inn sign."

Instead of making to leave when in the Autopothecary Booth:
	try going southeast.

The printed name of the Autopothecary Booth is "Autopothecary".

