Section - parts-List

The parts-list is a scenery thing in the Autopothecary Booth. Understand "sign", "parts list", "list", "instructions", "ingredients" as the parts-list. The printed name is "parts-list sign".

Rule for printing the description of the parts-list:
	say the table of autopothecary ingredients laid out as a long grid.

Section - Doors and Slots

Some tiny-doors are a privately-named, scenery, thing in the Autopothecary Booth. "Each [as a noun]door[end as]’s about big enough for a mouse, but if I put my [as a noun]coins[end as] in and only get a mouse in return I’ll be spitting screws until the thirteenth stroke. The [as a noun]doors[end as] are all labeled but not with what they sell, only with codes: B3 or D2, that sort of thing."

Understand "little/tiny doors", "doors", "controls", "slots", "coin slots/slot", "machine", "autopothecary" as the tiny-doors.

Instead of opening or searching the tiny-doors:
	say "Each door has a [as a noun]coin slot[end as]. ";
	say "My [as a noun]coins[end as] are limited, so I'll have to choose wisely!";

Instead of inserting a silver coins into the tiny-doors:
	say "My [as a noun]coins[end as] are limited, so I'll have to choose wisely!";



Section - Individual Doors

A tiny door is a kind of container, scenery, open, not openable. Some tiny [as a noun]doors[end as] in the Autopothecary Booth are defined by the table of autopothecary vending machine slots.

[The "effect" column in the table below interferes with Emily's Menus extension. --JM]

[Rule for printing the description of a tiny door:
	choose row with a tiny door of the noun in the Table of Autopothecary Vending Machine Slots;
	say "[label entry]: [product entry], [effect entry].";]

Rule for printing the description of a tiny door:
	choose row with a tiny door of the noun in the Table of Autopothecary Vending Machine Slots;
	say "[label entry]: [product entry], [purpose entry].";


Understand "tiny/little", "door", "slot" as a tiny door.

Understand "buy [a tiny door] from [tiny-doors]" as removing it from.

Instead of opening a tiny door:
