Part 4 - Upper Nave

Rule for printing the description of the Upper Nave during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The nave is wider here, wider than to the south and with more tightly-packed [as a noun]pews[end as] on either side. But right now only shadows are bowing their heads. To the north is the [as a noun]altar[end as], still gleaming.";

Rule for printing the description of the upper seal during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]seal[end as] is just a circle in the half-light.";

Section 1 - Calvin and Drake are overheard

Calvin_and_Drake are privately-named, proper-named, plural-named, men. Understand "calvin", "drake", "boy", "boys", "men", "calvin and drake", "drake and calvin" as Calvin_and_Drake. The description is "The two are hunched together, talking in tiny voices." The printed name is "Calvin and Drake".

After going to the Upper Nave during Return To The Cathedral:
	if Calvin Cutscene has not happened:
		change the current script to {SE_CALVIN_1, SE_CALVIN_2, SE_CALVIN_3};
	continue the action;

SE_CALVIN_1 is a scripted event. The display text is "There are two figures sitting at its base, with a single [as a noun]candle[end as] between them. When I recognize [as a noun]Calvin[end as] I duck immediately behind a [as a noun]pew[end as]. How am I supposed to get past those two?[paragraph break]They’re playing [as a noun]dice[end as]. They must have been here some time."

SE_CALVIN_2 is a scripted event. The display text is "'How are we meant to tell if someone’s grey in this light?' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] complains. He’s whispering. 'Why couldn’t the old man have given us a password to ask for or something?'[paragraph break][as a noun]Drake[end as] is more boisterous. By the look on his face he’s just rolled eleven. 'If the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says to keep people out, we keep people out. Maybe even people in grey... Unless they're the right person.'".

SE_CALVIN_3 is a scripted event. The display text is "'What’s that?' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] is up, suddenly, looking around. 'I heard something.'[paragraph break]'Better get to our places,' [as a noun]Drake[end as] says, sounding a little more nervous now. 'I’ll guard the Bishop's Room. You’re on patrol.'[paragraph break]'Me? Why...'[paragraph break]'Get on with it,' [as a noun]Drake[end as] snaps. The [as a noun]candles[end as] move out into darkness, one west and one north, then in and out of sight up a set of winding [as a noun]stairs[end as].".

Calvin Cutscene is a scene. Calvin Cutscene begins when SE_CALVIN_1 is listed in the current script. Calvin Cutscene ends when the current script is empty.

When Calvin Cutscene begins:
	move Calvin_and_Drake to the Upper Nave;
	move ebony dice to the Upper Nave.

When Calvin Cutscene ends:
	remove Calvin_and_Drake from play;

Before doing something during Calvin Cutscene:
	unless we are waiting or we are listening or we are taking inventory or we are examining or we are looking:
		say "[one of]I’m frozen with fear[or]If they found me here they'd tear me to pieces[or]I can't move[in random order]." instead;
	if we are taking inventory:
		continue the action;

Section 2 - Calvin's Patrol

Calvin's Patrol is a scene. Calvin's Patrol begins when SE_CALVIN_3 is fired.

When Calvin's Patrol begins:
	now Calvin is scenery;
	move Calvin to the West Apse;
	move the ebony dice to the Cathedral Altar;

Part 5 - Altar

Rule for printing the description of the Cathedral Altar during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]altar[end as] is still gleaming even though the candlelight has gone. It’s set between the workings of the [as a noun]great clock[end as] in the centre of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as]. Aisles lead off in all four directions, but the Crypt is to the east.";

Before hiding behind the iron altar during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] will walk right around it - there's nowhere there for me to hide..." instead.

Section 1 - Altar, Clock Machinery, Dais and Canopy

Rule for printing the description of scenery when in the Cathedral Altar during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "It would be worth enough to buy a house if it wasn’t too heavy to lift.";

Instead of taking scenery when in the Cathedral Altar during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I’m not here to steal anything. Well. Almost not anything.";


Rule for firing TRIG_NOINTERFERE when Return To the Cathedral is happening:
	say "I don't want to leave any traces."

Section 2 - Dice

There is a plural-named thing called two ebony dice. "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] has left his [as a noun]dice[end as] here." The description of the two ebony dice is "Two ebony [as a noun]dice[end as]. I don’t get things like that." Understand "die" as the ebony dice.

Instead of taking the ebony dice:
	say "Not now. No time for games.";

Before dropping the ebony dice: [ to catch "throw dice" ]
	try taking the ebony dice instead.

Understand "play dice" as a mistake ("No time for games now.") when the ebony dice are visible.

Section 3 - Clock

Rule for printing the description of the cathedral clock machinery during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "The clock-workings loom over me like a dark moon."

Part 6 - Choir

Part 1 - Description

Rule for printing the description of the Cathedral Choir during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "This is like standing in the bottom of the [as a noun]Abbey[end as]’s well: a circular pool of dark with the gigantic [as a noun]window[end as] just a different shade of dark. To the south is the [as a noun]altar[end as] but I can’t see it, and right overhead is the [as a noun]great engine[end as] of the [as a noun]clock[end as]. I can almost feel it ready to fall.";

Part 2 - Scenery

Rule for printing the description of the window panels during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The windows are dark.";

Rule for printing the description of the east stairs during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "Spiral steps leading up.";

Instead of going through the East Stairs from the Cathedral Choir during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[one of]I start up the [as a noun]stairs[end as] then freeze. Light – a single point. [as a noun]Drake[end as]’s [as a noun]candle[end as].[or][as a noun]Drake[end as] hasn’t moved. There’s no going up that way.[stopping]";
	say "[conditional paragraph break]";
	if TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE is fired and TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE is unfired:
		fire TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE;

Rule for printing the description of the west stairs during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "Spiral steps leading up.";

After going through the west stairs from the Cathedral Choir during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I dash quickly up the [as a noun]stairs[end as].";
	continue the action;

Part 3 - Choir Stalls

Rule for writing a paragraph about the choir stalls during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]choir stalls[end as] are like trolls in the dark. Their [as a noun]carvings[end as] are [as a noun]tattoos[end as].";

Rule for writing a paragraph about the choir stalls when the decoy Perpetuum is in the Cathedral Choir and TRIG_LOST_DECOY is not primed [ so when lost, it's actually lost! ] during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "By the base of the [as a noun]choir stalls[end as] is a tiny gleam of gold, almost completely hidden. It is the [as a noun]decoy Perpetuum[end as]."

Understand "tattoos", "tattoo", "engravings", "drill", "figures" as the choir stalls when Return to the Cathedral is happening.

Rule for writing a paragraph about the choir stalls when the secret panel is open during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]choir stalls[end as] have broken open, to reveal a dark opening inside."

Rule for printing the description of the Choir Stalls during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[as a noun]Carvings[end as] etch the wood, deeply, painfully.";

After printing the description of the Choir Stalls during Return To The Cathedral:
	if TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE is fired:
		try touching the Choir Stalls;

Instead of slicing the choir stalls with the knife during Return to the Cathedral:
	if TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE is fired:
		say "I could use the [as a noun]knife[end as] to trigger a switch - if I could find one!"

Instead of singing in the Cathedral Choir:
	say "I'm not here to be heard."

Instead of entering or sitting on or standing on or hiding inside the Choir Stalls during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "Now isn’t the time to practice my singing!";

Instead of entering or sitting on or standing on the Choir Stalls when the player can see the open secret panel during Return To The Cathedral:
	try going inside;

Instead of going inside when the player can see the closed secret panel:
	try going north instead;

Instead of pushing or pulling or searching or opening the Choir Stalls during Return To The Cathedral:
	try touching the Choir Stalls instead;

Instead of searching or touching the Choir Stalls when the Secret Panel is closed and the player carries a lit candle during Return To The Cathedral:
	now the Secret Panel is open;
	say "I run my hand across the embossed [as a noun]figures[end as]. Woodworkers, joiners, lathes, bevellers, the men who put the threads on screws. Their tools – hammers and files and [as a noun]drill[end as]s...[paragraph break]...one [as a noun]panel[end as] swings open revealing a dark [as a noun]passage[end as] beyond.";

Instead of searching or touching the Choir Stalls when the Secret Panel is closed during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[one of]I lean in close to examine the [as a noun]carvings[end as] but can’t make out any of the details. There are hundreds of [as a noun]figures[end as] – if there’s a catch here, it could be anywhere![paragraph break]Without a decent light, I’ll never find it (and with a decent light, [as a noun]Drake[end as] will certainly find <Italic>me</Italic>.)[or]I'll need a light if I'm going to search the [as a noun]carvings[end as] closely.[or]I'll need to find a light from somewhere. I'm sure I saw something the last time I was here...[stopping]".

Instead of touching the Choir Stalls when the Secret Panel is open during Return To The Cathedral:
	try closing the Secret Panel.

Instead of closing the choir stalls when the secret panel is open:
	redirect the action from the choir stalls to the secret panel, and try that;

Instead of closing the secret panel when the secret panel is open:
	say "[one of]I don't know how to close it. Another carving - but which one? I have no idea.[or]I can't close it. But maybe it doesn't matter.[stopping]".

Instead of going through the secret panel when the player carries the decoy Perpetuum:
	fire TRIG_SECRET_PANEL;

TRIG_SECRET_PANEL is a trigger.

Rule for firing TRIG_SECRET_PANEL:
	say "The false [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] is too wide to carry into the gap. I’m going to have to leave it here.";

A secret panel is a door, privately-named, scenery, closed, inside from the Cathedral Choir, outside from the Secret Stair. "The narrow [as a noun]passage[end as] leads away into molten dark."

Understand "secret", "narrow",  "dark", "opening", "door", "passage", "panel" as the secret panel when the secret panel is open. 

Instead of approaching the secret panel: try entering the secret panel instead.

Rule for deciding the concealed possessions of the Cathedral Choir:
	if the particular possession is the closed secret panel:
		yes;

The great-engine is privately-named, scenery. The printed name is "Cathedral clock". Understand "great engine", "engine", "clock", "cathedral clock", "great engine of the clock", "engine of the clock" as the great-engine. The description is "I know it too well now."

Instead of doing something when the great-engine is physically involved:
	say "It's high above me."

Part 7 - Shrine of the Saints

Rule for printing the description of the Shrine of the Saints during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "East of the nave, the shrine is lit by a single shaded [as a noun]lantern[end as]. From the walls, the eyes of [as a noun]statuettes[end as] glitter like rat faces. The [as a noun]stand[end as] of [as a noun]candles[end as] is like a blasted blackened tree stump.";

Part 2 - Scenery

Rule for printing the description of the statuettes during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "They are the [as a noun]icons[end as] of the saints. I can’t make out any detail beyond the smooth polished tonsures and the cog-teeth halos above.";

Instead of taking or pushing or searching the statuettes during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]statues[end as] are holy relics. I’m not here to steal relics – except, of course, I am. Only not these ones. More important ones.";

A devotional oil lamp is scenery, in the Shrine of the Saints. "An [as a noun]oil lamp[end as] held by a [as a noun]brass band[end as]. It flickers with a deep amber glow."

Instead of opening the devotional oil lamp:
	say "The [as a noun]lantern[end as] is a frame with several holes, for the air to get in, and for people to trim the [as a noun]wick[end as] when needed."

Understand "deep", "amber", "flickering", "flicker", "glowing", "glow", "oil", "lantern", "lamp", "brass", "band", "wick", "light" as the devotional lamp.

Instead of taking the devotional oil lamp:
	say "The [as a noun]lamp[end as] is attached securely to the wall[one of] (and anyway, it's much brighter than I'd want to be, sneaking around in here!)[or].[stopping]".

Instead of slicing the devotional lamp with the knife:
	say "It doesn't need trimming: the flame inside is still strong."

Instead of closing the devotional lamp:
	say "I can't. It has no cover, and will burn quietly all night."

Every turn when the player is in Shrine of the Saints and a random chance of 1 in 4 succeeds during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "[one of]The [as a noun]light[end as] from the [as a noun]lantern[end as] wavers a little[or]The [as a noun]lantern[end as]-[as a noun]light[end as] flickers[or]The [as a noun]lantern[end as] emits a tiny thread of heat[or]The [as a noun]lantern[end as] light dims, then rises once more[cycling].";

Part 3 - More candles

Rule for printing the description of the brass stand during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]stand[end as] is lined with [as a noun]candles[end as] meant for devotions to the saints.";

Some thin candles are scenery, part of the brass stand. "There are hundreds of thin candles." The indefinite article of the thin candles is "hundreds of".

Understand "candle" as the thin candles when Return to the Cathedral is happening and the single-candle is off-stage.

Rule for deciding the concealed possessions of the brass stand:
	if Return To The Cathedral is happening:
		if the particular possession is a candle dispenser:
			yes;
	otherwise:
		if the particular possession is the thin candles:
			yes;
	no;

Instead of taking the thin candles:
	if the player has a candle:
		say "I don’t need another one.";
	otherwise:
		now the player carries the single-candle;
		set pronouns from the single-candle;
		say "I pick a [as a noun]candle[end as] from the [as a noun]stand[end as].";

A candle can be lit or unlit. A candle is usually unlit. Understand "lit", "flame" as a candle when the item described is lit.

The single-candle is a candle, privately-named. The printed name of the single-candle is "candle". The wax is clear. Understand "narrow", "candle" as the single-candle.

When Return To The Cathedral begins:
	if the player has a candle:
		while the player has a candle:
			remove a random candle had by the player from play;
		now the player carries the single-candle;
	move the orange-light to the Lower Nave; 
	remove the heat from play;
	

Rule for printing the description of a candle during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[if unlit]It’s too dark to tell what colour the wax is. Not that it matters, now.[otherwise]The narrow [as a noun]candle[end as] throws out a circle of light.[end if]";

Instead of dropping the unlit single-candle:
	say "If I put it down I would lose it in the darkness.";

Before melting something on the devotional oil lamp:
	try inserting the noun into the devotional oil lamp instead.

Before burning something when the devotional oil lamp is visible during Return to the Cathedral:
	try inserting the noun into the devotional oil lamp instead.
	

Instead of inserting the single-candle into the brazier during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "All that seems like weeks ago now.";

Instead of inserting a candle into the devotional oil lamp during Return To The Cathedral:
	now the noun is lit;
	say "I tip the [as a noun]candle[end as] into the [as a noun]lantern[end as] flames. It sparks and hisses as it lights.";

Instead of inserting a candle into the devotional oil lamp:
	remove the noun from play;
	say "I light [the noun] and place it in the niche of [a random miniature statuette].";

After going from the Shrine of the Saints to the Lower Nave when the player has a lit candle:
	say "I go back to the nave, careful to shield the [as a noun]candle[end as]’s light behind my palm. Mustn’t be seen. Not here[if Calvin Cutscene has happened]. Not by those two[end if].";
	continue the action;

Part 8 - East Apse

Rule for printing the description of the East Apse during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The eastern apse is filled with [as a noun]pews[end as]. Through the heavy door to the southeast I can hear the [as a noun]Orrery[end as], still moving in its endless cycle. To the northeast is an iron [as a noun]grate[end as]: it leads to the crypts[one of]. [paragraph break]That way, Wren, and be bold about it.[or].[stopping]";

Rule for printing the description of the Hall Door during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The heavy door to the [as a noun]Orrery[end as] is locked.";

When Return To The Cathedral begins:
	now the Hall Door is closed;
	now the Hall Door is locked;

Before going southeast from the East Apse during Return to the Cathedral:
	try knocking on the Hall door instead.

Before opening the Hall Door during Return to the Cathedral:
	try knocking on the hall door instead.

Before unlocking the Hall Door with during Return to the Cathedral:
	try knocking on the hall door instead.

Instead of knocking on the Hall Door during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I don’t want to wake anyone[one of]. [as a noun]Sa’at[end as]’s probably still in there, working through his endless papers, putting each one he finishes to the bottom of his stack to do again[once only].";

Rule for printing the description of the crypt grate during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "This is the [as a noun]grate[end as] to the crypt. It’s smaller than a man – dating from a time before the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], before even the City. Perfect size for a Wren. Or a stooping [as a noun]Figure[end as].";

Instead of opening the locked crypt grate when the player does not have the old iron crypt key during Return To The Cathedral:
	fire TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE instead;

Instead of unlocking the locked crypt grate with something when the player does not have the old iron crypt key during Return To The Cathedral:
	fire TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE instead;

First unlocking rule for the Crypt Grate when the player does not have the old iron crypt key during Return To The Cathedral:
	fire TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE instead;

TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE is a trigger.

Rule for firing TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE:
	say "The [as a noun]grate[end as] is locked. The [as a noun]Abbot[end as] hasn’t left it open – he must have given the [as a noun]Figure[end as] the key. But the only key?[paragraph break]Not the only key. I saw one earlier. In the [as a noun]Bishop's desk[end as]. Where he kept it.";

Part 9 - West Apse

Rule for printing the description of the West Apse during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "Rows of [as a noun]pews[end as] face the altar back east. To the southwest, the door to the Great Library is closed.";

When Return To The Cathedral begins:
	now the Library Archway is closed;
	now the Library Archway is locked;

Instead of doing something when the Library Archway is physically involved during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The oak [as a noun]door[end as] is locked.";

Instead of knocking on the Library Archway during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[one of]I knock nervously. There is no response.[or]I try again, more boldly. Still nothing. And why should there be? And who here would help me? For all they know, Wren, <Italic>you’re carrying the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]</Italic>.[stopping]"

Part 10 - Calendar Shrine

Rule for printing the description of the Calendar Shrine during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]Calendar Unlimited[end as] is like a still pond in the darkness. The nave is back east.";

Rule for printing the description of the Calendar Unlimited during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The table-like machine seems still, but beneath the surface everything is whirring. All the indicators say Danger, Darkness and the jagged crack of the Fundamental Flaw.";

Instead of doing something when the Calendar Unlimited is physically involved during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I’m not here for that. One relic is enough.";

Rule for printing the description of the Brazier during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]brazier[end as] is cold.";

Instead of touching the brazier during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]brazier[end as] is cold, like it was never alight.";

Instead of inserting something into the Brazier during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "No time to play now." instead.

Part 11 - West Clerestory

Rule for printing the description of the West Clerestory during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The clerestory circles the Choir from a storey above, but now is nothing but a coal-pit. The [as a noun]balcony[end as] continues northeast. [as a noun]Stairs[end as] spiral downwards through a hole in the floor.";

Rule for printing the description of a stained glass window during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]windows[end as] are dark.";

Rule for printing the description of the West Stairs during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] lead down.";

Part 12 - North Clerestory

Rule for printing the description of the North Clerestory during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The clerestory curves here, from southwest to southeast, and that’s where [as a noun]Drake[end as] is standing with his [as a noun]candle[end as]: right outside the Bishop’s Library[one of]. [paragraph break]Who are they supposed to be guarding it from? Surely not the [as a noun]Figure[end as]? And then I realize. The [as a noun]Abbot[end as]’s set them to guard it from me – or anyone like me. Anyone who might get into the Library and somehow stop the [as a noun]Figure[end as] from reaching the crypt[once only].";

Instead of going to the East Clerestory from the North Clerestory during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I can’t let [as a noun]Drake[end as] find me[if TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE is fired]. There has to be another way[end if].";
	say "[conditional paragraph break]";
	if TRIG_CRYPT_GRATE is fired and TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE is unfired:
		fire TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE;

Rule for printing the description of the statues of St Breguet and St Babbage during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "It’s too dark to see them.";

TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE is a trigger. 

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE:
	say "Wait. Something the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] said, before, when talking to the [as a noun]Figure[end as], about secret [as a noun]doors[end as]...[paragraph break]<Italic>'Six? The only one I know is the one from the Choir to the Bishop’s Library! Where there’s a [as a noun]drill[end as]...'</Italic>[paragraph break]";

Every turn when TRIG_SECRET_PASSAGE has been fired for exactly one turn:
	say "Where there’s a [as a noun]drill[end as], Wren. <Italic>Where there’s a [as a noun]drill[end as] – there’s a way...!</Italic>[paragraph break]";

Part 13 - Calvin's Patrol

Every turn during Calvin's Patrol:
	consider the patrolling rulebook for Calvin;

The patrolling rules are an object-based rulebook.

Calvin has a list of objects [rooms] called the patrol. The patrol of Calvin is { West Apse, Cathedral Altar, Upper Nave, Lower Nave, Upper Nave, Cathedral Altar, East Apse, Cathedral Altar}.

COUNTER_CALVIN is a counter. The top end of COUNTER_CALVIN is 2.

A patrolling rule for Calvin (this is the only move on alternate turns rule):
	increment COUNTER_CALVIN;
	if the internal value of COUNTER_CALVIN is 2:
		if the player can see Calvin:
			anonymously abide by the detection rulebook;
		do nothing instead;

A patrolling rule for Calvin (this is the Calvin movement rule):
	rotate the patrol of Calvin backwards;
	move Calvin to entry 1 of the patrol of Calvin;

A patrolling rule for Calvin:
	if entry 1 of the patrol of Calvin is the location:
		if the player is in a pew:
			say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] is approaching. I stay frozen to the spot." instead;
		else:
			say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] is [one of]almost on me[or]coming closer[or]almost here[or]approaching[at random]. [one of]Either I run or I hide[or]There's no time to lose[or]He'll see me if I stay here[or]If he catches me with the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as], it's all over[as decreasingly likely outcomes]!" instead;

A patrolling rule for Calvin:
	if entry 2 of the patrol of Calvin is the location:
		say "The light of a [as a noun]candle[end as] is approaching from [the quick best route from the location to the location of Calvin][one of]. Time to get out of here, Wren![or].[stopping]" instead;

A patrolling rule for Calvin:
	if entry 3 of the patrol of Calvin is the location:
		say "I can hear footsteps approaching from somewhere to [the best route from the location to the location of Calvin]." instead;

Before listening when Return to the Cathedral is happening and the location is entry 3 of the patrol of Calvin and Calvin's Patrol is happening:
	say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] is approaching from the darkness." instead;

Before listening when Return to the Cathedral is happening and the location is entry 2 of the patrol of Calvin and Calvin's Patrol is happening:
	say "I can hear [as a noun]Calvin[end as] coming right this way." instead;

Before listening when Return to the Cathedral is happening and the location is entry 1 of the patrol of Calvin and Calvin's Patrol is happening:
	say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as]'s footsteps are mere footsteps away." instead;

The detection rules are a rulebook. The detection rules have default no outcome.

First detection rule:
	if the player is on a pew:
		say "[one of][as a noun]Calvin[end as] pauses, sniffing the air. His eyes are darting in the candlelight. They glance at me two or three times – straight at me. But he comes no closer.[paragraph break][once only][as a noun]Calvin[end as] moves on.";
		anonymously abide by the Calvin movement rule instead;

TRIG_CAL_DETECT is a trigger.

A detection rule when TRIG_CAL_DETECT is unfired:
	fire TRIG_CAL_DETECT;
	move the player to Public Yard instead;
 
Rule for firing TRIG_CAL_DETECT:
	say "[as a noun]Calvin[end as] is suddenly right in front of me. For a moment he’s silent. I can see the belting coming a mile off: he’s winding up his anger, getting it ready to let loose...[paragraph break]'Wren?' he hisses, under his breath. 'What are you <Italic>doing</Italic> here? If [as a noun]Drake[end as] finds you he’d <Italic>string you up![run paragraph on]</Italic>' His voice is shaking. I’ve never heard him so angry. Or not angry – afraid. The whites of his eyes have turned yellow. 'You’ve got to go. <Italic>This isn’t a game!</Italic>'[line break][paragraph break]He grabs my arm as he speaks – here it comes, I’m thinking – then he drags me away through the nave of the church, hissing at me to stay quiet, all the way to the front gate. 'Go. All right? Please. Don’t tell anyone.'[paragraph break]Then he – and his feeble [as a noun]candle[end as] – have disappeared back inside.";

A detection rule:
	say "[one of]'Wren, please!' [as a noun]Calvin[end as] hisses. He looks awful, almost sick. 'Don't stay here. You stupid <Italic>novice</Italic>. And if you do stay here, <Italic>hide</Italic>. Whatever you do. <Italic>Hide![run paragraph on]</Italic>'[line break][paragraph break]And then, just like that, he moves away.[or][as a noun]Calvin[end as] doesn’t seem to see me. Just passes straight through.[stopping]";
	anonymously abide by the Calvin movement rule;

Book 11 - Bishop's Library

Part 1 - Secret Stair

The Secret Stair is a room. "This is nothing but a tiny gap between the stones, maybe built for insulation or for the rats to build their nests. It winds upwards as it curves around the clerestory. A [as a noun]staircase[end as]."

Before going inside when in the Secret Stair: 	try going up instead.
Before going down when in the Secret Stair: try going outside instead.

Instead of going nowhere when in the Secret Stair:
	say "Either I climb the [as a noun]stairs[end as] - or I turn tail and run."

Understand "turn tail" as exiting when in the Secret Stair.
Understand "turn tail and run" as exiting when in the Secret Stair.

Instead of listening when in the Secret Stair: 
	say "A steady ticking echoes from somewhere: the heartbeat of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], maybe."


Instead of smelling when in the Secret Stair:
	say "The dust in here must be hundreds of years old."

After going up from the Secret Stair:
	say "I squeeze my way up the [as a noun]stairs[end as]."; continue the action;

After going outside from the Secret Stair:
	say "I slip out, grateful for the space to breathe."; continue the action;

The sloping floor is scenery, in the Secret Stair. "The floor itself tilts upwards."

Understand "staircase", "stairs", "steps", "stair", "stair case", "step", "sloping", "tilting", "slope", "tilt", "sloped", "tilted", "floor", "itself", "stone", "stones" as the sloping floor.

Instead of entering or approaching or climbing the sloping floor:
	try going up.

Up from the Secret Stair is the Ancient Landing.

Part 2 - Ancient Landing

The Ancient Landing is a room. "Dusty wooden floorboards. This is a tiny space between the walls. The [as a noun]stairs[end as] emerge onto a [as a noun]landing[end as], with a [as a noun]banister rail[end as] along one side. The [as a noun]rail[end as] is half buried in the stone. Maybe this was a larger room once: now it is only a piece, that ends to the west in solid stone...[paragraph break]...and to the east, ends [if the secret Clock Door is closed]in a [as a noun]wooden door[end as] carved with a clock[else if the enormous penduluum is ticking]in the workings of a [as a noun]beautiful Grandfather clock[end as][else]with the stalled workings of the Bishop's [as a noun]clock[end as][end if]."

Instead of going inside when in the Ancient Landing: 	try going east.
Instead of making to leave when in the Ancient Landing: 
	if the player is carrying the iron crypt key:
		try going down;
	else:
		try going east. 

Instead of smelling when in the Ancient Landing:
	say "The dust in here must be hundreds of years old."

Instead of listening when in the Ancient Landing:
	say "A steady ticking echoes from somewhere: the heartbeat of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], maybe."

Instead of listening when in the Ancient Landing and the secret Clock Door is open:
	say "The Bishop's [as a noun]clock[end as] ticks in steady, stately time."

Instead of listening when in the Ancient Landing and the secret Clock Door is open and the enormous penduluum is stopped:
	say "Nothing. The [as a noun]clock[end as] is silent."

Instead of going west in the Ancient Landing:
	say "It's solid stone.";

After going down from the Ancient Landing:
	say "I slip back into the [as a noun]staircase[end as]."; continue the action;

A gap in the stonework is scenery, in the Ancient Landing. "If I didn’t know they were there I’d think they were just a [as a noun]gap[end as] in the stonework." 

Understand "staircase", "stairs", "steps", "stair", "stair case", "step", "sloping", "tilting", "slope", "tilt", "sloped", "tilted", "floor", "itself" as the gap in the stonework.

Instead of entering the gap in the stonework:
	try going down;

A banister rail is scenery, in the Ancient Landing. "The wood is ancient and drilled with termite holes. In place to aid an old man, maybe, if he came through here to hide." Understand "bannister", "rail" as the banister rail.

Instead of pulling the banister rail:
	say "It's only just staying in place on its own. I don't want to break it."

Understand "floorboards", "landing" as the backdrop-floor when in the Ancient Landing.

Rule for printing the description of the backdrop-floor when in the Ancient Landing:
	say "It could give way any minute."

The solid-stone is privately-named scenery in the Ancient Landing. The printed name is "solid stone wall". Understand "solid stone wall", "stone wall", "stone", "solid stone" as the solid-stone. The description is "The [as a noun]landing[end as] has been bricked over."

Instead of doing something when the solid-stone is physically involved:
	say "I can't do anything to the walls themselves, can I?"

Chapter 1 - Clock Door

The secret clock door is a door, privately-named, scenery, closed, openable, not lockable, east of the Ancient Landing, west of the Bishop's Library. "A [as a noun]wood panel[end as] door carved with the single image of a [as a noun]clock face[end as]. It seems solid enough."

Understand "wood", "wooden", "secret",  "carved", "clock door",  "panel",  "door","face" as the secret clock door when Return To The Cathedral has happened.

Understand "clock face", "image of clock face" as the secret clock door when Return To The Cathedral has happened.

Understand "clock" as the secret clock door when the perfect workings are not visible.

Rule for printing the description of the open secret Clock Door:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] has slid back to reveal the inside of a [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as]. Beyond the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is moonlight, and the Library. ";
	if the enormous penduluum is ticking:
		say "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] strikes backwards and forward past the door.";
	otherwise:
		say "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is still.";

First before going through the closed secret Clock Door:
	say "Maybe it’s a [as a noun]door[end as]. Maybe it’s a wall." instead; [no implicit opening]

Instead of opening the open secret Clock Door:
	say "The panel has slid away into the wall.";

After opening the secret Clock Door:
	say "[one of]Getting my fingers in at six o’clock and eleven I heave with all my might – and almost fall. The [as a noun]wooden panel[end as] moves easily, drawing aside into the wall to reveal a real [as a noun]clock[end as]'s workings: [as a noun]weights[end as], and a beating [as a noun]penduluum[end as] like a heart… Once again, I'm inside a [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as]. And beyond the workings is the Bishop's Library.[or]I hop the [as a noun]clock door[end as] once more.[stopping]";

Instead of closing the closed secret Clock Door:
	say "The [as a noun]panel[end as] is already closed.";

After closing the secret Clock Door:
	say "The [as a noun]panel[end as] slides back across the clock. However old it might be, it moves with well-greased ease.";

Instead of going through the secret Clock Door from the Ancient Landing when the enormous penduluum is ticking:
	say "[one of]I move forward slowly, but the swinging [as a noun]Penduluum[end as] is stomping in my path like a giant blacksmith bashing a hammer down and aiming for my chest. Two beats a second – faster than I can move.[or]The heavy [as a noun]penduluum[end as] is blocking my path as sure as it was [as a noun]Drake[end as] hitting one fist after the other.[stopping]";

After going through the secret Clock Door from the Ancient Landing:
	say "I slip around the side of the [as a noun]penduluum[end as], through the [as a noun]glass doors[end as] and out into the Library.";
	continue the action;

Instead of taking or attacking the open secret Clock Door:
	try attacking the perfect clockwork workings.

Instead of taking or attacking the closed secret Clock Door:
	try opening the secret Clock Door.

Chapter 2 - Inner Workings

Section 1 - Penduluum

The Clock Workings is a privately-named room.

After deciding the scope of the player when the secret Clock Door is open and the player is in the Ancient Landing:
	place the clock workings in scope;

A rule for reaching inside the clock workings:
	if the secret Clock Door is open:
		allow access;

The enormous penduluum is scenery, in the clock workings. "The [as a noun]Penduluum[end as] is enormous. [if stopped]It hangs lifeless as a cow from a butcher’s hook.[otherwise]It beats heavy time, side to side, driving the workings of the [as a noun]clock[end as], driven in turn by enormous [as a noun]counterweights[end as] on either side.[end if]".  Understand "pendulum" as the enormous penduluum.

Instead of swinging or turning the stopped enormous penduluum:
	say "If I were to use all my weight and push, I could probably set it moving, but it would only fall still again. 'Momentum must always come from the heart,' as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] would say - though I doubt he's ever <Italic>tried</Italic> to start a [as a noun]clock[end as] like this by hand."

Rule for writing a paragraph about the stopped enormous penduluum:
	say "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] hangs completely still."

The enormous penduluum can be ticking or stopped. The enormous penduluum is ticking.

Instead of taking, pushing, pulling, repairing or attacking the stopped enormous penduluum:
	say "I’m not big enough to get that [as a noun]Penduluum[end as] to move.";

Instead of taking, pushing, pulling, turning, swinging or attacking the ticking enormous penduluum:
	say "[one of]I could try and grab the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] but I think i[or]I[stopping]t’d knock me flat!";

Section 2 - Weights

Some heavy brass weights are scenery, in the clock workings. "Two heavy brass cylinders that drive the clock, inch by weighty inch[if removed]. They are side by side on the bottom of the clock case[end if]."

Understand "counter-weights", "counter", "counterweight", "weight" as the heavy brass weights.

The heavy brass weights can be present or removed. The heavy brass weights are present.

Before repairing or taking or pulling or pushing the removed heavy brass weights:
	say "The [as a noun]weights[end as] are too heavy to carry, or to put back." instead;
																		 
Before inserting the removed heavy brass weights into something:
	try taking the heavy brass weights instead.

Instead of jumping over the removed heavy brass weights:
	say "They're just sitting on the floor. No need to be dramatic."

Instead of jumping over the heavy brass weights when the enormous penduluum is not stopped:
	say "The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] would clobber me."
	
Instead of jumping over the heavy brass weights:
	say "They're too big to get past like that."

Before putting the removed heavy brass weights on something:
	try taking the heavy brass weights instead.

Before fastening the removed heavy brass weights to something:
	try taking the heavy brass weights instead.

Instead of taking or attacking or pulling or pushing the present heavy brass weights:
	say "I wrap my whole body round the [as a noun]weight[end as] on the left-hand side and heave upwards, trying to slip it from its hook[if the enormous penduluum is ticking]. The giant [as a noun]penduluum[end as] comes so close to my head that it bellows in my ear, once, twice, three times[end if]. My muscles are straining so hard I can feel my shoulder-ball lifting from its socket.[paragraph break]And then suddenly the [as a noun]weight[end as] is free, and it drops the ground. [as a noun]Drake[end as] must have heard that, I think. Have to hope the [as a noun]Bishop[end as] locks his door.[paragraph break][if the enormous penduluum is ticking]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] winds to a stop. [fire TRIG_JAMMED_CLOCK][end if]";
	now the heavy brass weights are removed;	
	now the enormous penduluum is stopped;

COUNTER_WEIGHTS [ha!] is a counter. The internal value is 2. The top end is 2.

Every turn when the player can see the present heavy brass weights:
	increment COUNTER_WEIGHTS;

Rule for firing COUNTER_WEIGHTS when the player can see the present heavy brass weights:
	if the perfect clockwork workings are not jammed:
		say "The [as a noun]weights[end as] of the [as a noun]Penduluum[end as] ratchet one notch lower[one of].[or].[or]. If only I wait another three hours they might reach the base.[or].[stopping]";

Section 3 - Clockwork

TRIG_JAMMED_CLOCK is a trigger. 

Rule for firing [unfired] TRIG_JAMMED_CLOCK:
	say "[one of]<Italic>This is what will happen if the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] is taken</Italic>, I try to tell myself. <Italic>Only not just here. Everywhere</Italic>[or]Enough of this. I need to get a move on[stopping].";

Some perfect clockwork workings are a container, scenery, in the clock workings.  "The [as a noun]clockwork[end as] of the Bishop’s clock is perfect[one of]: carved with precision, fitted with reverence, tuned by blind masters with hearing that prevents them sleeping in the same rooms as moths or ants or woodworm[once only][if jammed]. And I’ve jammed the [as a noun]workings[end as][else if the heavy brass weights are removed]. But the two brass [as a noun]counterweights[end as] are off their hooks and on the floor and the [as a noun]clock[end as] is still[end if]." Understand "beautiful", "grandfather", "clock", "mechanism", "working", "bishop's", "bishops", "bishop" as the perfect clockwork workings.

Before closing the perfect clockwork workings:
	try closing the secret clock door instead.

Rule for printing the name of the perfect clockwork workings:
	say "workings";

The perfect clockwork can be running smoothly or jammed. The perfect clockwork is running smoothly.

Instead of inserting something unsuitable for jamming into the perfect clockwork workings:
	say "I can't see what good that will do.";

A procedural rule when inserting something suitable for jamming into the perfect clockwork workings:
	[ I hate to use procedural rules but right now you /can/ insert the knife! ]
	ignore the can't insert knife rule.

Instead of repairing the perfect clockwork workings:
	say "They are running beautifully. They couldn't be more perfect."

Before repairing the perfect clockwork workings when the heavy brass weights are removed:
	try taking the heavy brass weights instead.

Before repairing the jammed perfect clockwork workings:
	try taking a random suitable for jamming thing contained by the perfect clockwork workings instead.

Instead of inserting something suitable for jamming into the perfect clockwork workings when the perfect clockwork workings contain something suitable for jamming:
	if the noun is in the perfect clockwork workings:
		say "[The noun] is already there." instead;
	say "The [as a noun]workings[end as] are jammed already. I've done enough damage as it is!"

After inserting something suitable for jamming into the perfect clockwork workings for the first time:
	now the perfect clockwork workings are jammed;
	now the enormous penduluum is stopped;
	say "[as a noun]Clockwork[end as] – the movement of tick after tock – is the engine that drives the whole universe, every star, every planet, every leaf on every tree. It is unstoppable, unquestionable, unjudging, fair, precise and orderly. [as a noun]Clockwork[end as] continues, as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] says: it defeats us all.[paragraph break]But then the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] has never plunged [a noun] into the gear trace of a [as a noun]clock[end as] and watched the gears scream and shake and choke in fury. It is an ultimate heresy: the sound must be the sound of the demons that have come to take my springs.[paragraph break][if the enormous penduluum is ticking]The [as a noun]penduluum[end as] swings to a stop. There is sudden, terrible silence. [fire TRIG_JAMMED_CLOCK][end if]";

After inserting something suitable for jamming into the perfect clockwork workings:
	now the perfect clockwork workings are jammed;
	say "I jam the [as a noun]clock[end as] once more.";	

Instead of attacking the perfect clockwork workings:
	if the player is carrying something suitable for jamming (called the chock):
		try inserting the chock into the perfect clockwork workings instead;
	say "I don't have anything good to jam the [as a noun]clockwork[end as] with. Maybe I can the [as a noun]clock[end as] to stop another way."

Instead of attacking the perfect clockwork workings with something suitable for jamming (called the chock):
	try inserting the chock into the perfect clockwork workings.

[The after removing rule never fires, so instead, we use a dodgy instead line. ] 
[After removing something from the perfect clockwork workings:]

Instead of taking something when the noun is in the perfect clockwork workings:
	now the player is carrying the noun;
	[ since we never worry about inventory limits anyway!! ]
	now the perfect clockwork workings are running smoothly;
	if the heavy brass weights are present:
		now the enormous penduluum is ticking;
	say "I slide [the noun] from the [as a noun]workings[end as] of the [as a noun]clock[end as]. The cogs snap after them, but the clock, incredibly, begins to turn once more[if the heavy brass weights are present]. Even the [as a noun]penduluum[end as] begins to move[end if]."

Part 3 - Bishop's Library

First before making to leave when in the Bishop's Library and Return to the Cathedral is happening:
	try going west instead.
First before going inside when in the Bishop's Library and Return to the Cathedral is happening:
	try going west instead.

When Return To The Cathedral begins:
	remove the Archbishop from play;
	move the moon_backdrop to Bishop's Library.

Rule for printing the description of the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The small library is the first lit room I’ve seen in the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as], like the Bishop had somehow fixed the [as a noun]moon[end as] in place to shine through the [as a noun]stained glass[end as]. The room is filled with the shadows of saints and the letters of proverbs – across the desk floats <Italic>[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the Key</Italic>. But not now, Wren. Now speed is of the essence.[paragraph break]The [as a noun]Grandfather clock[end as] is [if the secret Clock Door is open]open and eerily silent[else]closed[end if]. The desk drawer is [if the single drawer is closed]closed[otherwise]open[end if].";

Instead of waiting in the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "This isn’t helping, Wren.";

Instead of going south from the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] must be locked – and even if it isn’t, [as a noun]Drake[end as]’s right outside.";

Rule for printing the description of the Archbishop's Desk during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "Moonlight from the [as a noun]window[end as] [if the blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk]picks out the [as a noun]blotter[end as] and[else]projects the words <Italic>[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the Key</Italic> across the surface and picks out[end if] the top [as a noun]drawer[end as] of the [as a noun]desk[end as].";

First unlocking rule for the Archbishop's Desk when the desk drawer key is off-stage during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I don’t have a [as a noun]key[end as]. There must be some other way to slip the [as a noun]catch[end as]." instead;

First unlocking rule for the single drawer when the desk drawer key is off-stage during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I don’t have a [as a noun]key[end as]. There must be some other way to slip the [as a noun]catch[end as]." instead;

Instead of unlocking the Archbishop's Desk with something during Return To The Cathedral:
	redirect the action from the Archbishop's Desk to the single drawer;
	try the current action instead;

Instead of opening or closing or pushing or pulling the Archbishop's Desk during Return To The Cathedral:
	redirect the action from the Archbishop's Desk to the single drawer;
	try the current action instead;

Understand "gap", "thin gap", "catch", "lock" as the single drawer when Return to the Cathedral is happening.

Rule for printing the description of the locked single drawer:
	say "There's a [as a noun]thin gap[end as] between the top of the [as a noun]drawer[end as] and the desk."
Rule for printing the description of the unlocked closed single drawer:
	say "The [as a noun]drawer[end as] is closed."
Instead of examining the open single drawer:
	try searching the drawer instead.
	

Definition: a thing is suitable for catch-slipping:
	if it is the knife, yes;
	if it is the blotter, yes;
	no;

Before inserting something suitable for catch-slipping into the locked single drawer during Return to the Cathedral:
	try unlocking the single drawer with the noun instead.

Instead of unlocking the locked single drawer with something suitable for catch-slipping during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I slide [the second noun] across the top of the [as a noun]drawer[end as] until it finds the [as a noun]catch[end as]. It shouldn’t be this easy – but it seems that the luck the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] is always saying doesn’t exist is with me. The [as a noun]lock[end as] turns over.";
	now the single drawer is unlocked;

Instead of opening or attacking the locked single drawer during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "[one of]I try and heave the [as a noun]drawer[end as], but it’s locked. Not very securely, perhaps, but locked all the same[or]I heave and struggle on the [as a noun]drawer[end as] - nothing. There's a [as a noun]catch[end as] closed inside[stopping].";

Instead of pushing the locked single drawer during Return to the Cathedral:
	say "I can't get my fingers in the [as a noun]gap[end as] between the [as a noun]drawer[end as] and the [as a noun]desk[end as] to move the [as a noun]catch[end as]."

Before pulling the single drawer:
	try opening the single drawer instead.
Before pushing the single drawer:
	try closing the single drawer instead.



After opening the unlocked single drawer during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I slide open the [as a noun]drawer[end as].";
	try searching the single drawer;

After searching the open single drawer during Return To The Cathedral:
	if the old iron crypt key is in the single drawer:
		say "Inside is the [as a noun]key[end as] the [as a noun]Bishop[end as] showed me: the [as a noun]old iron key[end as] that opens the crypt [as a noun]grate[end as].";
	otherwise:
		say "There’s nothing important inside.";

The description of the old iron crypt key is "The [as a noun]key[end as] is maybe as old as the [as a noun]Cathedralf[end as] walls themselves. Heavy and hooked like something broken off a boat’s anchor chain[one of]. Perhaps they use it to punish those who try to break into the crypts...[paragraph break]...the [as a noun]Figure[end as] had better hope he doesn’t meet me down there[once only].";

A blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk. It has description "[if the blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk]Projected across the [as a noun]blotter[end as] are the words <Italic>[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the Key</Italic>[otherwise]It’s just a [as a noun]blotter[end as], flat and wide, made of thin wood[end if]." 

Understand "words", "word", "proverb", "proverbs" as the blotter when the blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk.

Rule for writing a paragraph about the blotter when the blotter is not handled:
	now the blotter is mentioned;

Instead of looking under or pushing the blotter when the blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk:
	say "There's nothing there."

Instead of looking under or pushing the blotter when the blotter is on the Archbishop's Desk and the blotter is not handled [ not been picked up ever! ] and the single drawer has not been unlocked [ and we need a key ] and the desk drawer key is off-stage:
	move the desk drawer key to the player;
	say "I lift up a corner of the [as a noun]blotter[end as]. Underneath is a [as a noun]desk drawer key[end as]. I slip it free."
	
Instead of looking under the Archbishop's Desk:
	say "There's nothing there."

Instead of looking under the Archbishop's Desk when the single drawer has not been unlocked [ and we need a key ] and the desk drawer key is off-stage:
	move the desk drawer key to the player;
	say "I crouch down and look under the desk. There's a [as a noun]desk drawer key[end as]. I pick it up."

The desk drawer key is privately-named. It unlocks the single drawer. It has description "A small [as a noun]key[end as] for a [as a noun]desk[end as] [as a noun]drawer[end as]." Understand "desk key", "key", "drawer key", "desk drawer key" as the desk drawer key.

Rule for printing the description of the Archbishop's bookshelves during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I’m not here to read. I’m here to get that key!";

Rule for printing the description of the narrow windows during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The room is walled on one side by [as a noun]glass[end as] decorated with saints and wise proverbs. None of which is 'steal that key and then get running.'";

Rule for printing the description of the secret Clock Door when in the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The Bishop’s [as a noun]clock[end as] has stopped, maybe for the first time since it was made. And I did it. Me.";

After going through the secret Clock Door from the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "I ";
	if the player is carrying the blotter:
		move the blotter to the Archbishop's Desk;
		say "put the [as a noun]blotter[end as] back neatly on the desk and then"; 
	say "slip back through the [as a noun]clock[end as], closing its front [as a noun]doors[end as] as I go.";
	continue the action;

Rule for printing the description of the Bishop's Door when in the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] to the clerestory is almost certainly locked.";

Instead of going through the Bishop's Door from the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] to the clerestory is almost certainly locked.";

Instead of knocking on the Bishop's Door when in the Bishop's Library during Return To The Cathedral:
	say "That'd give [as a noun]Drake[end as] fright I'm sure - but I'd better not."

The moon_backdrop is a scenery, privately-named. Understand "moon" as the moon_backdrop. The printed name is "moon". "The [as a noun]moon[end as] is a silver pocket-watch, gazing down from far away."

Instead of entering or approaching the moon_backdrop:
	say "I can't fly, not tonight."

Instead of doing something when the moon_backdrop is physically involved:
	say "I can't fly, not tonight."

Part 4 - Choir

Chapter 1 - Lost Perpetuum trick

Last after going to the Cathedral Choir from the Secret Stair when the player is carrying the old iron crypt key during Return To The Cathedral:
	if TRIG_LOST_DECOY is unfired:
		now TRIG_LOST_DECOY is primed;
		try looking;
		fire TRIG_LOST_DECOY;
	otherwise:
		continue the action;

The decoy perpetuum can be lost. The decoy perpetuum is not lost.

TRIG_LOST_DECOY is a trigger. TRIG_LOST_DECOY can be primed. TRIG_LOST_DECOY is not primed.

Rule for firing TRIG_LOST_DECOY:
	now the decoy perpetuum is lost;
	say "The [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]’s not where I left it! It’s <Italic>gone</Italic>!";

After reading a command when the decoy perpetuum is lost:
	now the decoy perpetuum is not lost;
	now the player carries the decoy perpetuum;
	now TRIG_LOST_DECOY is not primed;
	say "Oh, no. There it is. I just didn’t see it in the dark.[paragraph break]I quickly pick it up. Now I'm ready.";
	reject the player's command;

Part 5 - Crypt

Before going inside when in the East Apse during Return to the Cathedral:
	try going northeast instead.

The matching key of the crypt grate is the old iron crypt key.

After unlocking the crypt grate with the old iron crypt key:
	say "I turn the [as a noun]key[end as] as slowly and quietly as I can. Tumblers move like ravens out of the way of a carriage: slowly, reluctantly, in hopping movements, croaking and clawing as they go.
Then the gate is open. Like a pin into a hinge joint, I enter the dark.";
	end the chapter;
	now the crypt grate is open;
	if the implicitly opening activity is not going on,
		try going the crypt grate instead;

Before opening the crypt grate when the player is carrying the old iron crypt key:
	try unlocking the crypt grate with the old iron crypt key instead.

Book 12 - Crypt

Part 0 - The Crypt Region

The Cathedral Crypt is a region. The Crypt Stairs, the Ossuary, the Crypt Landing, the Upper Vault, the Mortuary are in the Cathedral Crypt.

Every turn when in the Cathedral Crypt and a random chance of 1 in 3 succeeds and not looking and not listening and the remainder after dividing the turn count by 2 is 1 [ so we avoid consecutive turns ]: 
	say "[one of][as a noun]Cold wind[end as] leaks between the stones from somewhere.[or][as a noun]Damp[end as] trickles down the [as a noun]stone walls[end as].[or]A tiny noise echoes like a howl between the [as a noun]walls[end as].[or]Something scuttles across my foot.[or]A trickle of dust drifts from the ceiling.[or]A smell of dead things fills the air.[or][one of]I shiver.[or]The air tastes stale.[or]Something moves in the darkness.[at random][stopping]";

Instead of listening when in the Cathedral Crypt:
	say "There is utter silence down here, except for my tiny breathing."

Instead of smelling when in the Cathedral Crypt:
	say "The walls smell of dead things and old dirt."

Understand "crypt wall/walls", "stone wall/walls", "old", "ancient", "block/blocks", "stone block/blocks", "dust", "trickle of", "cold wind", "damp", "wind" , "foot" as the backdrop-walls when in the Cathedral Crypt.

Instead of examining the backdrop-walls when in the Cathedral Crypt:
	say "The walls are [as a noun]old stone blocks[end as], each the size of a man."

Instead of climbing the backdrop-walls when in the Cathedral Crypt:
	say "The only way out of here is with the real [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] in my hands."

Part 1 - Crypt Stairs

The Crypt Stairs is a room. "[one of]I turn and lock the [as a noun]grate[end as] behind me, snapping closed the padlock so that the [as a noun]Figure[end as] won’t suspect a trap - and trapping me down here with him.[or]This isn’t the time for fear, Wren, even if this is the place for it. There are steps leading down: I need to take them.[stopping]"

Instead of making to leave when in the Crypt Stairs: try going southwest.
Instead of going inside when in the Crypt Stairs: try going down.

The printed name of the Crypt Stairs is "Staircase".

[EU: I'm purposely omitting clickables from this passage.]
After going to the Crypt Stairs from the East Apse:
	now the crypt grate is closed; now the crypt grate is locked;
	say "From the window of my tiny room in the rafters I’ve often watched the shadow of the Cathedral of Time as it races round the streets of St Philip – leaping up with the dawn and not stopping for breath until nightfall.[paragraph break]Today I saw another shadow, grey instead of black, and inside the Cathedral not upon it. I followed that shadow all the way across the day.[paragraph break]And now night has fallen and I am a shadow myself. A shadow in the Cathedral: a darkness moving through the deeper darkness of the crypts. There are stairs leading down: I can smell them, cold air from far underground, with a hint of grease, a touch of the sweetness of lamps and beeswax.[paragraph break]Like a shadow, all the light is behind me.[paragraph break]Like a shadow, I won't stop moving till the day is done.";
	continue the action;

Instead of going southwest when in the Crypt Stairs:
	say "I can’t. I’ve locked the [as a noun]grate[end as] behind me[one of], and I need to move on[once only].";

Rule for printing the description of the crypt grate when in the Crypt Stairs:
	say "The thick iron [as a noun]grate[end as] leads back to the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as][one of], but I’m not going that way[once only].";

Before going through the crypt grate when in the Crypt Stairs:
	say "I need to keep going[one of]. I don’t have much time[once only]." instead;

Instead of opening the crypt grate when in the Crypt Stairs:
	try going the noun instead;

Instead of unlocking the crypt grate with something when in the Crypt Stairs:
	try going the noun instead;

Section 1 - Scenery

Some worn stone stairs are scenery in the Crypt Stairs. "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] are curved and rounded from hundreds of years of careful feet."

Instead of doing something when the worn stone stairs are physically involved:
	say "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] are almost invisible in the darkness. All I know is that they lead down."

Before entering or taking or climbing or approaching the worn stone stairs:
	try going down instead.

Part 2 - Ossuary

[
After going from the Crypt Stairs to the Ossuary when the player can see a lit candle (called their-light):
	remove their-light from play;
	say "My [as a noun]candles[end as] sputters, burning down to my fingers, and goes out. I drop the stub to the floor. <Italic>Perfect timing, Wren...</Italic> but I am not in the dark...";
	continue the action;
]

Chapter 1 - Description

The Ossuary is a room, down from the Crypt Stairs. "[one of]A few years ago, before I got to clock polishing, when I was still just coiling wire for my dinner, one of the Brothers died. His name was [as a noun]Brother Wilmslow[end as] but [as a noun]Calvin[end as] and [as a noun]Drake[end as] called him Brother Weak-slow because he was so old he couldn’t move any faster than a shuffle and had trouble lifting his fork. I don’t think I ever spoke to him and he was so near-blind he probably never saw me at all.[paragraph break]I don’t think he can see me now, either, even though that’s him, propped up in a stone [as a noun]niche[end as] in the [as a noun]wall[end as] of the [as a noun]tunnel[end as] to the south.[or]The [as a noun]walls[end as] are smooth stones but into some are built triangular [as a noun]niches[end as]. Can’t I just tell you about the [as a noun]niches[end as]? Do I have to tell you about the [as a noun]skeletons[end as] and [as a noun]bodies[end as] sitting up inside them?[paragraph break]Round the head of each is tied a small round dial – a [as a noun]deathwatch[end as]. They make no sound at all.[paragraph break]The [as a noun]tunnel[end as] goes south[if the Crypt Landing is unvisited]. So will I, once I gather the courage[end if].[stopping]"

Instead of listening to the Ossuary:
	say "The [as a noun]bodies[end as] are silent. They must be praying, counting seconds or reciting the Tangent.";

Before going inside when in the Ossuary:
	try going south instead.
Before making to leave when in the Ossuary:
	try going up instead.
Before going north when in the Ossuary:
	try going up instead.

Understand "gather courage" as a mistake ("[one of]It's not so easy for me, I'm down here![or]Okay, don't nag me, I'm trying![stopping]") when in the Ossuary.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Niches

Some triangular niches are scenery, in the Ossuary. "Not all the stone [as a noun]niches[end as] are full. Some have [as a noun]bodies[end as] sitting cross-legged, and some have sunken piles of bones with skulls surrounded by knees."
The printed name of the triangular niches is "niches".

Understand "triangle", "niche" as the triangular niches.

Instead of entering or climbing or approaching or hiding inside the triangular niches:
	say "I know I’m going to die down here all right, but I’m not that keen to get it over with!";

Instead of hiding from view when the triangular niches are visible:
	try entering the triangular niches.

Section 2 - Brother Wilmslow

Brother Wilmslow is a man, scenery, in the Ossuary. "[as a noun]Brother Wilmslow[end as] is sitting up, as if he’d fallen asleep at dinner. On his head is the small round dial of his [as a noun]deathwatch[end as]."

Understand "weakslow" as Brother Wilmslow.

Instead of doing something when Brother Wilmslow is physically involved:
	say "I don’t <Italic>want</Italic> to wake him.";

Rule for speaking with Brother Wilmslow:
	say "[as a noun]Brother Wilmslow[end as] was always deaf as well as blind. Now he’s mute too. It must be awful for him down here with nothing to do.";

Section 3 - Bodies

The bodies of dead monks are scenery, in the Ossuary. "They’re all Brothers, still robed, though some of the robes are only so much dust and cobweb."

Understand "dusty", "robed", "skeleton", "body", "skeletons", "heads", "sunken", "piles", "piles of",  "bones", "skull", "skulls", "knees", "robe", "robes" as the bodies of dead monks. The printed name of the bodies of dead monks is "skeletons".

Understand "bone" as the bodies of dead monks when the arm bone is not visible.

Before removing the bodies of dead monks from the bodies of dead monks [this covers "take bone from skeleton]:
	try taking the bodies of dead monks instead.

Before putting the linen strip on the bodies of dead monks:
	try fastening the linen strip to the bodies of dead monks instead.


Before wrapping the bodies of dead monks with the linen strip :
	try fastening the linen strip to the bodies of dead monks instead.

Before fastening the linen strip to the bodies of dead monks when the arm bone is off-stage and the dowel handle is not on-stage:
	try taking the bodies of dead monks;
	if the player is not carrying the arm bone, stop the action;
	try fastening the linen strip to the arm bone instead;


Before fastening the linen strip to the bodies of dead monks when the dowel handle is on-stage:
	say "Maybe I could use the [as a noun]dowel rod[end as] instead, that would be less icky." instead.

Before fastening the linen strip to the bodies of dead monks when the arm bone is off-stage:
	say "That's a horrible idea." instead.


Instead of taking or searching the bodies of dead monks:
	if the linen strip has been held and the dowel handle is not on-stage:
		now the player carries the arm bone;
		say "I pick out the oldest-looking [as a noun]bone[end as] I can, and hold it by one end, trying not to squirm. I can feel the ghost's hand holding me right back.";
	otherwise:
		continue the action;

Instead of taking or searching the bodies of dead monks:
	say "No chance ! I’m not digging around inside a [as a noun]skeleton[end as]. You must think I’m crazy!";

The arm bone is a thing. "I think it’s an [as a noun]arm[end as]." The printed name of the arm bone is "bone".

Section 4 - Deathwatches

Some deathwatches are scenery, in the Ossuary. "[one of]The [as a noun]deathwatch[end as] is a small round [as a noun]dial[end as] tied to the forehead of each Brother by a [as a noun]wire band[end as]. At first glance they seem still but really they are moving: the [as a noun]minute hand[end as] pushing forward then juddering back, as though the springs were too spent to get the hand around the dial.[paragraph break]But they aren’t – these springs will run until the soul has gone. Instead the watches themselves have been cobbled: for how could the [as a noun]watch[end as] move beyond the moment of death when their owners couldn’t?[or]The [as a noun]deathwatches[end as] tick endlessly over the moment of death, obsessing on it until the last of the soul is spent.[stopping]"

Understand "small round", "round", "dial", "dials", "wire", "wires", "band", "bands", "minute hand", "death watch", "watch" as the deathwatches.

Instead of taking the deathwatches:
	say "It’s not my time for one. Not yet.";

Instead of setting the deathwatches to something:
	try switching on the noun;

Instead of untying the deathwatches from:
	try taking the deathwatches.

Rule for supplying a missing second noun when untying the deathwatches from:
	change the second noun to the bodies of dead monks.

Instead of turning, repairing, pushing, pulling, switching on, switching off the deathwatches:
	say "If I turned the [as a noun]watch[end as] backward I might wake him. If I turned it forward he might crumble to dust. So I leave it alone.";

Section 5 - Tunnel

The tunnel is scenery in the Ossuary. "The [as a noun]tunnel[end as] leads southward, [as a noun]niches[end as] and [as a noun]skeletons[end as] on either side." Understand "stone", "passage" as the tunnel.

Instead of entering or approaching or climbing or taking the tunnel:
	try going south.

Part 3 - Landing

Chapter 1 - Description

The Crypt Landing is a room, south of the Ossuary. "[one of]Light! [once only]The [as a noun]tunnel[end as] from the north opens out here into a vaulted chamber and there are four iron [as a noun]torches[end as] burning in each corner. East and west are arched [as a noun]doorways[end as] like lidded eyes. By the south wall, a [as a noun]missing flagstone[end as] creates an [as a noun]opening[end as], further down into more darkness[one of]. Why is it I'm so certain that's the way I need to go?[or].[stopping]"

Before making to leave when in the Crypt Landing: try going north instead.
Before going inside when in the Crypt Landing: say "East or west ... or did you mean down?" instead.
Before going down when in the Crypt Landing: try going south instead.
Before going up when in the Crypt Landing: try going north instead.


The printed name of the Crypt Landing is "Main Chamber".

After going from the Ossuary to the Crypt Landing:
	say "[one of]I don’t want to meet the [as a noun]Figure[end as] here. Only one place to hide, and I don’t like it. So I keep going.[once only]";
	continue the action;

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Stairs

Some steep stairs are scenery, in the Crypt Landing. "Steep [as a noun]stairs[end as] – so steep I’d have to go backwards. They lead into pitch dark."

Understand "dark", "stair", "darkness", "steps", "staircase" as the steep stairs.

A missing flagstone is scenery, in the Crypt Landing. "A [as a noun]flagstone[end as] has been removed to reveal a [as a noun]dark stair[end as] leading down."

Understand "flag stone", "flag-stone", "opening" as the missing flagstone.

Before entering or approaching or hiding under or taking the missing flagstone:
	try going down instead.

Instead of doing something when the missing flagstone is physically involved:
	say "It's just a gap, leading into the [as a noun]dark[end as]."

Section 2 - Archways

Some carved archways are scenery in the Crypt Landing. "Each arch is topping by a carving: a [as a noun]skull[end as] on its side, the eye-[as a noun]sockets[end as] forming an [as a noun]hourglass[end as]."

Instead of approaching or taking or entering the carved archways:
	if the player's command includes "east/e", try going east instead;
	if the player's command includes "west/w", try going west instead;
	say "Which way, Wren? East or west?";

Understand "skull", "skulls", "arch", "archway", "arches", "archways", "doorway", "doorways", "carvings", "eye", "sockets", "hourglass", "hourglasses", "hour glass", "hour glasses", "east/e arch/archway/doorway", "west/w arch/archway/doorway" as the carved archways.

Section 3 - Lanterns

Some iron torches are scenery, in the Crypt Landing. "The [as a noun]torches[end as] are inlaid with carvings of the [as a noun]crescent moon[end as]. Their [as a noun]rope wicks[end as] burn slightly blue: fuel must be running into them through channels in the stone, but what kind of fuel it is that burns forever I can’t think[one of]. Maybe it’s dead men’s breath. That’d certainly explain the smell[once only]."

Understand "crescent", "moon", "rope", "wicks", "blue", "fire", "fires", "flame", "flames", "smelly", "light", "lights", "burning", "lantern", "lanterns", "gas" as the iron torches.

Understand "torch" as the iron torches when the player can not see the makeshift torch.

Instead of taking the iron torches:
	say "The [as a noun]torches[end as] are fixed to the wall[one of] – and if they’re fed from inside, they’d be no good removed anyway[or] and burn forever - I can't take them with me[or] forever[stopping]."
	
Instead of smelling the iron torches:
	say "The smell reminds me a little of the [as a noun]platform[end as] in the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s [as a noun]warehouse[end as]."

Before burning a candle when in the Crypt Landing:
	try inserting the noun into the iron torches instead.

Before closing a lit candle:
	say "I cup my hand around the [as a noun]flame[end as], for what little good it will do." instead.

Before extinguishing a lit candle with yourself when the Crypt Landing has been visited:
	now the noun is not lit;
	say "I pinch the [as a noun]candle[end as] [as a noun]flame[end as] between my fingertips." instead.

Before melting a candle on the iron torches:
	try inserting the noun into the iron torches instead.

Instead of inserting a lit candle into the iron torches:
	say "The [as a noun]candle[end as] is already lit.";

Instead of inserting an unlit candle into the iron torches:
	say "The [as a noun]candle[end as] melts at a ferocious rate, dowsing the wick so it cannot light. I take the [as a noun]candle[end as] back before it is lost completely."

Part 4 - Upper Vault

The Upper Vault is a room, west of the Crypt Landing. "[one of]I catch my breath as I enter. [once only]This room is a vault of ancient [as a noun]pagan relics[end as]: [if the player is carrying the lit makeshift torch]my torchlight[else][as a noun]torchlight[end as] from the [as a noun]arch[end as] back east[end if] gleams over [as a noun]water-clocks[end as] and [as a noun]candle-clocks[end as] and stranger [as a noun]devices[end as] I can’t even name, made of [as a noun]wood[end as] and [as a noun]bone[end as]. There’s no brass here, no cogs, no bearings, no blown glass. All these things: they should have all been destroyed.[paragraph break]As if I needed to add, the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] is <Italic>not</Italic> here[if sundial on shelf]. The only metal thing at all is the plate of a large [as a noun]sundial[end as], resting on a [as a noun]shelf[end as][end if]."

Before making to leave when in the Upper Vault: try going east instead.
Before going inside when in the Upper Vault: try going east instead.

An eastern arch is scenery, in the upper vault. "The [as a noun]arch[end as] leads back to the landing." Understand "east", "archway" as the eastern arch.

Instead of entering the eastern arch:
	try going east instead;

Some pagan relics are scenery, in the upper vault. Understand "candle", "clock/clocks", "water", "water-clock", "water-clocks", "candle-clock", "candle-clocks", "bone", "wood", "relic", "ancient", "device", "devices" as the pagan relics.

Instead of doing something when the pagan relics are involved:
	say "[one of]In the days before [as a noun]St Newton[end as] people had been ignorant of [as a noun]Penduluums[end as] and the Simple Approximation of the Small Sine, and were forced to use all sorts of foolish things to measure time. None of them worked, of course – water slides and marked candles and so forth. As [as a noun]Brother Reloh[end as] says, without a True understanding of Time, Time cannot <Italic>be</Italic>; just as without an understanding of Good, Good cannot.[paragraph break]Looking at all the things here I feel sorry for those primitive people, who must have always been waking up to find the day was over, or enduring nights that went on forever without any breakfast.[or]Leave them be. Their time is finally done.[stopping]"

A dusty shelf is scenery, in the upper vault. The printed name is "shelf". The description is "A stone [as a noun]shelf[end as] holding a few [as a noun]relics[end as][if the plate sundial is on the dusty shelf], including a brass [as a noun]sundial[end as][end if]."

Instead of searching the dusty shelf:
	try examining the dusty shelf.

A plate sundial is on the dusty shelf. The description is "The plate [as a noun]sundial[end as] is made of dark, lusty gold, and would probably be worth a packet if anyone could be demon-happy enough to buy it in the markets of the town. The [as a noun]gnomon[end as] is still precise but the underside is worn away, with a series of strange [as a noun]indentations[end as]."

After taking the plate sundial for the first time:
	say "I take the [as a noun]plate[end as] from the [as a noun]shelf[end as] - it's heavier than it looks.";

Understand "dark", "lusty", "gold", "razor", "sharp", "gnomon", "worn", "series", "series of", "sun dial", "dial" as the plate sundial.

Instead of looking under or turning or pushing or pulling the plate sundial when the plate sundial is not handled:
	say "It's not attached to anything or set anywhere. It's loose."

Instead of cleaning the plate sundial:
	say "There's some dust, but not as much as one might expect after so long... but it would still be heresy for a Church Clock Polisher to clean a [as a noun]sundial[end as]!"

Some strange indentations are part of the plate sundial. The description is "[as a noun]Notches[end as] and [as a noun]grooves[end as] in the metal, as if made to fit with something else." Instead of looking under or searching or touching the plate sundial: try examining the strange indentations instead. Instead of touching the strange indentations: say "They are beautifully carved." Understand "notch", "notches", "groove", "grooves", "underside" as the strange indentations.

Part 5 - Mortuary

The Mortuary is a room, east from the Crypt Landing. "[one of]The back of my neck starts prickling when I’m only a step inside. <Italic>Don’t be a fool, Wren. No ghosts in the machines down here</Italic>. No live ones anyway - this place is purely for the dead.[paragraph break][once only]The stone walls are cold, wet with the last breath of all the bodies that have been prepared on the large stone [as a noun]slab[end as] in the centre of the room. That's where they lie down. Blown-glass [as a noun]tubes[end as] lead from it either side, a third at the head end. The insides of the [as a noun]tubes[end as] are the colour of burnt bacon.[paragraph break]By the [as a noun]arch[end as] to the west is a [as a noun]shelf[end as] carved out of the rock. On it are the [as a noun]tools[end as] of the trade: [as a noun]spices[end as], [as a noun]cloth[end as], [as a noun]oil[end as], so forth. At least there’s no lathe or whetstone or screws."

Before making to leave when in the Mortuary: try going west instead.
Before going inside when in the Mortuary: try entering the slab instead.

Instead of sleeping when in the Mortuary:
	say "No chance. My eyes are staying wide open."

Instead of smelling the Mortuary:
	say "The room is heavy with death, like an October night heavy with rain.";

Instead of listening to the Mortuary:
	say "The wet walls are whispering, Wren.";

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Arch

A western arch is scenery, in the mortuary. "The [as a noun]arch[end as] leads back to the landing." Understand "west", "archway" as the western arch.

Instead of entering the western arch:
	try going west instead;

Section 2 - Slab

A slab is scenery, in the Mortuary. "The [as a noun]slab[end as] is a bit larger than a man, a bit wider than a man, and has a shallow [as a noun]impression[end as] in the shape of a man, with his legs together and wrists by the blown-glass [as a noun]tubes[end as]." Understand "large slab", "stone slab", "large stone slab", "impression", "shallow impression", "shape", "man", "shape of a man", "table" as the slab.

Instead of entering or approaching the slab for the first time:
	say "I can imagine myself getting on, lying down. Feet together, arms spread wide, wrists beside the two glass [as a noun]tubes[end as] leading away into the floor. Feeling myself falling asleep, the smell of cinnamon and anise in my nose. My eyes wide open. I can imagine it, as if the room was <Italic>making</Italic> me imagine it. As if the room was trying to <Italic>eat</Italic> me.[paragraph break]I take a step back from the stone [as a noun]slab[end as]. It's nothing, Wren. Just a big old block of rock. There's nothing to see here.";

Instead of entering or approaching the slab:
	say "You must think I’m crazy! The ghosts would eat my bones for breakfast!";

Instead of looking under, pushing or pulling or turning or taking the slab:
	say "It's an altar's worth of solid stone. It was never moved: the room was carved in around it."

Some blown-glass tubes are part of the slab. The description is "A [as a noun]tube[end as] runs from the top and bottom of the table. Two more run down on either side. Their colour is like rust." Understand "blown glass", "glass", "tube", "rust", "brown" as the blown-glass tubes.

Instead of opening or pushing or pulling or turning or taking or attacking or touching or cleaning the blown-glass tubes:
	say "[one of]They might shatter if I so much as touched them. And who knows what ghosts that might let free?[or]Honestly, I'm too scared to touch them, whatever else![stopping]".
	
Instead of tasting or inflating the blown-glass tubes:
	say "I don't dare find out what they taste of."
	
Instead of searching the blown-glass tubes:
	say "They lead away into the floor."

Section 3 - Shelf

A rock shelf is scenery, in the Mortuary. "The [as a noun]shelf[end as] is hewn from the rock itself, to hold the [as a noun]bandages[end as] and the [as a noun]oil[end as]. A rack of [as a noun]spices[end as], and behind that, a stack of [as a noun]wooden dowels[end as]."

Understand  "tools", "tools of the trade", "shelves" as the rock shelf.

Instead of entering the rock shelf:
	say "It’s too small for me to hide.";

Instead of putting something on the rock shelf:
	say "I’m not here to tidy things away.";

Instead of searching the rock shelf:
	try examining the rock shelf instead;

Section 4 - Spices

Some jars of spices are scenery, on the rock shelf. "The [as a noun]shelves[end as] are lined with the [as a noun]spices[end as] of death: [as a noun]cinnamon[end as], [as a noun]cardamom[end as], [as a noun]mace[end as] and [as a noun]star anise[end as]. They sit in [as a noun]jars[end as] between the rolls of [as a noun]bandages[end as] and the bowl of [as a noun]oil[end as].";

Understand "jar of", "jar", "cinnamon", "cardamon", "mace", "star anise", "anise", "of death", "death" as the jars of spices.

Instead of taking the jars of spices for the first time:
	say "I don’t think Cook would like me adding those to the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] soup.";

Instead of taking the jars of spices:
	try smelling the jars of spices;

Instead of looking under the jars of spices:
	say "There's a small pile of [as a noun]dowels[end as] on the [as a noun]shelf[end as] behind the [as a noun]jars[end as]."

Instead of inserting something into the jars of spices:
	say "I don't want to be carrying that smell around with me."

Instead of smelling, tasting, eating, pushing, searching, opening the jars of spices:
	if the player's command includes "cinnamon":
		say "[smell-text in row 1 of the Table of Spice Smells]";
	else if the player's command includes "cardamon":
		say "[smell-text in row 2 of the Table of Spice Smells]";
	else if the player's command includes "mace":
		say "[smell-text in row 3 of the Table of Spice Smells]";
	else if the player's command includes "anise":
		say "[smell-text in row 4 of the Table of Spice Smells]";
	else:
		say "The smell is enough to make me feel ill." instead;
	say paragraph break;

Table of spice smells
smell-text
"[as a noun]Cinnamon[end as]: a smell like an empty pit, the dust disappearing under my shoes at the edge."
"[as a noun]Cardamon[end as]: a smell like dizziness, or losing consciousness in water." 
"[as a noun]Mace[end as]: a smell like a rock, thumping down on my skull." 
"[as a noun]Anise[end as]: a smell like a strong wind, pulling me away into the air." 

Instead of smelling when in the Mortuary:
	choose a random row in the Table of Spice Smells;
	say "[smell-text entry][paragraph break]";

Section 5 - Oils

A bowl of thick black oil is scenery, on the rock shelf. "At one end of the [as a noun]shelf[end as] is a wide shallow [as a noun]bowl[end as], thick with dark black [as a noun]oil[end as]." Understand "dark black oil", "dark oil" as the bowl of black oil.

Instead of taking the bowl of black oil for the first time:
	say "I try lifting the bowl, only to find that it isn’t a [as a noun]bowl[end as]: it’s a carved part of the [as a noun]shelf[end as]."

Instead of taking the bowl of black oil:
	try attacking the bowl of black oil;

Instead of attacking or pulling or turning the bowl of black oil:
	say "I don’t have a stone-hammer with me.";

Instead of touching, tasting, searching, drinking, eating the bowl of black oil:
	say "The [as a noun]oil[end as] is as thick as slug-slime and as black as death. Who knows what it’s made from?";
	
Before smelling the oil:
	say "The [as a noun]oil[end as] is sweet, and thick, and smells of long darkness." instead.

Instead of inserting the dowel handle into the thick black oil:
	say "The [as a noun]dowel[end as] wouldn't hold the [as a noun]oil[end as] well."

Check inserting something into the thick black oil:
	say "I'd only get it horribly messy." instead.

Section 6 - Bandages

Some bandages are scenery, on the rock shelf. "Long [as a noun]rolls[end as] of [as a noun]cloth[end as] fill half the [as a noun]shelf[end as] – dry [as a noun]cloth[end as], the same colour as bone and as dry as bone[if the linen strip is off-stage]. One end of the [as a noun]roll[end as] hangs loose, like a lolling tongue[end if]."

Understand "bandage", "rag" as the bandages when the linen strip is not visible.
Understand "roll/rolls", "cloth", "roll/rolls of", "dry bandages"  as the bandages.

A linen strip is a thing. The description is "[if oiled]A strip of [as a noun]linen[end as], gleaming smugly with [as a noun]oil[end as].[else]I’ve torn a strip of [as a noun]linen[end as] from the [as a noun]bandage roll[end as], about the length of my arm.[end if]"

The linen strip can be oiled or unoiled. The linen strip is unoiled.

Understand "bandage", "cloth", "rag" as the linen strip.
Understand "dry", "bone dry", "bone-dry" as the linen strip when the linen strip is unoiled.
Understand "wet", "soaking", "dripping", "oiled", "oil soaked", "soaked", "oil-soaked" as the linen strip when the linen strip is oiled.

Instead of attacking the bandages:
	try taking the bandages.
Instead of attacking the bandages with something:
	try taking the bandages.
Instead of slicing the bandages with something:
	try taking the bandages.

Instead of taking the bandages when the linen strip is off-stage:
	say "I tear away a [as a noun]strip[end as] of the dry [as a noun]linen cloth[end as].";
	now the linen strip is unoiled;
	now the player carries the linen strip;
	set pronouns from the linen strip;


Instead of taking the bandages:
	say "I’ve already got enough. I’m not here to wrap myself up – or anyone else.";

Instead of inserting the oiled linen strip into the bowl of black oil:
	say "It’s already soaked through with [as a noun]oil[end as].";

Instead of inserting the unoiled linen strip into the bowl of black oil:
	now the linen strip is oiled;
	say "[one of]I dip the [as a noun]linen strip[end as] once, twice in the [as a noun]bowl[end as] of [as a noun]oil[end as]. It comes out gleaming.[or]I soak the [as a noun]linen strip[end as].[stopping]"

Rule for printing the name of the oiled linen strip:
	say "oil-soaked linen strip";


Rule for printing the name of the unoiled linen strip:
	say "bone-dry linen strip";
	
Instead of attacking the linen strip:
	say "I could shred it in moments, but it wouldn't do me any good."

Section 7 - Dowels

Some dowels are scenery, on the rock shelf. "There’s a pile of stocky [as a noun]wooden dowels[end as] on the [as a noun]shelf[end as]. I don’t even want to think what they’re for."

Understand "stocky", "wooden", "dowel", "rod" as the dowels when the dowel handle is not visible.
Understand "wooden dowels", "stocky dowels", "stock wooden dowels" as the dowels when the dowel handle is visible.

Instead of taking the dowels when the dowel handle is off-stage:
	now the player carries the dowel handle;
	set pronouns from the dowel handle;
	say "I pick up a [as a noun]dowel[end as]. It’s a good length, but far too light to use as a cudgel. (And I’m too much of a wimp to use it as one, too.)"

Instead of taking the dowels:
	say "I’ve already got one.";

The dowel handle is a thing. The description is "It’s a good length, the size and shape of my forearm." The printed name is "handle". Understand "wooden handle", "wooden dowel", "rod", "wooden rod" as the dowel handle.

Instead of slicing the dowel handle with the knife:
	say "The [as a noun]dowel[end as] is a just a stick of wood. I could whittle it, maybe, but it wouldn't make a good weapon."

Instead of burning the dowel handle:
	say "The wood on its own wouldn't burn with a flame; it would only smoulder."
Before melting the dowel handle on the iron torches:
	try burning the dowel handle instead.
Before inserting the dowel handle into the iron torches:
	try burning the dowel handle instead.


Chapter 3 - Making a torch

Section 1 - Construction

A thing can be a torch stick.
The dowel handle is a torch stick.
The bone is a torch stick.

Instead of wrapping a torch stick thing (called the stick) with the linen strip:
	remove the noun from play;
	remove the second noun from play;
	now the stick is part of the makeshift torch;
	now the linen strip is part of the makeshift torch;
	now the player carries the makeshift torch;
	say "I roll the [if the linen strip is oiled]oozing [end if][as a noun]linen[end as] around the end of the [if the stick is a dowel handle][as a noun]wooden handle[end as][else][as a noun]bone[end as][end if], once, twice, until it's tight. I’m left holding something that looks a lot like a [if the linen strip is unoiled]dry [as a noun]torch[end as][else][as a noun]torch[end as], ready to be lit[end if]."

Before putting the linen strip on a torch stick thing (called the stick):
	try wrapping the stick with the linen strip instead.
Before fastening the linen strip to a torch stick thing (called the stick):
	try wrapping the stick with the linen strip instead.


Section 2 - Description

The makeshift torch is a thing. The description of the makeshift torch is "It’s the best I can do for a [as a noun]torch[end as]: [as a noun]cloth[end as] wrapped around a [as a noun]handle[end as][if the arm bone is part of the makeshift torch] that once held a hand of its own[end if]. [if the linen strip is oiled]The [as a noun]cloth[end as] drips black [as a noun]oil[end as][else]The [as a noun]cloth[end as] head is dry[end if]."

Rule for printing the description of the lit makeshift torch:
	say "Clockwork can do a lot of things: tell the time, move the wheels of a cart, operate pistons and machines, move the stars and the Earth, even predict the weather. But clockwork can’t make Light. Only Fire makes Light – and that’s what I’ve got. I’m a Second Assistant Clock Polisher, and I’ve got fire on a [as a noun]stick[end as].";

Rule for printing the name of the lit makeshift torch:
	say "brightly burning torch";

Understand "handle" as the makeshift torch when the bone is part of the makeshift torch.

Section 3 - Oiling

Instead of inserting the lit makeshift torch into the bowl of black oil:
	say "No chance. It might explode.";

Instead of inserting the unlit makeshift torch into the bowl of black oil when the linen strip is oiled:
	say "The [as a noun]torch[end as] is already soaked with [as a noun]oil[end as]. Any more and it might blow up!";

Instead of inserting the unlit makeshift torch into the bowl of black oil:
	now the linen strip is oiled;
	say "I dip the head of the [as a noun]torch[end as] into the [as a noun]oil[end as] until it gleams.";

Section 4 - Lighting

Before burning the makeshift torch when in the Crypt Landing:
	try inserting the makeshift torch into iron torches instead.

Instead of burning the makeshift torch:
	say "I can't make the [as a noun]torch[end as] ignite just by looking at it."

Before putting the makeshift torch on iron torches:
	try inserting the makeshift torch into iron torches instead.

Before melting the makeshift torch on iron torches:
	try inserting the makeshift torch into iron torches instead.

Instead of inserting the lit makeshift torch into the iron torches:
	say "My [as a noun]torch[end as] is already burning fiercely."	

Instead of inserting the unlit makeshift torch into the iron torches when the linen strip is oiled:
	now the makeshift torch is lit;
	say "I lift the head of the [as a noun]torch[end as] up to the flame – and it catches in a second. The walls around me leap with light!";

Instead of inserting the unlit makeshift torch into the iron torches when the linen strip is unoiled:
	remove the linen strip from play;
	remove the makeshift torch from play;
	now the player carries everything that is part of the makeshift torch;
	say "[one of]I reach up to light the [as a noun]linen[end as] head of the torch. It ignites and begins to glower with flame. Then ashes break off, and soon the whole thing has burned to nothing.[or]I burn the [as a noun]linen[end as] on the torch-head to nothing. It’s no good without fuel![stopping]";

section 4b - lighting from a candle

Before burning the makeshift torch when the player can see a lit candle (called the firesource):
	say "(with [the firesource][command clarification break])";
	try melting the makeshift torch on the firesource instead.

Before putting the makeshift torch on a lit candle:
	try melting the makeshift torch on the second noun instead.
Before inserting the makeshift torch into a lit candle:
	try melting the makeshift torch on the second noun instead.

Instead of melting the lit makeshift torch on a lit candle:
	say "My [as a noun]torch[end as] is already burning fiercely."	

Instead of melting the unlit makeshift torch on a lit candle when the linen strip is oiled:
	now the makeshift torch is lit;
	say "I touch the head of the [as a noun]torch[end as] to the [as a noun]candle[end as] flame – and it catches in a second. The walls around me leap with light!";

Instead of melting the unlit makeshift torch on a lit candle when the linen strip is unoiled:
	say "The [as a noun]linen[end as] smoulders a little but simply won't catch."

Section 5 - Lighting the strip without a handle

Before burning the linen strip when in the Crypt Landing:
	try inserting the linen strip into iron torches instead.

Instead of burning the linen strip:
	say "I can't make the [as a noun]cloth[end as] ignite just by looking at it."

Before putting the linen strip on iron torches:
	try inserting the linen strip into iron torches instead.

Before melting the linen strip on iron torches:
	try inserting the linen strip into iron torches instead.

Instead of inserting the oiled linen strip into iron torches:
	remove the linen strip from play;
	say "The [as a noun]oil[end as] ignites quickly, and starts to burn my fingers! I drop it and stamp it to ashes on the floor."

Instead of inserting the unoiled linen strip into iron torches:
	remove the linen strip from play;
	say "The [as a noun]strip[end as] crackles into fire in seconds and burns away." instead.

Section 5b - Lighting on a candle

Before burning the linen strip when the player can see a lit candle (called the firesource):
	say "(with [the firesource][command clarification break])";
	try melting the linen strip on the firesource instead.

Before putting the linen strip on a lit candle:
	try melting the linen strip on the second noun instead.
Before inserting the linen strip into a lit candle:
	try melting the linen strip on the second noun instead.

Instead of melting the unoiled linen strip on a lit candle:
	say "The [as a noun]linen[end as] smoulders a touch but won't catch."

Instead of melting the oiled linen strip on a lit candle:
	remove the linen strip from play;
	say "The [as a noun]oil[end as] ignites quickly, and starts to burn my fingers! I drop it and stamp it to ashes on the floor."


Section 6 - Disassembling

Before removing something torch-part from the makeshift torch:
	say "A [as a noun]torch[end as] is a useful thing to have down here. I'm keeping it intact, not taking it apart!" instead.
Before untying something torch-part from the makeshift torch:
	say "A [as a noun]torch[end as] is a useful thing to have down here. I'm keeping it intact, not taking it apart!" instead.
Before taking the linen strip when the linen strip is a torch-part:
	say "A [as a noun]torch[end as] is a useful thing to have down here. I'm keeping it intact, not taking it apart!" instead.

Before doing something when a torch-part is involved:
	if the noun is a torch-part:
		redirect the action from the noun to the makeshift torch;
	if the second noun is a torch-part:
		redirect the action from the second noun to the makeshift torch;
	try the current action instead;

Definition: a thing is a torch-part:
	if it is part of the makeshift torch, yes;
	no;

Part 6 - Dark Stair

Chapter 1 - Description

The Dark Stair is a room, south from the Crypt Landing. "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] down from the landing end in midair. Everything beyond is [as a noun]darkness[end as][if the player is carrying a lit candle], so vast the light of my [as a noun]candle[end as] is lost, like a dust-mote in the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] itself. If I had a hundred [as a noun]candle[end as]s, maybe I could see something. As it is, with this [as a noun]candle[end as], I cannot see - I can only <Italic>be</Italic> seen...[else].[end if]"

Before going up when in the Dark Stair: try going north instead.
Before making to leave when in the Dark Stair: try going north instead.
Before going inside when in the Dark Stair: try going down instead.
Before going south when in the Dark Stair: try going down instead.

After going from the Dark Stair to the Crypt Landing when the lit makeshift torch was not visible:
	say "I climb gratefully back out of the [as a noun]darkness[end as].";
	continue the action.

Rule for printing the description of the Dark Stair when the player can see the lit makeshift torch:
	if the gnomon tip is in the location:
		say "There’s a gap, between the end of the [as a noun]stairs[end as], and the start of the smooth stone [as a noun]slope[end as] that disappears downwards into the dark. It’s no [as a noun]staircase[end as], but there are worn-down depressions down its length. It’s a handspan wide and no more.[paragraph break]";
	otherwise:
		say "I’m at the bottom of a staircase with nothing but the dark below.[paragraph break]";
	say "The step before the last step is wider than the others: on the left a metal [as a noun]sconce[end as] is fixed to the wall[if the makeshift torch is in the sconce], holding my [as a noun]torch[end as] proudly[end if]. On the right is a low stone [as a noun]pedestal[end as]. If I started to fall I could grab onto either!"

After going from the Crypt Landing to the Dark Stair when TRIG_DARK_STAIR is unfired:
	fire TRIG_DARK_STAIR;
	continue the action;

TRIG_DARK_STAIR is a trigger.

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_DARK_STAIR when the player has the lit makeshift torch:
	say "The [as a noun]stairs[end as] disappear downwards. I must be lower than the [as a noun]Abbey[end as] well by now, maybe even lower than the spring of [as a noun]St Philip[end as] itself. My hands are running over stone bricks – then slick rock seamed with rough minerals.[paragraph break]Then, quite suddenly, the [as a noun]stairs[end as] come to an end in mid-air. Below them is empty space: my [as a noun]torch[end as] is like the scratch of a fingernail on the Polar Ice. It’s as though the world was hollow and I had reached its rotten core.";

Rule for firing unfired TRIG_DARK_STAIR:
	if the player can see a lit candle:
		say "Almost total [as a noun]darkness[end as]. I'm taking it one step at a time, like I was climbing a ladder. Then just as I'm getting used to the pace, the next step gets deeper and deeper and I look down to see nothing, not the slightest glimmer of stone beneath my weight... ";
	else:
		say "Total [as a noun]darkness[end as]. I’m feeling my way – one step, then another, like I was coming down the ladder from my room. Then just as I’m getting the hang of it, the deep step gets deeper and deeper and I realise my foot is heading out into nothingness because the [as a noun]stairs[end as] have stopped… ";
	say "with no time to pull back I sit down, sharply. Below me is empty air – could be a handspan, could be the height of a tower. The air is still, and cold. The [as a noun]darkness[end as] around could be the size of the night.";

Instead of dropping something in the Dark Stair:
	say "I can’t risk it rolling and disappearing into space.";

Instead of examining the lit candle in the Dark Stair when the player cannot see the lit makeshift torch:
	say "The [as a noun]candle[end as] throws out a circle of light; nothing like enough to fill this cavernous space."

Instead of facing down when in the Dark Stair and the player cannot see the gnomon tip:
	say "Endless dark."

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Rule for deciding the concealed possessions of the Dark Stair:
	if the player can see the lit makeshift torch, no;
	if the particular possession is the sconce, yes;
	if the particular possession is the granite column, yes;



Section 1 - Staircase

A dangling stair is scenery, in the Dark Stair. "[if the player can see the gnomon tip]The [as a noun]stairs[end as] have joined to a smooth stone [as a noun]slope[end as] that slides downward into the dark[else]Each step is a carved stone block, dangling from the block before. In the end they’re all supported by nothing. Only [as a noun]darkness[end as] lies below[end if]."  Understand "staircase", "stairs" as the dangling stair.

Understand "darkness", "dark", "space" as the dangling stair when the player cannot see the gnomon tip.

Before ascending or climbing the dangling stair:
	try going up instead.

Before descending the dangling stair:
	try going down instead.

Instead of entering or approaching or taking the dangling stair:
	try going up instead.

Instead of entering or approaching or taking the dangling stair when the player can see the gnomon tip:
	try going down instead.

Instead of jumping off the dangling stair:
	try jumping instead.

Instead of touching the dangling stair:
	say "The stones are strangely warm - I had expected them to be cold."

Section 2 - Sconce

A sconce is scenery, a container, in the Dark Stair. "It’s an iron [as a noun]pike[end as] with a [as a noun]bracket[end as] set on the wall. The [as a noun]bracket[end as] is wrought to look like the rays of a sun." 

Instead of taking the sconce:
	say "It’s fixed into the stone. That means it’s a solid handhold.";
 
Instead of climbing the sconce:
	say "If I want to go back up I'm going to run back up the [as a noun]stairs[end as], not up the wall."

Instead of pushing or pulling or turning or attacking the sconce:
	say "[one of]It's been here for centuries. It takes more than the likes of me to move something like that[or]It's fixed into the stones[stopping]."

Understand "solid", "wrought", "iron", "metal", "sconce", "pike", "bracket", "sun", "rays", "holder" as the sconce.

After inserting the lit makeshift torch into the sconce:
	say "I slot the [as a noun]torch[end as] neatly into the [as a noun]sconce[end as]. Light pours down over the [as a noun]pedestal[end as] opposite.";

After taking the lit makeshift torch when the makeshift torch was in the sconce:
	say "I lift the [as a noun]torch[end as] back out of the [as a noun]sconce[end as].";

Section 3 - Pedestal

A granite column is scenery, a supporter, in the Dark Stair. "A [as a noun]granite column[end as], waist-high on a man which means chin-height to me. The surface is marked with [as a noun]ridges[end as] and [as a noun]slots[end as] in some kind of design – maybe there used to be something here, some kind of treasure that was ripped away – or a goblin – or a heretical device powered by water or the movement of ants." 

The printed name of the granite column is "pedestal". Understand "low", "stone", "pedestal" as the granite column.

Instead of pushing, pulling the granite column:
	try turning the noun instead;

After putting the plate sundial on the granite column:
	now the plate sundial is part of the granite column;
	say "The [as a noun]sundial[end as] fits the pedestal-top precisely, almost as if the ancients who built it were capable of engineering. But primitives had no Mechanics. It was [as a noun]St Newton[end as] who brought us out of the dark."

Instead of touching the granite column:
	say "[as a noun]Granite[end as] has a texture like sand, only harder than time itself."

Instead of putting something on the granite column:
	if the noun is the plate sundial, continue the action;
	if the noun is the decoy Perpetuum Mobile:
		say "I need to replace the <Italic>real</Italic> [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] - otherwise the [as a noun]Figure[end as] will know it's a trap!" instead;
	say "I don't think [the noun] [is-are] meant to fit the pedestal."

Instead of climbing or entering or standing on the granite column:
	say "It's pretty narrow. I don't want to be teetering on top of that, when there's a drop like this one below me..."

The granite surface is part of the granite column. Understand "ridge", "chiselled", "lines", "neat" , "ridges", "dust", "slot", "slots", "markings", "design" as the granite surface. The description is "A series of neat [as a noun]lines[end as], chiselled into the stone, too deep to see to down them. They must be filled with dust now." Instead of touching the granite surface: say "It's beautifully carved."

Section 4 - Gnomon Control (Assembled Pedestal)

Definition: the granite column is assembled if the plate sundial is part of it.
Definition: the granite column is unassembled unless it is assembled.

The granite column has a number called the shadow setting. The shadow setting of the granite column is 4.

Instead of rotating when the noun is the unassembled granite column:
	say "The [as a noun]pedestal[end as] seems to shift but only for a moment before locking up[one of]. It might be a crank with a cam or a cam without a key, as the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] would say, but either way it won’t go[once only].";

Before doing something when the plate sundial is involved and the granite column is assembled:
	redirect the action from the plate sundial to the granite column;
	try the current action instead;

Instead of taking the assembled granite column:
	say "The [as a noun]sundial[end as] seems to have locked into place and I can’t see how[one of]. With a sinking feeling I realise I’ve made things easier for the [as a noun]Figure[end as], not harder[once only].";

Rule for printing the description of the assembled granite column:
	say "The [as a noun]sundial[end as] sits on the [as a noun]granite pedestal[end as], slotted neatly into place. [if the lit makeshift torch is in the sconce]The [as a noun]torchlight[end as] casts a needlepoint shadow: the time is [shadow setting of the granite column in words][otherwise]The light of my [as a noun]torch[end as] is making the time leap like crazy, as though the Earth had come loose from its bearing and was rolling end over end past the sun[end if]."

Before rotating when the noun is the assembled granite column and the player carries the makeshift torch:
	say "If I'm going to shift this [as a noun]pedestal[end as] around I’ll need my hands free. I can't do it holding this [as a noun]torch[end as]." instead;

Instead of turning the assembled granite column:
	[ We rig turning forwards to go a bit faster towards 12 o'clock, for those who do things the brute force way. ]
	let time to midnight be 12 - the shadow setting of the granite column;
	let turn distance be a number;
	if time to midnight is greater than 1:
		if time to midnight is greater than 4:
			let time to midnight be 4;
		now turn distance is a random number between 1 and the time to midnight;
	else:
		now the turn distance is 1;
	let n be the shadow setting of the granite column + turn distance;
	while n is greater than 12:
		change n to n - 12; [13 becomes 1]
	try number-setting the noun to n;

Instead of turning the assembled granite column backwards:
	let n be the shadow setting of the granite column - 1;
	while n is less than 1:
		change n to n + 12; [0 becomes 12]
	try number-setting the granite column to n;

Instead of number-setting the assembled granite column to when the number understood is less than 1:
	say "The clock face reaches from one to twelve.";

Instead of number-setting the assembled granite column to when the number understood is greater than 12:
	say "The clock face reaches from one to twelve.";

Instead of number-setting the assembled granite column to when the number understood is the shadow setting of the granite column:
	say "The [as a noun]shadow[end as] is already pointing at [shadow setting of the granite column in words].";

Instead of number-setting the assembled granite column to when the shadow setting of the granite column is 12:
	say "I can’t get the [as a noun]gnomon[end as] to turn any more[one of]. Maybe the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] was hoping the [as a noun]Figure[end as] would simply run off this stair into the dark. And instead, I’ve saved him...[or].[stopping]";

Instead of number-setting the assembled granite column to:
	change the shadow setting of the granite column to the number understood;
	say "[one of]I grab the edges of the [as a noun]pedestal[end as] and heave. To my surprise, it turns without trouble, like some great weight had been quietly counterbalanced away.[or]I put both hands on the [as a noun]pedestal[end as] and drag it round.[or]I haul on the [as a noun]pedestal[end as].[stopping]";
	consider the gnomon rules;

Instead of setting the assembled granite column to "midnight/noon":
	try number-setting the granite column to 12 instead.

Instead of setting the assembled granite column to:
	say "The [as a noun]sundial[end as] is labelled with numbers, from one to midnight."

The gnomon rules are a rulebook.

A gnomon rule when the shadow setting of the granite column is 12:
	move the gnomon tip to the Dark Stair; 
	say "The shadow on the face of the [as a noun]gnomon[end as] creeps round to midnight and after settling for a moment, the [as a noun]stairs[end as] underneath me start to shake, as if they were being lifted from underneath! <Italic>Now you’ve done it, Wren. This whole place is going to fall! </Italic>[paragraph break]But it doesn’t. Instead, something massive – stone, moving on ancient castors – wheels into place at the end of the [as a noun]stairs[end as]. A smooth stone [as a noun]slope[end as], leading down." instead;

A gnomon rule when the shadow setting of the granite column is 1 or the shadow setting of the granite column is 11:
	say "The shadow settles at [shadow setting of the granite column in words] o’clock[one of]. In the [as a noun]darkness[end as], I hear something grumble and groan in response, like there was a giant trapped down there and the [as a noun]sundial[end as] was the lock on its cage.[or]. Whatever’s down there is right by me![or].[stopping]" instead;

A gnomon rule:
	say "The shadow moves to [shadow setting of the granite column in words] o'clock. [one of]In the dark, something whispers and mutters.[or]Whatever’s down there in the [as a noun]darkness[end as] grumbles again, like it was dragging gigantic feet.[or]The grumbling is louder now.[or]Something is definitely moving down there, in the dark, and by the sound of it, it’s the size of the [as a noun]Cathedral[end as] itself.[or]The machinery responds, huge and awful.[stopping]" instead;

Section 5 - Gnomon Tip

The gnomon tip is privately-named, scenery. 
Understand "smooth", "slope", "stone slope", "wire" as the dangling stair when the player can see the gnomon tip.

Instead of going down from the Dark Stair when the player can not see the gnomon tip:
	say "There’s nothing but space there! I’m not jumping!";

Instead of jumping in the Dark Stair:
	try going down;

Part 7 - Gnomon

After going from the Dark Stair to the Middle of the Gnomon:
	say "I make my way, one foot after another, down the narrow [as a noun]slope[end as] of stone...";
	continue the action;

The Middle of the Gnomon is a room, down from the Dark Stair. "The light from above quickly disappears. I’m left [if the player can see the lit makeshift torch]with just my torch[else]in darkness, worse than before[end if]. There aren’t even any walls to hang on to. I can feel the stone underfoot getting thinner and thinner, until I’m standing on a wire..."

The printed name of the middle of the gnomon is "Gnomon".

After deciding the scope of the player when in the middle of the gnomon:
	place the contents of the Henge in scope;

Instead of jumping when in Middle of Gnomon:
	say "Are you mad? The drop out there could go on forever!"
	
Before going north in the Middle of the Gnomon:
	try going up instead.
Before going south in the Middle of the Gnomon:
	try going down instead.
Before making to leave when in the Middle of the Gnomon:
	try going up instead.
Before going inside in the Middle of the Gnomon:
	try going down instead.

Part 8 - Henge

Instead of listening when in the Henge:
	say ""

After going from the Middle of Gnomon to the Henge:
	say "Another few minutes and I reach the bottom.";
	continue the action;

After assembling available exits when in the Henge and the giant stones are not explored:
	remove {west} from the viable directions, if present;

Chapter 1 - Description

The Henge is a room. "I’m standing at the base of the [as a noun]gnomon[end as] in a cavern that rivals the dome of the sky for its size. Around its perimeter are twelve [as a noun]stones[end as]: twelve giants, staring down at me. This is a [as a noun]sundial[end as], all in shadow, with no sun to cast a time. Water drips from the ceiling. The air is cold[if the giant stones are explored].[paragraph break]Between two of the [as a noun]stones[end as] is a [as a noun]door[end as] of dark iron. It’s almost invisible to the west[end if]."

Before making to leave when in the Henge: try going up instead.
Before going inside when in the Henge:
	if the giant stones are explored, try going west instead;
	say "I walk around the rim of the [as a noun]Henge[end as]. [run paragraph on]";
	try entering the giant stones instead;
Before going down when in the Henge:
	try going inside instead..

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Stones

Some giant stones are scenery, in the Henge. "Twelve [as a noun]stones[end as], one for every hour, with the tallest to the twelve."  Understand "twelve", "tallest", "stone", "rock", "rocks", "hour", "hours" as the giant stones.

The giant stones can be explored. The giant stones are not explored.

Before entering or approaching the giant stones when the giant stones are explored:
       try going west instead.

Instead of entering, pushing, pulling, turning, approaching, searching the giant stones:
	now the giant stones are explored;
	say "Between two [as a noun]stones[end as] is a shadow and that shadow is a [as a noun]door[end as], to the west.";

Instead of examining the giant stones when the giant stones are not explored:
	try entering the giant stones instead.
        
The iron_door_glimpse is a glimpse backdrop, in the Henge, identifying the Iron Vault Door. Understand "iron", "door", "dark", "shadow" as the iron_door_glimpse. The printed name is "iron door". The description is "A squat [as a noun]door[end as] of iron lies like a shadow between two [as a noun]stones[end as] to the west."

Instead of entering the iron_door_glimpse: try going west instead.

Section 2 - Gnomon

The stone slope is a door, scenery, open, not openable, up from the Henge, down from the middle of the gnomon. Understand "step", "steps", "stair", "stairs", "staircase", "gnomon" as the stone slope.

Rule for printing the description of the stone slope when in the middle of the gnomon:
	say "It must have taken a million footsteps from a thousand people to wear these [as a noun]steps[end as] into the [as a noun]stone[end as].";

Rule for printing the description of the stone slope when in the Henge:
	say "The [as a noun]gnomon[end as] of the sundial climbs away into the darkness overhead.";

Instead of taking or approaching or climbing the stone slope:
	try entering the stone slope.

Instead of jumping off the stone slope when in the Middle of the Gnomon:
	try jumping instead.

Section 3 - Sundial

The face of the sundial is scenery, in the Henge. "[one of]The dial must be the work of a king, built by slaves for the worship of masses. It must have been ground level once, in full sunlight, maybe between a river and the sea. Now the whole of [as a noun]St Philip[end as] lies above it. Every bog, spring and screw, built on top of this: a clock that runs by the sun. All that machinery, Wren, built above a <Italic>lie</Italic>.[or]The [as a noun]sundial[end as] is vast, stopped with twelve giant standing [as a noun]stones[end as].[stopping]"

Understand "dial" as the face of the sundial.

The printed name of the face of the sundial is "sundial".

Part 9 - Iron Door

Chapter 1 - Description

A room called the Iron Entry is west of the Henge. "[if the Iron Vault Door is closed]The iron [as a noun]door[end as] is closed, hung between two [as a noun]stones[end as] as though it was just a curtain of metal and not a [as a noun]door[end as] at all. On its surface is embossed the Winding Key, but it looks small and feeble compared to the great [as a noun]gnomon[end as] behind me[else]The great iron [as a noun]door[end as] has swung open. Time for me to go inside[end if].[paragraph break]Above the [as a noun]door[end as] is a [as a noun]head[end as] carved in brass. The [as a noun]Head[end as]’s eyes are watching me."

The printed name of the Iron Entry is "Iron Door".

Instead of waiting in the Iron Entry for the first time:
	say "It doesn't do any good.";

Instead of waiting in the Iron Entry:
	say "[one of]It doesn't do any good.[or]Still nothing.[purely at random]";

Before going inside when in Iron Entry:
	try going west instead;

Before going up when in Iron Entry:
	try going east instead.

Before making to leave when in Iron Entry:
	try going east instead;

Chapter 2 - Scenery - Iron Door

The Iron Vault Door is a door, scenery, west of the Iron Entry, east of the Inner Vault. The Iron Door is closed and locked. The description is "[if closed]The [as a noun]door[end as] is solid iron and closed as solidly as iron can be. The [as a noun]Head[end as] looms over it, eyes staring[else]The door is open. Beyond is some kind of chamber[end if].".


Understand "solid" as the Iron Vault Door.

The printed name of the iron vault door is "iron door".

[Leaving this out improves the response to "unlock door", although the response to "west" suffers somewhat. ]

An unlocking rule for the iron Vault Door when the current script is empty:
	say "I try the [as a noun]door[end as]. It’s locked, of course.";
	change the current script to {SE_BRASSHEAD_1, SE_BRASSHEAD_2, SE_BRASSHEAD_3, SE_BRASSHEAD_4, SE_BRASSHEAD_5, SE_BRASSHEAD_6};

An unlocking rule for the locked iron Vault Door:
	say "It's locked, of course." instead.

Instead of opening the locked Iron Vault Door when the current script is empty:
	say "I try the [as a noun]door[end as]. It’s locked, of course.";
	change the current script to {SE_BRASSHEAD_1, SE_BRASSHEAD_2, SE_BRASSHEAD_3, SE_BRASSHEAD_4, SE_BRASSHEAD_5, SE_BRASSHEAD_6};

Instead of opening the locked Iron Vault Door:
	say "It’s locked tight, of course.";
	
Instead of unlocking the Iron Vault Door with something:
	say "There is no <Italic>keyhole</Italic> on the [as a noun]door[end as]."

After going through the iron vault door from the iron entry:
	say "I step through into the vault, expecting sudden stinging death any second.";
	continue the action;
	
Instead of opening the open Iron vault Door:
	say "I don't need to - the Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] has opened it for me.".
	
Instead of closing the open Iron Vault Door when the Inner Vault is not visited:
	say "[one of]Why? I need to go in. [once only]There's no turning back, not any more."

Understand "chamber" as the Iron Vault Door when the Iron Vault Door is open.

The embossed winding key is part of the Iron Vault Door. Understand "winding-key" as the embossed winding key. The description is "The Winding Key is moulded deep into the iron." Understand "embossing" as the embossed winding key.

Instead of pushing or pulling or attacking or turning or touching or cleaning the embossed winding key when the Iron Vault door is locked:
	say "The [as a noun]door[end as] does not magically open.";

Instead of pushing or pulling or attacking or turning or touching or cleaning the embossed winding key:
	say "The metal is cold as ice."

Chapter 3 - Scenery - Brass Head

Section 1 - Description

A solid brass head is a person, scenery, in the Iron Entry. "The [as a noun]head[end as] is solid brass. Its dull [as a noun]eye-sockets[end as] [if the current script is empty]have opened[else]stare right back at me[end if]. The expression could skin a cat.". Understand "dull", "open eyes", "eye sockets", "eyes", "eye-sockets", "eye-socket", "eye socket" as the solid brass head.



Section 2 - Script

The solid brass head has a number called the loop count. The loop count of the solid brass head is 0.

SE_BRASSHEAD_1 is a scripted event. The display text is "[one of]Then, with a terrible silence, the eyelids of the Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] slide open. The moment is so smooth I don't notice until it's already staring at me. [once only]The [as a noun]Head[end as] opens its mouth and speaks. 'Time. Is.'".

SE_BRASSHEAD_2 is a scripted event. The display text is "[one of]The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] says nothing more. It’s [as a noun]eyes[end as] are watching me. It’s waiting.[or]The Brass Head is waiting.[stopping]".

SE_BRASSHEAD_3 is a scripted event. The display text is "The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] speaks again[from-distance]. 'Time. Was.'".

SE_BRASSHEAD_4 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]Head[end as] waits again[one of]. For me to prove myself, although I can’t think how[once only].".

SE_BRASSHEAD_5 is a scripted event. The display text is "The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] speaks[from-distance]. 'Time. Past.'".

SE_BRASSHEAD_6 is a scripted event. The display text is "The [as a noun]eyes[end as] above the door snap closed. The chamber is silent once more.".

To say from-distance:
	if the location is not the Iron Entry, say " [one of]from across the Henge[or]voice booming across the [as a noun]sundial[end as][cycling]";

Section 3 - Script loop count

Before firing SE_BRASSHEAD_1:
	increment the loop count of the brass head by 1;

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_4 when the loop count of the brass head is 1:
	say "If this [as a noun]door[end as] is the vault and the [as a noun]Head[end as] is a lock, then the [as a noun]Figure[end as] must know a way through. There’s no keyhole. No mechanism to be undone. Nothing the [as a noun]Abbot[end as] could have given him to get by, except perhaps a word.[paragraph break]But what word would serve as a key to the vaults?";

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_6 when the loop count of the brass head is 1:
	say "This is where it ends then, Wren: this is how far you get. To the final door, where I’ll wait, [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] in hand, until the [as a noun]Figure[end as] finds me and kills me, down here in the dark where no-one will ever find my body. If I’m really lucky he’ll fill me up with oil and spices and leave next to [as a noun]Brother Wilmslow[end as].[paragraph break]I’m try to think of something comforting. All the [as a noun]Abbot[end as]’s old phrases. [as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s rough manner. The [as a noun]Archbishop[end as] – who spoke to me, a polisher! – and told me to be calm. [as a noun]Patience[end as], he’d said. [as a noun]Patience[end as] is the key.[paragraph break]So I’d better be patient. The [as a noun]Figure[end as] will come, soon enough.";

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_1 when the loop count of the brass head is 2:
	say "'[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the key.' The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] is nothing but patient. For a crazy moment I wonder if I could somehow escape the [as a noun]Figure[end as] when he finds me here by pretending to be a machine, by standing as still – as patient – as the [as a noun]Head[end as] itself.[paragraph break]But it wouldn’t work. My arms are shaking. I’m tired. I’m sweating. I’m no machine. I’m just a fledgling. I don’t even know how I <Italic>got</Italic> here.";

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_3 when the loop count of the brass head is 2:
	say "If there’s a word, I’m thinking, it must be a sacred word to survive in a place like this. Something from the Heart of the Church: something based on its precision, its perfect cog-teeth and meshing spokes. Every wheel within a wheel, every part in its place.";

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_5 when the loop count of the brass head is 2:
	say "The Great Principles: [as a noun]Timing[end as], [as a noun]Precision[end as]... something else? Something that talks to the tick-tock of things, the even pace of history, that never rushes and trips but always gets there in good time?";

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_2 when the loop count of the brass head is 3:
	say "'[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the key,' I mutter to myself. '[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the key.'[paragraph break]The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] is <Italic>watching</Italic> me. It almost looks <Italic>hungry</Italic>."

After firing SE_BRASSHEAD_5 when the loop count of the brass head is 3:
	now the iron vault door is unlocked;
	now the iron vault door is open;
	change the current script to {};
	say "'[as a noun]Patience[end as] is the key...' I murmur.[paragraph break]The [as a noun]Head[end as] frowns. '[as a noun]Patience[end as],' it replies.[paragraph break]The iron [as a noun]door[end as] swings open.";

Section 4 - Interaction

Instead of talking to the solid brass head when SE_BRASSHEAD_1 is unfired:
	say "It's just a statue. It can't hear me." instead.

Instead of talking to the solid brass head when the current script is empty:
	say "'Hello there?'[paragraph break]But the [as a noun]Head[end as] is lifeless.";

Instead of talking to the solid brass head:
	fire CT_HEAD_HELLO.

Instead of waking the solid brass head when SE_BRASSHEAD_1 is unfired:
	say "It's just a statue.";

Instead of waking the solid brass head when the current script is empty:
	say "It seems lifeless. Completely inert. ";
	if the Iron Vault Door is open:
		say "Almost like it never moved.";
	else:
		say run paragraph on;
		try opening the Iron Vault Door;

Instead of touching the solid brass head:
	say "The [as a noun]Head[end as] is smooth and cool."

Instead of waking the solid brass head:
	say "The [as a noun]Head[end as] is awake... if the [as a noun]Head[end as] is awake at all."

Instead of giving something to the solid brass head:
	try showing the noun to the solid brass head.

Instead of showing something to the solid brass head:
	if SE_BRASSHEAD_1 is unfired:
		try waking the solid brass head instead;
	if the current script is empty:
		say "The [as a noun]Head[end as] appears lifeless now." instead;
	say "The [as a noun]Head[end as] doesn't appear to notice [the noun]. It's still staring at my eyes. Perhaps that's all it knows how to see."


Rule for speaking with the solid brass head when SE_BRASSHEAD_1 is unfired:
	say "It’s a statue of a [as a noun]Head[end as]. It can't hear me!" instead;

Rule for speaking with the solid brass head when the current script is empty:
	say "Nothing. [one of]The [as a noun]Head[end as] is totally lifeless. It’s not listening any more than the stones of the Henge listened to me whimpering on the way down the [as a noun]Gnomon[end as]. [once only]Maybe I only dreamt that it moved." instead;

The conversation table of the solid brass head is the table of brass head conversation.

Table of brass head conversation
topic			conversation
"patience"		CT_HEAD_PATIENCE
"hi" or "hello" or "hiya" or "open" or "sesame"	CT_HEAD_HELLO

The default topic of the solid brass head is CT_HEAD_WRONG

CT_HEAD_PATIENCE is a conversation topic.

Rule for firing CT_HEAD_PATIENCE:
	now the iron vault door is unlocked;
	now the iron vault door is open;
	change the current script to {};
	say "The Brass [as a noun]Head[end as] frowns slightly, as if concentrating. '[as a noun]Patience[end as],' it replies. Nothing happens.[paragraph break]Then the iron [as a noun]door[end as] swings open.";

CT_HEAD_WRONG is a conversation topic. The response text is "[one of]The [as a noun]Head[end as] raises a single eyebrow. 'No,' it intones.[or]The [as a noun]Head[end as]’s mouth arches down at the edges.[or]The [as a noun]Head[end as] rolls dull [as a noun]eyes[end as].[or]'No,' the [as a noun]Head[end as] intones.[stopping]".

CT_HEAD_HELLO is a conversation topic. The enquiry text is "[one of]'Hello?'[or]'Can you open, please?'[or]'Is there anyone there?'[cycling]". The response text is "[one of]The [as a noun]Head[end as] looks back, expressionlessly. 'No.'[or]'No,' the [as a noun]Head[end as] intones.[cycling]".



Part 10 - Inner Vault

Chapter 1 - Description

The Inner Vault is a room. "[one of]I woke up this morning a Second Assistant Clock Polisher, who would have been on slops-duty if I’d touched any mechanisms. Now, through some kind of horrible accident – the kind that shouldn’t happen in a clockwork world – I’m here, in the Inner Vaults of the [as a noun]Cathedral of Time[end as].[paragraph break]The [as a noun]door[end as] swings shut behind me. I guess now I'm a relic too.[paragraph break][once only]This room is completely bare, except for a steel [as a noun]altar[end as] like a fallen brick. Ticking quietly to itself on top is the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as][if the steel altar supports the decoy perpetuum mobile]. Or so it seems..[end if]. The door is back east."

Before making to leave when in the Inner Vault:
	try going east instead.

Before going up when in the Inner Vault:
	try going east instead.

Before going inside when in the Inner Vault:
	say "There's no further I can go." instead.


Does the player mean doing something with the real perpetuum mobile when the location is the Inner Vault:
	it is likely.

Chapter 2 - Scenery

Section 1 - Iron Door

After going through the iron vault door from the iron entry:
	now the iron vault door is closed;
	continue the action;

Rule for printing the description of the iron vault door when in the inner vault:
	say "The door has closed.";

Rule for deciding the concealed possessions of the iron vault door when in the inner vault:
	if the particular possession is the solid brass head:
		yes;
	no;



Section 2 - Steel Altar

The steel altar is a supporter, scenery, in the inner vault. "The [as a noun]altar[end as] is plain metal, almost dull. On the [as a noun]altar[end as] is [if the steel altar supports the decoy perpetuum][as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s [as the decoy perpetuum]replacement[end as] for [end if]the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as]."

Rule for printing the description of the real Perpetuum Mobile when the Inner Vault is visited:
	say "It’s more beautiful than I remember. The rolling [as a noun]bearing[end as] across its surface is like the sun on a hazy winter evening. Watching it is hypnotic.";

Instead of putting the decoy perpetuum mobile on the steel altar:
	try taking the real perpetuum mobile;

Instead of replacing the decoy perpetuum mobile with the real perpetuum mobile:
	try taking the real perpetuum mobile;

Instead of replacing the real perpetuum mobile with the decoy perpetuum mobile:
	try taking the real perpetuum mobile;

Does the player mean putting the decoy perpetuum on the steel altar:
	it is very likely;

Does the player mean taking the real perpetuum when the steel altar supports the real perpetuum:
	it is very likely;

Does the player mean taking the decoy perpetuum when the player is carrying the decoy perpetuum:
	it is very unlikely.

Does the player mean taking the decoy perpetuum when the steel altar supports the decoy perpetuum:
	it is very likely;

