Section 7 - Game end - attacking the wound spring


Instead of attacking the wound spring:
	now the player wears my lucky clock key;
	try attacking the wound spring with my lucky clock key instead;

Instead of attacking the wound spring with my lucky clock key:
	say "The [as a noun]winding slot[end as] on the [as a noun]Figure[end as]'s neck is a thumb-width big. A tiny target, like fitting a tweezer's head into the cracks of a mesh-system. But precision is what clockwork’s all about. I’m a novice. I’m trained. And I know that the [as a noun]winding slot[end as] goes all the way in. Right into the [as a noun]Figure[end as]’s clockwork soul.[paragraph break]I grab the [as a noun]key[end as] from around my neck and plunge it in. The [as a noun]Figure[end as] doesn’t notice – it can’t feel! – until I start to turn.[paragraph break]The spring begins to coil. The [as a noun]Figure[end as] jerks up, releasing [as a noun]Covalt[end as], who falls back to the floor. It struggles to turn, trying to make a grab for me, but the [as a noun]ravens[end as] on either side are battering it back. I only need another turn or two. The spring inside the neck is getting almost impossible to move and then...[paragraph break]CRACK![paragraph break]I can hear the spring spinning off inside the Figure’s chest. Hear the awful noise as it catches in a mechanism deep inside. I quickly make the sign of the Winding Key as the [as a noun]Figure[end as] crumples: first one leg, then the other. The floor shakes as it falls.[paragraph break]The last of its energy is enough to look up at me. 'You,' it hisses through lips that don’t move as it speaks. Its voice sounds almost sad. 'You know not what you do.'[paragraph break]And then, silence. The [as a noun]ravens[end as] settle, one on each of [as a noun]Covalt[end as]’s shoulders. The old man looks exhausted, his face the colour of bruised tomatoes.";
	now the third-state of Covalt is 10;

After reading a command when the third-state of Covalt is 10:
	say "'That’s the most incredible piece of clockwork I ever saw,' he says slowly, furiously, his eyes glowing with anger. 'And you just destroyed it.'[paragraph break]Can [as a noun]Covalt[end as] really be angry that I...[paragraph break]'That was well done, young Wren.' He coughs. 'But I tell you, when I build something like that metal man, I’m going to give him an off-switch. Stupid to have to break such a fine machine. Whoever built that... a genius.' He strokes his beard, and gets to his feet – taking the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as] from me as he does so. He’s looking back to normal already: a sour-faced moody old man. 'A genius, and an idiot, too.'[paragraph break]'But who was it?' I demand. 'Who built it?'[paragraph break]'That’s the question,' [as a noun]Covalt[end as] answers, gravely. 'For every spring that’s wound on this Earth, there’s a human hand turning the key. No divine mechanics down here. We may be free of this thing –' and he lands a kick squarely in the still automaton’s chest - 'but someone out there was behind it. Someone planning an army and after the [as a noun]Perpetuum[end as].'[paragraph break][as a noun]Covalt[end as] grunts, and scratches his chest with a thick finger. He seems almost to be falling asleep.[paragraph break]'There's clockwork, Wren,' he says. 'But then again, there's <Italic>clockmakers</Italic>.'";
[ Bodge: it seems in debug you can win the game in this activity, but in release you can't. So we fix the player's command to something safe... ]
	replace the player's command with "look";
	end the game saying "To Be Continued...";

[ ... and then ignore it to end the game ]
First before when the third-state of Covalt is 10: [ this is absurd, I'm just trying to get the game to stop! ] 
